window.PROSE=window.PROSE||{};Object.assign(window.PROSE,{"frankie-knuckles-tears":["A slow, melancholy house track made with the Japanese producer Satoshi Tomiie and sung by Robert Owens. Soft piano chords, deep bass and a gentle drum-machine groove support Owens's rich, grieving voice, which sings of a love that has ended. The texture is spacious and elegant. Nothing is rushed. Where most dance records aim for euphoria, this one aims for heartbreak, and the piano's sad, circling chords are its signature.","By 1989 Knuckles was back in New York and house had become an international phenomenon, especially in Britain. The London label FFRR issued Tears as part of a wave of American house sold to British buyers. Tomiie was a young producer from Tokyo, and Owens a Chicago singer who had worked with Larry Heard. The track belongs to the 'deep house' current that brought jazz and soul harmonies back into the music.","Listen for how sparse the drum programming is; Knuckles leaves room for Owens's voice rather than filling every gap, a lesson learned from years of mixing soul records on the Warehouse's system. The piano part moves through richer harmonies than most 1980s house, a sign of Tomiie's jazz leanings.","It became a club classic and a template for a more sophisticated, song-based deep house, the strand later carried by producers such as Kerri Chandler. Critics consistently place it among Knuckles's finest productions, and among the records that showed house could express sorrow as convincingly as joy. It also launched Tomiie's long international career, an early sign that house was no longer only an American music.","Play it after Your Love to hear Knuckles's romanticism turn melancholy. Listen to how Owens phrases slightly behind the beat, letting lines trail over the bar, and to how the piano answers him in the gaps. It works best late at night at moderate volume. Then hear Inner City's Good Life for Detroit's warmer, more hopeful answer."],"orbital-chime":["A chiming melodic pattern, bright and bell-like, repeats over a skipping breakbeat and a warm, rounded bassline. The track builds slowly in layers, adding and removing sounds, and moves between rushing euphoria and quieter, reflective passages. It lasts about twelve minutes in its full form and never loses its sense of joyful discovery. The bell-like chime that gives it its name is the sound of British rave at its most innocent.","The brothers Phil and Paul Hartnoll recorded Chime at home in Sevenoaks, Kent, reportedly for next to nothing, and it came out first on a small label before FFRR reissued it in 1990 and it became a hit. They named the duo after the M25 orbital motorway that carried ravers to illegal parties in fields and warehouses around London. It was the high tide of rave, and the Conservative government was already moving against unlicensed parties.","When they performed it on television, the brothers reportedly wore T-shirts protesting the poll tax, a quiet sign of the politics that ran under British rave. Listen too for how much of the track is made from a single melodic cell, changed by adding voices and shifting the rhythm around it, a technique closer to minimalism than to pop song.","It was one of the first British rave records to become a genuine hit and made Orbital's name. It is still regarded as a defining record of its moment, and a proof that rave could be tender and melodic rather than merely hard.","Listen to the full twelve-inch version rather than an edit. Notice when the breakbeat drops out and the chime is left almost alone. Then compare Phuture's Acid Tracks to hear what the Chicago sound became once it crossed the Atlantic."],"frankie-knuckles-the-whistle-song":["A light, airy instrumental built around a playful melody on flute, whistling and fluttering over a smooth house groove. Soft keyboard chords and a gentle, rolling bass fill in beneath. It is cheerful, elegant and uncomplicated, closer to a gentle jazz-funk tune than to the stark machine tracks of early Chicago house. That bird-like flute tune is the whole identity of the record.","The track came from Knuckles's album Beyond the Mix, made for Virgin in the early 1990s after he had moved back to New York and joined David Morales in the Def Mix production team. Major labels were now signing house producers, and Def Mix remixes were polishing pop records for clubs. This record showed what house sounded like when it had money, studio time and live players.","Listen for how much of the track is played rather than programmed; the flute and keyboards breathe in ways a sequencer does not. That shift back toward live musicianship, in a genre born of drum machines, was part of what later came to be called 'soulful house'.","It was a big club hit and remains one of Knuckles's most loved records, though some critics found it too pretty and easy compared with the raw Chicago tracks of the 1980s. Today it tends to be heard as an early example of the polished, musical house of the 1990s.","Enjoy it for what it is: sunny, graceful dance music. Listen for the conversation between the flute and the keyboard, and for the moments the drums fall back and the tune floats alone. Then go back to Your Love to hear how far house had travelled in four years, from a hypnotic machine loop to something close to jazz-funk, and on to Daft Punk's Discovery for its sunny heirs."],"the-prodigy-charly":["Fast, chopped breakbeats, a buzzing synth riff and, cutting through them, a sample of a cartoon cat's meow and a child's voice from a British public-information film. The effect is manic and comic, a nursery rhyme dropped into a rave. The track moves quickly through sections, piling on sound. The meow, instantly recognisable to British listeners who grew up with the films, is its hook.","Liam Howlett made the track in Essex at the peak of the British rave boom, when records were being pressed and sold for weekend parties in fields and warehouses. The sample came from the Charley Says films, short safety cartoons shown on British television in the 1970s in which a cat warned children about strangers and dangers. XL Recordings released it in 1991, and it became a surprise hit.","The cartoon's safety messages, about not wandering off with strangers, took on a knowing double meaning at raves, where drug use was widespread; listeners heard both the childhood memory and the joke. Listen too for the breakbeats: they are chopped and sped up in a style that would soon grow into jungle.","Its success set off a rash of imitations sampling children's television, and critics derided the resulting 'toytown techno' as a cheap novelty. The Prodigy moved quickly away from the style, but the track is now remembered fondly as a period piece and a first sighting of their chaos.","Take it as a snapshot of 1991 rave: listen for the speed of the breakbeats and the delight in collage. Then hear Music for the Jilted Generation three years later to hear how much darker and more serious the band became."],"jeff-mills-waveform-transmission-vol-1":["An album of fast, relentless techno tracks built from pounding drum-machine kicks, clattering hi-hats and short, harsh synth loops that repeat and slowly mutate. There are no vocals and almost no melody; each track is a single idea pushed to its limit through repetition, with small changes creating huge tension. The effect is hypnotic and physical, like being inside a machine running flat out. The hard, dry sound of the 909 drum machine is everywhere.","Mills had left Underground Resistance and was working between Detroit and New York when Berlin's Tresor club and label, based in a vault beneath a former department store near the Berlin Wall's former line, released this album. Techno and the new reunited Berlin were growing up together, and Detroit producers found a devoted audience there. The album marks the moment Detroit techno turned harder and more minimal.","Listen to how Mills uses tiny changes: a loop may repeat for minutes with only a filter slowly opening or a hi-hat shifting position, and your ear starts to hear the change as dramatic. This is a DJ's music; many tracks were designed to be mixed and layered live rather than heard alone.","It became a touchstone for the harder, faster strand of 1990s techno, especially in Europe, and helped cement Mills's reputation as a producer of fierce minimalism. Some listeners find it abrasive and monotonous; admirers hear it as pure, disciplined intensity.","Play it loud and let the repetition work; resist skipping. Pick one sound in a track, a hi-hat or a synth stab, and follow it for several minutes: what seemed static turns out to be constantly shifting. Then compare Steve Reich's Drumming for another music where sustained repetition and gradual change create drama, and Robert Hood's Minimal Nation for the even sparer path it opened."],"underground-resistance-hi-tech-jazz":["Released under the alias Galaxy 2 Galaxy, it is a soaring, melodic techno piece built around a bright synth lead that sings like a jazz horn over lush, shifting chords and a propulsive drum-machine groove. It builds, breaks down, and builds again, with the melody lifting over each new entry of the rhythm. It is uplifting and cinematic rather than hard. That singing lead line, climbing and falling like an improvised solo, is the signature.","Underground Resistance, led by 'Mad' Mike Banks, positioned themselves as a masked, militant collective opposed to the major-label music industry and to the neglect of Detroit. Yet alongside their aggressive records they made music of great beauty, and Hi-Tech Jazz is the most famous. The Galaxy 2 Galaxy project aimed to link techno to the heritage of Detroit jazz and funk, and to Sun Ra's cosmic Afrofuturism.","The title is a manifesto: Banks was arguing that techno belonged to the lineage of black American music, especially jazz, rather than to European electronic music alone. In later live performances the group played the piece with musicians, including a saxophonist, making that connection audible.","It has become one of Detroit techno's most loved records, frequently cited as proof that the genre can be as emotionally expressive as any jazz or soul. Critics have increasingly read UR's work as part of an Afrofuturist tradition that runs from Sun Ra through Parliament.","Listen to the lead melody as if it were a trumpet solo. Then play Sun Ra's Space Is the Place and Parliament's Mothership Connection to hear the cosmic lineage UR claimed, and Strings of Life for another Detroit record reaching for the sky."],"orbital-orbital-2":["Orbital's second album, known by the colour of its cover, flows as a continuous journey through long, evolving tracks. Warm, layered synths, rolling breakbeats and slowly unfolding melodies build from quiet ambience to euphoric climax. Tracks such as Lush 3, Halcyon + On + On and Impact (The Earth Is Burning) move between the dancefloor and contemplation. Halcyon's shimmering, looping vocal sample and bell-like synths are the album's emotional centre.","By 1993 British rave had moved from illegal parties into licensed clubs and festivals, and a new 'intelligent' or 'album' electronic music was taking shape for home listening. The Hartnolls were becoming known as an extraordinary live act, playing sequencers and synthesisers on stage rather than miming. The Conservative government was drafting the law that would target outdoor raves the following year.","Halcyon is named after the tranquilliser Halcion, and the Hartnolls have said it was written for their mother, who had been dependent on it; knowing this, the track's aching beauty takes on a different colour. The album also opens with a phased spoken fragment about time becoming a loop, which links back to their first album; the two records seem to circle into each other.","It was widely praised on release and is now regarded as one of the defining British electronic albums of the 1990s. Their live performance of Halcyon at Glastonbury in 1994 became a landmark in the acceptance of electronic music at rock festivals.","Listen straight through; the album is designed as a single arc. Start with Halcyon if you want one track. Then hear Brian Eno's Music for Airports for the ambient mood, and Steve Reich for the method of slowly layered repetition."],"robert-hood-minimal-nation":["An album of radically stripped-down techno. Each track is built from a few elements: a steady kick drum, a ticking hi-hat, a single clipped chord or synth stab repeating in a tight, funky pattern. Almost nothing else. But the grooves are relentless, and tiny changes in accent and timing keep them alive. The music sounds skeletal, but it swings. Those short, repeating stabs, like a machine hiccuping in rhythm, are the sound of minimal techno.","Hood had been part of Underground Resistance and moved with Jeff Mills toward a leaner sound; Minimal Nation came out on Mills's Axis label. Hood has described minimalism as a response to Detroit itself, a stripping back to what was essential, a funk made from very little. The title named a subgenre that was about to spread across Europe, especially in Berlin and Cologne.","Listen for what is missing: basslines, melodies, breakdowns, vocals. Hood removes almost everything that dance music usually relies on, and yet the tracks remain funky because of precise rhythmic placement. The swing comes from how the hi-hats and stabs sit just off the beat, a drummer's sensibility carried into a machine.","On release it was a cult record; within a few years it was recognised as the foundation of minimal techno, a genre that dominated European clubs in the 2000s. Critics now treat it as one of the most important techno albums of the 1990s.","Pick one track and follow a single element, say the hi-hat, all the way through. Then compare Steve Reich's Clapping Music, another piece that finds drama in almost nothing, and Jeff Mills's Waveform Transmission for the hard sound it grew out of."],"the-prodigy-music-for-the-jilted-generation":["The Prodigy's second album is harder, darker and more aggressive than their early rave singles. Heavy, chopped breakbeats, distorted guitars, snarling synth riffs and sampled voices collide in tracks such as Voodoo People, Poison, No Good (Start the Dance) and the multi-part Narcotic Suite. The mood is defiant and cinematic, closer to rock than to dance-pop. The big, lurching breakbeats, heavy enough to rival a rock drummer, are its signature.","The album was made as the British government pushed through the Criminal Justice and Public Order Act of 1994, which gave police powers against outdoor gatherings with music defined, notoriously, by repetitive beats. Rave culture felt criminalised. Liam Howlett responded with an album that took the side of the ravers, and whose sleeve artwork showed a raver cutting a rope bridge between his world and the police.","The track Their Law, made with the band Pop Will Eat Itself, is a direct protest against the Criminal Justice Act. Listen also for the album's breakbeats: many are borrowed from old funk and rock records, chopped and reassembled so completely that the sources are almost untraceable.","It was widely acclaimed and marked The Prodigy's transformation from a rave novelty act into a serious album band. Many critics now regard it as one of the key British records of the 1990s, and as a bridge between rave and rock that anticipated big beat.","Start with Voodoo People, then play the album in order. Listen for how the guitars and breakbeats interact. Compare Public Enemy's It Takes a Nation of Millions for another protest record built on sampled beats, and The Fat of the Land for what came next."],"underworld-dubnobasswithmyheadman":["Underworld's breakthrough album blends long, hypnotic techno and house grooves with Karl Hyde's murmured, fragmentary vocals. Tracks like Dark & Long, Mmm Skyscraper I Love You, Cowgirl and Dirty Epic stretch out over many minutes, with shimmering synths, pulsing basslines and guitars washed in echo. Hyde's voice drifts through, repeating phrases like overheard conversation. The mood is urban, nocturnal, dreamlike. The half-sung, half-spoken vocal over a relentless groove is unmistakable.","Hyde and Rick Smith had been an unsuccessful synth-pop band in the 1980s before reinventing themselves with the young DJ Darren Emerson. They were also members of Tomato, a London design collective whose visual style shaped the band's look. The album was released on the independent Junior Boy's Own label as British electronic music was moving toward albums and a wider audience.","Hyde's lyrics were often assembled from notes of conversations overheard in the street, snatches of signs and advertisements, and fragments from his notebooks, cut up and repeated. Listen for how the words work as rhythm and colour rather than a story; the effect is of drifting through a city at night catching pieces of other lives.","It was widely hailed as a breakthrough in British electronic music, showing that techno could carry songs, words and emotional depth without losing its hypnotic power. Rock critics who had ignored dance music took notice, partly because Hyde sounded like a singer rather than a sampled diva. It is frequently named among the best albums of the decade, and it opened the way for the band's festival-headlining years.","Start with Dark & Long or Cowgirl and let it run; the tracks need their full length to work. Listen for the voice as another instrument, a rhythm and a colour rather than a story, and for the guitars smeared in echo underneath. Then compare Orbital's Brown Album for a sister record, and Born Slippy .NUXX for where Underworld went next."],"underworld-born-slippy-nuxx":["It opens with washes of shimmering synth chords, slow and ambient; then a pounding, galloping beat bursts in, and Karl Hyde's voice chants and stammers fragments of a night out, repeating and circling. The track keeps building, dropping back to its floating chords, then surging again. It is ecstatic and exhausting at once. The moment the beat crashes in after the ambient opening is one of the most famous in dance music.","It first appeared as a B-side in 1995, then was reissued in 1996 after the director Danny Boyle used it at the climax of his film Trainspotting, about heroin users in Edinburgh. It became a huge hit and a symbol of British 1990s culture. The title reportedly comes from the name of a greyhound on which the band once placed a winning bet.","Hyde's vocal was written as a portrait of drunken self-destruction, the voice of someone lost in a night of heavy drinking; it was never meant as a celebration. Many crowds nevertheless chant its most famous word as a party anthem, which the band have found both funny and troubling. Listen for the desperation under the euphoria.","The song became inseparable from Trainspotting and from the image of 1990s Britain, and is often voted among the greatest dance records. Critics have debated whether its massive popularity obscured its darker meaning, turning a portrait of a man drowning in drink into a stadium singalong. It also fixed a public image of Underworld that the band spent years gently resisting, while still closing concerts with it.","Listen from the start and wait for the beat to drop; the long ambient opening is the setup, not an intro to skip. Pay attention to the voice as a rhythm, stammering and circling rather than singing a tune. Then play Dubnobasswithmyheadman for the album-length version of Underworld's world, and The Prodigy's The Fat of the Land for its louder contemporary."],"jeff-mills-live-at-the-liquid-room":["A recorded DJ set by Jeff Mills at a Tokyo club, where he mixes dozens of techno tracks at astonishing speed. Records are cut in and out every minute or two, layered and flipped, sometimes with a drum machine played live on top. The flow is relentless and dense, a surging wall of rhythm. It is less a playlist than a new composition built in real time from other people's records. The sheer velocity of the mixing is the point.","Mills was famous in Detroit in the 1980s as 'The Wizard', a radio DJ whose rapid mixing amazed listeners. By the mid-1990s he was an international figure, especially in Japan and Europe. Mix albums were becoming a major format, but few captured the athletic, improvisational side of DJing as vividly as this one, recorded at the Liquid Room in Tokyo.","Mills often used three turntables and a drum machine, allowing him to layer records and add his own rhythms; listen for moments when a beat appears that is on no record at all. Several of the tracks in the mix are his own, reworked on the fly.","It is widely regarded as one of the great DJ mix albums, and a benchmark of technical skill. Some listeners find it too relentless for home listening; admirers regard it as a document of DJing as a live art comparable to jazz improvisation.","Listen loud and try to count how often the records change; you will lose count quickly. Notice the moments where two records run together and create a rhythm neither has alone. Treat it like a live jazz album rather than background music. Then compare Larry Levan's Paradise Garage set for a completely different idea of the DJ's art: slow, spacious, patient."],"jeff-mills-the-bells":["A short, relentless techno track built on a pounding kick drum and a bright, ringing synth pattern that sounds like bells or a carillon chiming in endless loops. The pattern repeats insistently, shifting subtly as other layers enter, and the effect is both hypnotic and joyful. It is simple, but the tension between the bell-like melody and the driving rhythm makes it irresistible. The chiming figure is instantly recognisable.","Mills released it on his Purpose Maker label, a vehicle for funkier, more club-functional tracks. By then he was one of the most influential DJs in the world, and The Bells became one of the most played techno tracks of the late 1990s. It fitted his approach: minimal material, maximum impact.","In 2005 Mills performed The Bells with the Montpellier Philharmonic Orchestra, translating the synth pattern into orchestral bells and strings; hearing that version reveals how close the track is to minimalist concert music. Listen for how the pattern changes by adding rather than altering notes.","It became a club staple and is now considered one of the classic techno tracks. Critics have noted its bridge between minimal techno and minimalist composition, and it is often cited as an example of how techno can be both simple and deeply engaging.","Listen to how the bell pattern interacts with the kick drum, and notice that the pattern grows by adding notes rather than changing them, so the melody seems to assemble itself. Then seek out the orchestral version from Mills's Montpellier concert, and compare Steve Reich's Music for 18 Musicians, and Robert Hood's Minimal Nation for the minimal Detroit sound it grew from."],"daft-punk-homework":["Daft Punk's debut album is raw, looping French house: filtered disco samples, buzzing synths, thumping drum machines and robotic vocoder voices. Tracks such as Da Funk, Around the World and Revolution 909 repeat simple ideas until they become hypnotic. The sound is gritty and playful, made to be played loud in clubs. The filter sweep, in which the sound seems to open and close like a door, is the album's signature.","Thomas Bangalter and Guy-Manuel de Homem-Christo made Homework in Bangalter's Paris bedroom. French house was emerging from Paris clubs and labels such as Roulé and Crydamoure, drawing heavily on Chicago house and disco. The duo took their name from a British music press review that had dismissed their earlier indie band as 'daft punky'. Virgin released the album internationally.","The track Teachers is a roll-call of the Chicago and Detroit producers the pair learned from, a thank-you note to the house and techno pioneers. Listen for the repeated names as a sign of how consciously Daft Punk saw themselves as students of American black dance music.","It was a major critical and commercial success, bringing French house to global attention and helping define the late-1990s dance boom, helped by inventive videos from directors such as Spike Jonze and Michel Gondry. Some reviewers found it too long and repetitive for home listening. Critics now regard it as a key album in the internationalisation of house music, and as the moment Paris joined Chicago and Detroit on the map.","Start with Around the World, then Da Funk. Listen for the filter sweeps: the sound seems muffled behind a door, then the door swings open. Notice how few elements each track uses, repeated with a DJ's patience. Then compare Frankie Knuckles's Your Love for the Chicago sound Daft Punk loved, and Discovery for where they went next."],"the-prodigy-the-fat-of-the-land":["The Prodigy's third album fuses breakbeat electronics with punk and metal aggression. Keith Flint and Maxim snarl over huge, distorted beats, grinding synth riffs and guitar samples in tracks such as Firestarter, Breathe and Mindfields. The sound is loud, abrasive and theatrical, closer to a rock album than a dance record. The combination of sirens, chopped breakbeats and screamed vocals is unmistakable.","By 1997 the Prodigy had become a major international act, and The Fat of the Land went to number one in both Britain and the United States, the height of the 'big beat' and 'electronica' boom. One track's title and video drew protests and bans for their confrontational imagery, adding to the band's notoriety.","Firestarter samples a guitar figure from The Breeders and a fragment from the Art of Noise; listen for the rock sources inside the electronics. Many of the album's guitar sounds are sampled rather than played, a sign of how thoroughly the Prodigy treated rock as raw material.","It was a huge commercial success and critically acclaimed, though some dance purists saw it as a sell-out to rock. It is now regarded as a defining album of the big beat era and as a bridge between electronic dance music and rock audiences.","Start with Breathe, where the call-and-response between the two vocalists sits over a lurching, stop-start beat. Listen for how the beats are built from chopped fragments and how often a 'guitar' is really a sample. Play it loud, as intended. Then compare Music for the Jilted Generation to hear the transformation, and Underworld's Born Slippy for a very different British dance anthem."],"underground-resistance-knights-of-the-jaguar":["Credited to DJ Rolando of Underground Resistance, it is built on a sweeping string-like synth melody, grand and melancholy, over a propulsive, driving techno beat. The melody rises in long phrases, repeated and layered, while the rhythm pushes forward relentlessly. It feels heroic and wistful at once. That soaring string line, sounding like a film theme, is the track's signature.","Rolando Rocha, a Mexican-American DJ from southwest Detroit, made the track as part of UR. The title refers to the Jaguar Knights, an elite warrior order of the Aztec Empire, a reference to his heritage and to UR's warrior imagery. The track became a massive European club hit. UR refused to license it to a major label, which then released a sound-alike version; the resulting dispute became a celebrated case of underground integrity against the industry.","Listen for how simple the melody is; its power comes from repetition and the way it floats over the beat. The track's popularity, and the major label's cover version, became part of its meaning: a battle between the independent Detroit ethos and corporate imitation.","It is regarded as one of the greatest Detroit techno tracks and a late flowering of the genre's melodic side. Critics also see it as a symbol of UR's refusal to compromise, and of the Latino presence in Detroit's music.","Wait for the melody to fully unfold; it arrives in stages, each repetition adding weight. Listen to how the drums never let up underneath, so the grandeur is always moving forward. It rewards a big system or good headphones. Then compare Strings of Life for its Detroit ancestor, Hi-Tech Jazz for another soaring UR record, and Vangelis's Blade Runner for a kindred synthetic melancholy."],"daft-punk-discovery":["Daft Punk's second album is brighter and poppier, drawing on disco, soft rock and 1980s pop. Filtered samples, vocoder vocals, guitar solos and glittering synths fill tracks such as One More Time, Aerodynamic, Digital Love and Harder, Better, Faster, Stronger. The mood is nostalgic and joyful, like a childhood memory of pop music. The heavily processed, auto-tuned vocal on One More Time is the album's signature sound.","The duo described the album as an attempt to recapture the feelings of childhood discovery, when they first heard pop and disco. They adopted robot helmets and a fictional backstory to hide their faces. The album was accompanied by the animated film Interstella 5555, made with the Japanese artist Leiji Matsumoto. One More Time features the vocals of the house singer Romanthony.","The album is built from samples of obscure 1970s and 1980s records, disguised by filtering and chopping: Harder, Better, Faster, Stronger uses Edwin Birdsong's Cola Bottle Baby, and Digital Love draws on George Duke. Kanye West later sampled Harder, Better, Faster, Stronger for his hit Stronger. Listen for the disguised sources.","Initially some critics found it too cheesy and pop-oriented, objecting to the soft-rock guitar solos and the heavily processed vocals that dance purists considered kitsch. Its reputation has grown enormously since. It is now widely considered one of the most influential albums of the 2000s, shaping electronic pop, EDM and hip-hop production, and its embrace of guilty-pleasure sounds now looks prophetic.","Start with One More Time, then Digital Love, and listen for how the vocoder makes a machine sound lovesick. Hear the guitar solo in Aerodynamic, closer to heavy metal than to house. Play the album in order; it flows like a mixtape. Then compare Chic's Good Times for the disco sound that inspired them, and Random Access Memories for their later tribute."],"daft-punk-random-access-memories":["Daft Punk's final studio album abandons samples in favour of live musicians, recreating the lush sound of 1970s disco and soft rock. Nile Rodgers's guitar, Nathan East's bass and top session drummers drive tracks like Get Lucky, Lose Yourself to Dance and Giorgio by Moroder, with guest vocals from Pharrell Williams, Julian Casablancas and Paul Williams. The sound is warm, expansive and meticulous. Nile Rodgers's rhythm guitar on Get Lucky is the album's hallmark.","Recorded in expensive studios in Los Angeles, Paris and New York, the album was a reaction against the laptop-produced EDM that was dominating dance music in the early 2010s. Daft Punk wanted to honour the musicians and studios of the disco era. Columbia released it in 2013, and it won the Grammy for Album of the Year.","On Giorgio by Moroder, the veteran producer tells his life story; the band recorded him with microphones from different decades, so the sound of his voice changes to match the era he is describing. Listen for that subtle shift in tone.","It was a massive commercial success and won major awards, and Get Lucky became one of the most played songs of its year. Some critics felt it was too nostalgic or overproduced, a museum piece for a vanished studio culture. Others heard a sincere argument for musicianship in a laptop age. It is now regarded as a celebration of dance music's history and of studio craft, and as the duo's farewell statement.","Start with Get Lucky, then Giorgio by Moroder, where Moroder's spoken story gives way to a long, building instrumental. Listen for the live drums and Rodgers's choppy rhythm guitar, and hear how the album sounds warmer than a sample-based record. Then compare Chic's Good Times and Donna Summer's I Feel Love for the sources they honour."],"aphex-twin-selected-ambient-works-85-92":["Thirteen instrumental tracks, most of them built from a slow techno pulse, a soft analogue bass line and one or two melodies that loop and drift rather than develop. 'Xtal' opens with a breathy, wordless pad over a lazy kick drum; 'Heliosphan' and 'Ageispolis' are brighter and more mechanical; 'Tha' stretches a murky groove past nine minutes. Everything sits under a faint layer of hiss, so the record sounds less like a club than like a bedroom at three in the morning.","Richard D. James made most of these pieces as a teenager in Cornwall, on cheap and modified synthesizers, recording straight to cassette. They were collected in 1992 by Apollo, the ambient offshoot of the Belgian techno label R&S, just as British rave was splitting into the dancefloor and the living room. Warp's 'Artificial Intelligence' series appeared the same year, and critics began to talk about electronic music made for listening rather than dancing.","The hiss is not a mastering fault that nobody caught; the album was taken from the cassette tapes, and the noise floor is part of the colour. Listen too for how little happens: many tracks repeat a four-bar idea for minutes, changing only a filter or one drum. 'We Are the Music Makers' borrows a line of film dialogue spoken by Gene Wilder, the only human voice on the record that says real words.","Reviewers in 1992 had few categories for it; 'ambient techno' was the label that stuck. Within a decade it had become a fixture on lists of the best electronic albums ever made, and it is often the first record people reach for when explaining what IDM meant before the term existed. The one lasting argument is over the word ambient itself, since most of these tracks keep a steady beat that Eno's ambient music avoids.","Start with 'Xtal' and 'Ageispolis' on headphones at moderate volume, and let the loops settle rather than waiting for a drop. Then compare Eno's Music for Airports, which has no pulse at all, to hear what James added. After that, Boards of Canada's Music Has the Right to Children shows where the same warm, worn sound went next."],"aphex-twin-selected-ambient-works-volume-ii":["Two and a half hours of drones, slow chords and dim, half-heard textures, almost entirely without beats. Some tracks are a single organ-like chord sequence repeated with infinite patience; others are metallic rumbles, distant voices or tones that seem to be leaking from another room. The piece fans call 'Rhubarb', a few soft synthetic chords rising and falling for seven minutes, is the one most listeners recognise, and it could almost be a hymn.","Released on Warp in 1994, when James was already known for abrasive, jokey techno, it confounded almost everyone. The tracks carried no titles, only photographs of textures and objects keyed to a pie chart on the sleeve, and fans later assigned names of their own. James told interviewers that some of the material came to him in lucid dreams, a claim that may be partly mischief but which shaped how the record was heard.","Because the tracks are untitled, the album works like one continuous installation, and it rewards being played in order rather than shuffled. Beneath several pieces are real acoustic sources slowed and smeared beyond recognition. 'Rhubarb' uses almost nothing: a short cycle of chords, no melody to speak of, no change of speed. It is the same wager Satie and Arvo Pärt made, that a few plain sounds left alone long enough will start to feel alive.","The music press was divided in 1994, with some reviewers calling it empty and others hearing a new, darker kind of ambient music than Eno's. Its reputation has grown steadily, and it is now often preferred to the first volume by listeners who come from classical minimalism or drone music. Debate continues over how much of the dream story to believe, and over whether the whole thing should ever be heard in one sitting.","Play 'Rhubarb' first, then Pärt's Für Alina, and listen to how both let a few notes ring for as long as they need. Then try a whole disc late at night, lights low, at a volume just above silence. Morton Feldman's Rothko Chapel makes a revealing companion for the darker tracks."],"aphex-twin-richard-d-james-album":["A short record that crams sweet, almost nursery-rhyme melodies against drum programming so fast and fractured it sounds like a breakbeat being fed through a shredder. '4' opens with a floating keyboard tune over a skittering, impossibly busy rhythm; 'Girl/Boy Song' sets a tender string arrangement over jungle drums chopped into tiny slivers. The contrast between the lullaby on top and the chaos underneath is the whole idea.","By 1996 jungle and drum and bass were the sound of London, and James, now living there, took their chopped breakbeats and pushed the editing to an extreme that dancers could not follow. The style became known, half mockingly, as drill 'n' bass. Much of the album was programmed on a computer rather than played on hardware, which let him place drum hits at speeds no drummer or sampler performance could reach. His own grinning face filled the cover.","Several titles are Cornish place names, including 'Carn Marth' and 'Logan Rock Witch', a private map of the landscape he grew up in. Listen past the drums in 'Girl/Boy Song' and the melody is a simple, plaintive tune that would sit comfortably on a music box. In the fastest passages the drum hits are so close together that they stop being rhythm and become pitch, a buzzing tone of their own.","Received as James's most approachable record despite its violence, it sold well and turned the face on its sleeve into a kind of logo. Critics then and now split between those who hear real tenderness and those who hear a sophisticated joke. It is often cited as the moment IDM's technical ambition and its melodic warmth fitted together most tightly.","Begin with 'Girl/Boy Song' and follow only the strings the first time, then only the drums. After that, listen to '4' and notice how the melody never hurries while the percussion races. Compare Nancarrow's Studies for Player Piano, another composer who used a machine to play faster than hands can."],"aphex-twin-windowlicker":["A six-minute track that begins as a slinky, almost R&B groove, with a lazy swung beat and a honeyed synth line, then gradually warps. James's own voice, pitched and sliced until it sounds like a woman cooing, floats in and out. Midway the drums start to stutter, glitch and fold back on themselves, and the smooth surface curdles into something wonky and unsettling before sliding back into the groove.","Released as a single on Warp in 1999, it arrived with a long video by Chris Cunningham that parodied the conventions of hip-hop videos, pasting James's leering face onto a troupe of dancers. The video became a fixture of late-night music television. James had spent the late 1990s trading more and more on his public persona, and this was the peak of that absurdist image-making.","Put the end of the track through a spectrogram, a picture of sound frequencies, and a spiral appears; the B-side, titled with a mathematical formula, hides an image of James's face the same way. So the music contains pictures only a computer can see. The warped vocal is not a sampled singer but James himself, transformed.","It became one of the most widely heard pieces of experimental electronic music of its decade, helped by the video. Some critics hear a satire of commercial R&B; others hear a straightforward love of it bent through James's sensibility. The hidden images have become part of internet folklore, sometimes overshadowing the track itself.","Listen once without the video, following the drums as they move from smooth to stuttering and back. Then watch Cunningham's film. For the lineage, hear Donna Summer's I Feel Love, the machine-made groove this is partly descended from, and Afrika Bambaataa's Planet Rock."],"aphex-twin-avril-14th":["Two minutes of solo piano: a gentle, rocking left hand and a short, tender melody that repeats with small variations and then simply stops. There are no drums, no synthesizers and no processing. The sound is close and slightly muffled, with the mechanism of the instrument audible, as if you were sitting inside the piano. It sits on the double album Drukqs among some of James's most violent drum programming, which makes its quiet all the more striking.","Drukqs, released by Warp in 2001, alternated frantic electronic tracks with pieces for prepared piano and for a Yamaha Disklavier, a piano that can be played by a computer. James programmed the notes rather than performing them, then recorded the real instrument. Many tracks on the album carry Cornish-language titles, and James said the release was hurried along after he left an MP3 player full of unreleased music on a plane.","Because a machine is playing, the dynamics are unusually even and the rubato is written in, not felt, which gives the piece its slightly uncanny, music-box quality. Listen for the soft thuds of the dampers and keys, left prominent in the mix. Kanye West built his 2010 song 'Blame Game' on this music, which introduced it to millions who had never heard of Aphex Twin.","Drukqs was received coolly in 2001, often called overlong and unedited, but this short piano piece escaped the criticism and became James's most widely known composition, used in films, adverts and countless cover versions. Pianists argue over whether to play it with the flat evenness of the original or to add the expressive shaping it seems to invite.","Listen first on headphones to hear the mechanism. Then play Satie's first Gymnopédie and notice the same rocking accompaniment and unhurried melody. Nancarrow's player-piano studies show the opposite use of the same idea, a machine playing what no human could."],"autechre-amber":["Eleven long instrumentals of slow, heavy, softly lit electronic music. The beats are there but restrained, often a muffled kick and a clicking pattern at walking pace; above them hang mournful, slowly shifting synthesizer chords. 'Foil' opens with a clanking rhythm that seems to be searching for its own groove, and 'Nil' closes with a long, warm, sad drift. The overall colour is exactly what the title suggests: something ancient, glowing and preserved.","Rob Brown and Sean Booth, two friends from Rochdale near Manchester, had grown up on electro and hip-hop mixtapes before Warp signed them. Their early tracks appeared on the label's 1992 'Artificial Intelligence' compilation, which framed a new kind of electronic music for home listening. Amber was their second album, and its sleeve showed an eroded rock landscape in Cappadocia in Turkey, a picture of slow geological time.","The rhythms are more complex than they first sound: several tracks layer patterns of different lengths so that the downbeat quietly drifts. Listen also for how often a track stays on one or two chords for its whole length, letting texture rather than harmony carry the change. The hip-hop breakbeat, the duo's first love, is still audible, but slowed down and blurred.","Amber was well received but overshadowed at the time by Aphex Twin's more flamboyant releases. It has since become the Autechre album most often recommended to newcomers, precisely because their later, harder abstraction has not yet arrived. Fans debate whether it is their warmest record or merely their least characteristic one.","Play 'Montreal' and 'Silverside' first; both balance melody and machinery perfectly. Listen in one go, as an album, in the evening. Compare Aphex Twin's Selected Ambient Works 85–92 for a brighter version of the same moment, and Kraftwerk's Computer World for the source of the clean electronic pulse."],"autechre-tri-repetae":["Harder and colder than Amber: ten tracks of metallic, echoing percussion and spare synthesizer lines, the sound of machinery in an empty hall. 'Dael' opens with a lopsided, clanging rhythm that keeps promising to settle; 'Clipper' is a long, grinding study in a single repeated figure; 'Rotar' and 'Leterel' pare the melodies down to a few notes. The music is repetitive in the way a factory is repetitive, but it keeps slightly changing shape.","Recorded in 1995 as Brown and Booth were acquiring more hardware and taking their studio sound in a more industrial direction, it came out on Warp that November. In America the album was expanded with two EPs as Tri Repetae++. British electronic music was splitting into factions, and Autechre were placing themselves firmly on the side of structure rather than the party.","The title suggests threefold repetition, and repetition with tiny changes is the method: listen for the moment a percussion sound shifts in timbre or position while everything else stays still. The reverb is part of the composition, not decoration; many tracks are as much about the space around the sounds as the sounds themselves.","It is now regarded by many fans and critics as Autechre's finest work of the 1990s, the point where they left dance-music conventions behind without yet going fully abstract. Some listeners find it austere and airless; others hear a hard, physical kind of beauty. It is often compared with the process music of Steve Reich.","Play it loud on good speakers rather than headphones so the reverb fills the room. Start with 'Dael' and follow one percussion sound through the whole track. Then listen to Reich's Piano Phase, where the same idea of gradual shift inside repetition is played by human hands."],"autechre-confield":["Nine tracks in which rhythm stops behaving like rhythm. Beats accelerate, decelerate, jam and splinter; sounds bounce around like objects in a closed box. 'VI Scose Poise' opens with a clean, almost pretty melody that is then broken apart by stuttering percussion, and 'Pen Expers' is a relentless, lurching pattern that never repeats exactly. There are no loops in the ordinary sense; the music feels as if it is thinking in real time.","By 2001 Brown and Booth were building their own software environments, using programs such as Max to create systems of rules that generated rhythms and textures as they played. They performed and recorded with these systems, adjusting parameters rather than writing notes. The laptop had just become a viable instrument, and the album arrived at the height of glitch and computer music's crossover into rock and pop circles.","Many of the patterns were not written but generated: the duo set conditions, such as how one drum should respond to another, and let the machine decide. That is why no two bars are quite the same. It is the same basic idea as Xenakis's use of mathematics or Cage's chance operations, arrived at from the dancefloor rather than the conservatory.","Confield divided listeners sharply. Some reviewers called it unlistenable, others the most radical record Warp had released. It is now widely seen as a turning point after which Autechre stood closer to the avant-garde of Stockhausen and Xenakis than to techno. The debate over whether it is music to feel or only to admire continues.","Start with 'VI Scose Poise', whose opening melody gives you something to hold. Treat the rest like a piece of contemporary chamber music rather than a dance record. Pair it with Xenakis's Persephassa or Stockhausen's Kontakte to hear the older lineage of rule-built sound."],"boards-of-canada-music-has-the-right-to-children":["An hour of slow, warm, slightly out-of-tune electronic music built on lazy hip-hop beats, wobbling synthesizer melodies and fragments of children's voices. 'Roygbiv' is a short, bright, instantly memorable tune; 'Turquoise Hexagon Sun' and 'Aquarius' rest on thick, soft drums; 'Olson' is a brief wistful fragment. Everything sounds as if it had been left in the sun on an old tape, the pitch wavering as the tape stretches.","The Scottish brothers Michael Sandison and Marcus Eoin had worked privately for years in the Pentland Hills near Edinburgh before Warp and the smaller Skam label released this in 1998. Their name comes from the National Film Board of Canada, whose educational films they watched as children during time spent in Canada. The album arrived just as sampling technology made it easy to rebuild the sound of old recordings.","The wavering pitch is deliberate: the brothers ran material through tape and old equipment to mimic the wow and flutter of worn cassettes and film soundtracks, so the music sounds like memory. Several tracks contain voices from children's television and documentary sources, and the sleeve's faded family photograph has the faces bleached out. The unease beneath the nostalgia is easy to miss on first hearing.","Critics praised it immediately, and it has grown into one of the most influential electronic records of its era, a source for chillwave, hauntology and much lo-fi hip-hop. Some listeners hear pure comfort; others find it quietly disturbing, a childhood recalled slightly wrong. That mixture of innocence and menace remains the thing people argue about.","Start with 'Roygbiv', then 'Turquoise Hexagon Sun'. Listen for the pitch drift in held notes, the signature of the whole record. Then hear Delia Derbyshire's The Dreams and her Doctor Who theme: the BBC Radiophonic Workshop's sound of 1960s and 70s British childhood is the ghost this album is chasing."],"boards-of-canada-geogaddi":["Twenty-three tracks, many under two minutes, that take the warm sound of the first album into darker territory. 'Music Is Math' sets a slow, heavy beat under a queasy, detuned melody and a child's voice counting; 'Dawn Chorus' is sunlit and hazy; 'Gyroscope' is claustrophobic. Short interludes of drones and voices connect the longer pieces, so the whole plays like a single, unsettled dream.","The brothers spent about four years making it in their rural studio, releasing it on Warp in early 2002 after a long near-silence. By now their first record had a devoted following that pored over every detail, and they responded with an album full of hints, numbers and references to nature, mathematics and the occult, seemingly designed to be decoded.","The album runs sixty-six minutes and six seconds, and that is no accident. It is full of patterns drawn from spirals, the golden ratio and the Fibonacci sequence, and listeners have found fragments that carry reversed voices. Titles such as 'A Is to B as B Is to C' point to proportion. The childlike surface hides a record about order, belief and fear.","Received warmly, though some reviewers found it too long and too uniform. Its reputation has risen as listeners have taken its darkness seriously, and it is often named alongside the debut as their best work. The decoding culture it inspired online, with fans mapping every number, is now part of how the record is known.","Listen straight through with no shuffle; the interludes matter. Start by noticing the running time, then listen to 'Music Is Math' for the way a simple beat becomes ominous. Pair it with Laurie Spiegel's Kepler's Harmony of the Worlds for older music built from mathematical ideas about nature."],"boards-of-canada-tomorrows-harvest":["Seventeen tracks that sound like the soundtrack to a lost science-fiction film about the end of the world. The warm nostalgia of earlier records gives way to bleak, pulsing synthesizer lines, cold arpeggios and long, desolate drones. 'Reach for the Dead' builds from a slow, sad theme to a heavy, rolling beat; 'Cold Earth' and 'Jacquard Causeway' are grim and hypnotic. The mood is of a deserted landscape after something terrible has happened.","Released on Warp in 2013 after eight years without an album, it was announced through an elaborate puzzle: a secret record slipped into a shop on Record Store Day, then numerical codes broadcast on radio, television and online that fans had to combine to unlock the release date. The hunt became a news story in its own right, a sign of how much mystique the brothers had built by saying almost nothing.","The palette is deliberately borrowed from late-1970s and early-1980s film scores, the synthesizer music of John Carpenter and Vangelis, a new source for the brothers. Titles hint at a story of famine, surveillance and collapse. The sleeve shows a city skyline bleached almost white, as if seen through heat haze or something worse, and the tracks move like scenes cut from a film you never see.","Critics welcomed it as a strong return, if less surprising than their early records. Some found it too consistent in mood; others heard their most coherent album, a single sustained vision. It is often described as a landscape record for an age of ecological anxiety, and it drew many younger listeners back to the brothers' earlier work.","Play it in one sitting, preferably while travelling or at dusk, and begin with 'Reach for the Dead'. Notice how rarely the beat arrives, and how much weight it carries when it does. Then hear Vangelis's Blade Runner score, the earlier synthesizer language this album echoes in spirit, and Tangerine Dream's Rubycon."],"squarepusher-feed-me-weird-things":["The debut: long tracks in which frantic, hyper-edited jungle breakbeats run under fluid, jazzy electric bass lines and glowing synthesizer chords. 'Squarepusher Theme' sets a funky, walking bass line over drums that race and stutter; 'Tundra' is spacious and melancholy, all cold pads and distant beats. The combination of a real bassist's phrasing and a programmer's impossibly fast drumming is what makes it unlike anything else of its year.","Tom Jenkinson, a young bass player from Essex who had grown up on jazz fusion and rave, was signed to Rephlex, the label co-founded by Aphex Twin. The album came out in 1996, when drum and bass was at the height of its fame and jazz was seeping into it from several directions. Warp signed Jenkinson soon afterwards, and the record has since been reissued there.","The bass is played, not programmed; listen for slides, ghost notes and small timing variations that no machine would produce, set against drums placed with inhuman precision. Jenkinson's debt to Jaco Pastorius, the fusion bassist of Weather Report, is audible in the singing, melodic lines high on the neck. The whole record is a duet between a human and a sequencer played by the same person.","It was received as a striking debut and an unusual meeting of jazz musicianship and electronic production. It is often ranked just behind his next record, though some fans consider its rawness more exciting. The question of whether its jazz is homage or parody has never quite been settled, and jazz critics have mostly left it alone.","Start with 'Squarepusher Theme' and follow only the bass, then go back and follow only the drums. Then hear Jaco Pastorius's solo debut and Weather Report's Heavy Weather to find the source of that fluid, singing bass tone, and Aphex Twin's Richard D. James Album for the drum side of the family."],"squarepusher-hard-normal-daddy":["Jazz fusion played through a rave sound system: glossy electric-piano chords, virtuoso bass runs and drum programming so fast and detailed it seems to be improvising. 'Beep Street' opens with a bright, looping groove before the breaks begin to fracture. Other tracks slow into dreamy, 1970s-flavoured passages of keyboard harmony. It is the most playful and melodic of his early records.","His first album for Warp, released in 1997, it arrived as drill 'n' bass was briefly fashionable in British electronic music. Jenkinson was still in his early twenties, working alone at home, programming every drum hit and playing the bass parts himself. The record drew heavily on the fusion of the 1970s: Miles Davis's electric bands, Weather Report and Herbie Hancock.","The keyboard chords are voiced like a 1970s fusion record, rich and extended, while the drums are chopped from breakbeats at a speed no drummer could play. That collision between two decades is the whole joke and the whole beauty. Listen for moments when bass and drums lock into unison, a flash of perfect coordination between a human and a machine.","It is often cited as Squarepusher's best record and one of the high points of drill 'n' bass. Critics in 1997 enjoyed its humour; later listeners have taken its musicianship more seriously. Jazz critics have been more sceptical, hearing pastiche rather than real improvisation, and that argument sits at the heart of how he is judged.","Start with 'Beep Street'. Listen for the bass first, then the drums, then how they fit together. Compare Miles Davis's Bitches Brew, where the same fusion textures were improvised by a band in a room, and the Mahavishnu Orchestra for the speed and sheen."],"plaid-not-for-threes":["Bright, intricate, cheerful electronic music: chiming synthesizer melodies, clicking and popping rhythms, interlocking patterns that sound like mechanical toys. Björk sings on 'Lilith', a slow, dark and breathy song, and other guest voices appear, but most tracks are instrumental. Compared with Autechre, Plaid are melodic and warm; compared with Aphex Twin, they are tidy and precise, like a small chamber group of synthesizers.","Ed Handley and Andy Turner had been two-thirds of The Black Dog, a pioneering British home-listening techno group, before concentrating on Plaid. Not for Threes, released in 1997, was their first album for Warp and arrived in the label's richest period. They had already worked with Björk, which is how she came to sing on this record, a sign of how closely IDM and adventurous pop were then connected.","Many of the rhythms are built from overlapping patterns of different lengths, so the accent shifts from bar to bar, a technique shared with Steve Reich and with West African drumming. Listen for how the melodies are handed between synthesizer voices like instruments in a chamber group, each taking a phrase and passing it on.","It was well received, though Plaid have always stood somewhat in the shadow of their Warp colleagues. Many listeners now regard them as one of the most consistently melodic groups in IDM, and this album as their breakthrough. Critics sometimes find them too polite; admirers answer that craft and charm are rare in the genre.","Start with 'Lilith' for Björk, then move to the instrumental tracks and follow a single melody as it passes around. Compare Björk's Homogenic, released the same year, and Reich's Drumming, where the interlocking-pattern idea is played by live percussionists on bongos and marimbas."],"mu-ziq-lunatic-harness":["Big, lush, hummable synthesizer melodies over furiously edited jungle drums. 'Hasty Boom Alert' sets a soaring, almost orchestral tune against breakbeats chopped to shrapnel; other tracks are slower and more wistful, with long string-like pads. Where much IDM is cool and cerebral, this is emotional and even bombastic, closer to a film score played over a drum-and-bass rhythm section.","Mike Paradinas, a London producer, had been releasing records since the early 1990s and was a friend and sometime collaborator of Aphex Twin. Lunatic Harness appeared in 1997, when drill 'n' bass was briefly in fashion. Paradinas had founded the label Planet Mu in 1995, which he later ran independently; it became one of the most important homes for electronic music, from IDM to Chicago footwork.","Paradinas often builds melodies from a few very simple notes, repeated and harmonised in thick chords, which is why they are so memorable. Listen for how the melody stays steady and slow while the drums race around it. The drums themselves are edited with enormous care: every hit is placed by hand.","Reviewers in 1997 saw it as a highlight of the drill 'n' bass moment, and it has kept a strong reputation. Later listeners sometimes find it overripe; fans find it more emotionally direct than anything else in the genre. Paradinas's influence as a label owner has sometimes overshadowed his own music.","Start with 'Hasty Boom Alert'. Listen to the melody first and treat the drums as a storm around it. Then hear Aphex Twin's Richard D. James Album, from the previous year, to compare two very different temperaments working in the same style."],"four-tet-rounds":["Warm, intricate music built from acoustic samples: plucked harps, bells, acoustic guitar, hand percussion, snatches of jazz drumming, all chopped and looped on a laptop. 'She Moves She' and 'My Angel Rocks Back and Forth' are gentle and intimate; 'Spirit Fingers' is jittery and bright; 'As Serious as Your Life' is propulsive. The record feels handmade, like electronic folk music played by one person at a kitchen table.","Kieran Hebden, a London musician who also played guitar in the post-rock band Fridge, made Rounds on a laptop at home and released it on Domino in 2003. The word 'folktronica' was coined partly to describe his sound, a label he disliked. Laptop production had just made it possible for one person to assemble a whole record from fragments of acoustic instruments.","Many of the drum patterns are sampled from jazz records and re-edited so they stumble and swing unpredictably; Hebden is a devoted fan of free and spiritual jazz. Listen for how acoustic sounds are looped with slight irregularities so they never quite repeat. The title 'As Serious as Your Life' borrows the title of Valerie Wilmer's classic book on free jazz.","Rounds was widely praised and has come to be seen as one of the defining electronic albums of the 2000s. Some critics found it too pretty; many musicians cite it as a model for the warm, sample-based electronic music of the following decade. Its reputation has only grown as the folktronica tag has faded.","Start with 'She Moves She' and listen for the harp and the loose jazz drums underneath. Then hear Alice Coltrane's Journey in Satchidananda for the harp and spiritual-jazz mood Hebden loves, and Reich's Music for 18 Musicians for the shimmer of many small patterns at once."],"four-tet-there-is-love-in-you":["A gentler, more dance-oriented record than Rounds. 'Angel Echoes' opens with a wordless female vocal fragment looped and layered into a shimmering chorus; 'Love Cry' is a nine-minute build over a steady, patient beat; 'Sing' and 'Plastic People' drift and pulse. The acoustic samples are still there, but now set to a house tempo, with long, gradual arcs of change.","By 2010 Hebden had become a club DJ with a residency at the London venue Plastic People, and his music had moved closer to the dancefloor. He had also begun collaborating with Burial, and the ghostly treatment of voices on this record shows that exchange. It was released on Domino in January 2010, just as British club music was dissolving the line between home listening and dancing.","'Love Cry' takes its title from an Albert Ayler album, a signal of the free-jazz devotion behind the calm surface. Listen to 'Angel Echoes' for how a single short vocal fragment is turned into a whole choir by layering and pitch-shifting. 'Plastic People' is named after the club where much of this music was first tested on a crowd.","It was widely praised as his most accomplished record, a bridge between his earlier home-listening music and the dancefloor. Some fans missed the rougher edges of Rounds; others heard a new maturity and patience. It is often credited with helping bring gentle, emotional house music back into critical favour after a decade of harder club sounds.","Start with 'Angel Echoes', then 'Love Cry'. Let 'Love Cry' play through without skipping: its change is slow, and the reward is in the last minutes. Compare Burial's Untrue for the same ghostly vocal technique used in a darker way, and Caribou's Swim from the same year."],"burial-burial":["Dark, rainy, sub-bass-heavy music at a slow dubstep tempo, full of crackle, hiss and fragments of distant voices. 'Distant Lights', 'Southern Comfort' and 'Night Bus' sound like south London at night: rain, empty streets, a half-heard song from a passing car. The beats are loose and shuffling rather than tight, and the bass is felt as much as heard.","William Bevan made the album in south London and released it in 2006 on Hyperdub, the label founded by the producer Kode9. He stayed anonymous for the first years of his career, refusing interviews and photographs until a tabloid threatened to unmask him in 2008, when he posted a picture of himself online. Dubstep was emerging from London's underground garage scene, and Burial was its most mournful voice.","Bevan built the tracks in a simple sound-editing program rather than a sequencer, placing sounds by eye without a grid, which is why his beats feel loose and human. The vinyl crackle and rain are part of the composition, not decoration. He has said that some of the clicks and textures came from video games he played.","The album was widely praised and placed high on many critics' lists for 2006. It was quickly seen as a defining document of dubstep, though Burial has always stood at an angle to the genre. Some critics hear pure melancholy; others hear a lament for the rave culture of the 1990s that Bevan was too young to have fully known.","Listen at night, ideally while travelling through a city, on headphones with decent bass. Start with 'Distant Lights', then 'Night Bus'. Compare Eno's Ambient 4: On Land, an earlier record that also turns a remembered place into sound, and Radiohead's Kid A for another dark turn of the same period's mood."],"burial-untrue":["Burial's second album brings voices to the front: fragments of R&B singing pitched up and down until they sound genderless and ghostly, floating over shuffling two-step garage rhythms and deep bass. 'Archangel' is the best-known track, a lovelorn vocal loop over a limping beat; 'Near Dark' and 'Etched Headplate' are darker and more fragmentary. The whole album sounds like someone walking home alone after a night out.","Released on Hyperdub in November 2007, it was shortlisted for the Mercury Prize in 2008, and the attention sent tabloids after Bevan's identity. Dubstep was by now a recognised genre, and Untrue took its bass and space but mixed them with the swing of UK garage, a sound Bevan had loved as a teenager through an older brother's records and pirate radio.","The vocal samples come from R&B and pop recordings, but they are so transformed that most are nearly impossible to identify; the pitch-shifting makes men sound like women and women like children. The beats are placed by hand, without a grid, so the rhythm has a subtle, human drag. Listen for the sound of lighters, rain and footsteps between the drums.","Untrue was widely acclaimed and is now often named among the most important electronic albums of the century. Its influence runs through British electronic music, pop production and even film scoring. Critics still debate whether it is a dance record, an ambient record or something else entirely, and whether its nostalgia is for a real scene or an imagined one.","Start with 'Archangel' and listen for how the vocal loop bends and returns. Then play the album straight through on headphones late at night. Arvo Pärt's Cantus in Memoriam Benjamin Britten makes a surprising companion: another piece that turns grief into a descending line repeated until it becomes weather."],"jon-hopkins-immunity":["An hour-long arc from dense, pounding techno to quiet piano. 'Open Eye Signal' and 'Collider' are long, relentless, distorted tracks with a physical, almost crushing weight; the second half turns inward with 'Abandon Window' and 'Form by Firelight', where slow piano chords emerge; the title track closes with a hushed song sung by the Scottish songwriter King Creosote. The shape is like a night out and the long walk home.","Jon Hopkins, a classically trained pianist from south-west London, had played keyboards for Imogen Heap and worked alongside Brian Eno before making Immunity, released on Domino in 2013. It was shortlisted for the Mercury Prize that year. Home studios now allowed a single producer to make music with the weight and detail of a full band, and Hopkins used that freedom to shape a whole album as one piece.","Many of the percussion sounds began as everyday noises recorded in and around Hopkins's studio, struck and handled objects processed until they hit like drums. The album was conceived as a single journey, and tempo and intensity are carefully mapped across its hour. The piano in the second half is played softly with the pedal held down, so the notes blur into each other.","Immunity was one of the most acclaimed electronic records of its year, and many listeners came to it from outside dance music. Some critics found its dramatic arc too polished; others heard a model of how to make an electronic album that works as a whole. It is often credited with bringing techno to audiences who otherwise heard ambient and modern classical music.","Play it through in one go, ideally loud, and notice the shift in the middle from heavy to gentle. Start with 'Open Eye Signal' if you can only hear one track. Then compare Arvo Pärt's Spiegel im Spiegel for the stillness of the closing piano, and Eno's Apollo for the ambient side of Hopkins's education."],"jon-hopkins-singularity":["A sequel in shape to Immunity: a first half of massive, surging techno and a second half that dissolves into ambient drift and piano. 'Emerald Rush' rides a bright, rising synthesizer figure over a thudding beat; 'Everything Connected' is long, dense and euphoric. Then 'Feel First Life', sung by a small choir, wordless and hymn-like, opens the quiet half, and the closing piano pieces are slow and luminous.","Hopkins wrote the album after years of interest in meditation and altered states of consciousness, and presented it as music about connection between mind and world. It was released on Domino in 2018, five years after Immunity had made him one of the best-known British electronic musicians. By then the border between electronic music and contemporary classical composition had become very porous, and he moved freely across it.","Hopkins has described the album as beginning and ending on the same note, so the record forms a closed loop and can be played round and round. Listen for how the choir in 'Feel First Life' is treated almost like a synthesizer, the voices held and blended until the words no longer matter. The two halves mirror each other in length and shape.","It was widely praised, though some critics found it too close in design to Immunity. Many listeners regard its first half as among the most powerful electronic music of the decade, while the choral and piano pieces divided opinion between those who heard depth and those who heard spa music.","Play 'Emerald Rush' loud, then 'Feel First Life' quietly, and notice how the album earns its calm. Compare Ligeti's Lux Aeterna, a choral piece of pure, suspended sound, and Pärt's Summa for the plainness of the closing mood. Then go back to Immunity and hear how the same two-part plan has grown bigger."],"oneohtrix-point-never-replica":["Short loops cut from television adverts of the 1980s and 1990s, slowed, repeated and set against drifting synthesizer chords and a few piano notes. The title track is a slow, melancholy piano loop that circles like a memory you cannot quite place; 'Andro' is dense and hazy; 'Sleep Dealer' is a stuttering collage of breaths and syllables. Old commercial music, made to sell, becomes strangely sad and uncanny.","Daniel Lopatin, working in Brooklyn, released the album in 2011 on Software, the label he had co-founded. He built much of it from compilations of old TV adverts, hunting through them for fragments of music and speech. Internet culture was then preoccupied with nostalgia for late-twentieth-century commercial media, and Replica gave that preoccupation a serious artistic form, halfway between ambient music and collage.","The adverts are chopped so finely that their products are almost never named; you hear a breath, a vowel, a scrap of jingle. Listen for how a loop repeats just long enough to become hypnotic, then changes. The name Oneohtrix Point Never is itself a found sound: a pun on the frequency of a Boston radio station Lopatin grew up hearing.","Replica was widely praised and is now often seen as an important work in the history of sample-based ambient music, and a key record of what critics called hypnagogic music. Some found it too clever by half; others heard a genuine meditation on memory and consumer culture, the way advertising lodges in us like folk song.","Start with the title track and follow the repeated piano fragment as it shifts. Then hear Steve Reich's Different Trains, where recorded speech becomes melody, and Boards of Canada's Music Has the Right to Children for an earlier record about media memory."],"oneohtrix-point-never-r-plus-seven":["Glassy, fragmented music built from the stock sounds of digital instruments: synthetic choirs, bell tones, bright plucked presets. Tracks such as 'Boring Angel', 'Zebra' and 'Still Life' move abruptly between ideas, cutting from a sustained chord to a burst of arpeggios to sudden silence. The result feels like a gallery of digital surfaces, beautiful and slightly hollow, with no beat to hold on to.","Lopatin's first album for Warp, released in 2013, it moved away from the sample collages of Replica towards a deliberately artificial palette. He was increasingly working with visual artists and on film, and the album shares their interest in the look and feel of digital technology: screensavers, stock imagery, the default settings of software. It marked his move from the underground into wider view.","Many sounds are the factory presets that come with software and keyboards, the very sounds most musicians avoid as clichés. Lopatin uses them precisely because they are cheap and familiar, and frames them so they sound strange and even holy. Listen for the abrupt cuts that break any sense of flow; the silences are part of the composition.","It was widely praised, though some listeners found it cold and arch. It is now seen as a key record in experimental electronic music's turn towards digital surfaces, and as the bridge to Lopatin's later work in film and pop. Whether its detachment is critique or pleasure is still argued.","Start with 'Zebra' and listen for the cuts, then 'Boring Angel' for the synthetic choir at its most hymn-like. Treat the silences as part of the music. Compare Yellow Magic Orchestra's BGM, another record that treated digital sound as its subject, and Laurie Spiegel's The Expanding Universe for an earlier, gentler computer music."],"fennesz-endless-summer":["Warm guitar melodies and chords, some almost like pop songs, dissolving into digital static, crackle and glitch. 'Made in Hongkong' is all shimmering digital fizz; the title track sets a sunny, sad guitar figure against gathering distortion; 'Caecilia' is slow and glowing. The record sounds like a summer memory being slowly eaten by the machine that stores it, the tune always just visible through the noise.","Christian Fennesz, a Viennese guitarist, made the album on a laptop with software such as Max, processing his guitar as he played. It was released in 2001 on Mego, the Vienna label at the centre of glitch music, where most artists worked with pure digital noise. The title borrows from a Beach Boys compilation, and the record is full of affection for 1960s Californian pop.","Beneath the noise, many tracks rest on simple, pretty guitar chord sequences that could come from a Beach Boys song, and the melodies are allowed to surface for only a few seconds at a time. Listen for the moments when the static clears and the guitar is heard clean. The digital errors are deliberate and carefully shaped, like a painter's scumble.","Endless Summer was widely praised and is now seen as a landmark of glitch and laptop music, the record that showed noise could be tender. It persuaded many rock listeners that computer music could be warm. Some hear nostalgia; others hear loss, and the album seems designed to hold both at once.","Start with the title track and listen for the guitar under the noise. Then hear the Beach Boys' Good Vibrations for the harmonic world Fennesz is remembering, and My Bloody Valentine's Loveless for the idea that melody can live inside distortion."],"fennesz-venice":["Denser and more mournful than Endless Summer: slow washes of guitar and noise that swell and recede like water against stone. Most tracks are instrumental, ranging from quiet, glowing drones to storms of distortion that break and ebb. David Sylvian sings on 'Transit', the album's one song, his low voice floating over a shimmering, unstable backdrop. The effect is of a city seen through mist.","Released in 2004 on the British label Touch, known for its sound-art and field-recording catalogue, the album took its name from the city where some of the material was developed. Fennesz was by now collaborating widely, with Sylvian and with Ryuichi Sakamoto among others, and his processed guitar had become one of the most recognisable sounds in experimental music.","Listen for how single guitar chords are stretched and smeared so that they last for minutes, the attack of each note removed so it seems to fade in from nowhere. The city of the title is present less as a picture than as a condition: water, reflection and slow decay. Venice has a long history of music written for its spaces, from the Gabrielis to Luigi Nono.","It was well received, though inevitably measured against Endless Summer. Many listeners prefer its darker, more enveloping tone, and 'Transit' is often singled out as one of the best meetings of song and glitch. Some critics found the instrumentals too similar to one another; admirers answer that the sameness is the point, a single weather system heard from different angles.","Start with 'Transit', then play the instrumentals in the dark at low volume. Compare Luigi Nono's La lontananza nostalgica utopica futura, another Venetian meditation in which a live instrument meets tape, and Eno's On Land for an earlier landscape of blurred sound."],"tim-hecker-harmony-in-ultraviolet":["Walls of distorted, luminous sound: chords that swell to overwhelming volume, then recede to fragile drones. The four-part 'Harmony in Blue' is a set of slow, glowing variations on a single mood; other tracks pile distortion onto soft, organ-like tones until they crack and fizz. The music is at once violent and tender, like looking at sunlight through a stained-glass window that is slowly shattering.","Tim Hecker, a Vancouver-born musician working in Montreal, released the album in 2006 on Kranky, the Chicago label that was home to much of North America's drone and ambient music. He was part of a wave of musicians who brought the textures of noise and shoegaze rock into ambient music, playing it at volumes Eno's ambient never intended.","Listen for the melodies buried under the distortion: often a simple, almost hymn-like chord progression is still audible beneath the roar. Hecker shapes the noise as carefully as a composer shapes an orchestra, so the swells have a clear architecture of build, peak and release. Some of the source sounds are organs and guitars, disguised almost completely.","It was widely praised and is often seen as the turning point in his career, the record where his style came fully into focus. Some listeners find it overwhelming or abrasive; others find it deeply moving. It helped establish loud, distorted ambient music as a genre in its own right.","Play it loud on speakers, not quietly in the background. Start with the 'Harmony in Blue' sequence. Compare Ligeti's Atmosphères, another music of clustered, shimmering sound in which there is no melody, only colour and mass, and My Bloody Valentine's Loveless for the rock side."],"tim-hecker-ravedeath-1972":["Built from recordings of a church pipe organ, processed into roaring, distorted drones and fragile, decaying chords. 'The Piano Drop' opens with an eruption of sound; the three-part 'In the Fog' is a long, slow storm; the 'Hatred of Music' pieces are bleak and hymn-like. The organ is recognisable throughout but transformed, like a church service heard through a broken radio.","Hecker recorded the organ in a single day in a church in Reykjavík, working with the producer Ben Frost, then processed the material at home in Montreal. The album was released on Kranky in 2011. Its cover shows students dropping a piano from a university roof in 1972, a picture of music as a physical object being destroyed, at a moment when music was dissolving into digital files.","Listen for moments when the organ's own sound breaks through the processing, a reminder of the stone room where it was captured. The title and cover point to a meditation on the end of music as a thing you could hold. The organ, the oldest machine in Western music, is here fed through the newest.","It was widely acclaimed and is often named Hecker's finest album and one of the key ambient records of its decade. Listeners from contemporary classical music have heard in it the massed organ sonorities of Ligeti and Messiaen; others hear the distortion of heavy rock. The argument over whether it is ambient at all is part of its fame.","Play it loud in a dark room and start with 'In the Fog I'. Compare Ligeti's Volumina, an organ piece of pure clustered sound, and Messiaen's L'Ascension for the organ as a vehicle of overwhelming awe. Aphex Twin's Selected Ambient Works Volume II is the gentler cousin."],"the-field-from-here-we-go-sublime":["Steady four-to-the-floor techno made from tiny fragments of pop songs, looped and layered into shimmering, blurred clouds. 'Over the Ice' and 'Sun & Ice' pulse with a gentle, hypnotic lift; tracks change slowly, adding and removing layers over six, eight, ten minutes. The kick drum never stops, but above it everything is soft focus, so the music is euphoric and dreamy at once.","Axel Willner, working in Stockholm, released the album on the Cologne label Kompakt in 2007. Kompakt was the centre of a soft, melodic kind of minimal techno, and Willner brought to it the washed-out textures of shoegaze rock and the looping methods of hip-hop producers. It was his debut album, and it made him one of the label's best-known artists almost overnight.","The loops come from pop and soul records but are so short, a fraction of a syllable, that the sources are almost unrecognisable; the title track lets its closing fragment, from a 1950s doo-wop hit, come briefly into focus. Listen for how the loops are set slightly out of step with one another, so they shimmer like light on water.","It was widely praised as one of the best electronic albums of its year and has remained a touchstone for melodic techno. Some listeners find it too repetitive; others hear a demonstration that repetition itself can be euphoric. Its influence on later ambient techno, and on producers such as Jon Hopkins, is widely acknowledged.","Start with 'Over the Ice' and listen for one loop at a time as it enters and leaves. Compare Steve Reich's Music for 18 Musicians for the same idea of pulsing, shimmering repetition made by players, and Kraftwerk's Trans-Europe Express for the machine pulse underneath."],"caribou-swim":["Liquid, off-kilter dance music with a soft human voice at its centre. 'Odessa' opens with a quirky, bouncing bass line and Dan Snaith's gentle falsetto; 'Sun' repeats a single word over a steadily building groove; 'Kaili' and 'Bowls' add horns, chimes and rattling percussion. The sounds seem to bend and melt as they play, as if heard underwater, while the beat stays firmly on the ground.","Dan Snaith, a Canadian with a doctorate in mathematics, had made psychedelic pop as Caribou and won Canada's Polaris Prize for Andorra. With Swim, released in 2010, he turned to the dance floor, influenced by nights in London clubs. He said he wanted dance music that sounded fluid, like water, rather than hard and mechanical, and he recorded much of it in his London home studio.","Many sounds are bent in pitch as they play, so notes slide rather than land, which is where the underwater feeling comes from. Listen for the small acoustic details that ride on top of the synthesizers: chimes, bells, breaths and struck metal. Behind the dance-floor surface the songs are about the end of relationships, sung without drama.","It was widely praised as his best record to that point and brought him a new audience in dance music. Some listeners missed his earlier psychedelia; others heard a songwriter finding the right form. It is often paired with Four Tet's album of the same year as a sign of electronica's warm turn.","Start with 'Odessa' and listen for the sounds that bend. Then play 'Sun' and notice how much comes from one repeated word. Compare Four Tet's There Is Love in You, from the same year, and Talking Heads' Remain in Light for an earlier band that also made dance music out of looping, layered parts."],"caribou-our-love":["Warm, soulful house music about love, family and commitment. 'Can't Do Without You' builds a single repeated vocal phrase, pitched and filtered, into a surging, joyous climax; 'Silver' and 'All I Ever Need' are tender and slow; 'Second Chance' features the Canadian singer Jessy Lanza. The textures are warmer and rounder than on Swim, with R&B and garage in the rhythms.","Snaith made the album after becoming a father and after years of touring Swim, and he described it as a record about the people close to him. It was released in 2014, when his club side project Daphni had also taken him deeper into DJ culture. The album brought Caribou to festival headline slots and a much wider audience.","Listen for how 'Can't Do Without You' changes over four minutes with only a handful of elements: the vocal loop, a bass, a kick drum, a rising filter. The repetition is the meaning, a phrase said so often it becomes both obsession and devotion. Several tracks borrow the pitched-up vocal technique of UK garage.","It was widely praised and became his most popular record. Some critics found it softer and less adventurous than Swim; others heard the ideal balance of songcraft and club music. 'Can't Do Without You' became one of the best-loved dance tracks of its year, played equally in clubs and at weddings.","Start with 'Can't Do Without You' and listen to how the build is made from almost nothing. Then compare Donna Summer's I Feel Love, the ancestor of every euphoric electronic love song, and Burial's Untrue for a darker use of the same pitched vocal technique."],"the-winstons-amen-brother":["A brisk instrumental B-side built on a gospel tune, 'Amen', given a soul-band workout: organ and horns trade the melody over a busy, bright groove. It is pleasant, short and unremarkable until about halfway through, when everyone else drops out and the drummer, Gregory Coleman, plays four bars alone. Those six or so seconds of snare, kick and ride cymbal, with a skipping syncopation in the third and fourth bars, are the reason the record is known to millions who have never heard the rest of it.","The Winstons were a Washington, D.C. soul group led by Richard Lewis Spencer, and 'Amen, Brother' was the flip side of their gospel-tinged hit 'Color Him Father', released on Metromedia in 1969. Drum breaks like this were a normal bit of arrangement, a chance for the band to breathe before the last chorus. No one in the studio could have imagined that the break would be lifted off the vinyl, looped and chopped by machines that did not yet exist.","The break reached hip-hop producers in the mid-1980s through bootleg compilations of drum breaks, and by the early 1990s British producers were slicing it into single hits and rearranging them on samplers, then speeding it up far beyond anything a human drummer could play. Listen for the ghost notes on the snare and the loose, open ride cymbal: those small human irregularities are exactly what made it sound alive when chopped and re-sequenced.","For decades the record was a collector's footnote while its four bars became probably the most sampled drum passage in recorded music, the backbone of jungle and drum and bass. Neither Coleman, who died in 2006, nor Spencer received royalties for that use. In 2015 two British DJs raised a public fund of about twenty-four thousand pounds for Spencer, a gesture that made the break a standing case in arguments over sampling and credit.","Play it straight through once as a soul record, then wait for the break and count the four bars. Then put on Goldie's 'Terminator' or Shy FX's 'Original Nuttah' and try to find Coleman in there: sped up, reversed, stretched, reassembled. Compare James Brown's 'Funky Drummer', the other great donor of the sampling era."],"lee-perry-blackboard-jungle-dub":["Credited to Perry's house band, the Upsetters, this is a set of reggae rhythms stripped almost to the bone: bass and drums carry everything, while organ stabs, guitar chops, horn phrases and scraps of voice drop in and out as if someone were switching the lights on and off in a house. Echo trails hang in the air after each fragment. There are no songs here in the usual sense, only grooves being taken apart and put back together in real time.","By 1973 Kingston producers had been issuing instrumental 'versions' on the B-sides of singles for a few years, so sound-system selectors and DJs could toast over them. Perry, a veteran of Studio One and producer of the Wailers' early records, went further and made a whole album out of versions, working with King Tubby on the mixing. It appeared in a tiny Jamaican pressing, and is usually counted among the very first dub albums.","Early pressings reportedly carried a stark stereo mix, with instruments split hard between the two speakers, so on headphones the band seems to be playing in two different rooms. Notice how often the bass is left entirely alone: the mixing desk is being used as an instrument, and silence is one of its sounds. Several rhythms come from Perry's earlier productions, recycled into something unrecognisable.","Rarely heard outside Jamaica on release, the album was rediscovered through reissues and is now treated as a founding document of dub, alongside King Tubby's early work. Arguments continue about exactly who did what at the desk, Perry or Tubby, and which pressing is the true one; scholars of dub treat the question as partly unanswerable, since the music was made live in the mix.","Use speakers with real bass or good headphones. Pick one instrument, perhaps the organ, and follow when it is allowed in and when it is taken away; the drama is in the removals. Then hear Public Image Ltd's Metal Box or The Slits' Cut to see how quickly London's post-punks learned the lesson."],"king-tubby-dub-from-the-roots":["A set of dub mixes in which King Tubby takes finished reggae rhythms and rebuilds them at the mixing desk. Drums and bass form a steady floor; everything else, the offbeat guitar, a horn line, a piano figure, appears for a bar, is thrown into a long spring-reverb tail, and vanishes. The signature sound is a sudden sweep that thins the whole band to a tinny rattle before the bass drops back in with physical force.","Osbourne Ruddock ran a small studio in his home in Waterhouse, a poor district of Kingston, where he cut dub plates for his sound system and voiced singers over rhythms recorded elsewhere. He was an electronics repairman first, and he modified his equipment himself, including a mixing console bought from the Dynamic Sounds studio. Albums like this one were made quickly, for local sound systems and a small record market, not for posterity.","The sweeping effect that makes the band sound as if it were being sucked down a drain is a filter on Tubby's console: he could cut away everything below a chosen pitch and slide the cutoff by hand. He was reportedly also fond of slamming the spring reverb unit so it crashed like thunder. Listen for these two gestures; they are his fingerprints on almost every record he touched.","In the 1970s dub albums were cheap by-products and rarely reviewed. Since the 1990s writers on electronic music have treated Tubby as a figure comparable to the tape composers of Paris and Cologne, a claim sometimes pushed too far, since he worked inside a commercial, collaborative culture. Credits on his albums are notoriously loose, and the exact provenance of many rhythms is still argued over.","Start with any track and listen for the moments when the whole band thins out and then crashes back. Then play Stockhausen's Kontakte or Varèse's Poème électronique to hear, from a very different direction, the same idea: that the studio itself can compose. A good reissue with the original mono or early stereo mix is worth seeking."],"lee-perry-super-ape":["Dub at its thickest and most humid. Where earlier dub stripped rhythms bare, Super Ape piles them up: murmuring backing vocals, hand percussion, a thick swirl of phasing over the cymbals, horns that arrive muffled as if from another room. Voices chant and exhort in fragments. The record moves slowly and heavily, and its sound, a blurred, swampy haze with a bass line cutting through, is instantly recognisable as Perry's Black Ark studio.","Perry built the Black Ark behind his home in Washington Gardens, Kingston, around 1973–74, and by 1976 it was producing a stream of records on modest equipment: a four-track tape machine, a tape echo unit, a phaser. Island Records, flush with the international success of Bob Marley, released this album abroad, and its gorilla cover became an emblem of Perry's mythology. It was issued in Jamaica with a different title.","Much of the density comes from 'bouncing': Perry recorded several parts, mixed them down onto one track, and then recorded more on top, so each generation of tape adds a layer of hiss and blur. What sounds like an effect is partly the accumulated grain of his tiny studio. Listen also for the phaser on the hi-hats, a sweeping shimmer that became one of the Ark's trademarks.","Received abroad as a curiosity alongside Island's roots reggae releases, the album is now considered one of the summits of dub and a key text for the British musicians who were listening closely: punk and post-punk bands took both its sound and its attitude. Some reggae purists prefer Perry's leaner early work, but Super Ape is usually the record cited when producers talk about his studio as an instrument.","This is an album to play loud, at night, on a system with real low end. Listen for the way voices drift past like people in a crowd. Then hear The Clash's Complete Control, which Perry produced the following year, to hear the Ark's reach across the Atlantic."],"augustus-pablo-king-tubby-meets-rockers-uptown":["Augustus Pablo's melodica, a humble school instrument that sounds like a wheezing harmonium, floats over slow, heavy rhythms from his Rockers label, while King Tubby mixes them into dub. The mood is sombre and eastern-tinged, built on minor modes. The title track, a dub of Jacob Miller's song 'Baby I Love You So', is the best-known dub in the world: a stately drum pattern, a bass that seems to walk on the floor, and Miller's voice surfacing and sinking.","Horace Swaby took the name Augustus Pablo and a melodica sound he called 'far east' in the early 1970s. He ran his own small label, Rockers, and brought his rhythms uptown to Tubby's studio for mixing. The album, compiled from dubs made over the preceding years, was issued in 1976, when roots reggae and Rastafarian devotion were at their height in Kingston and reggae was beginning to reach London and New York.","The drums on the title track are among the most imitated in reggae, but listen to the space: Tubby repeatedly lets the reverb swallow the snare, so each backbeat is followed by a long, fading room. Miller's vocal is never allowed a whole line; it is a ghost. And the melodica is often doubled with organ, which is why it sounds larger than a small reed instrument should.","Initially a specialist release, the album has become the dub record most often recommended to newcomers, and appears on nearly every list of essential reggae. Some critics find its reputation tilts attention toward the title track at the expense of the moodier pieces around it. Its sound fed directly into British sound-system culture and, through it, the bass-heavy music of the 1990s and 2000s.","Start with the title track, then listen to the whole album in one go as a single mood. Follow the bass rather than the melody. Then play Mala in Cuba or Digital Mystikz's 'Anti-War Dub' to hear how London's dubstep producers kept this weight and space thirty years on."],"scientist-rids-the-world-of-the-evil-curse-of-the-vampires":["Ten dubs of hard, spare dancehall rhythms played by the Roots Radics band, mixed by the young engineer Scientist with a comic-horror theme. Creaking doors, howls and cackles are scattered through the tracks, but underneath is serious mixing: rimshot snares ricochet in echo, the bass is round and enormous, and instruments are cut in and out with a drummer's timing. Each track is named after a horror episode, such as a plot of zombies or a mummy.","Scientist, Overton Brown, trained at King Tubby's studio as a teenager and became its leading engineer at the turn of the 1980s, when the slower, rootsy style of the 1970s was giving way to dancehall. Producer Henry 'Junjo' Lawes supplied the rhythms. The London label Greensleeves issued a series of themed Scientist albums, with lurid comic-book covers, aimed at a British market that bought dub avidly.","The horror sound effects are a joke, but the rhythms are not: they are the same Roots Radics tracks that carried hit vocal records by singers of the day, so Jamaican listeners would have recognised the songs behind the dubs. Listen for how Scientist uses the echo on the snare to create a second, off-kilter rhythm that answers the real one.","Long dismissed as a novelty, partly because of its cover and title, the album is now respected as a showcase of early-dancehall dub mixing and as Scientist's best-known work. Debate over who deserves credit for the golden age of Tubby's studio, the master or his gifted engineers, often centres on records like this.","Enjoy the silliness, then listen past it to the drums and bass. Compare it with King Tubby Meets Rockers Uptown: here the rhythms are tighter, drier, faster, and the echo is sharper. The comic-book cover is worth seeing on vinyl."],"4hero-mr-kirks-nightmare":["An early breakbeat hardcore track from North London: fast, jittering sampled breakbeats, a rubbery bass line, and a stark spoken-word sample in which a police officer tells a father that his son has died of an overdose. The effect is unsettling, a dance track that repeatedly stops to deliver bad news. Synthesizer stabs and rave sirens keep it moving.","4hero, Mark Clair and Dego McFarlane, co-founded Reinforced Records in Dollis Hill around 1989–90, when the acid-house rave boom was mutating in Britain into something faster and more breakbeat-driven. Pirate radio stations and warehouse raves were the channels; tracks circulated on white labels. This record was a hit in that underground and a template for hardcore's darker turn.","The drug narrative made the record controversial as a pro- or anti-drug statement; in fact it is ambiguous, and its bleakness points ahead to the 'darkside' hardcore of 1992–93 from which jungle grew. Listen for the way the breakbeat is chopped and rearranged rather than simply looped, an early sign of the techniques Reinforced's artists, including Goldie, would push much further.","Treated at the time as a rave anthem, it is now cited in histories of hardcore and jungle as one of the first records to put darkness and narrative into the music. 4hero themselves moved on to the jazz-inflected Parallel Universe and then broken beat, and their early hardcore is sometimes overshadowed by that later work.","Play it loud and notice how often the music is interrupted: the groove builds, the spoken scene cuts in, and the dancefloor has to wait. Listen for the break being re-sequenced rather than looped. Then hear Goldie's 'Terminator', also on Reinforced, to hear the breakbeat pulled further out of shape two years later, and Afrika Bambaataa's 'Planet Rock' for the electro ancestor."],"goldie-terminator":["Released under the name Metalheads (a Rufige Kru production), 'Terminator' is a track in which the drums seem to melt. Sampled breakbeats are sped up, slowed down and phased against each other while staying in time, so a snare roll suddenly stretches and warps like tape pulled through a machine, then snaps back. Dark synthesizer pads and film-derived vocal fragments set a sci-fi mood. It sounds like a drummer being bent by the forces of a black hole.","Goldie, a graffiti artist from the Midlands who had spent time in New York's hip-hop scene, fell in with the Reinforced Records crowd in London in the early 1990s. New samplers and computer software allowed time-stretching: changing a sample's speed without changing its pitch, or vice versa. Goldie and his engineers used the technique not to hide the process but to make it the point, in the thick of London's hardcore rave scene.","The warping sound is a side effect: early time-stretching algorithms produced a grainy, metallic smear when pushed hard, and Goldie used that artefact deliberately. Listen closely to the snare in the passages where it shifts: the timbre changes too, becoming more metallic and shimmering. That digital flaw became one of drum and bass's signature textures.","A landmark for producers rather than the public, it is widely credited as the first record to make time-stretching an expressive device, and it moved hardcore toward the darker, more intricate sound of jungle. Some historians caution that several producers were experimenting with the technique simultaneously; Goldie's record is the one that made it famous.","Ignore the pads at first and focus on the drums alone: wait for the moments when a snare roll stretches, goes metallic and snaps back into time. Those bends are the whole point. Then play the Winstons' 'Amen, Brother' to hear the kind of raw material being bent, and Goldie's Timeless to hear where the technique went three years later."],"origin-unknown-valley-of-the-shadows":["A jungle track that feels like drifting through a dark corridor. A spoken sample, a woman describing a near-death experience of travelling through a long dark tunnel, repeats over a sparse, rolling breakbeat, a deep sub-bass and wide, ghostly synthesizer pads. The track builds and releases without drama; it is patience and atmosphere, not aggression, that carry it.","Origin Unknown was the partnership of Andy C and Ant Miles, from Essex; Andy C was a teenager when they started Ram Records in 1992. The track came out in the transitional year when hardcore rave was splitting into jungle and the more atmospheric strain soon called drum and bass. Pirate radio and club DJs built its reputation long before most listeners knew who had made it.","The spoken fragment is looped and repeated until its meaning slips into sound, a technique familiar from Steve Reich's early tape pieces. Listen to how the breakbeat underneath is kept surprisingly low in the mix: the vocal and pads carry the foreground, which was unusual in a scene obsessed with drums.","Still one of the most requested tracks in drum and bass, often cited as the scene's first atmospheric classic and reissued many times. Andy C became one of the genre's biggest DJs, and critics sometimes contrast the track's restraint with the louder, harder sound he later championed.","Listen on headphones in the dark and follow the vocal as it turns from speech into texture; by the fifth repetition you hear its rhythm more than its story. Then notice the sub-bass entering underneath. Compare Steve Reich's Different Trains for another looped voice, and LTJ Bukem's 'Horizons', which took this atmospheric direction and made it lush."],"shy-fx-original-nuttah":["Ragga jungle at full tilt. A dancehall-style MC, UK Apachi, chants and boasts over hyperkinetic chopped breakbeats at about 160 beats per minute, while a sub-bass line moves at half that speed, like a reggae bass line trapped inside a drum and bass track. Sirens, gunshot samples and rewind cues from the sound-system world punctuate the chaos. It is loud, joyful and aggressive at once.","Andre Williams, Shy FX, was a teenager from North London when he made this. In 1994 jungle was at the peak of its first wave, Jamaican sound-system culture fused with rave: MCs, dubplates and bass weight from reggae, breakbeats from hardcore. The track was a club and pirate radio phenomenon, and it became one of the records that brought jungle into the national press.","The layering is the key: the fast breakbeats and the slow bass line are really two tempos, one double the other, which is how jungle keeps a reggae body under a rave pulse. Listen for how the MC phrases his lines against the bass, not the drums, just as a Kingston toaster would over a version on a sound system.","Celebrated as a ragga jungle anthem and sometimes criticised at the time for its violent imagery, which Shy FX later described as part of the sound-system language rather than a literal message. The ragga strand fell out of favour with drum and bass tastemakers in 1995–96, but the track's reputation has only grown.","Turn it up and listen for the two tempos at once: dance to the bass or to the drums. Then compare Scientist's dubs to hear where the bass weight comes from, and Dizzee Rascal's Boy in da Corner to hear where the MCing went next."],"basic-channel-quadrant-dub":["A long, hypnotic piece of dub techno: a muffled kick drum, a single chord stab that echoes into infinity, a haze of hiss, and slow filter sweeps that open and close the sound like breathing. Over many minutes almost nothing changes, and yet the chord's echoes pile up and shift so that the texture is never static. It feels like listening to a techno track through a wall, or underwater.","Basic Channel was the duo of Moritz von Oswald and Mark Ernestus, who ran the Hard Wax record shop in Berlin and a small group of labels. In the early 1990s, after reunification, Berlin techno was raw and industrial; Basic Channel combined it with the techniques of Jamaican dub, which both men loved, releasing plain-sleeved records that set a quiet, severe template.","The chords are processed with the same tools King Tubby used, delay and filter, but applied to a single repeated gesture rather than a band. Listen for the tiny pitch and timbre shifts in the echoes: most of the music happens in the decaying tails, not the notes themselves. Their mastering studio, Dubplates & Mastering, was also where the pair cut their vinyl.","Influential far beyond its sales, the track and its siblings founded dub techno as a genre and shaped minimal techno for decades. Critics sometimes debate whether the style's austerity became a formula in lesser hands; the originals are widely treated as untouchable.","Put it on while doing something else and notice the moment you start hearing the echoes rather than the beat; that shift of attention is how the piece works. Play it at moderate volume on speakers, not earbuds, so the hiss fills the room. Then play King Tubby's Dub from the Roots and Kraftwerk's Trans-Europe Express to hear the two parents of this sound."],"goldie-timeless":["A double album that treats drum and bass as a medium for grand statements. It opens with the twenty-one-minute 'Timeless' suite: Diane Charlemagne's soaring soul vocal on 'Inner City Life' floats over lush strings and a frantic, intricately edited breakbeat, which then plunges into darker, heavier movements before returning. The rest of the album moves between jazz-inflected softness and aggressive, metallic drum programming. Everything is dense, cinematic and deliberately ambitious.","By 1995 jungle was becoming a national story in Britain, and Goldie, with his gold teeth and graffiti background, was its public face. He signed to London Records' FFRR imprint and worked with engineer Rob Playford for months in the studio. The album appeared when major labels were competing to find the jungle album that would cross over, as trip-hop and Britpop were doing.","The orchestral sounds are mostly samples and synthesizers, arranged with a film composer's attention; listen for how the strings in 'Timeless' move in slow, sustained chords while the drums race, a double tempo borrowed from dub and ragga jungle but turned toward the symphonic. Much of the drum editing was done hit by hit, not loop by loop.","Hailed as the album that proved jungle could be art, it reached the British top ten and is often called the genre's first great long-form work. Some in the scene felt it was overproduced and too eager for respectability; that argument over ambition versus dancefloor function has followed drum and bass ever since.","Begin with the 'Timeless' suite, all twenty-one minutes, and let it play through. Then sample the rest. Compare Roni Size's New Forms, the next attempt at a big statement, and Brian Eno's Music for Airports for the ambient side of Goldie's ambition."],"ltj-bukem-horizons":["Drum and bass at its most serene. Soft, rolling breakbeats move quickly but lightly, like rain on a window, beneath warm synthesizer pads, gentle electric piano chords and a deep, smooth bass line. Nothing is harsh; the track seems to hover. It is dance music whose main aim is to be beautiful and spacious, with a mood closer to 1970s jazz fusion than to rave.","LTJ Bukem, Danny Williamson, founded Good Looking Records around 1991 and built a following as a DJ with a smooth, jazz-influenced style. At his club night Speed in London, with Fabio, he championed the 'intelligent' or atmospheric strand of drum and bass, a reaction against the ragga and darkside sounds dominating jungle in 1994–95.","Listen for the chords: they use the extended, jazz-derived harmony of fusion keyboards, with added notes that give a sense of openness rather than resolution. The drums are programmed with lots of ghost notes and soft hits, giving a more human, brushed feel than most jungle breaks.","Admired by many as drum and bass's most refined voice, and criticised by others, especially at the time, as coffee-table music that sanded off the scene's energy. The labels 'intelligent' and 'atmospheric' were themselves resented by some who saw in them a class-coded snobbery toward ragga jungle.","Play it in the evening at moderate volume and follow the chords rather than the drums. Compare Miles Davis's In a Silent Way for the fusion mood it echoes, and Origin Unknown's 'Valley of the Shadows' for its darker cousin."],"roni-size-new-forms":["A double album of drum and bass with live instruments at its heart. On 'Brown Paper Bag', a jazz upright bass walks at half-time while the drums skitter above it, and a soul singer, Onallee, and an MC, Dynamite, add voices. Elsewhere the sound is by turns funky, dark, jazzy and minimal, always rhythmically busy, with a warmth that comes from acoustic textures rather than synthesizers.","Roni Size, Ryan Williams, came from Bristol, where he worked with Krust, DJ Die and Suv under the Full Cycle label. Bristol's scene, home also to Massive Attack and Portishead, mixed sound-system culture with hip-hop and soul. Signed to Gilles Peterson's Talkin' Loud label, the collective called itself Reprazent and performed live, which few drum and bass acts attempted.","The upright bass player, Si John, often plays a straightforward jazz line, but the programmers edit and loop it to sit against the breakbeat, so what sounds like a live take is partly assembled. Listen for this in 'Brown Paper Bag': the bass is both human and machine-cut.","It won the 1997 Mercury Prize, beating Radiohead's OK Computer, a result that became a symbol of drum and bass's moment in British culture. Some scene insiders felt the album's success came at the expense of its dancefloor edge; others saw it as the genre's high point of musicianship.","Start with 'Brown Paper Bag', then 'Heroes'. Follow the double bass line alone for a minute, then switch to the drums, and hear how the two run at different speeds. The album is long, so take one disc at a time. Compare A Tribe Called Quest's Low End Theory and Miles Davis's Bitches Brew for other meetings of jazz players and the studio."],"photek-ni-ten-ichi-ryu":["Drum and bass as precise as a sword stroke. Sharp, clipped drum hits and metallic percussion are placed with obsessive care over a deep, slow bass; there is little melody and a great deal of silence. Each snare and cymbal seems to have been individually sculpted. The effect is tense, minimal and martial, as the title, taken from a Japanese school of swordsmanship, suggests.","Photek, Rupert Parkes, from Ipswich, emerged in the mid-1990s as the most technically obsessive producer in drum and bass. The track appeared as a single and on his debut album Modus Operandi (1997), released by Virgin's Science imprint in a year when major labels were signing drum and bass artists in the hope of crossover.","Most of the drums are not a looped break at all but programmed hit by hit from tiny fragments, which is why they sound so clean and dry. Listen to the gaps between hits: Photek treats silence as a percussion instrument, closer to Japanese court music or Feldman than to rave.","Celebrated as a peak of drum and bass programming and later used in films and advertising, the track is still a reference point for producers. Some critics found Photek's precision cold; his admirers call it exactitude, and the argument sums up the 'intelligent' drum and bass debate of the period.","Listen with headphones and focus on individual hits: each snare has its own shape and decay. Count the silences between them, and notice how the tension comes from waiting. Compare Goldie's 'Terminator', its opposite in texture, Feldman's Triadic Memories for another music of carefully placed sounds, and Stockhausen's Kontakte, another work of sculpted, isolated events."],"mj-cole-sincere":["UK garage at its most elegant: a skipping two-step beat in which the kick drum seems to hop over the second beat, a warm bass line, jazzy piano chords and chopped-up snatches of a female soul vocal. The whole thing swings with a light, sophisticated feel, sitting halfway between London club music and American R&B. It is music for dressing up to go out.","MJ Cole, Matt Coleman, trained as a classical musician before working in a London studio in the mid-1990s. At that time a scene was growing around 'speed garage', a London take on New York house with heavier basslines, which by 1998 was shifting to the two-step rhythm heard here. Pirate radio and Sunday-afternoon clubs were its heartland.","The vocal is cut into syllables and re-sequenced so it becomes part of the rhythm, a technique shared with jungle's breakbeat chopping; listen for how the fragments land on offbeats. The piano chords owe as much to his classical training as to house.","A garage anthem when released, it gave its name to his album (2000), which was shortlisted for the Mercury Prize. Critics now see it as a high-water mark for garage's smooth, musical side before the scene darkened and fed into grime and dubstep.","Listen for the skip in the drums: count one-two-three-four and hear which beats the kick avoids, then notice how the vocal fragments fill the gaps it leaves. The piano chords reward a second listen on their own. Then play Artful Dodger's 'Re-Rewind' for garage in party mode and Burial's Untrue, which takes the same vocal chops into a lonelier place."],"artful-dodger-re-rewind":["The record that took UK garage to the top of the pop charts. Over a jaunty two-step beat, rubbery bass and bright keyboard stabs, a young Craig David sings a slick, fast-moving R&B vocal about a DJ at the peak of the night, when the crowd demands the track be pulled back and played again. It is short, catchy and relentlessly upbeat.","Artful Dodger, Mark Hill and Pete Devereux, were producers from Southampton who ran a record shop and nightclub scene there. Garage in 1999 was at its commercial peak in Britain, with pirate radio feeding directly into chart hits. Craig David, also from Southampton, was an unknown teenager when he recorded the vocal.","The 'rewind' in the title is a sound-system ritual borrowed from Jamaican dancehall: when a crowd loves a track the DJ spins it back to the start. Garage inherited that ritual directly from reggae and jungle, and the song describes it. Listen for the MC-style exclamations, another sound-system habit.","A big hit, it launched Craig David's career and remains a symbol of the garage boom. Critics once dismissed it as pop; nostalgia and garage revivals have made it a beloved anthem, and its title phrase became a British catchphrase.","Enjoy it first as pure pop, then listen again to the drums alone: they are the two-step pattern of MJ Cole's 'Sincere' in a party mood, with the kick dodging the second beat. Notice how quickly Craig David's syllables move against it. Compare Chic's 'Le Freak' for an earlier dancefloor hit, and The Streets' Original Pirate Material for the same beat turned toward storytelling."],"the-streets-original-pirate-material":["Mike Skinner half-raps, half-talks in a flat Birmingham-inflected voice over garage beats, cheap-sounding strings and piano loops. The songs are short stories of ordinary British life: a night in the pub, a fry-up, a phone running out of credit, a weekend of clubbing and its comedown. The beats are skeletal and home-made, and the tone swings from comic to tender to bleak.","Skinner made most of the album in his bedroom, partly in Brixton, reportedly using a wardrobe stuffed with mattresses as a vocal booth. It came out on the garage label Locked On in 2002, when UK garage was beginning to fall out of fashion and British rap was still seeking a voice distinct from American hip-hop.","The 'garage' beats are often not club tracks at all but stripped, downbeat sketches borrowed from garage's rhythms; listen to how often the kick drum is missing, leaving Skinner's voice nearly alone. The strings are unashamedly cheap preset sounds, deliberately evoking pirate radio's scrappy production. And the album is sequenced like a night out, from anticipation through the club to the comedown and the walk home.","It was shortlisted for the Mercury Prize and hailed by critics as one of the defining British albums of the decade, though garage purists scoffed at its sound and some rap fans at its flow. It is now seen as a bridge between garage, British rap and indie-style songwriting.","Listen straight through as a narrative, the way you would read a book of short stories. Start with 'Has It Come to This?' for the manifesto, then let the album's night unfold. Notice the humour, then the sadness underneath. Compare Dizzee Rascal's Boy in da Corner, released a year later, for a harsher view of the same Britain, and The Clash's London Calling for an earlier portrait of the city."],"horsepower-productions-in-fine-style":["An album of dark, spacious two-step: garage's skipping rhythms slowed, emptied out and filled with dub echoes, reggae samples, Latin percussion and film-noir atmosphere. The bass is deep and heavy, and melody is kept to a minimum, a few keyboard stabs or a snatch of horn at most. Tracks run long and change slowly, built for DJs to mix. It sounds like garage wandering at night through a reggae record shop, stopping to listen at every door.","Horsepower Productions, a south London collective led by Benny Ill, made records for the Tempa label as garage split in two directions: toward MC-driven grime and toward the darker, instrumental 'dark garage' that soon became dubstep. Croydon's record shops, especially Big Apple Records, and the club night FWD>> in central London were the hub. The scene was tiny, a few hundred people, and its records were pressed in small runs for DJs rather than the shops.","Many of the samples come from old reggae, soul and jazz records, recontextualised in the half-empty space of the beats. Listen for the half-time feel: the drums are at garage speed, but the snare often lands at half that rate, which is the key rhythmic signature of dubstep.","Often called the first dubstep album, though that label came only later and the members themselves did not use it. It was little noticed outside the scene at the time, and its reggae sampling seemed old-fashioned beside grime's raw synthesizers. Retrospectives now credit it as the missing link between garage and dubstep, and producers such as Skream have spoken of its influence on their early tracks.","Listen for how much space there is: count how long the bass is left alone between drum hits. Try to feel the snare at half the speed of the hi-hats. Compare MJ Cole's 'Sincere' to hear how far garage has been stripped, King Tubby for the dub method, then Skream's 'Midnight Request Line' and Burial's first album for where it went."],"wiley-eskimo":["An instrumental built from raw, cold-sounding square-wave synthesizer notes that bounce and slide over a sparse, stuttering beat at about 140 beats per minute. There is no singing, only a chilly, video-game-like melody and a heavy, buzzing bass. The sound is bare and a little eerie, and it left huge space for MCs.","Wiley, Richard Cowie, from Bow in east London, was an MC and producer in the garage crew Pay As U Go and then led Roll Deep. Around 2002 he began making instrumentals he called 'eskibeat', with cold titles and cheap synthesizer tones, for pirate radio sets where teenagers from Bow and nearby estates rapped in turns. Grime was taking shape.","The sounds are default or near-default presets from affordable software and keyboards, deliberately left raw. Listen for the gaps in the drum pattern: the rhythm is garage's skeleton with the swing removed, making it jerky and angular, which suits MCs who want to rap in fast bursts.","Initially a white label for the scene, 'Eskimo' is now regarded as one of the founding grime instrumentals, and Wiley as the 'godfather of grime'. Critics have noted how its chilly sound reflected east London's estates, though Wiley's own explanations of the icy theme varied.","Imagine it on pirate radio, with a crowd of MCs taking turns. Then hear Dizzee Rascal's Boy in da Corner to hear what an MC could do over this kind of beat, and Missy Elliott's 'Get Ur Freak On' for the Timbaland production style that influenced grime's jagged rhythms."],"dizzee-rascal-boy-in-da-corner":["A teenager's debut, made largely on a home computer, full of abrasive synthesizer tones, clattering off-kilter beats and dense rapping in an east London accent. Dizzee raps at great speed, with a high, strained voice, about the pressures of estate life, teenage relationships, violence and ambition. Tracks such as 'I Luv U' and 'Fix Up, Look Sharp' are jagged and minimalist, the beats often unsettling rather than pleasant.","Dylan Mills, Dizzee Rascal, grew up in Bow and was a member of Wiley's Roll Deep crew, sharpening his style in turns on pirate radio sets. He had started making beats in a school music class. Grime was an underground scene of pirate radio, MC clashes and white labels when XL Recordings released the album in 2003. That year it won the Mercury Prize, making Dizzee a national figure at nineteen and the scene's first mainstream star.","'Fix Up, Look Sharp' is built on a huge drum break from Billy Squier's rock song 'The Big Beat', a staple of hip-hop DJs; much of the rest of the album leans on stark, cheap synthesizer sounds. Listen for how the beats seem to stumble: they are designed to throw a listener off balance.","The Mercury win announced grime to the mainstream, and critics hailed it as one of the defining British albums of the century. Some worried about its violence and bleakness; most now see it as a vivid portrait of a generation. It is often compared with Illmatic as a debut that defined a scene.","Listen for Dizzee's rhythm rather than his words at first: how he crams syllables into bursts, then leaves a gap, like a drummer. Try 'I Luv U' and 'Fix Up, Look Sharp' first, then the quieter, stranger tracks. Compare Nas's Illmatic for a different city's debut portrait, Wiley's 'Eskimo' for the beat style he grew from, and The Streets for a lighter view of Britain."],"skream-midnight-request-line":["A dubstep track built on a woozy, circling synthesizer melody that sounds like a music box played underwater, over a half-time beat and a sub-bass that is felt more than heard. The melody repeats with small variations, and the drums stay sparse, leaving huge space. It is catchy and eerie at once.","Skream, Oliver Jones, was a teenager in Croydon, where he worked at Big Apple Records, a shop where garage and dubstep producers traded dubplates and ideas; his older brother was part of that circle. He made tracks at speed on a home computer. The track, released by Tempa in 2005, became the scene's first crossover anthem, played by DJs well beyond the small circle of FWD>> club nights and championed on national radio.","The melody is in an unusual, slightly off-kilter mode that gives it its dreamlike feel. Listen for how the bass follows the melody an octave or two lower, a technique that makes the track feel huge on a big sound system and almost empty on small speakers.","Hailed as a dubstep classic and widely credited with taking the genre beyond its south London base, it showed that the style could carry a melody as well as bass pressure. Some purists felt its hook was too accessible, a warning of the brash, aggressive dubstep that later conquered America; most now see it as the scene's calling card, and its gentleness as the road not taken.","Play it on a system with real bass or good headphones; on a phone speaker half the track disappears. Hum the melody, then try to feel the bass shadowing it far below. Notice how few drum hits there are. Compare Horsepower Productions and Digital Mystikz to hear the scene's darker, more minimal side, and Overmono for its descendants."],"digital-mystikz-anti-war-dub":["A heavy, slow dubstep track in which a mournful vocal, half-sung and half-spoken, repeats an anti-war plea over a deep, rolling bass and sparse, echoing drums. The atmosphere is meditative and grave, closer to 1970s roots reggae than to club music. The bass moves slowly enough that each note seems to fill a room.","Digital Mystikz, Mala and Coki from south London, started the DMZ label and a club night in Brixton in 2004–05, which became the spiritual home of dubstep: a dark room, a huge sound system and a crowd there to feel the bass rather than dance showily. The track appeared during the Iraq War, when anti-war feeling in Britain was strong, and its message reflects the protest traditions of roots reggae as much as the club scene.","The drums are placed on a half-time grid so the snare lands only once a bar, leaving the bass to fill the space; this is the same idea as a dub mix where the drummer stops and the bass holds the floor. Listen for the echo on the vocal, a direct inheritance from King Tubby's studio.","Celebrated as a dubstep classic and a statement of the scene's roots-reggae conscience, it circulated first as a dubplate played at DMZ before its release. Critics have praised it as proof that dubstep could carry message and spirituality, not just bass pressure, and it is often set against the louder, more aggressive dubstep that followed as a reminder of the style's meditative beginnings.","Listen on a system with real bass, loud enough to feel it in your chest; this music was built for a room, not earbuds. Follow the vocal and its echoes, then the long notes of the bass. Compare Augustus Pablo's King Tubby Meets Rockers Uptown to hear the mood it comes from, and Public Enemy's 'Fight the Power' for protest in a louder key."],"kode9-memories-of-the-future":["A collaboration between Kode9 and the poet-vocalist The Spaceape, whose deep, slow, spoken voice delivers dark, apocalyptic poems over minimal, heavy dubstep beats. The mood is paranoid and dystopian, with sparse drums, deep bass, sirens and sci-fi textures; a few instrumentals give the voice a rest. Tempos stay slow and the tracks rarely build. It sounds like a radio broadcast from a ruined city, heard late at night.","Kode9, Steve Goodman, was both a producer and an academic who studied sound and culture, and he founded Hyperdub in 2004. The Spaceape, Stephen Gordon, was a poet from south London. The album appeared in 2006 as dubstep emerged, and it draws on the traditions of dub poetry as well as science fiction.","The Spaceape's delivery owes much to Jamaican dub poetry, which combined spoken verse with reggae rhythms; listen for how he stretches words over the beat. Kode9's sub-bass often sits just below where small speakers can reproduce it, so on a big system the poems seem to float on pressure rather than notes.","Admired for its darkness and intellect, it is considered one of dubstep's key early albums, though it has been far less widely heard than Burial's debut from the same label and year. Some found it too bleak and too slow for the dancefloor; others praised it as the genre's most conceptual record. The Spaceape's death in 2014 gave its themes of illness and endurance a new weight.","Listen with headphones and focus on the voice: its slowness, its accent, the way words are stretched across bars. Then shift attention to what happens in the silences between lines. Compare King Tubby's dubs for the space, Public Enemy's Fear of a Black Planet for a louder dystopia, and Burial's album, also on Hyperdub, for a different kind of darkness."],"actress-splazsh":["An album of warped, murky electronic music that draws on house, techno, dubstep and hip-hop but sounds like none of them. Beats are wonky or buried in hiss; synthesizer lines are fragmented and smeared; some tracks are almost ambient. The mood is strange and dreamlike, as if dance music were being remembered badly.","Actress, Darren Cunningham, from Wolverhampton, had been a promising young footballer before turning to music, and ran the Werkdiscs label. The album, released by Honest Jon's in 2010, appeared as the London dubstep scene was diversifying into new, less defined forms, often called post-dubstep or 'outsider house', and as Detroit techno and Chicago house were being rediscovered by British producers who wanted something stranger than the club mainstream.","Many sounds are deliberately degraded, run through low-quality effects and filtered until they lose their edges, which gives the record its blurry character. Listen for how the rhythms drift slightly out of time, and for drum-machine patterns that recall Detroit techno but seem to have been left out in the rain. The imperfection is intentional: familiar club forms are being made unfamiliar.","Critics praised it for its originality and it appeared on many year-end lists, though some listeners found it too obscure and unfinished-sounding. Its reputation has grown with Actress's later albums, and it is now seen as a key text of the post-dubstep era, the point where British bass music turned inward and began to sound like an art music of memory and texture.","Listen once as you would to ambient music, in the background, then again as dance music, at volume, paying attention to the beats. The two listens give different albums. Do not expect hooks; expect moods. Compare Burial's albums, Brian Eno's On Land and David Bowie's Low for other ways of making electronic music feel like a place."],"mala-mala-in-cuba":["Mala fuses dubstep's deep bass and half-time beats with Cuban piano, percussion and voices, recorded with musicians in Havana. The result is a warm, spacious album where congas and batá drums move against heavy sub-bass, and repeating piano figures in the Cuban style weave through dub echo. Some tracks are almost pure dubstep, others close to Cuban dance music, and a few are slow and devotional. The album unfolds over more than an hour.","Mala, of Digital Mystikz and the DMZ label, was invited by the DJ and broadcaster Gilles Peterson to Cuba as part of a project connecting London producers with Havana musicians. He recorded sessions with local players, then spent a long time in London editing and building tracks around them. The album appeared in 2012 on Peterson's Brownswood label, as dubstep was fragmenting and its loudest American form was dominating the charts.","Listen for how the Cuban rhythms, built on the interlocking patterns of the clave, sit against the steady half-time of dubstep: the two systems never quite merge, and that tension is part of the appeal. Many percussion and piano parts were edited and rearranged after recording, so a phrase that sounds like a live take may be a collage. Jamaica and Cuba, neighbours across a narrow sea, meet here via London.","Praised for its originality and its respect for the Cuban players, who are heard as partners rather than exotic samples, it was taken as a sign of dubstep's maturity. Some purists missed the harder edge of Mala's DMZ work, and some world-music critics questioned how much of Cuba survived the editing. It remains the most cited example of dubstep reaching beyond London.","Listen for the conversation between percussion and bass: the drums chatter quickly while the bass moves slowly underneath. Pick one piano phrase and follow it through its echoes. Play it loud on speakers with real low end. Compare Augustus Pablo's King Tubby Meets Rockers Uptown for the dub roots, and Miles Davis's Sketches of Spain for another cross-cultural album."],"floating-points-promises":["A single continuous work in nine movements. A short, gentle keyboard figure repeats throughout, while Pharoah Sanders's tenor saxophone improvises around it, sometimes tender, sometimes raw. Strings of the London Symphony Orchestra gradually swell, and synthesizers add depth. The music builds slowly to a climax, then falls away into silence.","Floating Points, Sam Shepherd, a producer with a doctorate in neuroscience and a career in club music, collaborated with the veteran spiritual-jazz saxophonist Pharoah Sanders, John Coltrane's former partner, then in his late seventies. The two had met through Sanders's admiration for Shepherd's earlier album. The sessions stretched over several years, with the London Symphony Orchestra's strings added later. The album appeared in 2021 during the pandemic, and was Sanders's last major recording before his death in 2022.","The repeating seven-note figure is played on different instruments at different moments, including harpsichord, piano and celesta, so it seems to change colour while staying the same. Listen for how Sanders often plays around the figure rather than with it, creating a gentle tension. In one passage he sets the saxophone down and simply vocalises; it is easy to miss, and one of the most human moments on the record.","Widely acclaimed and named on many lists of the best albums of the year, it was praised for its patience and beauty and brought Sanders to a young audience who had never heard Karma. Some jazz critics wished he had been given more room to burn as he did in the 1960s; others saw the restraint as the point, an elder's late style. Whether it is jazz, ambient or classical is still argued.","Listen from start to finish in one sitting, with no distractions and the volume fairly high, since much of it is quiet. Hold on to the repeating keyboard figure as a landmark, and notice when the strings begin to swell beneath it. Compare Pharoah Sanders's Karma for his fiery youth, and Arvo Pärt's Spiegel im Spiegel for another music built from stillness."],"overmono-good-lies":["An album of emotional, euphoric UK bass music. Chopped and pitched vocal samples, rushing breakbeats, deep bass and bright synthesizers combine into tracks that feel both nostalgic and new. The music draws on rave, garage and dubstep, with an emphasis on melody and feeling rather than hardness. Most tracks are compact, four or five minutes, built around a single vocal hook that is repeated, pitched and cut until it becomes the melody.","Overmono are the Welsh brothers Tom and Ed Russell, who had separately produced hard techno and breakbeat music as Truss and Tessela before joining forces. After several years of club EPs and a reputation for their live shows, their debut album appeared in 2023 on XL. It arrived at a time when British club music, after the pandemic's closed venues, was eagerly revisiting its own rave and garage past.","Listen for the vocal chops, which owe much to Burial and to garage producers like MJ Cole: fragments of words are pitched and re-sequenced until they become hooks with no clear meaning. The breakbeats often recall the classic breaks of jungle, but slowed to house tempo, so a sound once built for frenzy now carries longing. The brothers' techno past shows in the tight, punchy kick drums.","Widely praised as one of the best electronic albums of its year, it also found listeners well beyond the clubs. Some critics found it too nostalgic, a greatest-hits tour of British dance music's past rather than a step forward; others praised it as a joyous celebration of that heritage, made with rare craft. The debate mirrors wider arguments about revivalism in 2020s club music.","Listen loud, ideally on a good system, and let the vocal hooks lodge themselves before you try to hear the words. Notice how the drums push forward while the voices sound wistful. Compare Burial's Untrue for the emotional source, Skream's 'Midnight Request Line' for the dubstep roots, and New Order's 'Blue Monday' for older British dance music with a sad heart."],"raga-megh-malhar":["The rain raga. Five notes going up, more coming down, with two forms of the seventh degree rubbing against each other — a flattened one on the way down that gives the raga its heaviness. Performances tend to be broad and slow-swelling rather than ornate, with long held notes that the singer lets thicken and then break, and a characteristic swing between the second and fourth degrees that imitates, by common agreement, the roll of approaching thunder.","Ragas in the North Indian system are tied to hours and seasons, and Malhar belongs to the monsoon, the weeks in July and August when the whole subcontinent waits for water. The family is old: Megh appears in medieval treatises, and the Malhar group grew through the Mughal centuries into a dozen related ragas. The legend that Tansen's Megh Malhar brought rain to Akbar's drought-struck court is the best-known story in Indian music.","Listen for the two different sevenths. Most ragas settle on one form of a note; here the flattened seventh appears in descending phrases while the natural one is reserved for the climb, so the same scale degree keeps changing its colour. That instability is the raga's weather. Notice also how rarely the performer ornaments the tonic itself — it is left plain, a horizon line under the moving clouds.","Whether Tansen or anyone else can cause rain is not a question musicians spend much time on, but the seasonal system itself is argued over: concert life, radio schedules and air conditioning have loosened the old rules, and many performers now play a monsoon raga in February without apology. Scholars also disagree about how much of the modern Malhar family descends from a single older raga and how much was assembled later.","Hear it in the rainy season if you can — the association is half the effect. Start with a vocal performance rather than an instrumental one, since the words of the traditional compositions are almost all about clouds, peacocks and absent lovers. Then compare a Miyan ki Malhar, the grander cousin, and listen for how much darker the same weather can be made to sound."],"raga-malkauns":["Five notes only: the home note, a flattened third, the fourth, a flattened sixth and a flattened seventh. No second degree and — the crucial absence — no fifth. Performances are slow, low and dark, usually beginning far below the tonic and taking twenty minutes to climb an octave. The gesture you will recognise is the long glide down from the flattened sixth onto the fourth, a heavy, sagging fall that the raga keeps returning to.","Malkauns is a midnight raga, and the tradition surrounds it with warnings: it belongs to ascetics, to Shiva in his destroying aspect, to cremation grounds, and older musicians would say it should not be sung carelessly or in daylight. Something similar exists in the Carnatic south as Hindolam. The name is usually read as a compound of Mala and Kaushik, though the etymology, like most raga etymologies, is contested.","The missing fifth is the whole point. In almost every other system the fifth above the home note is the anchor that tells the ear where it stands; without it, and with the tanpura drone sounding the tonic and fourth instead, the music floats. That is why Malkauns sounds unmoored even to listeners who cannot name a single note in it.","Because so few notes are permitted, Malkauns is treated as a test: there is nowhere to hide, and a performance stands or falls on the shaping of individual notes rather than on display. Some critics complain that its solemnity has become a mannerism and that every performer now sounds like a priest. Its scale has been borrowed freely by film composers, which purists have resented for eighty years.","Give it time — the first fifteen minutes will seem to contain almost no events, and then you will notice you have learned the raga's gravity. Sarod suits it particularly well, since the fretless fingerboard makes the descending glide continuous. Afterwards, play any Western piece built on five notes and hear how differently a pentatonic scale can be inhabited."],"raga-darbari-kanada":["The grandest of the late-night ragas, and the slowest. It lives in the lower octave, where a singer's voice loses brightness and an instrument's strings sound thickest. Two notes — the flattened third and the flattened sixth — are never played straight: they are given a wide, slow oscillation, a shake so unhurried you can hear each swing separately. A full performance can spend forty minutes below the home note before rising above it.","Darbar means court, and tradition assigns this raga to Tansen, the singer at the Mughal emperor Akbar's court in the later sixteenth century, who is said to have shaped it for performance late at night before a tired ruler. The Kanada family it belongs to is older and large. Whatever Tansen actually sang, the raga as performed today was fixed in the nineteenth century by the gharanas of Gwalior, Jaipur and Kirana.","The oscillation on the flattened third is the raga's signature and its trap. It must be slow and must land neither on the note above nor on the note below; speed it up and you are in a different raga altogether, as teachers demonstrate by doing exactly that. Listen for how the singer enters the shake from underneath, so the note seems to arrive out of tune and then settle.","The Tansen attribution is legend rather than documentation, and scholars treat the four-century pedigree with caution. Musicians argue instead about tempo: the very slow tempos favoured since the 1950s, especially by Amir Khan, are heard by some as the raga's fullest form and by others as an indulgence recordings made possible. Its use in Hindi film scores has made its opening phrase instantly recognisable across South Asia.","Start with a vocal performance rather than an instrumental one, because the oscillation is easiest to hear in a human voice. Count how long it takes the singer to reach the octave above the home note. Then hear the same raga on sarod and notice how the fretless instrument turns the shake into a continuous bend rather than a series of separate pitches."],"raga-miyan-ki-todi":["A morning raga, and one of the strangest-sounding to an ear raised on European scales: flattened second, flattened third, flattened sixth and a sharpened fourth, all at once. The result is a scale in which almost nothing sits where a listener expects. Performances are plaintive rather than heavy, full of sliding approaches from below, and they lean hard on the sharpened fourth, which is bent and held until it aches against the drone.","The prefix Miyan marks it as belonging to Tansen, who was given that honorific at Akbar's court, and it distinguishes this Todi from a crowd of related ragas — Gujari Todi, Bilaskhani Todi, Multani — that share most of the same notes and differ in their permitted phrases. Todi gave its name to one of the ten thaats, the scale-families into which Vishnu Narayan Bhatkhande sorted North Indian music in the early twentieth century.","Listen for what the performer does with the flattened second. It is approached from above, pulled down onto, and almost never struck cleanly; teachers say the note should sound as though it is being wept rather than sung. Notice too that the raga's morning identity survives in the compositions, whose texts are full of waking, sunlight and the business of the household starting up.","The Todi group is the standard argument about how finely a raga can be split: several of its members use identical notes and are distinguished only by which phrases are allowed and which note is emphasised, and musicians disagree about whether those distinctions are real or inherited etiquette. Bhatkhande's thaat system, which made Todi a category as well as a raga, is itself still debated as a tidy scheme imposed on an untidy practice.","Play it in the morning, then in the evening, and see whether the tradition is on to something. A khayal performance in slow tempo shows the raga best. Afterwards listen to Multani, an afternoon raga built from the same notes, and try to hear what makes them different — that comparison teaches more about ragas than any definition."],"raga-yaman":["Yaman uses the seven notes of the major scale with the fourth raised a semitone. A full recital moves through three stages: an unmeasured alap in which the soloist unfolds the raga note by note over a tanpura drone, a pulsed jor, and then a fixed composition set in a rhythmic cycle and driven from slow to fast. The sound to recognise is the long descent that leans on the raised fourth and rests a semitone below the home note before arriving.","Yaman belongs to the Kalyan family and is performed in the first hours after sunset. Its double name carries its history: Kalyan is the older Sanskrit term, Yaman the Persianised one that spread through the courts of northern India, where musicians, Sufi poets and instrument makers from further west worked alongside temple-trained singers. By the seventeenth century it appears in treatises and in the ragamala paintings that gave each raga a face. It is still the first raga most students are given.","Listen to the note a semitone under the home note. In European music that is a leading tone, obliged to resolve upward at once; in Yaman it is a resting place, and phrases will sit on it, circle it, and only then fall home. Notice too how rarely the soloist approaches the tonic head-on. The raga is defined as much by the paths it forbids as by the notes it permits.","Musicians argue about where Yaman ends and Yaman Kalyan begins — the latter admits the natural fourth, and opinions differ on whether that makes a separate raga or a licence inside the same one. Gharanas disagree about how much time the alap deserves and whether the character lives in the composition or the improvisation. Recordings have quietly shortened performances that once ran two hours into something that fits an album.","Start with an unaccompanied alap, where nothing distracts from the notes themselves; a sitar or a khayal voice will do. Give it ten minutes before expecting a rhythm. Then hear its southern cousin Kalyani and listen to what the two traditions make of the same scale: one unfolds it in silence, the other sets it to words and a drum cycle."],"raga-bhairavi":["All four alterable notes are flattened — second, third, sixth and seventh — which gives Bhairavi a soft, minor-sounding base. But the raga is famous for what it allows on top of that: in practice performers borrow any of the remaining notes as passing colours, so a Bhairavi can touch all twelve pitches without leaving itself. It is usually short, warm and song-like, with thumri and bhajan texts rather than grand abstract compositions.","By convention Bhairavi ends a concert, whatever the hour, and the audience knows the evening is over the moment it begins. Its original place was early morning, and temple singers used it at dawn; the closing role grew out of that association with completion and blessing. Its openness made it the natural raga for lighter forms — the thumri, the ghazal, the devotional song — and for film composers, who have used it more than any other.","The name travels badly. In the Carnatic south, Bhairavi is a different raga entirely, while the northern Bhairavi's scale corresponds to the southern Hanumatodi — so two musicians from either side of the country can agree on a raga's name and play nothing alike. Listen also for the moment a performer slips in a natural note that the strict scale forbids: that licence is the raga's identity, not a mistake.","Because so much is permitted, teachers warn that Bhairavi is the easiest raga to play badly and the hardest to play well; the standard complaint is that it has been loosened into a mood rather than a raga. Its use as a closing number has also been criticised for turning a morning raga into a curtain call. Nobody proposes changing the custom.","Listen to the last ten minutes of any long Hindustani concert recording and you will probably be hearing it. A thumri in Bhairavi, with its half-sung, half-spoken text, is the fastest way in. Then find a Carnatic Bhairavi and hear the confusion for yourself — the same word, an entirely different piece of music."],"purandara-dasa-jagadoddharana":["A devaranama — a devotional song in Kannada — of four short verses over a refrain. Yashoda rocks the infant Krishna on her lap and the poet points out the absurdity: she is dandling the one who upholds the world. The melody is simple enough for a child, moves in a narrow range, and is usually sung slowly with a violin shadowing the voice a beat behind and a mridangam keeping a light eight-beat cycle.","Purandara Dasa was a wealthy merchant in the Vijayanagara empire who, by tradition, gave away everything and became a wandering singer. He wrote in Kannada rather than Sanskrit, for ordinary listeners rather than temple specialists, and he devised the graded exercises with which Carnatic students still begin — the same finger patterns and syllables, five centuries on. Later musicians named him the grandfather of the tradition.","The tune most singers use is not his. What survived was text and a raga name; the melodies heard today are largely nineteenth- and twentieth-century settings, and the familiar version in raga Kapi owes much to performers of the recording era. Listen for the refrain returning after each verse with a different weight of ornament — that variation is the performer's contribution, not the composer's.","The corpus attributed to Purandara Dasa runs into the hundreds of thousands of songs, a figure no scholar accepts literally; sorting his work from four centuries of accretion is unfinished. What is not in doubt is the pedagogy, which is documented, continuous and still in daily use. M. S. Subbulakshmi's recordings made this particular song known far beyond Karnataka.","Hear it sung by a great voice with almost no accompaniment first, then in a full concert setting; the song survives both. Follow the refrain and count how many times it returns. If you want to understand what the Carnatic tradition owes him, listen to a beginner's first lesson — the exercises are his, and they have not changed."],"raga-kalyani":["The southern cousin of Yaman: the major scale with a raised fourth, and in the Carnatic reckoning the sixty-fifth of the seventy-two parent scales. What differs is the treatment. Where the north unfolds the raga in free time, a Carnatic performance takes a shorter improvised alapana and then settles into composed songs, with the raised fourth given a shaking, sliding ornament that never lets it sit still. A long Kalyani may end in a rapid exchange between singer and drummer.","Venkatamakhin's seventeenth-century treatise sorted South Indian ragas into seventy-two parent scales by counting every possible combination of altered notes — a piece of systematic thinking that has no exact parallel in the north, and that gave later composers a map of scales nobody had yet used. Kalyani sits in the middle of that scheme and became one of the most performed ragas of all, carrying major works by every composer of the Trinity.","Listen to the raised fourth. In the north it is bent and leaned on; here it is shaken, with an oscillation applied to the note as a property of the raga rather than a choice of the performer. Carnatic music notates such ornaments as part of the raga's identity, which is why a plain, unshaken performance sounds not austere but wrong.","The seventy-two-scale system has been criticised since it was published for generating scales that no one sings and for implying that ragas are scales, which they are not. Its defenders point out that it gave composers a usable grammar and that dozens of once-theoretical scales have since become living ragas. The overlap with Hindustani Yaman is a standard exhibit in arguments about how far the two systems were ever one.","Listen to a Carnatic Kalyani and a Hindustani Yaman back to back — the same notes, two completely different manners. Notice how much faster the southern performance gets to a fixed composition, and how the drum joins in. A violin performance shows the ornaments most clearly, since the player can slide where a singer must breathe."],"dikshitar-vatapi-ganapatim":["The piece that opens more Carnatic concerts than any other. A Sanskrit invocation to Ganesha, set in a bright five-note raga, Hamsadhwani, that leaves out the fourth and sixth degrees and so climbs in clean leaps. The form is the standard kriti — an opening refrain, a second section, a longer verse — with room for the singer to improvise sequences of note-names before returning to the words. Ten minutes, brisk, and built to clear the air.","Muthuswami Dikshitar was one of the three composers who defined Carnatic music in the decades around 1800, all three living within a few miles of one another near Thanjavur. He had spent years in Varanasi, absorbed North Indian ragas, wrote almost entirely in Sanskrit, and composed pilgrimages: song after song addressed to a specific deity in a specific temple. Vatapi is Badami, in the Deccan, from which the image of Ganesha was said to have been carried south as war booty.","The raga is younger than the song. Hamsadhwani is generally credited to Ramaswami Dikshitar, the composer's father, which makes this one of the first pieces written in a scale invented within the family. Listen also for the word guruguha buried in the final verse — Dikshitar signed every composition with it, a name for the god Murugan and for himself.","Hamsadhwani travelled back north in the twentieth century and is now sung in Hindustani concerts too, one of the few clear cases of traffic running that way. Performance practice is argued over: how much improvisation an opening item should carry, whether the tempo has crept up over the past century, and how far singers may stray from the version transmitted by Dikshitar's disciples.","Notice the first phrase — it lands on a high note almost immediately, which is why the song works as a concert opener. Then listen for the passage where the singer abandons words for note-names and the violin answers each phrase. Compare two recordings a generation apart and you will hear the tempo question for yourself."],"tyagaraja-endaro-mahanubhavulu":["The last of the five Pancharatna, the Five Gems, and the longest. A Telugu text saluting the many great souls who have praised the divine, set in raga Sri and an eight-beat cycle. After the opening refrain the song runs through a chain of verses, each beginning with a passage of sung note-names before the words return — a structure that turns the piece into a tour of the raga. It is normally performed by everyone present at once, in unison, rather than by a soloist.","Tyagaraja lived in the village of Tiruvaiyaru on the Kaveri, sang to Rama every day, and by all accounts refused the patronage of the Thanjavur court when it was offered — the standard telling has him rejecting wealth in a song composed on the spot. He wrote in Telugu, the language of the region's elite, and left several hundred kritis. Each January musicians gather at his samadhi and sing the Five Gems together.","The unison performance is a modern invention, not a composer's instruction: the mass singing at the annual festival dates from the twentieth century, when rival factions of musicians were brought together and given something they could all perform side by side. Listen for the sections of pure note-names — they are written out, not improvised, which is unusual, and they are the reason the piece doubles as a lesson in raga Sri.","Nothing survives in Tyagaraja's hand. His songs passed through disciples and their disciples, and different schools transmit different versions of the same kriti, so editions argue over notes and even words. Scholars have also questioned how much of the biography — the rejected patronage, the brother throwing the Rama idols in the river — is hagiography. The music's authority has never depended on settling either question.","Find a recording of the January festival, with hundreds of musicians singing together and the violins doubling them, then hear a single voice perform the same piece: the difference tells you what the tradition does with its founder. Listen for the moment each verse switches from words to note-names, and follow how the melody climbs a little higher each time."],"tyagaraja-nagumomu-galaleni":["A reproach disguised as a love song: the poet asks Rama why he withholds his smiling face, and whether the god has forgotten who carried the earth on his back. The raga, Abheri, uses a flattened third and seventh with a plain second and sixth, which gives it a wistfulness close to the blues. The melody falls more than it rises, and the refrain returns each time from a little further above, so the song seems to keep sinking back.","This is Tyagaraja in his most personal mode — not doctrine but complaint, addressed to a deity he treated as a household presence. The kriti form he worked in had been a devotional song; he made it a vehicle capable of argument, doubt and irritation, with the improvised passages placed where the emotion needed room. Its popularity outside the temple circuit came through the concert stage and then the gramophone.","Which raga this actually is has been argued for a century. Sung with one form of the sixth degree it is Abheri; with another it is closer to the raga now called Karnataka Devagandhari, and performers have quietly changed sides over the decades. Listen to two recordings from different generations and you may hear two different ragas under the same title — a rare case of the tradition disagreeing in public.","The song's fame has made it a test piece and, some critics say, a crowd-pleaser performed too often and too sweetly. Its scale is close enough to a minor pentatonic with added notes that it has been picked up by film composers and by jazz musicians, which has both spread it and blurred it. Scholars continue to work on establishing texts from the disciple lineages.","Listen for the falling refrain and how each return is approached from higher up. Then hear the passage where the singer improvises on a single line of text, repeating it with a new shape each time — Carnatic music calls this neraval, and this song is the standard place to learn what it sounds like."],"bismillah-khan-red-fort-recital":["The shehnai is a conical double-reed pipe with a flared metal bell, loud enough to be heard across a temple courtyard and traditionally played in groups, with one player holding a single unchanging note as a drone beneath the soloist. Bismillah Khan's playing bends between pitches rather than stepping, using the reed and the half-covered finger holes, and his closing Bhairavi — short, sweet, endlessly ornamented — became the sound the instrument is remembered by.","He played at the Red Fort in Delhi as India became independent in August 1947, and for decades afterwards his shehnai opened the Independence Day broadcast on All India Radio, so that the instrument became a national sound before most listeners had ever seen one. Until then the shehnai had belonged to weddings and temple processions; Bismillah Khan, who played daily at the Balaji temple in Varanasi, carried it onto the concert platform.","Listen for the breath that never stops. Reed players in this tradition inhale through the nose while keeping air pressure in the cheeks, so a phrase can run for a minute without a seam. Listen also for the second shehnai droning underneath: it takes the place of the tanpura, and because it is a wind instrument, the drone itself breathes and wavers rather than sitting still.","A Shia Muslim whose life's work was performed in and for a Hindu temple, he became the standard example in arguments about India's shared culture, and was invoked by every side. He received the Bharat Ratna in 2001 and died in Varanasi in 2006, having refused to move abroad. Critics who wanted him to record more concert-length ragas got fewer than they hoped; his fame rested on short pieces.","Start with a Bhairavi rather than a long raga — his gift was for the closing miniature. Listen for the drone shehnai underneath and notice it moving. Then hear the same raga on a stringed instrument and you will understand why a wind instrument's inability to sustain forever changes the shape of every phrase."],"kesarbai-kerkar-jaat-kahan-ho":["Three and a half minutes of khayal in Bhairavi, cut to fit one side of a shellac disc. The text asks a young woman where she is going alone. Kesarbai Kerkar sings in a voice of unusual weight and squareness, attacking notes straight rather than sliding into them, and the compression is audible: phrases that would take five minutes to unfold in concert are stated once, complete, and left.","She was trained in the Jaipur-Atrauli style by Alladiya Khan, whose method built long, complex melodic sentences rather than displays of speed, and she guarded her art fiercely — she recorded rarely, controlled what was issued, and reportedly forbade broadcasts she had not approved. The 78rpm disc, with its four-minute limit, forced an entire tradition of hour-long performances into a form it was never designed for.","This recording is in interstellar space. The committee assembling the Voyager Golden Record in 1977 chose it to represent India, and copies are aboard both probes, now beyond the edge of the solar system. Listen to how she ends phrases — cleanly, on the beat, with none of the trailing ornament most singers use — and you can hear a performer who had learned to make a shellac side sound finished.","Her reputation among musicians was always higher than her public profile, precisely because she recorded so little; the handful of surviving sides are treated as documents. The Voyager selection has been discussed ever since as a piece of cultural politics — why this singer, this raga, this fragment — and the fact that the Golden Record's most-played Indian track is four minutes of a two-hour art form is part of the argument.","Play it twice: once as a piece of music, once as a message to no one in particular. Listen for the squareness of her attack, which sounds almost blunt beside later singers. Then find a full-length Jaipur-Atrauli khayal and hear what the disc had to leave out."],"ali-akbar-khan-morning-and-evening-ragas":["One raga to a side, played on sarod — a fretless instrument with a polished metal fingerboard, a skin belly and a bank of sympathetic strings that ring untouched. The absence of frets means every note can be slid into, and Ali Akbar Khan's sound is built on that: a plucked note that keeps moving after it is struck. Chatur Lal's tabla enters only after the long unmeasured opening, and the drone runs underneath throughout.","Yehudi Menuhin heard him play in India, arranged an American visit, and in 1955 Ali Akbar Khan performed on New York television and recorded this disc, with a spoken introduction by Menuhin to explain what listeners were about to hear. It was the first long-playing record devoted to Indian classical music issued in the West. The LP itself was the enabling technology: nothing shorter could have held a raga in recognisable form.","The sarod descends from the Afghan rabab, and the metal plate that replaced the fretted wooden neck was a nineteenth-century modification made for exactly this sliding style. Listen for the sympathetic strings: they are never plucked, but after a loud phrase you can hear the instrument continuing to sound by itself. His father Allauddin Khan of Maihar, who taught him, also taught Ravi Shankar.","The record established the terms on which Indian classical music would be received in the West for a generation — as spiritual, ancient and wordless — which musicians have been arguing against ever since. It also created the audience that made Shankar's career possible and that Coltrane, among others, listened into. Ali Akbar Khan later founded a college in California and spent forty years teaching Americans the system rather than the mystique.","Listen first for the difference between sarod and sitar: the sitar rings and buzzes, the sarod is drier and slides further. Track the moment the tabla enters and the music acquires a pulse. Then go to a live concert recording of the same raga, three times the length, and hear how much the LP compressed."],"shankar-three-ragas":["Three ragas, each compressed into the span an LP side allows, and each following the full arc: an unmeasured alap in which the sitar states the raga alone, a pulsed jor, a composition in a rhythmic cycle with the tabla, and a closing jhala where the drone strings are strummed between melody notes at increasing speed. The sitar sound — metallic, buzzing at the bridge, ringing on afterwards — is the thing most listeners had never heard before.","Ravi Shankar recorded it in London on his first Western tour, having spent his teens touring Europe with his brother Uday's dance company and then seven years in the village of Maihar learning from Allauddin Khan. He was thirty-six, already the director of All India Radio's orchestra, and he arrived with a plan: explain the form from the stage, then play. The record reached musicians in New York and Los Angeles within a few years.","Listen for what the sitar does after the note stops. Thirteen sympathetic strings run under the frets, tuned to the raga, and they keep sounding, so the instrument accompanies itself with a haze of the same notes. The curved bridge that produces the buzz is shaped by hand and adjusted with a strip of thread; players call the resulting shimmer jawari, and the whole sound of the record depends on it.","Shankar spent the rest of his life managing what this music became in the West — the applause for his tuning at Monterey, the audiences who came for the associations rather than the ragas, his own discomfort with the drug culture that adopted him. Critics in India sometimes accused him of simplifying for foreigners. The counter-argument is the record itself, which concedes nothing structurally and merely shortens.","Do what he asked audiences to do: learn the four stages before you listen, then follow them. The entry of the tabla is the easiest landmark. Notice how the jhala at the end works — the melody is the same speed, but the drone strums between the notes multiply. Then hear a two-hour concert version and the shape will be familiar."],"vilayat-khan-jalsaghar":["A score for Satyajit Ray's film about a landowner who spends the last of his money on concerts in his crumbling mansion. Vilayat Khan wrote and played the sitar music that runs between scenes, and the film's long performance sequences are shot almost unbroken: a thumri singer, a kathak dancer with ankle bells answering the drummer, a room of guests going quiet. The sitar outside the room is thin, wandering and unaccompanied; the music inside is full and social.","Ray had used Ravi Shankar for his first films; here he wanted a musician from inside the courtly world the film was mourning, and Vilayat Khan came from seven generations of players. The subject was the collapse of the zamindar class in Bengal after the 1793 land settlement and its final ruin at independence — patrons whose vanity had paid for this music for two centuries, filmed at the moment the money ran out. Begum Akhtar appears as a singer.","Vilayat Khan's style is called gayaki ang, the singing manner: he pulled the string sideways across the fret to bend up to five notes from one stopped position, so the sitar could imitate a vocalist's slides instead of striking separate pitches. He also altered the instrument's stringing to suit it. Listen to the solo passages between scenes and you are hearing a sitar deliberately trying not to sound like a sitar.","The film was slow to travel and is now among Ray's most admired; the music is one reason. Vilayat Khan's relationship with the Indian establishment was thorny — he declined state honours more than once — and the long-running comparison with Ravi Shankar, pressed by partisans of both, irritated him for fifty years. Critics still debate whether his cinema work diluted his concert art or simply preserved it.","Watch the film if you can; the music is inseparable from the rooms it echoes in. If you only listen, start with the unaccompanied sitar passages and notice how far a single note is bent. Then hear one of his concert ragas and listen for the same vocal shapes stretched over forty minutes instead of forty seconds."],"amir-khan-raga-marwa":["A sunset raga sung at a tempo so slow the beat almost disappears: a single cycle can take a minute, and the words of the composition arrive in fragments minutes apart. Marwa has a flattened second and a raised fourth, and it starves the listener of the home note, hovering instead around the sixth. Amir Khan's voice is unforced, almost conversational, and he builds by permutation — the same few notes reordered, again and again, each time revealing another path.","He came from Indore, learned from his father, a sarangi player, and took from the Kirana, Bhendibazaar and Gwalior traditions rather than belonging cleanly to any. His slow tempos were unusual enough to be considered a school of their own by the 1950s, and younger singers everywhere adopted them. He also sang on the soundtrack of Baiju Bawra in 1952, which put a strict classical voice in front of a mass film audience.","Notice how seldom the home note is sounded. Marwa leans on the note a major sixth above it, so the drone keeps insisting on a tonic the melody keeps avoiding, and the ear is left permanently off balance — the tension is the raga. Listen also for the way he arrives at a phrase: not by ornament but by reordering the notes he has already used, a permutational method borrowed from the old merukhand discipline.","The slowness divided opinion at the time and still does: admirers hear concentration, detractors hear a performance that refuses to begin. His influence, though, is not contested — most Hindustani vocalists after 1960 sing slower openings because of him. He died in a road accident in Calcutta in 1974, and the recorded legacy is smaller than his stature, which has kept the surviving concert tapes in constant circulation.","Do not start at the beginning if you are impatient; drop in twenty minutes and then go back. Listen for the drone and try to sing the home note yourself — you will feel how rarely the singer confirms it. Then hear a fast composition in the same raga and notice how the tension survives the tempo."],"subbulakshmi-venkatesa-suprabhatam":["A morning hymn to wake the deity at Tirumala, sung in Sanskrit over some thirty verses. It is closer to chant than to song: most of it moves within a few notes, in a measured recitation that opens out into melody only at the sections that close the sequence. M. S. Subbulakshmi's voice is bright and perfectly even, the accompaniment is thin, and the whole thing proceeds at walking pace, as though the listener is genuinely being woken.","Recorded for HMV in 1963, it became the most widely heard piece of devotional music in South India: temples, radio stations and households play it at dawn, and for millions it is simply the sound of five in the morning. Subbulakshmi had come to the concert stage from Madurai by way of the cinema, starring as the poet-saint Meera in 1945, and she carried a film star's reach into a repertoire of temple texts.","The piece works because it is not fully sung. Recitation on two or three notes gives the words room and keeps the pitch low enough for anyone to join, which is why it is one of the few classical recordings people sing along with. Listen for the shift near the end, where the flat recitation finally blooms into a shaped melody — the moment the deity is assumed to be awake.","Her career has been reassessed more than once. Admirers point to the discipline and the extraordinary evenness of tone; critics have examined how far her repertoire and public image were shaped by her husband and by a Brahminical cultural establishment into which she, from a devadasi family, was carefully assimilated. She was the first musician to receive the Bharat Ratna, in 1998, and the arguments have not dented the recording's reach.","Play it at dawn, which is what it is for. Listen for how few notes the first ten minutes use, then for the moment the melody widens. Compare it with a Latin office hymn sung at the same hour and you will find two traditions solving the same problem — how to sing before anyone is properly awake — in almost the same way."],"ali-akbar-khan-chandranandan":["A raga Ali Akbar Khan made himself, by folding together four of the Kaunsi ragas — Malkauns among them — so that the ear keeps recognising familiar phrases and finding them in the wrong company. The result is dark and pentatonic-feeling, like Malkauns with extra doors. On sarod it is played low, with the long sliding descents the fretless board allows, and the composition that follows the opening is unusually song-like for something invented in a studio.","By the mid-1960s he was recording regularly for Western labels and dividing his year between India and California, where he founded a school of music in 1967. The commercial demand for new material collided with a tradition in which the repertoire is inherited, not composed; the standard account has him producing this raga when asked, more or less on the spot, for something that had not been recorded before.","New ragas are proposed constantly and almost never take. This one did: other musicians learned it, taught it, and it now appears on concert programmes without explanation, which within a living oral tradition is the strongest possible verdict. The name means something like delight of the moon. Listen for the moments where a Malkauns phrase begins and then turns aside into a note Malkauns does not allow.","Purists object in principle to invented ragas, arguing that a raga is not a scale a person can design but a body of practice that accumulates; others point out that every raga was new once and that the Kaunsi family in particular has always bred hybrids. The debate has largely been settled by use. Ali Akbar Khan's own view, expressed in teaching, was that the raga found him rather than the reverse.","Listen to Malkauns first, then this, and the pleasure is in catching the differences. Notice how the sarod's slides let one raga slip into another without a seam. Then hear a recording by a musician of the next generation and consider that you are listening to a tradition absorbing something made within living memory."],"bhimsen-joshi-raga-multani":["An afternoon raga built from the same notes as Miyan ki Todi and handled entirely differently — the flattened second is skipped on the way up, the sharpened fourth carries the weight, and the mood is drier and less plaintive. Bhimsen Joshi sings it with enormous breath: single phrases arch across a whole rhythmic cycle without a pause, and the fast passages that close the performance are delivered in unbroken runs at a volume most singers cannot sustain.","He left home in Karnataka at eleven to find a teacher, travelling across northern India for years before ending up with Sawai Gandharva of the Kirana tradition, whose method concentrated on the precise placing of each note rather than on ornament or speed. He later founded the festival in Pune named after that teacher, which became the largest annual gathering in Hindustani music, and he sang there almost every year for half a century.","Kirana singing approaches a note from below and settles on it visibly, so you can hear the pitch being found and fixed; other schools prefer to arrive already there. Listen to the first minute and count how long each phrase lasts before he breathes. The famous power is a by-product of that breath control, not the aim: the school's concern is intonation, and the volume is what a well-placed note happens to sound like.","His devotional recordings in Marathi and Kannada sold in enormous numbers and brought him an audience far outside the classical circuit, which some critics treated as a lowering of standards and others as the natural continuation of a tradition that was always devotional. He received the Bharat Ratna in 2008. Recordings of his very late concerts, when the breath had gone, are argued over by admirers who would rather not hear them.","Listen for the breath, then for the moment the tempo doubles and the runs begin. Compare his Multani with a Miyan ki Todi by anyone — the notes are the same and the two performances share almost nothing. If you want the popular side, his Marathi devotional songs are the other half of the career."],"subbulakshmi-un-concert":["A Carnatic recital given in the General Assembly hall on United Nations Day, 1966: kritis by the great Tamil and Telugu composers, a violin answering each vocal phrase, a mridangam holding the cycle, and a tambura drone behind everything. The programme closes with Maitreem Bhajata, a Sanskrit benediction written for the occasion, asking the nations to cultivate friendship — a text composed for a hall full of delegates and sung in a language almost none of them knew.","India was two decades independent, at war with Pakistan the previous year, and looking for a face to present abroad; Subbulakshmi, already the best-known singer in the country, was sent as a cultural ambassador in the most literal sense. The concert was arranged around the UN's anniversary, broadcast, and issued on record. She sang in a hall designed for speeches, with no temple acoustic and an audience of diplomats.","The closing benediction was written for that room. Its text asks for peace between nations rather than for any deity's favour, which is unusual in a repertoire almost entirely devotional, and the Sanskrit is deliberately plain. Listen for the violin: in Carnatic concerts it does not accompany but shadows, repeating each improvised phrase a beat later, so you hear every invention twice, once sung and once bowed.","Whether this was a concert or a diplomatic event has been asked since the day it happened, and the answer is plainly both. Critics have noted that the programme was shortened and smoothed for an audience with no grounding in the form. Others point out that the same compression happens on every record. For Indian listeners the occasion mattered more than the performance, which is itself a kind of criticism.","Listen to the violin, not the voice, for one whole item: following the shadowing is the fastest way to understand what improvisation means here. Then hear the closing benediction on its own. Compare the concert with her Suprabhatam recording of three years before to hear the same voice doing two entirely different jobs."],"chaurasia-call-of-the-valley":["A sequence of short pieces tracing a day in the life of a Kashmiri shepherd, played by three instruments that had barely appeared in classical music before: Hariprasad Chaurasia's bamboo flute, Shivkumar Sharma's santoor — a hundred-string box struck with light wooden hammers — and Brijbhushan Kabra's guitar, laid flat and played with a steel slide. Each track takes a raga appropriate to its hour, and none outstays the format. The santoor's rapid tremolo is the sound that lingers.","The santoor was a Kashmiri folk instrument used for Sufi song; Sharma's father set him to making it a classical instrument, and elders objected that its struck strings could not sustain a note and therefore could not carry a raga. Kabra had adapted an imported guitar to do what the sarod does. The record came out in India in 1967 and became one of the best-selling Indian classical albums ever made, then a fixture in Western shops in the 1970s.","The concept — a programme of short tracks describing a day, rather than one long raga — comes from the LP, not from the tradition, and that is why the record travelled. Listen to how the santoor handles a sustained note: it cannot, so Sharma trills it, and the shimmer that results became the instrument's signature rather than its apology. The slide guitar solves the same problem by sliding.","Purists called it light music and said the raga sequence was decorative. The counter-case is that two instruments entered the classical canon because of it, and that millions of listeners met Hindustani music here first. Chaurasia and Sharma later composed Hindi film scores together as Shiv-Hari, which confirmed the critics' suspicions and did not trouble either of them.","Take the tracks as miniatures, not as a compromised raga recital, and listen for the three instruments passing phrases between them. Notice the santoor's tremolo, then the flute's breathiness where the player overblows. Afterwards hear Chaurasia play a full-length raga alone and you will hear how much the album left in reserve."],"shankar-west-meets-east":["Yehudi Menuhin and Ravi Shankar, with Alla Rakha on tabla, in pieces Shankar wrote so that a classical violinist could take part in music built on improvisation he had no training in. The violin plays written lines; the sitar plays around them; the tabla holds cycles that do not divide the way a violinist's bar does. The most affecting moments are the slow ones, where Menuhin abandons vibrato and tries instead to bend between notes.","Menuhin had been visiting India since the 1950s and had already introduced Ali Akbar Khan to American audiences. By 1967 Shankar was famous beyond music — Monterey was that June, Harrison was studying with him — and the album arrived in the middle of a Western enthusiasm for India that was equal parts curiosity and fashion. It won a Grammy the following year in a chamber-music category, which tells you how the industry filed it.","The compromise is audible in the notation. Shankar had to write out the violin part because the idiom cannot be sight-read, and what Menuhin plays is therefore fixed while everything around him is invented — the opposite of the usual relationship between a soloist and an accompanist. Listen for the moments Menuhin drops vibrato entirely; that continuous shimmer, standard in European playing, is exactly what a raga cannot use.","It has been dismissed as a diplomatic exercise and defended as the first honest attempt at the problem, and both readings survive. Musicians on the Indian side noted that the traffic ran one way: the violinist adapted, the ragas did not. What is beyond argument is the influence — the record made collaboration between Indian and Western classical musicians a normal ambition rather than a novelty.","Start with the slow movement and listen only to the violin, then only to the sitar; the two are playing under different rules. Notice where Menuhin's intonation sits against the drone. Then hear Shakti, nine years later, where the Western player improvises and the compromise runs the other way."],"kumar-gandharva-nirguni-bhajans":["Settings of Kabir and other poets of the formless divine, sung in short breaths and plain tunes that owe as much to the folk singers of central India as to any classical school. The best known, about a swan that will one day fly off alone, is barely five minutes and uses perhaps six notes. The voice is reedy and a little cracked, the phrases stop where a lesser singer would push on, and the effect is of speech that has become song without meaning to.","A prodigy who was performing at ten, Kumar Gandharva contracted tuberculosis in his late twenties, lost the use of a lung, and spent years in bed in Dewas, forbidden to sing. What he did instead was listen — to the folk singers, pedlars and mendicants passing the house — and when he returned he had a shorter breath, a smaller voice and a repertoire nobody else was singing. Nirgun poetry, which rejects image and ritual, suited all three.","The style is built from the illness. Short phrases, abrupt silences, a refusal to sustain — all of it began as physical necessity and hardened into an aesthetic that younger singers now imitate deliberately, breathing where they do not need to. Listen for the silences: they fall in the middle of lines, where the sense is still hanging, and the poems are about impermanence, so the gaps do half the work.","Traditionalists accused him of abandoning gharana discipline, of singing folk tunes and calling them classical, and of building a career on eccentricity. He replied, in effect, that a tradition that cannot absorb what is sung in the street outside is already dead. His standing rose steadily after his death in 1992, and the nirguni recordings are now the part of his work most widely heard.","Start with the song about the swan and read a translation of the words first, because here the text is the point. Listen for where he stops. Then hear a long, grand khayal by any other singer of his generation and notice how much air this music has in it by comparison."],"kishori-amonkar-sahela-re":["A short composition in raga Bhoop whose text invites a companion to come and sing — the music is about its own making. Bhoop uses five notes, the same five as the major pentatonic that Western ears know from folk tunes, with no fourth and no seventh. Kishori Amonkar sings it slowly, with long swelling notes that she lets bloom and then thin out, and a way of leaving the last note of a phrase suspended rather than resolved.","She was trained by her mother, Mogubai Kurdikar, in the Jaipur-Atrauli manner of Alladiya Khan, which builds long, intricate melodic sentences across the rhythmic cycle rather than decorating single notes. She spent years resisting and then reworking that inheritance, and her mature style — more emotional, slower, less concerned with structural display — was her own argument with the school that produced her.","The scale is the most ordinary in the world, and that is the lesson. Play a pentatonic folk tune, then this, and you will hear that a raga is not a set of notes but a set of permitted moves — which note you may linger on, which you must pass through, where you may begin a phrase. Listen for how she approaches the fifth degree from above each time, never from below.","She lost her voice for a period in mid-career and returned with the sound she is now known for, a story admirers treat as a transformation and sceptics as a convenient legend. She was famously exacting with audiences and would stop for a ringing phone or a whisper. Critics divided over whether her late style deepened the Jaipur method or diluted its architecture; the recording has outlasted the argument.","Sing the five notes first, so you know exactly what material she has. Then listen for what she refuses to do with them. The piece is short enough to hear three times in half an hour, which is the right way to take it. Afterwards, any Western pentatonic melody will sound curiously unexamined."],"lalgudi-jayaraman-thillanas":["The thillana is the fast closing piece of a Carnatic concert or a dance recital, built mostly out of drum syllables rather than words, with a short passage of text near the end. Lalgudi Jayaraman wrote a set of them that entered the standard repertoire in his lifetime. Played on his own violin they are bright, springy and full of cross-rhythm: the melody lands off the beat and the mridangam catches it, over and over, until the final cadence arrives three times.","The violin reached South India around 1800 and was absorbed completely — retuned, played seated on the floor with the scroll braced against the ankle, and given the job of shadowing the singer. Lalgudi came from a family in Tyagaraja's disciple line and was among the first violinists to be billed as a soloist rather than an accompanist. His compositions, written for dancers as well as concert players, extended the tradition from inside it.","The posture is the technique. With the instrument's scroll resting on the player's ankle, the left hand is free of any duty to support the neck, so it can slide continuously up and down a string instead of shifting between fixed positions — which is precisely what the oscillating ornaments of a raga require. The tuning is in alternating fourths and fifths rather than fifths throughout, so two strings sound the home note.","Carnatic concerts have long argued about the balance between soloist and accompanist, and his career is the case study: admirers say he raised the violin to equal standing, others that the shadowing role was the more interesting job and is now performed with less care. His thillanas are sometimes called too charming, a criticism that has never stopped anyone from programming them.","Listen to the last three minutes of any Carnatic concert recording and you will find this form. Follow the drum syllables — they are nonsense words that describe strokes, and the melody is shaped to them. Then watch a Bharatanatyam dancer perform one, and the reason for all that cross-rhythm becomes visible."],"shakti-with-john-mclaughlin":["Recorded in concert, and the first record on which North Indian and South Indian musicians and a jazz guitarist improvise together as equals. No drum kit, no chords, no amplification to speak of: L. Shankar's violin and McLaughlin's guitar trade lines at impossible speed, Zakir Hussain's tabla answers them phrase for phrase, and Vikku Vinayakram's clay pot supplies a bass drum sound made by pressing the opening against his stomach.","McLaughlin had just come from the Mahavishnu Orchestra, the loudest band in jazz, and turned to an acoustic guitar with a scalloped fingerboard and added drone strings. The Indian musicians were young professionals from two traditions that rarely shared a stage with each other, let alone with a jazz player. The mid-1970s appetite for fusion gave the group a platform; what it did with it was harder and less decorative than most of what shared the shelf.","The rhythm is worked out in words before it is played. South Indian musicians recite drum patterns as spoken syllables, and the long unison passages here were learned that way, counted in cycles of odd lengths that resolve exactly on the downbeat. Listen for the moment all four players land together after a passage that sounded like it was falling apart; nothing about it is accidental.","Jazz critics called it fusion and filed it with the electric records it was reacting against; Indian critics asked what a guitar was doing in a raga. Both objections have faded, and the group's reunion decades later as Remember Shakti was received as a homecoming. The lasting argument is about influence: whether this opened the door for genuinely two-way collaboration or simply set a standard nobody has matched.","Start with a fast track and just follow the tabla; then play it again following the violin. The pleasure is in the unisons. Compare it with West Meets East from nine years earlier — there the Western player read from a page, here he improvises, and the difference is the whole story."],"nikhil-banerjee-raga-manj-khamaj":["A light-classical raga that mixes two parents — the sweet, folk-leaning Khamaj and the graver Bageshri — and is usually placed at the end of a concert, when the strict repertoire is finished and the audience wants melody. Nikhil Banerjee plays it without display: the alap is long and quiet, the tabla enters late, and even the fast closing section is articulated rather than hurled. The tone is rounder and less percussive than most sitarists', with very little buzz.","He trained with Allauddin Khan at Maihar, in the same lineage as Ravi Shankar and Ali Akbar Khan, but stayed almost entirely in India and avoided the festival circuit and the crossover projects. He played long concerts to small halls, refused to shorten ragas for records, and died in 1986 at fifty-four. Most of what survives is concert tape made by listeners and organisers rather than studio sessions.","A mixed raga has to keep its parents apart. Phrases from Khamaj and phrases from Bageshri each have their own places in the cycle, and the skill lies in moving between them without letting either take over — which is why performers use this raga to show judgement rather than technique. Listen for the point where the melody turns grave for a few seconds and then brightens again.","His reputation has grown steadily since his death, partly because the tapes kept surfacing and partly because the qualities he was admired for — patience, tone, refusal of spectacle — became scarcer. Dating those recordings is often guesswork, and reissues carry years that should be treated as approximate. Some listeners rank him above his more famous contemporaries; the comparison would have embarrassed him.","Give the unaccompanied opening its full time; that is where he is unmistakable. Listen for the absence of the usual metallic rasp, then compare a Ravi Shankar recording of any raga and hear how differently two players from the same teacher set up the same instrument. Concert tapes rather than studio discs are the place to start."],"pran-nath-ragas-of-morning-and-night":["Two ragas, one for each end of the day, sung very slowly by a thin, unforced voice over two tanpuras tuned with unusual care. There is almost no ornament in the usual sense and no display at all; what there is instead is duration — single notes held until the ear stops hearing them as pitches and starts hearing them against the drone. The recording is close and dry, so the beating between voice and drone is audible.","Pandit Pran Nath trained in the Kirana style, spent years living as an ascetic, and in 1970 moved to New York at the invitation of La Monte Young and Marian Zazeela, who had heard his recordings and wanted to study with him. Terry Riley joined them. For the next quarter-century he taught a small circle of American composers in lofts and at the Dia Art Foundation, and sang far more often to them than to concert audiences.","The lesson his students took was about tuning. He insisted that each note of a raga has an exact place against the drone, audible as the point where the beating stops, and that most singers are approximate. That idea runs straight into the just-intonation obsessions of American minimalism — the tuned piano, the long-duration drone installations — which is one of the few clean cases of an Indian master reshaping Western composition directly.","Indian critics were lukewarm: the voice was slight, the repertoire narrow, the career spent abroad. His American reputation was the opposite, and the two assessments have never been reconciled. What is documented is the transmission — a generation of New York composers learned to sit with a drone and listen for beats because of these lessons, and said so repeatedly.","Use headphones and listen for the beating between the voice and the drone; when it slows to nothing, that is the note arriving. Give each raga twenty minutes before judging. Then play La Monte Young or Terry Riley immediately afterwards and the debt will be unmistakable."],"zakir-hussain-making-music":["Eight short pieces for tabla, Hariprasad Chaurasia's bamboo flute, John McLaughlin's guitar and Jan Garbarek's saxophone, recorded with the spacious, slightly cold sound the label is known for. Nothing here is a full raga; the tracks are scenes, some barely three minutes. The tabla is the lead voice as often as the accompanist, and the striking thing is how melodic it sounds — the lower drum bends its pitch under the heel of the hand, sliding between notes like a voice.","Zakir Hussain, son of the tabla master Alla Rakha, moved to the United States as a teenager and spent the 1970s and 1980s in projects that put Indian rhythm next to jazz, rock and West African drumming. This record came out of the European jazz world rather than the Indian concert circuit, produced in the label's house style, with a Norwegian saxophonist whose long tones sit on the drone like a second flute.","Listen to the pitch of the lower drum. Its black tuning patch is not decoration but mass, lowering and focusing the tone, and pressing the heel of the hand into the skin while striking bends the note upward — so a drum plays a melodic line. The smaller drum is tuned to the tonic with a hammer before every performance, which is why a tabla set belongs to one raga at a time.","The record sold well and was faulted, predictably, for prettiness; the label's aesthetic has always attracted that charge. Its defenders point out that it gave a mass audience the tabla as a melodic instrument rather than an exotic pulse. Hussain went on to win Grammys for cross-cultural percussion projects and became the most recognisable Indian musician of his generation after Shankar.","Listen to one track for the tabla alone and ignore everything else; the bends will emerge quickly. Then hear a straight tabla solo with only a drone for company, and finally an Indian classical duet, and you will have the three settings the instrument lives in. The original label mix is the one to find."],"fela-kuti-gentleman":["Three long tracks by Fela and his band Africa 70, the title piece filling most of the first side. It opens with Fela alone on tenor saxophone, a raw, searching solo, before the guitars lock into a tight, circling riff, the bass settles, Tony Allen's drums start their loose, rolling pulse and the horns punch in. Only after several minutes does Fela sing, in Pidgin English, answered by a chorus. The whole record moves like a slow tide rather than a song.","By 1973 Fela was running his own club, the Afrika Shrine, in Lagos, and had fully absorbed what he heard in America in 1969: James Brown's funk and the politics of Black Power. Nigeria was under military rule, flush with oil money, and its elite prized Western manners. Fela's answer was Afrobeat, sung in the Pidgin English that ordinary Nigerians across language groups could understand, and released on EMI Nigeria to a huge local audience.","The title song mocks Africans who wear a suit, tie and heavy socks in tropical heat to seem civilised, and Fela proudly declares himself no such gentleman. Listen to how the band withholds the downbeat: the bass and rhythm guitar play small, repeated figures that sit slightly off the beat, so the groove seems to float. The saxophone opening is deliberately rough; Fela was a limited but fearless player, and he knew it.","Gentleman was a local hit and is now counted among the first fully formed Afrobeat records, the point where the style's long form and its satire settled together. Critics outside Nigeria heard it only decades later through reissues from the late 1990s onward. Some jazz listeners find Fela's own solos crude; most now hear them as part of the record's candour rather than a flaw.","Give the title track its full length; the point is the build. Notice the moment each instrument enters over the first three minutes and how the horns stab rather than play melodies. Then listen to Tony Allen alone for a minute. Compare it with James Brown's Sex Machine to hear what Fela borrowed and what he slowed down."],"fela-kuti-expensive-shit":["Two long tracks, one per side. The title piece rides a bright, hurrying groove with stuttering guitars and blaring horn riffs, Fela's organ and saxophone breaking in before his vocal and the call-and-response chorus. The second side, Water No Get Enemy, is slower and warmer, a stately horn theme over a swaying beat that many listeners find the most beautiful thing Fela recorded. Both show Africa 70 at its tightest.","The title track comes from an incident in 1974: police raided Fela's commune, the Kalakuta Republic, and accused him of possessing cannabis; he is said to have swallowed the evidence, and when the authorities waited to retrieve it, he arranged for a clean sample to be handed over. He was freed and turned the humiliation of the police into a song. Water No Get Enemy uses a Yoruba proverb about the power of water, which no one can fight.","Water No Get Enemy is really an argument about power: water is gentle, needed by everyone and impossible to defeat, and Fela casts himself as water against the government. Listen for the tenor guitar, playing a short, choppy figure in the high register, and the rhythm guitar underneath it; the two interlock like the two hands of a drummer. The organ, Fela's own, often doubles the bass line in thick chords.","Nigerian audiences loved the cheek of the title track; outside Nigeria the crude title long kept the album out of polite discussion. Reissues since the late 1990s have made Water No Get Enemy one of Fela's most covered and sampled pieces. Historians note that the raid stories come mainly from Fela and his circle, and details vary between tellings.","Start with Water No Get Enemy and listen for the horn theme, then for the way the band drops almost to nothing and swells back. Then play the title track for the contrast in speed. Parliament's Mothership Connection, from the same year, is a good companion for hearing how two funk empires chose different paths."],"fela-kuti-zombie":["The title track is a twelve-minute march turned into a dance: a fast, driving beat, a curt, repeated horn riff that sounds like a bugle call, and Fela barking military commands that the chorus answers with the word zombie. Saxophone and trumpet solos stretch out the middle; then the commands return, faster and more absurd. The energy is relentless, the tone jeering. The companion side keeps the same band and the same attack on blind obedience.","Nigeria had been under military rule since 1966, and Fela's commune, the Kalakuta Republic, sat near a Lagos barracks. The song, which pictures soldiers as mindless zombies following orders, spread fast and was reportedly chanted at soldiers in the streets. In February 1977 around a thousand soldiers stormed Kalakuta, burned it, beat Fela and threw his elderly mother, the activist Funmilayo Ransome-Kuti, from an upstairs window. She died of her injuries the following year.","The horn riff is built like a parade-ground bugle signal, and the vocal is a parody of drill: Fela shouts orders such as attention, quick march and salute, and the chorus replies zombie each time. The satire is in the form itself. Listen to how the drums push slightly ahead of the band, making the march feel frantic rather than orderly, as if the parade is about to fall apart.","Zombie was a huge hit in Nigeria and the most costly record of Fela's life. Abroad it became his signature: Talking Heads, Brian Eno and later hip-hop producers cited it, and David Byrne said Remain in Light grew from listening to Fela. Critics now rank it among the defining protest records of the century, a case where a dance record brought down real state violence.","Put it on loud and let the first two minutes build before the vocal. Listen for each command and the chorus answer, then for the soldiers' march breaking into the band's much looser swing. Follow with James Brown's Cold Sweat, which it grows from, and Talking Heads' Remain in Light, which grew from it."],"fela-kuti-coffin-for-head-of-state":["A single piece in two parts across both sides of a record, played by Fela's new, larger band Egypt 80. The groove is slow, heavy and funereal, a mournful horn line over a dragging beat, and the whole first part is instrumental. When Fela finally sings, it is a long, narrated account: of Christianity and Islam as imported tools of control, and of the day he carried a coffin to the seat of government.","After the 1977 raid on Kalakuta, Fela's mother died in 1978. In 1979 he and his followers carried a coffin through Lagos and left it at Dodan Barracks, the residence of the military head of state Olusegun Obasanjo, a gesture both of mourning and accusation. Nigeria returned briefly to civilian rule that year. Fela had also founded his own political party and tried to run for president; the song is his record of a personal and national grief.","The song names names: Fela lists officials and lays blame on specific people, which almost no Nigerian musician would risk. It also refuses Christian and Muslim consolation, part of Fela's turn toward Yoruba religion and his own title, Anikulapo, meaning one who carries death in his pouch. Listen for the chorus gradually taking over the narration, as if the whole community were testifying.","In Nigeria the song was more reverenced than danced to; abroad it is less known than Zombie but many consider it Fela's most moving record. Some critics find its length and its religious polemic heavy going. It has become central to accounts of Fela as a political figure and to the Broadway musical Fela!, where the coffin procession is a climactic scene.","Treat it as a lament: let the long instrumental opening establish the mood before the words. Listen to how the horns keep returning to the same falling phrase like a dirge. Pair it with Billie Holiday's Strange Fruit and Marvin Gaye's What's Going On, other records that turned private grief into public accusation."],"ali-farka-toure-talking-timbuktu":["Ten songs in which Ali Farka Touré's guitar and quiet, rounded voice meet the American guitarist Ry Cooder's slide, bass and small studio band. The songs move in long, circling guitar figures over steady pulses, some with a light shuffle, some almost static. Touré also plays the njarka, a one-string fiddle. Cooder mostly listens and colours, his bottleneck guitar sliding into the gaps. The sound is spacious and unhurried, like a conversation on a porch.","Touré had become known outside Mali through albums for the London label World Circuit from 1987, and journalists kept comparing him to John Lee Hooker. Cooder, a scholar-guitarist with a gift for cross-cultural sessions, flew to meet him, and the two recorded quickly in Los Angeles with a few chosen players. The album won the Grammy for Best World Music Album and sold far beyond the usual audience for African records.","The American comparison was not flattery on either side: Touré said that when he heard Hooker he recognised the music of his own region, not the other way round. Listen to how many songs rest on one chord or a single bass note, the melody spiralling above: that is a Sahelian habit, not a blues one. Cooder also plays unusual instruments on some tracks; follow the textures rather than the solos.","The album was a critical and commercial success and fixed the phrase desert blues in Western minds. Some African music critics argued that the American framing made Touré seem an echo of the Delta rather than a source, and that his more rooted Malian albums show him better. Most now value both: the collaboration is gentle and the playing never strains for effect.","Play it at low volume in the evening. Pick one guitar line and follow it round; notice how small changes feel large after a while. Then play John Lee Hooker's Boogie Chillen' and Son House's Death Letter Blues and decide for yourself which way the river flows. Follow it with Niafunké for Touré at home."],"ali-farka-toure-niafunke":["Touré's homecoming record, made with his own musicians rather than foreign guests. His electric and acoustic guitars circle over calabash percussion and handclaps, answered by a small chorus; on some songs he takes up the njarka fiddle, whose thin, buzzing line sounds ancient beside the guitar. The pieces are sung in Songhai, Peul, Bambara and Tamashek, the languages of his region. The textures are dry and close, the grooves patient, sometimes almost trance-like.","After Talking Timbuktu, Touré had largely retreated from touring to farm rice and work his land in Niafunké, a town on the Niger in northern Mali, where he later became mayor. Rather than travel to a studio, he had the recording brought to him: the sessions were made with mobile equipment in a disused building on the edge of town in the heat, with the generator and the river never far away.","Several songs are about farming, work and community, and some are praise songs; Touré spoke of his music as rooted in the spirits of the river. Listen for small sounds of place: the room's ring, the dry click of the calabash, the loose timing between voices. The guitar sometimes imitates the njarka's buzzing drone. That texture is the link between his instruments and his region's older music.","Critics welcomed the album as a truer portrait of Touré than his American collaboration, less polished and more rooted, and it is often ranked among his finest. Some listeners find its long, repetitive songs monotonous without the Western frame; admirers answer that repetition is the point, as in the blues before it.","Listen right through, perhaps on headphones for the room sound. Notice how different the njarka tracks are from the guitar ones, and how the chorus answers rather than harmonises. Compare with Talking Timbuktu for the difference between a visit and a home, then with Tinariwen for the next generation of the Sahel."],"ali-farka-toure-in-the-heart-of-the-moon":["Largely instrumental duets between Touré's guitar and Toumani Diabaté's kora, the twenty-one-string harp-lute of the Mande griots. The guitar lays down steady, droning figures from the north; the kora answers with cascading runs and rippling patterns, often playing bass, harmony and melody at once. Voices appear only briefly. The music is intimate and flowing, two masters trading phrases like old friends in a quiet room.","The album was recorded in a few sessions at a hotel in Bamako, on the bank of the Niger, largely improvised with very little preparation. Touré was already ill with the cancer that killed him in 2006. The pairing had meaning in Mali: Touré came from the Songhai north, Diabaté from a long line of Mande griots in the south, two musical cultures of one country meeting as equals.","Many of the pieces start from Mande songs Diabaté knows from the griot repertoire, including praise songs for historical figures, while Touré's lines come from northern traditions; they meet on common ground in real time. Listen for moments where one player stops to let the other finish a phrase, and for the kora's thumbs plucking a low line that functions like a bass guitar.","It won the Grammy for Best Traditional World Music Album and has been widely admired as among the most beautiful records in African music. A few critics find it too gentle and easy on the ear; most hear it as a summation of two careers. A second volume of duets, Ali and Toumani, appeared posthumously in 2010.","Listen as you would to a string duo or two jazz players: follow who leads and who accompanies, and notice when they swap. Start with any track and simply try to separate guitar from kora. Pair it with Shankar's duets with Yehudi Menuhin, another meeting of traditions on equal terms."],"youssou-ndour-immigres":["Four long songs by Youssou N'Dour and Super Étoile de Dakar in full flight. The sound is mbalax: sabar drums and the tama, the small talking drum held under the arm, firing rapid cross-rhythms beneath chiming guitars, keyboards and a saxophone, with N'Dour's high, ringing voice riding on top, sometimes gliding in long melismas, sometimes almost shouting. The drums often break forward in hard, clattering bursts that invite dancers to answer.","By the early 1980s N'Dour was the biggest star in Senegal, playing weekly at his Dakar club. Many Senegalese had emigrated to France for work, and when he played for them in Paris he sang about their lives. Recorded in Dakar and released first on cassette at home, the album was licensed to European labels a few years later, just as world music was being marketed in London and Paris.","The title song speaks to Senegalese workers abroad, urging them to remember home and to return; its Wolof title means roughly the same thing, and the song avoids easy sentiment about exile. Listen for the tama: its pitch bends as the player squeezes the drum's cords, so it seems to talk, answering the voice. That speech-like drumming is the core of mbalax, not decoration.","European critics hearing it in the late 1980s treated it as a revelation, the purest example of mbalax available outside Senegal. Later N'Dour albums polished the sound for Western ears, and many critics still hold that Immigrés, rougher and closer to the Dakar club, is his best. Its relatively poor recording quality is part of its appeal for some and a barrier for others.","Turn it up and follow the drums rather than the voice at first: notice how often they seem to argue with the beat the guitars are keeping. Then follow N'Dour's voice as it floats free above. Compare with King Sunny Adé's Juju Music, another West African dance band recorded at the same moment."],"youssou-ndour-7-seconds":["A slow, hushed duet between N'Dour and the Swedish-born singer Neneh Cherry, over a soft, programmed hip-hop beat, keyboards and a spare guitar. N'Dour opens in Wolof, his voice light and floating; Cherry answers in English; both touch on French. The verses stay low and intimate, the chorus lifts only slightly. There is nothing of the clattering sabar drums of his Dakar records: this is late-night pop, built on mood and the contrast between two voices.","The song appeared on N'Dour's album The Guide (Wommat), made for an international audience after years of touring the world with Peter Gabriel and Amnesty International. Neneh Cherry, stepdaughter of the jazz trumpeter Don Cherry, was a star of British pop and hip-hop. The single became one of the best-selling records in France and across Europe that year, and the most widely heard song by any African singer of the decade.","The title refers to the first seconds of a newborn child's life, before it has learned anything of racism or division, and the song's three languages are the message itself: a plea that difference need not mean conflict. Many European listeners had no idea what the Wolof verses said, which suits the song's theme of hearing across languages.","It was an enormous hit in Europe, much less so in America. Some admirers of N'Dour's mbalax regretted the smoothness and thought him diluted for export; others defend it as a fine piece of pop craft that opened the door to his later work. It has been covered and sampled many times and remains a staple of European radio.","Listen for the moment each voice enters, and notice how neither singer pushes; the song works by restraint. Then play the title track of Immigrés straight after it to hear the same voice in its home setting. Sade's Love Deluxe, from the same years, is a good neighbour in mood."],"youssou-ndour-egypt":["Eight devotional songs in which N'Dour sings in Wolof over an Egyptian orchestra led by Fathy Salama, with Senegalese players joining in. The sound is lush and slow: massed strings in the style of Cairo film and concert orchestras, the plucked qanun zither and oud, the breathy nay flute, and frame drums. N'Dour's voice is gentler than in his dance music, bending around the orchestra's modal melodies rather than cutting through a band.","The songs praise the Sufi saints of Senegal, above all Cheikh Amadou Bamba, founder of the Mouride brotherhood, to which N'Dour belongs. He had grown up hearing Egyptian singers such as Umm Kulthum on Senegalese radio and wanted to join the two worlds of Islam he knew. Recorded in Cairo and Dakar, the album was held back for some time; he felt the moment after September 2001 was wrong for it.","The songs were controversial at home: some Senegalese objected to devotional texts being set as popular music and sold commercially, especially during Ramadan, and N'Dour defended the record as an act of faith. Listen for places where the Senegalese percussion enters beneath the Egyptian strings: two rhythmic habits meeting within one song, not always comfortably, which is part of the record's honesty.","Western critics praised it widely and it won the Grammy for Best Contemporary World Music Album. It gave many listeners a view of African Islam as tolerant and mystical at a time of hostile headlines. In Senegal it took longer to be accepted. Some fans of mbalax found it too far from his core style; many now count it among his best work.","Listen as you would to sacred music: in one sitting, without shuffle. Notice the orchestral introductions, then how N'Dour's voice enters softly and grows. Compare with Aretha Franklin's Amazing Grace or Coltrane's A Love Supreme, other records where a popular musician gave a whole album to devotion."],"salif-keita-mandjou":["A long praise song by Salif Keita with Les Ambassadeurs Internationaux. It opens with a slow, elaborate introduction, guitars and horns tracing a Mande melody, before a medium dance groove settles. Keita's voice enters high and keening, stretching words into long, ornamented lines with the declamatory force of a griot. Horn riffs and a thin, wiry organ answer him; the band keeps an even, rocking pulse beneath the soaring voice. The effect is both courtly and danceable.","Les Ambassadeurs, a rival of Bamako's Rail Band, had moved to Abidjan in Côte d'Ivoire amid Mali's political tensions. Mandjou praises Ahmed Sékou Touré, the president of Guinea, and his lineage, in the way griots traditionally praised rulers and patrons. Touré, who promoted Guinean popular music as national culture, honoured Keita in return. Recorded in Abidjan, the song became one of the best-known in West Africa.","Keita, a descendant of Mali's royal Keita line, was by birth someone whom griots praised, not a griot himself; that he took on the praise-singer's role scandalised his family. Mandjou therefore inverts an old order. Listen for the long unaccompanied-seeming vocal phrases where the band nearly stops: that freedom of timing is the griot style carried into a dance band.","It was a huge hit across West Africa and remains among the most loved songs of the period. Its praise for Sékou Touré, whose regime imprisoned and killed many opponents, sits uneasily today, and admirers usually separate the song's musical force from its dedication. Reissues have made it a classic for Western listeners discovering 1970s West African bands.","Wait through the introduction: it is part of the ceremony. When the voice enters, follow one long phrase to its end and notice how it curves. Compare with Aretha Franklin's Amazing Grace for another voice trained in praise and ornament, and with Soro to hear what Keita did with the same gifts in Paris a decade later."],"salif-keita-soro":["Six long songs in which Keita's voice meets a full Paris studio production: layered synthesisers, crisp drum machines and programmed rhythms, bright horn charts, electric guitars, kora and a female chorus. Each song builds from a spacious, atmospheric opening into a powerful groove. Keita's singing is at its most commanding, soaring above the arrangements in long, keening phrases. The sound is huge and glossy, the most expensive-sounding African record of its day.","Keita moved to Paris in 1984, where a large West African community and several labels were turning African music into a European market. Soro was produced by the Senegalese producer Ibrahima Sylla and arranged by the French keyboardists François Bréant and Jean-Philippe Rykiel. Released in 1987, the year the term world music was adopted by British record companies, it became a model for how African pop might sound on international radio.","Beneath the technology the songs rest on Mande tradition: melodies and praise formulas a griot would recognise, and rhythms from Mali's hunters' and dance music. Listen for the kora, often buried, playing interlocking patterns that the synthesisers imitate. Keita's lyrics address Mande history, social duty and his own experience as an outsider born with albinism; the production does not change what he sings about.","It was a critical success in Europe and made Keita an international star; some reviewers called it the best African album yet produced. Others, then and since, find the 1980s production dated and prefer his acoustic albums, such as Moffou (2002). It remains a key document of Paris as the capital of African pop in the 1980s.","Start with the opening track and wait for the voice to arrive over the synthesiser haze. Listen for the kora and the horn stabs. Compare it with Mandjou to hear how the same voice fares in a Bamako dance band and a Paris studio, and with Youssou N'Dour's Immigrés for a rougher contemporary."],"miriam-makeba-qongqothwane":["A short, bright song in Xhosa, sung by Makeba over a light accompaniment of guitar, bass and percussion. Its hook is the language itself: the Xhosa click consonants, which she snaps out with rhythmic precision so they work almost as percussion within the melody. The tune is simple and repetitive, a gentle, lilting verse sung over and over, and the whole song lasts barely two minutes. Makeba's voice is clear, relaxed and smiling.","Qongqothwane is a traditional Xhosa wedding song, sung to bless a bride as she leaves for her new home. Makeba recorded it early in her American career, on her first album for RCA, when Harry Belafonte was introducing her to American audiences. She was soon to be barred from returning to South Africa, and songs like this became, for foreign listeners, the first sound of Black South African culture.","The title names a beetle, the knocking beetle, whose tapping was traditionally taken as a good omen, and the song asks it to bless the journey. English-speaking audiences called it The Click Song because they could not say the title, which Makeba often joked about on stage before teaching them the sounds. Listen for the three kinds of click; each is a different consonant, not a sound effect.","It became Makeba's signature in concert, and for millions of Western listeners the first African language they ever heard sung. Some critics later worried that it was received as novelty, the clicks treated as exotic tricks; Makeba herself used it to insist on the dignity of her language. It remains one of the most performed South African songs in the world.","Listen once for the melody, then again just for the clicks, and notice how they fall in rhythm. Then find a live recording where Makeba explains the song, because her introduction is part of the performance. Pair it with the spirituals Steal Away and Wade in the Water, other traditional songs that crossed into concert life."],"miriam-makeba-evening-with-belafonte-makeba":["An album of South African songs sung by Makeba and Harry Belafonte, in solo and duet, with small acoustic accompaniment of guitars, bass and percussion and occasional chorus. The songs are in Zulu, Xhosa, Sotho and Swahili: lullabies, courtship songs, work songs and pointed political songs. The two voices contrast well, Belafonte's husky and conversational, Makeba's clear and agile. The recording is intimate and warm, closer to a folk session than a show.","Makeba had been in exile since 1960; in 1963 she testified against apartheid at the United Nations, and South Africa banned her records. Belafonte, at the height of his fame and deeply involved in the American civil rights movement, had sponsored her move to the United States. The album was released in 1965, as the civil rights struggle peaked in America, linking the two causes for American listeners.","Among the gentle songs sit direct protests: one warns the architect of apartheid, Hendrik Verwoerd, that Black South Africans will not be held down, sung in a language most American buyers could not understand. The album's sweetness is partly camouflage. Listen for the change of tone when a political song arrives; the rhythm hardens and Makeba's voice darkens.","It won the Grammy for Best Folk Recording, the first Grammy for an African artist, and was a best-seller. Some critics felt Belafonte's star power overshadowed Makeba; others hear a genuine partnership. Its standing has grown as histories of the anti-apartheid movement recognise music's role in it, and it is now a key document of African music's arrival in America.","Listen through in order, and notice how lullabies and protest songs sit side by side. Follow Makeba's voice alone on her solo tracks. Pair it with Pete Seeger's We Shall Overcome and Bob Dylan's Blowin' in the Wind, the American protest songs of the same moment, to hear two freedom movements sharing a folk language."],"miriam-makeba-pata-pata":["A joyous, bouncing dance song. A loping, circular bass line and chugging guitar give it the lilt of South African township jive; a small group of backing singers answers Makeba; organ and horns fill out the sound. Makeba sings partly in Xhosa and partly in English, including a spoken introduction explaining the dance. The whole thing is built on a short, endlessly repeated chord cycle, so it seems as though it could go on for ever.","Pata Pata was a dance in the Johannesburg townships of the 1950s, and Makeba had recorded a song about it with her group the Skylarks before exile. In 1967, now living in New York, she recorded a new version for Reprise with the American producer Jerry Ragovoy. It became a hit in the United States and around the world, the first by a South African artist, shortly before her marriage to the Black Power activist Stokely Carmichael, a match that cost her American bookings.","The name means touch touch, describing a dance in which partners pat each other. Makeba privately thought it one of her most trivial songs and was bemused that it was her biggest hit. Listen to the repeating chord cycle; it is the same circular pattern that runs through South African township music from marabi to mbaqanga, and through much later Afropop.","It was an international hit and has been rerecorded countless times, including by Makeba herself in later decades. Some critics dismissed it as lightweight next to her political songs; others argue its pleasure was itself a statement from an exile. The authorship of the original 1950s song has been debated, with other South African musicians credited in some accounts.","Just move to it. Listen to the bass: it never stops circling, and everything else is decoration. Then play Qongqothwane and the Belafonte album to hear the range of her voice. For the later life of this groove, move on to Ladysmith Black Mambazo and the South African side of Paul Simon's Graceland."],"ladysmith-black-mambazo-amabutho":["Twelve short a cappella songs in Zulu. Joseph Shabalala's high tenor leads, calling out a line; the choir answers in a deep, soft, rolling chord, basses underneath, tenors above, in close harmony that swells and recedes like breathing. There are no instruments. Some songs are gentle hymns, others brisk and playful, with whistles, ululations and clicked consonants. The texture is velvety rather than loud, a quality the style's name, isicathamiya, from a word meaning to walk softly, suggests.","Isicathamiya grew among Zulu men working far from home in the mines and factories of Johannesburg and Durban, who held all-night singing competitions in their hostels. Shabalala had rebuilt his group's style after dreams in which he said he heard new harmonies. After their success on Radio Zulu, they recorded Amabutho for Gallo in 1973. It became the first album by Black South African musicians to sell enough to be certified gold.","The soft singing and tiptoeing steps are a legacy of the hostels, where men had to rehearse without disturbing sleeping workers or attracting the guards. The title means warriors, referring to the old Zulu regiments. Listen to how the basses sustain a long, low chord while the leader sings free phrases above: the church harmony the missionaries brought is carrying an older Zulu call-and-response.","It made the group stars across South Africa, where they recorded prolifically through the 1970s. Outside the country it was almost unknown until the Graceland period, when earlier albums were reissued. Scholars of South African music now value these early records as the purest form of the group's style, before the international years polished it.","Listen with the lights low. Follow the leader and the choir as a conversation, and notice how the bass voices move very little. Compare with the American gospel quartets, such as the Golden Gate Quartet, whose records reached South Africa and influenced the hostel choirs, then with Shaka Zulu fourteen years later."],"ladysmith-black-mambazo-shaka-zulu":["Ten a cappella songs, some in Zulu, a few mixing in English. The group sings with its trademark cushioned bass sound and sweet leader's voice, but the production is spacious and clean, each voice audible, the tempos slightly more relaxed. The songs range from hymns of consolation to celebrations of Zulu history, including the title song about the nineteenth-century Zulu king. The effect is serene, warm and devotional.","Paul Simon heard the group on a cassette, went to South Africa in 1985 and recorded with them for Graceland (1986), which made them world famous. He then produced this album for Warner Bros. The period was the height of the cultural boycott of apartheid South Africa; Simon was widely criticised for breaking it, while the group's members pointed out that the collaboration brought their music and its context to a global audience.","Several songs look back to Zulu kingship and pride at a time when the apartheid state promoted ethnic division; listening now, the choice of Shaka as subject is not merely picturesque. Listen also for the vocal effects between lines, the clicks, whistles and small percussive grunts; those are the sounds of the dancing, the soft stomps and kicks that accompany the songs on stage.","It won the Grammy for Best Traditional Folk Recording and sold widely. Critics praised the singing; some felt the production made the style more polite than the earlier Gallo records. The wider Graceland debate, about cultural boycott, credit and payment, continues, though the group's members have consistently defended Simon and their collaboration.","Begin with the title song. Listen for the bass voices underneath and how they move together like one instrument. Then return to the early album Amabutho to hear the rougher original, and look for Graceland's Homeless, sung with Simon, to hear how the collaboration sounded."],"thomas-mapfumo-chimurenga-singles":["A gathering of singles recorded by Mapfumo in the last years of Rhodesia. Two or three electric guitars play short, interlocking, repeating patterns copied from the mbira, the thumb piano of Shona ceremony, over a rolling bass, drums and the steady hiss of hosho gourd shakers. Mapfumo's voice, low and husky, sings in Shona in a declamatory style, often answered by a chorus. Each song cycles like a ceremony rather than following a verse-chorus plan.","In the 1970s a guerrilla war was fought against Ian Smith's white-minority government of Rhodesia. Mapfumo's songs, broadcast and sold as singles, used Shona proverbs and imagery to express support for the fighters and to urge people to resist, in ways the authorities could not always prove. His records were restricted and he was detained without charge in 1979. Zimbabwe became independent in 1980.","The guitar patterns are not decorative: they reproduce the mbira's two interlocking parts, each hand playing a different line so that a third melody seems to emerge between them. Even the heavy guitar tone imitates the mbira's buzzing bottle-caps. Ancestral songs sung to call spirits at ceremonies are here aimed at political liberation; the music's function, not just its sound, carries across.","In Zimbabwe these songs are part of the story of the war, and Mapfumo was celebrated as a national hero at independence. International listeners came to them later through reissues, and critics often rank them as among the most original fusions of traditional and electric music in Africa. His later criticism of the Mugabe government complicated his standing at home.","Pick one guitar and follow it; then shift to another and notice how together they make a pattern neither plays alone. Compare with Steve Reich's Music for 18 Musicians or Ligeti's piano études, which use similar interlocking ideas, and then look for a recording of traditional mbira to hear the source."],"thomas-mapfumo-corruption":["A later album by Mapfumo and the Blacks Unlimited, fuller and more polished than the wartime singles. Real mbiras now sit alongside the electric guitars, their bright, buzzing tones interlocking with the guitars' imitation of them; horns and keyboards add colour; the rhythm section is tight and propulsive. Mapfumo's voice is as husky and declamatory as ever, the songs long and cyclical, built for dancing and listening at once.","By the late 1980s the hopes of independence were souring: Robert Mugabe's government was accused of corruption and of repression, notably in Matabeleland. Mapfumo, once a hero of the liberation war, turned his songs against the new elite. Released internationally on Island's Mango label, the album brought his music to Western audiences just as world music was being marketed, while making him enemies at home.","The title song, sung partly in English, is aimed at officials who had become the new oppressors, which in Zimbabwe of 1989 was a risky thing to say. Listen for the mbira and guitars playing the same kind of interlocking pattern side by side: the album makes audible how Mapfumo's electric style began as a translation of the mbira, and now brings the original back in.","It was well received abroad, less welcome among Zimbabwe's rulers. Critics generally prefer the rawer wartime recordings, but this album is valued as the moment the liberation singer turned critic. After years of harassment, Mapfumo left for the United States in 2000 and has lived there since, which gives the album a prophetic weight.","Listen for the mbira first, then for the guitars copying it. Compare with the wartime singles to hear how much has been added. Pair it with Fela's Coffin for Head of State for another African musician turning his art on a post-independence government."],"franco-tpok-jazz-mario":["A long song in two halves, as is usual in Congolese rumba. The first part is a relaxed, swaying story sung in Lingala, Franco's gruff voice alternating with the band's sweeter vocalists over gently interlocking guitars and saxophones. Then comes the sebene, the fast instrumental section: bright lead guitars ring in quick, cycling patterns, the saxophones repeat short riffs, the drums lift, and the song becomes pure dance music, driven by Franco's thick, chopping rhythm guitar.","By 1985 Franco's TPOK Jazz was the most popular band in Zaire and across much of Africa, with dozens of musicians and a vast catalogue, working in Kinshasa and Brussels. Franco was famous for songs of social comment, often thinly veiled, which had landed him in prison under Mobutu. Mario was one of his biggest hits, selling widely across Central and East Africa.","The song tells the story of Mario, a young man with diplomas who lives off an older, wealthy woman, spends her money and treats her badly, and the narrator scolds him at length; listeners heard in it a satire of a whole class of idle, educated young men in Zaire. Listen for how the story's tone shifts from amused to exasperated, then is swept away by the sebene.","Mario was an enormous success and remains one of the best-known Congolese songs. Critics see it as a peak of Franco's late style, combining storytelling with the band's power. Some modern listeners find its sexual politics, in which the older woman is both victim and joke, uncomfortable; others hear sharp social observation of Mobutu-era Kinshasa.","Listen to the whole song. Enjoy the story's easy swing, then notice the moment the sebene arrives and the guitars take over. Follow one guitar pattern round, then the saxophones. Compare with Dizzy Gillespie's Manteca for the Cuban sound that Congolese rumba grew from, and Bob Dylan's Like a Rolling Stone for another long song scolding a fallen character."],"franco-tpok-jazz-attention-na-sida":["A long, slow, sombre song. Over a gentle rumba pulse, lightly picked guitars and soft saxophones, Franco speaks and sings in French and Lingala, more narrator than singer, while a chorus answers him. The music is warm but restrained; the emphasis falls on the words. There is no exuberant dance section of the usual kind; the band keeps a grave, steady sway, as if accompanying a public announcement delivered with care.","By the mid-1980s AIDS was spreading across Central Africa, including Zaire, but was surrounded by silence and stigma. Franco made the song as a public-health message, addressing the whole of society. He was himself seriously ill at the time; he died in Brussels in 1989, amid widespread speculation about the illness, which was never publicly confirmed. The song has often been heard in that light.","The song does not preach from outside. Franco addresses doctors, governments, the young and the old in turn, calls on people to be tested and to care for the sick rather than cast them out, and speaks of the disease without the moral blame then common. That care for the sick, not the warning itself, is what gives it lasting weight.","It was widely played and discussed across Africa and is often cited as one of the first major popular songs about AIDS anywhere. Critics outside Africa, hearing it later, were struck by its seriousness from a musician known for dance songs and satire. It is now studied as an example of popular music's role in public health.","Listen with a translation if you can find one, since the words matter most. Notice how the band's sound is pared back and how the chorus answers Franco like a congregation. Compare with Mario to hear the range of his band, and with Marvin Gaye's What's Going On for another message record delivered with grace."],"tinariwen-aman-iman":["Twelve songs in Tamashek. Two or three electric guitars play rolling, looping riffs, some crisp and dry, some fuzzed and droning, over a loping bass, calabash and hand drums, and handclaps. The lead singers' voices are low and weathered, answered by a chorus of women who ululate and sing short refrains. The songs are mostly in slow to medium tempo, hypnotic and repetitive, with brief, stinging guitar solos. The whole record sounds like heat and distance.","Tinariwen formed among Tuareg exiles in Libyan and Algerian camps; some members fought in the Tuareg rebellion in Mali in the early 1990s. After peace they became musicians full-time and found an international audience from the early 2000s. This third international album was recorded in Bamako with the British guitarist and producer Justin Adams and was their breakthrough to rock audiences in Europe and America.","The title means water is life, and the songs return again and again to thirst, exile, the loss of land and the scattering of a nomadic people: the music is political without slogans. Listen for the guitar parts that copy the phrasing of the tehardent lute and older Tuareg songs; the riffs that sound like blues come from their own tradition as much as from any record.","It was widely praised by rock critics and musicians; several major rock guitarists named them as favourites, and the band toured with big rock acts. Some critics warned against romanticising rebel musicians as desert exotics; the band itself emphasised that the songs were about real hardship. It remains the album most listeners start with.","Start with the opening track and let the guitars settle into your ears. Notice the women's voices and the handclaps, which carry as much rhythm as the drums. Compare with Ali Farka Touré's Niafunké for an older Malian guitar style, and with John Lee Hooker's Boom Boom and Jimi Hendrix for the Western cousins of the sound."],"tinariwen-tassili":["A largely acoustic album. Tinariwen's guitars ring out unplugged, their looping riffs lighter and more intimate than on the electric albums, over hand percussion, handclaps and soft chorus. The voices are close and quiet, some songs almost murmured. Guest musicians from American rock add subtle touches: a horn section on one song, a second voice or an extra guitar on others. The mood is reflective, spacious and open-air.","Growing insecurity in northern Mali made recording at home difficult, so the band recorded in the Tassili n'Ajjer, a region of sandstone plateaus in south-eastern Algeria, camping in the desert with a mobile studio. American guests, including members of TV on the Radio and the guitarist Nels Cline, came out to join them. The following year, 2012, war broke out in northern Mali, and the region became dangerous for the band.","Listen for the outdoor sound: no studio walls, voices and guitars dissolving into open air, and occasionally the sounds of the camp itself. The songs return to exile and to the fracture of Tuareg society, but in a quieter voice than before. The guests stay in the background; one test of the album is how little they change its character.","It won the Grammy for Best World Music Album and was widely praised as a new direction, showing the band's songs could stand without electric power. Some fans prefer the rougher electric records. Its release on the indie-rock label Anti- confirmed the band's place in Western alternative rock culture as much as in world music.","Listen outdoors if you can, or at least with windows open. Notice how the unplugged guitars bring out the melodies inside the riffs. Compare directly with Aman Iman to hear the same band electric and acoustic, and with Ali Farka Touré and Toumani Diabaté's In the Heart of the Moon for another quiet desert conversation."],"amadou-and-mariam-dimanche-a-bamako":["Fifteen bright, catchy songs sung in Bambara and French. Amadou's guitar lines are crisp and bluesy, Mariam's voice plain and warm, the rhythms often a quick, bouncing Malian pulse. The producer Manu Chao wraps them in a collage of street sounds: sirens, radio snatches, crowd noise, traffic, whistles and his own voice. Songs run into one another like a walk through a city on a Sunday, the day of weddings and parties in Bamako.","The couple had been recording since the 1980s, with modest success in Mali and later in France. Manu Chao, the French-Spanish singer whose collage style had been hugely successful, heard them and offered to produce. The album was made partly in Bamako and partly in Paris and became one of the best-selling records by African artists in France, winning major French music awards and a new audience across Europe.","The title song pictures a Bamako wedding, and much of the album describes ordinary life: markets, radios, neighbours, love. Listen for the Manu Chao signature sounds, some of which he reuses from his own records, and for the way Amadou's guitar keeps the traditional Malian patterns underneath the pop sheen. The couple's blindness is not a theme; the album is about seeing the city through sound.","It was a huge success in France and Europe; critics praised its warmth and wit. Some critics found Manu Chao's production too dominant, with the couple sounding like guests on his record; others argued the collaboration was genuinely equal and gave them a sound that suited them. It remains their best-known album.","Listen straight through, as the album is designed to flow. Notice the street sounds between songs and imagine the city. Then listen to Welcome to Mali's Sabali for their later, electronic sound. Compare with Talking Heads' Remain in Light for another Western studio's encounter with African pop."],"amadou-and-mariam-sabali":["A gentle, electronic love song. Mariam sings alone, her voice processed slightly, over pulsing analogue synthesisers, a soft, looping drum-machine beat and bubbling electronic arpeggios. There is little of the couple's usual guitar or Malian percussion. The melody is simple, repetitive and tender, and the synthesisers create a dreamy, floating sound. It is short, spare and quite different from anything else on the album around it.","The track appeared on the album Welcome to Mali, which followed the success of Dimanche à Bamako. Damon Albarn of Blur and Gorillaz, who had been visiting and recording in Mali since 2000, produced Sabali and played on it. Albarn's interest in West African music, and his use of vintage synthesisers, shaped its sound; the track became a favourite of Western indie and electronic audiences.","The title means patience in Bambara, and the song is a plea for patience and gentleness in love. Listen to how Mariam's melody follows the shape of Malian song even over the synthesisers; the electronic sound is a costume, not a replacement. Albarn's arrangement uses synthesisers like the old organ sounds of 1970s Malian bands, which he loved.","Critics singled it out as the highlight of Welcome to Mali, and it was widely played and remixed. Some Malian music purists thought it strayed too far from roots; many indie critics thought it the couple's best song. It has been used in films and advertisements and remains their most streamed track.","Listen on headphones for the synthesiser textures. Notice how simple the melody is and how much the sound does around it. Compare with Brian Eno's Another Green World for similar electronic gentleness, and with Dimanche à Bamako for the busier side of the couple."],"king-sunny-ade-juju-music":["Seven long, flowing songs by King Sunny Adé and his African Beats. Several electric guitars play small, overlapping figures; a pedal steel guitar slides and swoops; talking drums chatter in dialogue; percussion shakers, bells and congas fill the rest; a bass holds everything together. Adé's voice is smooth and relaxed, answered by a chorus. The sound is dense but light, like a cloud of tiny patterns moving together, with occasional dub-style echo effects and synthesiser.","Jùjú was Yoruba party music, played for patrons at weddings and celebrations in Lagos. After the death of Bob Marley in 1981, Island Records looked for a new international star from outside the West and signed Adé. The album was produced by the French producer Martin Meissonnier, who selected and trimmed songs, often much longer at home, for a Western audience.","The talking drums are not merely rhythmic: Yoruba is a tonal language, and the drums imitate speech, sending proverbs and praise phrases that Yoruba listeners can understand. The pedal steel, an instrument associated with American country music, was an unusual addition that Adé had adopted and made his own. Listen to how it floats over the dense guitar web.","The album was widely acclaimed in Europe and America and toured with great success, though it did not make Adé the star Island had hoped for. Critics now see it as a landmark of world music's first wave. Some Nigerian listeners saw it as a trimmed, exported version of what they heard at home; the home recordings remain more expansive.","Choose one sound, the pedal steel or a talking drum, and follow it. Then step back and hear the whole web. Compare with Steve Reich's Drumming for interlocking patterns and with Talking Heads' Remain in Light for American musicians trying to build a similar web."],"cesaria-evora-miss-perfumado":["Ten songs, mostly mornas, the slow, melancholy song form of Cape Verde, plus a few coladeiras, their quicker, lighter cousins. Évora's voice is low, unhurried and unforced, sung in Cape Verdean Creole over acoustic guitars, the small four-string cavaquinho, piano, violin, clarinet and light percussion. The music is intimate, the rhythms gently swaying, and many listeners hear kinship with Portuguese fado and Brazilian samba-canção.","Cape Verde, an archipelago off West Africa, was a Portuguese colony until 1975, and many of its people emigrated. Évora sang in the bars of Mindelo on São Vicente for decades, with little reward. Recorded in Paris for the small Lusafrica label founded by her producer José da Silva, the album became her international breakthrough when she was over fifty, and made her a star in France.","The album includes Sodade, a song about the longing of emigrants that has become a Cape Verdean anthem; the word means yearning. Listen to Évora's phrasing: she sings behind the beat with a slightly lazy ease, never pushing, which is the essence of morna. The cavaquinho's steady, brushed chords carry the rhythm while the guitar plays the bass line.","It was a surprise success in France and later across Europe and America, and made morna internationally known. Critics praised the purity and dignity of her voice and the simplicity of the arrangements. Some Cape Verdeans noted that the album presented a romantic view of their islands for foreign listeners; many others celebrated it as recognition of their culture.","Listen in the evening, perhaps with a glass of something. Notice how she lets phrases fall behind the beat. Start with Sodade, then listen through. Compare with Billie Holiday's God Bless the Child for another singer who works through understatement, and with Stan Getz and João Gilberto for Brazil's cousin tradition."],"cesaria-evora-voz-d-amor":["A later album in which Évora's mornas and coladeiras are set with fuller arrangements: string sections, piano, accordion and brass alongside the traditional guitars and cavaquinho. Her voice has deepened with age, still unhurried, now darker and more grained. The songs alternate slow, aching mornas and lightly swinging coladeiras. The production is polished but never busy; the voice remains the centre of every song.","By 2003 Évora was an international star, touring constantly and selling widely in France, Portugal, Brazil and beyond. She continued to record new songs by Cape Verdean writers alongside traditional material. Her Cape Verdean touring musicians formed the core, with arrangers adding strings and other colours around them, and the album, made for Lusafrica like her other international records, became among her most successful late releases, carrying morna to audiences who had first met it through Sodade.","Listen for the arrangements' borrowings: some strings recall Brazilian and Cuban orchestral traditions, reflecting the wide Lusophone and Atlantic world that Cape Verdean music always belonged to. The accordion has its own history in the islands' funaná style. Évora's voice is audibly older than on Miss Perfumado; its roughness adds weight to the songs of loss.","It won the Grammy for Best Contemporary World Music Album. Critics praised the arrangements; some felt the fuller production softened the intimacy of her earlier records. It stands as a summation of her late career, the record of a singer secure in her fame and free to widen her palette, and her death in 2011 gave these recordings added poignancy.","Compare a song here directly with one from Miss Perfumado: listen for the changes in the voice and the arrangements. Notice the strings and accordion. Follow it with Billie Holiday's late recordings, another great voice that deepened and roughened with age."],"mulatu-astatke-mulatu-of-ethiopia":["A short instrumental album. Mulatu's vibraphone and organ lead a band of horns, electric guitar, bass and drums through slow, brooding grooves and brighter funk pieces. The melodies use Ethiopian modes, five-note scales with unusual gaps that make them sound mysterious and minor, harmonised with jazz chords. The horns play in unison or close harmony; the rhythm section sits in a heavy, loping pulse. The atmosphere is dark, cool and hypnotic.","Mulatu, who had studied in London and at Berklee in Boston and played Latin jazz in New York, recorded this album in New York with local musicians. It shows the fusion he had been developing in Addis Ababa: Ethiopian modes set to jazz and funk. Released on a small label, it had little impact at the time and became rare, prized later by collectors and DJs.","The Ethiopian modes, called qenet, have names such as tezeta, a mode of nostalgia and longing; Mulatu writes melodies in these modes and harmonises them in ways that make them sound both jazz and not. Listen for the vibraphone, then unusual in Ethiopian music: Mulatu chose it partly because its sound suggested the resonance of traditional Ethiopian instruments.","Little noticed on release, the album was reissued in the 2000s and hailed as a lost classic of Afro-jazz. Critics now see it as an essential document of Ethio-jazz, and its tracks have been widely sampled. Its New York recording is sometimes contrasted with the rougher Addis recordings on the Éthiopiques series.","Listen in the dark. Follow the vibraphone melody and notice the gaps in the scale. Compare with Coltrane's Africa/Brass and Miles Davis's Kind of Blue for the modal jazz Mulatu drew on, and then with the Éthiopiques recordings made in Addis."],"mulatu-astatke-ethiopiques-4":["A compilation of instrumental recordings Mulatu made in Addis Ababa, collected decades later. Slow, minor-key grooves dominate: vibraphone and organ play long, snaking melodies in Ethiopian modes over bass and drums; horns enter in weighty unison; guitar and percussion add colour. Some tracks are brighter and funkier, others languid and eerie. The recording is raw and warm, with a live, slightly smoky room sound that has become the signature of the whole Éthiopiques series.","Addis Ababa in the late 1960s and early 1970s had a lively nightclub scene, with bands playing Ethiopian songs alongside soul, jazz and Latin music. Mulatu recorded many sessions for local labels, especially Amha Records. The 1974 revolution that overthrew Haile Selassie and brought a military regime ended the scene. The French producer Francis Falceto collected these recordings on Buda Musique's Éthiopiques series; this volume appeared in 1998.","Several of these tracks, including the brooding Yèkèrmo Sèw, were used in Jim Jarmusch's film Broken Flowers (2005), which brought Mulatu a new generation of fans. Listen for the tezeta mode, the Ethiopian scale of longing, in the slower pieces. The rough recording is not a flaw; Addis studios were basic, and the grit is part of the sound.","The compilation made Mulatu an international name and helped launch a global revival of interest in Ethiopian music. Critics praise it as essential listening; some have debated whether the series presents a romantic view of a vanished golden age. Mulatu has since collaborated with Western bands and toured widely, a late recognition that owes much to this album.","Start with Yèkèrmo Sèw. Listen to the vibraphone and horns in turn. Then play it after Coltrane's Africa/Brass or Ornette Coleman's Dancing in Your Head to hear different routes between Africa and jazz. Watch Broken Flowers for the music in its film setting."],"carlos-gardel-mi-noche-triste":["A man alone in the room his lover has left, talking to the things she left behind: that is the whole drama of Mi noche triste, sung by Carlos Gardel over a single guitar. The melody is an older instrumental tango, Lita, by Samuel Castriota; the words, by Pascual Contursi, turned it into a first-person lament. Gardel sings it almost conversationally, sliding into notes, leaning on the ends of phrases, a baritone that sounds like it is confiding rather than performing.","Before 1917 tango was mostly danced, not sung, and its lyrics, where they existed, were jaunty and often ribald. Gardel was then half of a popular folk duo with José Razzano, singing rural songs of the pampas. Recording Mi noche triste for the Odeon company moved him into the city's own music and changed the direction of both his career and the genre. Buenos Aires was swelling with immigrants, and its cafés and tenements wanted songs about their own heartbreak.","Listen to the vocabulary rather than the tune. Contursi wrote in lunfardo, the Buenos Aires street slang that mixed Italian, Spanish and underworld cant, and the lyric sets the abandoned lover among ordinary domestic objects, a bed, a mirror, a guitar hanging on the wall. That shift from pampas imagery to a rented room in the city is the birth of the tango-canción: the song as a small urban short story.","Historians of tango almost unanimously date the tango-canción, the sung, narrative tango, from this recording, which makes it one of those rare records that plainly starts something. The acoustic recording is thin to modern ears, and the guitar sits far back, so it is often admired more than played. Later Gardel recordings show a riper voice, but none has quite the same historical weight.","Hear it in a good transfer and forgive the surface noise. Follow the voice: notice how Gardel stretches a phrase and then catches up, never quite on the beat. Then play one of his mid-1930s film songs, such as Por una cabeza, to hear how far the singer and the genre travelled in under twenty years."],"carlos-gardel-por-una-cabeza":["Por una cabeza, by a head, is a tango about a man who loses at the racetrack and in love in exactly the same way: by a nose, every time, and he keeps betting. Gardel's melody opens with a rising, swaying phrase over a lilting orchestra, then turns darker and more insistent in the second section before swinging back. His voice, by now fuller and polished, sits on top of a small orchestra with violins, guitars and piano.","Gardel wrote the music, with lyrics by his regular partner Alfredo Le Pera, for the Paramount film Tango Bar, made in New York in 1935. By then he was an international film star across Latin America, recording and filming in Paris and New York. A few months later, in June 1935, he died in a plane collision at Medellín, Colombia, and the songs of his final films became the relics of a national grief.","The tune works as well without words as with them, and that is how most of the world now knows it. Notice how the first section keeps climbing by small steps, as if pushing its luck, while the second drops into a minor-key sway that sounds like the moment the horse is overtaken. The racing metaphor is carried by the music itself, not just the lyric.","In Argentina it was always a beloved standard; abroad it had a second life through film, above all the tango scene in Scent of a Woman (1992), and later in Schindler's List and True Lies. That fame has made it a violin encore and a dance-class staple, which some tango purists find reductive. Heard in Gardel's own voice, it recovers its wit and its ruefulness.","Start with Gardel's 1935 recording, then an instrumental version by a violinist, and notice what goes missing when the words go: the bitter joke about the gambler who never learns. Compare it with Mi noche triste to hear the young café singer become a movie star."],"piazzolla-tango-zero-hour":["Seven pieces for Piazzolla's Quinteto Tango Nuevo: bandoneón, violin, piano, electric guitar and double bass. The sound is lean and fierce, with rhythmic accents slammed across the beat, fugue-like passages in which the instruments chase each other, and slow sections where the bandoneón sighs over a single sustained chord. Tanguedia III opens in a blaze of accents; Milonga del ángel is its quietest heart; Contrabajissimo gives the bass a long, singing solo.","Produced in New York by Kip Hanrahan for his small American Clavé label, the record caught Piazzolla's second quintet at its peak, with Fernando Suárez Paz on violin, Pablo Ziegler on piano, Horacio Malvicino on guitar and Héctor Console on bass. By 1986 the battles of the 1950s and 60s, when traditionalists accused him of killing tango, had largely been won abroad, and he was touring Europe and North America as a concert artist.","Behind the street-fighting energy is a conservatory training. Piazzolla studied composition with Alberto Ginastera in Buenos Aires and with Nadia Boulanger in Paris, and the counterpoint here, the lines entering one after another in the manner of a fugue, is Bach learned through Boulanger. Listen for the percussive effects the violinist makes by scraping behind the bridge: a tango orchestra's rhythm section imitated by strings.","Piazzolla himself reportedly called it the best record he ever made, and critics have mostly agreed. It became the entry point to nuevo tango for listeners outside Argentina and helped fuel the boom in classical performers adopting his music. Some tango dancers still object that this is music for listening, not dancing, which is exactly Piazzolla's point.","Start with Milonga del ángel for the melody, then Tanguedia III for the attack. Listen for the violin's squeaks and slides as rhythmic colour, and for the moment in Concierto para quinteto when all five lock into one accent. Then hear his classical-concert pieces such as Adiós Nonino to judge where the border lies."],"elizeth-cardoso-cancao-do-amor-demais":["An LP of songs written entirely by Antonio Carlos Jobim and the poet Vinicius de Moraes, sung by Elizeth Cardoso, then one of Brazil's great samba-canção voices. Most of it is lush and melancholy, arranged by Jobim for strings and piano. But on two songs, Chega de saudade and Outra vez, a young guitarist from Bahia named João Gilberto plays accompaniment in a new, clipped, syncopated way that would soon have its own name.","Festa was a tiny label devoted largely to poetry recordings, and this album was a partnership project: Vinicius, a diplomat-poet, had recently teamed up with the young Jobim for the play Orfeu da Conceição. Rio de Janeiro in 1958 was optimistic, with a modernising president, Juscelino Kubitschek, and a new capital, Brasília, rising in the interior. The record appeared months before Gilberto's own single of Chega de saudade.","Listen past the singer to the guitar on Chega de saudade. Gilberto plucks the bass note with his thumb while his fingers snap out chords slightly off the beat, a compressed version of a whole samba percussion section. Cardoso's big, vibrato-rich voice sits on top of it in the older style, and the mismatch is the sound of one era handing over to the next.","Historians usually call it the first bossa nova record, or at least its prehistory, and it is cited more often than heard. Cardoso's operatic phrasing can sound old-fashioned beside Gilberto's whisper, which is precisely why the comparison is instructive. Its reputation rests on its songs and on those few minutes of guitar.","Play Cardoso's Chega de saudade, then João Gilberto's own single of the same song from a few months later. Same melody, same guitarist, but his voice drops to a murmur and everything else is stripped away. That before-and-after is the clearest lesson in what bossa nova changed."],"joao-gilberto-chega-de-saudade":["Twelve short songs, most under three minutes, in which João Gilberto sings almost at speaking volume while his nylon-string guitar ticks out a syncopated pattern. Jobim's arrangements add a flute here, a few strings there, a brushed snare, never more than the song needs. The title track, Desafinado, Brigas nunca mais and Bim bom set out the whole language: cool voice, warm harmony, rhythm felt rather than hammered.","Odeon released Gilberto's single of Chega de saudade in 1958, reluctantly; one executive was said to dislike it. It became a hit with young middle-class listeners in Rio's beach neighbourhoods, and the LP followed in 1959. Jobim served as arranger and producer-in-all-but-name. Brazil was booming, and this quiet, urbane music, made in apartments rather than samba schools, sounded like the confident new country.","The guitar is doing the work of a whole samba percussion section in miniature: thumb for the surdo bass drum, fingers for the tamborim's off-beat chatter. Meanwhile Gilberto's voice floats slightly ahead or behind the guitar, so the two never quite coincide. Desafinado, meaning out of tune, is a sly answer to critics who called the new style's rich chords and gentle voices unmusical.","Its impact is hard to overstate: within a few years a generation of Brazilian musicians, including Caetano Veloso and Gilberto Gil, said this record decided their lives. Older critics dismissed the whispered singing as weak. American jazz musicians, from Stan Getz to Charlie Byrd, soon borrowed the style. It is now treated as the founding document of bossa nova.","Listen with headphones and ignore the words at first: follow the guitar's thumb and fingers as two separate parts. Then notice how little the voice pushes. After it, hear Getz/Gilberto for the international version, and Miles Davis's Birth of the Cool for the American cool that Rio heard as a kindred spirit."],"joao-gilberto-joao-gilberto-1973":["Often called the white album for its plain cover, this is João Gilberto almost alone: voice, guitar and the faintest percussion. Ten songs, old sambas and new ones, including Jobim's Águas de março, Caetano Veloso's Avarandado and Gilberto Gil's Eu vim da Bahia. Undiú is a wordless drift of hummed syllables. The effect is of a man playing for himself late at night, every phrase weighed to the gram.","Gilberto had lived mainly in the United States and Mexico since the early 1960s, and recorded this in New York. Back in Brazil the military dictatorship was at its harshest, and the bossa nova he founded had been overtaken by Tropicália and MPB, whose leading figures, Caetano and Gil, he now recorded. The album is a sort of reply: the master acknowledging his heirs in his own spare terms.","Listen to how Gilberto slightly rewrites every song's rhythm and harmony, so that a tune you know from another singer arrives subtly re-cut. In Águas de março he sets the famous list of images with no drama at all, letting the descending bass line in the guitar carry the song's sense of things running down. The percussion is barely there, a brush of sound.","Many musicians rank it above his early records, precisely because it is so extreme in its restraint; some listeners find it too hushed to hold them. Its reputation has grown steadily, and it is often named as a favourite by singer-songwriters and producers interested in intimacy and space. It is the late summation of the Gilberto style.","Play it quietly, in a quiet room. Start with Águas de março, then compare Elis Regina and Jobim's duet version from the next year to hear how much drama Gilberto chooses to leave out. Undiú is the track to test whether this music has caught you."],"jobim-wave":["Ten short, mostly instrumental pieces in which Jobim plays piano and guitar against Claus Ogerman's orchestration: soft strings, flutes, a warm trombone, bass and brushed drums. The title track rides a gently rocking melody over rich chords; Triste and Look to the Sky are yearning ballads; Mojave and Captain Bacardi add a little bite. It sounds like the music of a Rio apartment at dusk, arranged with the care of a film score.","Jobim was by then an international name thanks to The Girl from Ipanema. This record was produced in New Jersey by Creed Taylor, who was building the polished CTI sound, with Ron Carter among the players. Taylor and Ogerman made Jobim's harmonies the centre of the record rather than his singing, and it became one of the most played albums of 1960s easy listening and late-night jazz radio.","Jobim loved Debussy and Villa-Lobos, and you can hear both. Ogerman's sustained string chords and the parallel moving harmonies under the tunes come straight out of French impressionism, while the modal melodies owe something to Villa-Lobos's Bachianas. Jobim's own piano is almost shy, often a single-note line; the sophistication is in what moves underneath.","Jazz purists sometimes dismissed it as background music, and it was indeed played everywhere as exactly that. But musicians have always treated its songs, especially Wave and Triste, as serious repertoire, and the album is now valued as the purest statement of Jobim as a composer rather than a pop star.","Listen for the chords under the title track: they keep shifting colour while the melody barely moves. Then play Debussy's Prelude to the Afternoon of a Faun and Villa-Lobos's Bachianas Brasileiras No. 5 aria, and hear where the harmony came from. Finish with Getz/Gilberto for the same composer in song."],"jobim-aguas-de-marco":["The waters of March are the late-summer rains in Rio, and the song is a list: stick, stone, the end of the road, a sliver of glass, life, the sun, night, death, a snare, a fish, one image after another, each over the same short melodic figure. Underneath, the bass keeps stepping downward. The accumulation becomes hypnotic, and by the end the list has turned from scattered debris into a promise of renewal.","Jobim first released it in 1972 on a small disc given away with the satirical Rio weekly O Pasquim, then on his next album. It was written at his country house near Petrópolis during a rainy season, and came in the grimmest years of the military regime, when plain speech was risky. Jobim later wrote his own English version, Waters of March, in which the season shifts, for northern listeners, from the end of summer to the promise of spring.","The melody barely moves; almost all of the drama is in the harmony and in that falling bass line, a device older than Bach. Jobim also wrote the English lyric using mainly words of one syllable, rather than translating, so that the list keeps its tumbling rhythm. Some listeners hear, in the lists of odds and ends, a coded picture of a country under dictatorship.","It has topped at least one major Brazilian newspaper poll of musicians and critics as the greatest Brazilian song, though such polls vary. The best-known version is the 1974 duet with Elis Regina, whose laughter as they sing became part of its legend. Its reputation among songwriters worldwide rests on its structure as much as its tune.","Start with the Elis Regina and Jobim duet, then Jobim's own earlier recording, then João Gilberto's hushed 1973 version. Listen each time for the falling bass line, and notice how the tiny melodic figure becomes something you want never to stop."],"elis-regina-elis-and-tom":["Fourteen Jobim songs sung by Elis Regina, Brazil's most powerful popular singer, with Jobim himself on piano, guitar and occasional vocals. The arrangements, by César Camargo Mariano, add electric piano and a jazz rhythm section to the bossa nova palette. Águas de março, sung as a duet, opens with a playful exchange of lines; Triste, Corcovado, Só tinha de ser com você and Retrato em branco e preto show Elis moving from whisper to full cry.","The album was recorded in Los Angeles in early 1974, a prestige project backed by her label. Jobim, living partly in the United States, was wary of the younger musicians and their electric instruments, and the sessions were tense before they were joyful. Brazil was still under military rule; Elis had been pressured by the regime, and a project with Jobim was a return to a less dangerous, more classic repertoire.","Listen for the moments when Elis nearly laughs during Águas de março: the duet was kept loose, not polished. The tug of war between Jobim's acoustic sound and Mariano's Fender Rhodes is audible throughout, the older master's piano ringing out beside the younger arranger's electric shimmer. That friction gives the record its warmth.","It is widely regarded as one of the best Brazilian albums ever made, and the definitive vocal treatment of Jobim's songbook. Some Jobim devotees prefer the dry João Gilberto approach; Elis's admirers answer that she finds emotions in these songs that no one else does. Its standing has only grown since her death in 1982.","Start with Águas de março, then Retrato em branco e preto for the other side of Elis, dark and dramatic. Compare Getz/Gilberto's cool, and ask which approach you prefer. Then follow Elis into her later, harder repertoire with Como nossos pais."],"elis-regina-como-nossos-pais":["A slow-building song that begins almost spoken over piano and grows, verse by verse, into a full-band cry. Elis Regina sings a young person's bitter realisation that despite all the rebellion, the generation that promised to change everything is ending up living just like its parents. Her voice moves from confiding to furious and back, every syllable given dramatic weight, until the last line lands like an accusation.","The song was written by Belchior, a songwriter from Ceará in the northeast, and Elis made it the centrepiece of her stage show and album Falso brilhante in 1976. Brazil's military regime had begun a slow political opening, but censorship was still active. A song about a generation's disillusion was understood by everyone as being about the dictatorship and the failure of the 1960s dream, without naming either.","Notice how the song's accompaniment stays harmonically simple while Elis does all the work of escalation: the drama is in the singer, not the arrangement. Belchior's lyric also nods to the counterculture's heroes and slogans, but with the tone of someone who no longer believes them. The title's ordinariness, like our parents, is the whole blow.","It became one of Elis's signature performances and one of the anthems of her generation. Belchior's own recording, released the same year, is sparer and more ironic, and some listeners prefer it; most Brazilians know Elis's. Her early death in 1982 gave the song an extra layer of elegy, and it is still covered and quoted in Brazilian political debate.","Listen for the long crescendo: note the moment Elis stops sounding sad and starts sounding angry. Then hear Belchior's own version for the contrast. Compare Chico Buarque's Construção for another song that sneaks politics past the censors through structure. Any live recording from the Falso brilhante tour shows the song at full stretch."],"caetano-veloso-alegria-alegria":["A bright, marching pop song in which the singer walks through the city, against the wind, with no handkerchief and no documents, noticing newspaper headlines, film stars, bombs and Coca-Cola as they pass. Caetano Veloso sings over an electric rock band rather than the acoustic guitars expected of Brazilian song. The tune is simple and catchy; the lyric is a collage of the modern city in the present tense.","Caetano performed it with an Argentine rock group, the Beat Boys, at the 1967 festival of Brazilian popular music on TV Record in São Paulo, a televised contest watched by millions. Electric guitars were a political statement: the nationalist left saw them as American imperialism. At the same festival Gilberto Gil sang Domingo no parque with Os Mutantes. Together the two performances launched the movement soon called Tropicália.","Listen to the list of things the singer sees: images from magazines, cinema and the news appear side by side with equal weight, like a Pop Art canvas. Caetano borrowed the tone from Godard's films and from the Beatles as much as from Brazilian poetry. The song's cheerfulness is deceptive; it is about drifting unprotected through a world of signs, under a dictatorship.","The festival audience initially booed the electric guitars, then took to the song; it finished fourth but became the bigger hit. Nationalist critics called the new movement a sell-out; later historians see it as the moment Brazilian music absorbed rock on its own terms. The song is now a classic of Brazilian pop.","Hear it next to João Gilberto's Chega de saudade, which Caetano revered, to understand the leap: same country, nine years apart, whisper against electric band. Then go straight to the Tropicália ou Panis et Circencis album. Look for footage of the 1967 festival performance, where you can hear the audience turn from jeering to singing along."],"tropicalia-ou-panis-et-circencis":["A manifesto in the form of an LP: twelve songs by Caetano Veloso, Gilberto Gil, Gal Costa, Os Mutantes, Tom Zé and Nara Leão, bound together by Rogério Duprat's orchestral arrangements. The styles collide deliberately, northeastern baião, sentimental bolero, psychedelic rock, church hymn and radio jingle. Panis et circencis is sung by Os Mutantes and ends in a staged dinner party; Baby, sung by Gal Costa, is a soft ode to consumer culture; Geléia geral piles Brazilian clichés into a comic heap.","Recorded in São Paulo in 1968, the year the military regime issued Institutional Act No. 5, suspending civil liberties. By December, Caetano and Gil were arrested, and they were later pushed into exile in London. The album's idea, drawn from the 1920s poet Oswald de Andrade's Cannibalist Manifesto, was that Brazil should devour foreign culture, from the Beatles to the avant-garde, and digest it into something its own.","Listen for Duprat, a conservatory-trained composer who had studied the European avant-garde; he slips orchestral quotations and deliberate kitsch into the arrangements, including snatches of familiar classical tunes. The cover, a family-portrait parody with Caetano holding a photograph of Nara Leão and Gil holding a picture of Capinam, echoes Sgt. Pepper's group costume while mocking Brazilian bourgeois respectability.","It was divisive: the left accused it of Americanisation and frivolity, the right distrusted its irreverence. Since the late 1990s, with reissues and David Byrne's advocacy, it has been celebrated internationally as one of the great albums of the psychedelic era. In Brazil it is treated as a founding cultural document, as much studied as listened to.","Take it as a whole, like a film. Start with Panis et circencis to hear the joke land, then Baby for the melting sweetness. Pair it with Sgt. Pepper and with Os Mutantes' own debut; then read a little about Oswald de Andrade's cannibalism metaphor, which unlocks the record."],"os-mutantes-os-mutantes":["The debut of a São Paulo trio, the brothers Arnaldo Baptista and Sérgio Dias with singer Rita Lee, and a riot of ideas: fuzz guitars, harpsichord, tape tricks, orchestral swells, a bossa nova turned inside out. Panis et circencis appears again, this time in their own version; Bat macumba builds a chant syllable by syllable; A minha menina, a Jorge Ben song, is garage rock with a samba heart. The voices are young, playful and unafraid to sound silly.","Os Mutantes were the house band of Tropicália, backing Gilberto Gil and Caetano Veloso on television and on record in 1967 and 1968. Rogério Duprat arranged the orchestral parts. Sérgio Dias's brother Cláudio built their guitars and effects devices at home, because imported gear was scarce and expensive under the import rules of the day. They made records in Brazil's studios with a lab-experiment attitude.","Much of the strange sound comes from homemade equipment and studio accidents deliberately kept. Listen, too, for how often they send up both Brazilian and Anglo-American clichés at the same time: a sentimental bolero phrase sung with a straight face, then broken by a sound effect. Bat macumba mixes Afro-Brazilian religious language with the Batman TV theme.","In Brazil they were a TV sensation and a critical puzzle; abroad they were barely known until the 1990s, when musicians such as Kurt Cobain and Beck praised them and a 1999 compilation on David Byrne's Luaka Bop label revived their name. They are now cited as one of the most inventive psychedelic bands anywhere.","Start with Panis et circencis, then compare it with the version on the collective Tropicália album. Listen for the home-built guitar tones and the moments where tape is simply cut. Pair it with Revolver to hear what they took and what they added."],"caetano-veloso-transa":["Seven long, loose songs, mixing English and Portuguese, played by a small band that sounds as if it is in the room. You Don't Know Me opens in an easy groove; Nine Out of Ten drifts through London street life; Triste Bahia sets a seventeenth-century Brazilian poem over berimbau and chanting; It's a Long Way stretches out for six minutes, stitching fragments of other songs together. The mood is homesick, curious, alert.","After their arrest in December 1968, Caetano and Gilberto Gil were forced into exile, and Caetano spent 1969 to 1972 in London. Transa was made there, with guitarist Jards Macalé leading the band, and released after his return to Brazil. London then was full of rock, and also of new Jamaican music; Caetano absorbed both while writing about displacement and a Brazil he could not reach.","In Nine Out of Ten Caetano mentions hearing reggae on Portobello Road, often cited as the first reference to reggae in Brazilian music. Triste Bahia sets lines by the Baroque satirist Gregório de Matos, a 1600s poet who also mocked colonial Bahia; the band is quoting four centuries at once. It's a Long Way threads in snatches of Beatles and Brazilian folk material.","At the time it was a modest seller, but its reputation has grown to the point that many Brazilian critics call it Caetano's best album. Listeners abroad, discovering it later, hear it as a bridge between Tropicália's collage and a more intimate singer-songwriter style. The live, unpolished sound is now considered a virtue.","Listen straight through, preferably on a remaster that keeps the room sound. Start with Triste Bahia and follow the berimbau. Then play Joni Mitchell's Blue or Bob Dylan's Blonde on Blonde, and João Gilberto's white album from the next year, which records one of these songwriters' songs."],"gilberto-gil-expresso-2222":["Gilberto Gil's homecoming record, bright with acoustic guitar and northeastern rhythms. The album brings in the fifes and drums of the Banda de Pífanos de Caruaru, rural northeastern music heard rarely on a pop record, then moves into Back in Bahia, a joyful song of return, the jaunty title track about a train to the future, and Oriente, a long meditative guitar piece. Gil's guitar playing, percussive and supple, is everywhere.","Gil, like Caetano, was arrested in 1968 and spent roughly three years in London, where he played with rock musicians and heard everything from Hendrix to reggae. Expresso 2222 was recorded soon after he came home to Brazil in 1972, still under dictatorship. It is the sound of a musician rejoining his own traditions, northeastern forró, baião and samba, with new ears.","Listen to how Gil's acoustic guitar playing borrows from the zabumba drum and triangle of northeastern forró, strumming patterns that become a whole percussion band. Chiclete com banana, bubblegum with banana, is a slyly political song about mixing Brazilian and American culture, arguing that the exchange should go both ways.","It was well received in Brazil and is now counted among his classic records. Critics abroad came to it later, through world-music reissues. Gil went on to serve as Brazil's Minister of Culture from 2003 to 2008, and this album is often cited as the essential statement of his fusion of northeastern roots and global pop.","Start with Back in Bahia, and follow Gil's guitar like a drummer. Then compare Caetano's Transa, made in London at the same moment, to hear the two exiles' different moods: Caetano homesick, Gil coming home. Then hear the collective Tropicália album where they began."],"chico-buarque-construcao":["Ten songs of controlled anger and tenderness, framed by Rogério Duprat's orchestral writing. The title track tells the last day of a construction worker who falls from a building, in a single long, relentless sentence over a pounding samba rhythm and swelling brass and strings; the song then repeats its story with the words shuffled. Cotidiano is a gentle, trapped portrait of daily routine; Deus lhe pague offers bitter thanks.","Chico Buarque had left Brazil for Italy after 1968 and returned in 1970 to a country at its most repressive under President Médici. His songs were routinely censored, and he became a master of double meaning. Construção was a huge seller, and its title song, written about a labourer's anonymous death during the so-called Brazilian economic miracle, was understood as an indictment of the regime's development without justice.","In Construção nearly every line ends on a word stressed on its third-last syllable, a rare shape in Portuguese that gives the song its tumbling, mechanical gait. In the second telling, those end-words are swapped among the lines, so the worker's day is rebuilt from the same bricks in a new order, and the meaning shifts: the song itself is constructed like a building.","Critics immediately recognised it as one of Brazil's great albums, and it has kept that rank. The title track is studied in Brazilian schools for its form. Some listeners find Duprat's orchestra overwhelming; others hear it as part of the argument, the machine that crushes the man. The album secured Chico's place as the conscience of Brazilian song.","Listen to the title track with a translation, and then again without, noticing only the rhythm of the line-endings and the orchestra's build. Compare Jobim's Águas de março, another list song, and Elis Regina's Como nossos pais for politics carried by structure."],"milton-nascimento-clube-da-esquina":["A double album by Milton Nascimento and the young Lô Borges with a circle of musicians from Belo Horizonte. Milton's high, church-trained voice floats over songs that mix Minas Gerais folk and Catholic processional music, Beatles harmony, jazz chords and progressive rock. Tudo que você podia ser opens with rolling guitars; Cais is a slow, oceanic ballad; O trem azul is sunny pop; San Vicente and Nada será como antes are darker and more ambitious.","Clube da Esquina, the corner club, was the nickname for a group of friends who met on a street corner in Belo Horizonte, including Lô and Márcio Borges, Beto Guedes, Toninho Horta, Wagner Tiso and the lyricist Fernando Brant. Released by EMI-Odeon in Rio in 1972, it came from the interior state of Minas Gerais, far from the Rio-São Paulo centres of bossa nova and Tropicália, and brought a new regional sound into Brazilian pop.","Listen for the harmonic language: Milton and Toninho Horta use chords that move in unexpected, modal ways, closer to Jobim's impressionism and to church music than to rock. The famous cover photo of two boys sitting by a dirt road does not show the musicians; it was a snapshot of local children, chosen for its feeling of a rural childhood.","It sold modestly at first but is now regularly voted among the greatest Brazilian albums, sometimes at the very top. Jazz musicians, notably Wayne Shorter, who recorded Native Dancer with Milton in 1975, spread his name abroad. Critics discuss it alongside Pet Sounds and Abbey Road as a landmark of studio pop songwriting.","Take it in two sittings, one disc at a time. Start with Cais and Tudo que você podia ser, and listen for the choir-like voices. Then compare Abbey Road for the Beatles connection, and Villa-Lobos's Bachianas Brasileiras No. 5 for a classical Brazilian vocalise with the same soaring feeling."],"machito-tanga":["A big-band piece in which a Cuban rhythm section, congas, bongos, timbales and a repeating bass pattern, drives while trumpets and saxophones play jazz-harmonised riffs and open up for improvised solos. It has no song in the usual sense: a vamp, a set of layered horn figures, a building intensity. The feeling is of two separate musics, Cuban dance and swing-era jazz, locking into one groove for the first time.","Tanga was composed by Mario Bauzá, trumpeter and music director of Machito and his Afro-Cubans, the New York band led by the singer Frank Grillo, known as Machito, Bauzá's brother-in-law. It grew out of a jam at a club in 1943. Bauzá had played in Chick Webb's and Cab Calloway's bands and knew jazz from the inside; Machito's band played for dancers across Harlem and midtown.","Listen to how the horn riffs are written on top of the clave, the two-bar Cuban rhythmic key, rather than on top of swing: the jazz harmonies are there, but the rhythm is Cuban at root. This band's fusion is the origin of the Afro-Cuban jazz that Bauzá's friend Dizzy Gillespie would take to Carnegie Hall with Chano Pozo, whom Bauzá introduced to him, in 1947.","Tanga is widely described as the first Afro-Cuban jazz composition, though such firsts are always debatable. The band was a hit with dancers and admired by jazz musicians; Charlie Parker later recorded with it. Historians now credit Bauzá and Machito as architects of Latin jazz and, through the mambo era, of salsa.","Listen first to the rhythm section alone, then add the horns in your ear. Then play Gillespie's Manteca and hear the same idea in bebop clothes. Later, Tito Puente's Dance Mania shows where it went in the dance halls. Any compilation of the Afro-Cubans from the 1940s will do; the drums matter more than the fidelity."],"tito-puente-dance-mania":["Twelve dance numbers, mambos, cha-cha-chas and guarachas, played by Tito Puente's big band with the singer Santos Colón. Brass sections punch out riffs over a dense percussion engine led by Puente's own timbales, and the pianist hammers a repeating montuno pattern. El cayuco and Complicación are among the best known. Everything is tight, precise and built for dancers, yet full of jazz harmony and fierce solos.","Puente, born in Spanish Harlem to Puerto Rican parents, trained at the Juilliard School after wartime navy service and ruled the Palladium Ballroom in midtown Manhattan, the temple of the 1950s mambo craze, alongside Machito and Tito Rodríguez. RCA Victor issued Dance Mania at the height of that craze, and it became his best-selling album and a standard for Latin bandleaders.","Listen to the brass voicings: Puente arranged like a jazz composer, stacking trumpets in close, bright dissonances borrowed from Stan Kenton and Gillespie. And listen to the timbales: Puente's fills between sections are not decoration but cues to the band and the dancers, a drummer conducting from behind. His solos are short and structural.","It is frequently named the most important Latin album of the 1950s. Later salsa musicians studied its arrangements closely. Carlos Santana's 1970 cover of Puente's Oye como va, a later song, spread his name to rock audiences. Dance Mania's reputation rests on its sheer craft. Musicians still copy its charts note for note in rehearsal rooms from San Juan to Tokyo.","Put it on and move. Then listen for the three layers: percussion, piano montuno, brass riffs. Compare Machito's Tanga and Gillespie's Manteca for the jazz side, and Celia Cruz's work with the Fania musicians for where this sound went next."],"fania-all-stars-live-at-the-cheetah":["Two volumes recording one August night in 1971 at the Cheetah club in Manhattan, where Fania Records assembled its stars into a single band: Johnny Pacheco leading, Ray Barretto on congas, Willie Colón on trombone, Larry Harlow on piano, and singers including Héctor Lavoe, Cheo Feliciano, Ismael Miranda and Pete El Conde Rodríguez trading verses. Long jams, Anacaona, Quítate tú, build through improvised vocal calls and brass mambos to crowd-roaring peaks.","Fania, founded in 1964 by the Dominican flautist Johnny Pacheco and the lawyer Jerry Masucci, was selling Cuban-rooted dance music to New York's Puerto Rican and Latin communities under a new marketing word, salsa. The concert was filmed for Leon Gast's documentary Our Latin Thing, shown in 1972, which carried the scene to cinemas across Latin America. The club was packed, and the heat is audible.","Listen to the soneos, the improvised lines each singer throws in between the chorus's repeated refrain: they are competing, name-checking each other and the crowd, and the chorus answers every time. Quítate tú, meaning get out of the way so I can take over, is literally about this: a ritual of friendly rivalry that is the heart of salsa.","It is generally seen as the founding live document of the salsa era, and helped turn a New York label into an international movement. Some Cuban musicians and critics argued that salsa was simply Cuban son and mambo rebranded; salseros answered that the New York barrio had made it something new. The debate still runs.","Start with Quítate tú and count the singers. Listen for the moment when the brass cuts in with a new riff, the mambo section, and the crowd lifts. Then hear Tito Puente's Dance Mania for the big-band parent, and Siembra for the thinking-person's salsa that came next."],"celia-cruz-celia-and-johnny":["Celia Cruz, the great Cuban singer, joins Johnny Pacheco's conjunto, a band of trumpets, piano, bass and hand percussion rooted in Cuban son rather than big-band mambo. Her voice is enormous, contralto-dark and rhythmically exact, commanding the band. Quimbara opens with a torrent of rhythmic syllables; Toro mata draws on Afro-Peruvian music; Vieja luna slows the tempo for a bolero-flavoured lament.","Cruz had sung with the Sonora Matancera, Cuba's most famous band, through the 1950s, left Cuba in 1960 after the revolution and never returned. Her career in the United States had stalled until Fania's owners paired her with Pacheco on their Vaya label. This album brought her to the young salsa audience and made her its queen. She had been singing professionally for more than twenty years.","Listen to Quimbara: the lyric is largely drum-syllables, the voice imitating percussion, and Cruz treats her singing like another drum in the section. Her rhythmic placement, slightly ahead of the beat and then sharply on it, is what makes the band feel faster than it is. Her famous shout of azúcar, sugar, is said to have begun as a joke about how she took her coffee.","It was a hit and turned Cruz from nostalgic figure to present star; it is regularly listed among the most important salsa albums. Critics praise the pairing of her Cuban vocal authority with the leaner Dominican-led conjunto. Her later career, into her seventies, confirmed her as the defining voice of Latin dance music.","Start with Quimbara and follow her against the percussion. Then play a 1950s Sonora Matancera track to hear her younger voice, and her late La vida es un carnaval to hear the same power at seventy-three. Notice, too, how rarely the trumpets play at the same time as her voice: the arrangements clear space for her."],"celia-cruz-la-vida-es-un-carnaval":["A buoyant salsa anthem urging the listener not to weep over hardship because life is a carnival, and it is better to sing through it. Celia Cruz's voice, in her seventies, is still enormous. The song starts slowly, almost as a spoken exhortation over piano, then kicks into full band with brass and a chorus that answers her every line, building toward a jubilant finale.","The song was written by the Argentine songwriter Víctor Daniel and recorded for Cruz's album Mi vida es cantar, released in 1998 on the RMM label. Salsa by then had softened into romantic ballad-style salsa, but Cruz stayed with the harder, brass-driven Cuban tradition. She had been in exile nearly four decades, and her audience included three generations. It was a late triumph in a career of five decades.","The song's optimism is doubled by its singer's biography: a Cuban exile who could never go home, singing that there is no need to cry. Listen for the phrasing on the chorus's key words; Cruz leans on the vowels and bends the rhythm in a way that no younger salsa singer of the 1990s did. The slow introduction makes the first full band hit feel like sunrise.","It became her most famous late song, a fixture at parties, funerals and protests across Latin America, and one of the most played Spanish-language songs of its decade. After her death in 2003, it took on the role of an epitaph. Critics note that it is simpler than her 1970s work; audiences have not minded.","Wait for the full band to enter, and feel how the chorus lifts. Then go back to Quimbara from Celia & Johnny, twenty-four years earlier, to hear what age added and what it took away. Watch a live performance from her last years to see what her audiences saw: a party with a matriarch in charge."],"hector-lavoe-el-cantante":["A ten-minute salsa epic about a singer who makes everyone happy on stage while nobody asks about his own sorrow. It opens with a slow, string-laden introduction, almost a film overture, before the band surges into a hard-swinging groove. Héctor Lavoe's nasal, sweet, cutting voice carries the verses, then launches into improvised lines over a repeating chorus, and trombones blaze in the mambo sections.","The song was written by Rubén Blades and arranged by Willie Colón for Lavoe's 1978 Fania album Comedia. Lavoe, born in Ponce, Puerto Rico, had come to New York at seventeen and become the voice of Willie Colón's band before going solo. By 1978 he was salsa's most beloved singer and struggling with addiction; the song's picture of the performer's hidden pain was read as his portrait.","Blades wrote it and gave it away, and it fits Lavoe so well that the gift became part of the legend. Listen to the strings of the introduction: they set up a theatrical frame, a curtain opening, before the salsa band enters. In the improvised section Lavoe turns the song into a direct conversation with his audience, the singer about the singer.","It became Lavoe's signature and, after his death in 1993, his epitaph; the 2006 biopic starring Marc Anthony took its title. Salsa historians regard it as one of the high points of the Fania era. Some find the introduction overblown; most hear it as essential theatre. Live versions from his later concerts show the song taking on more weight each year.","Let the introduction play out; it is the point. Then listen for the change of gear when the band arrives, and count the minutes of improvisation. Follow with Siembra to hear Blades's own voice and the songwriting behind this song."],"ruben-blades-siembra":["Seven songs by the Panamanian singer-songwriter Rubén Blades with Willie Colón's trombone-heavy band. Plástico opens with a mock disco beat before snapping into salsa, satirising status-obsessed consumer life and calling for Latin American unity. Pedro Navaja tells in cinematic detail the story of a street hustler and a sex worker on a New York corner. Buscando guayaba, Juan Pachanga and the title track, sow, complete a record that thinks as it dances.","Credited to Willie Colón and Rubén Blades, Siembra was released by Fania in 1978 and became, by most accounts, the best-selling salsa album of its time. Blades, a law graduate from Panama who had worked in Fania's mailroom, brought the literate, political storytelling of Latin American nueva canción into New York dance music, then dominated by love songs and party tunes.","Pedro Navaja borrows its idea, and its opening, from Kurt Weill and Bertolt Brecht's Mack the Knife in The Threepenny Opera, transplanted to a Manhattan street. Listen for the sound effects, a car, a siren, at the end: salsa as radio drama. Plástico's disco-style introduction is a joke on the dance fashion that was then threatening salsa's audience.","It was a commercial and critical landmark, and is often cited as the album that gave salsa a political and literary conscience, sometimes called salsa consciente. Some dancers found the long narrative songs too wordy. It remains among the most acclaimed Latin albums, and Blades later ran for president of Panama.","Start with Plástico for the argument, then Pedro Navaja for the story; read a summary of the lyric first. Then play Weill's Threepenny Opera to hear the source. Héctor Lavoe's El cantante, written by Blades the same year, completes the picture."],"buena-vista-social-club-buena-vista-social-club":["Fourteen songs of Cuban son, bolero and danzón played by veteran Havana musicians, many then in their seventies and eighties: the singer and guitarist Compay Segundo, the crooner Ibrahim Ferrer, the pianist Rubén González, the singer Omara Portuondo and guitarist Eliades Ochoa. Chan chan, with its four-chord loop and laconic voices, opens it. The sound is warm and roomy, acoustic instruments in a big studio, with Ry Cooder's slide guitar threaded in.","The project began when a planned session pairing Malian and Cuban musicians fell through because the Malians could not travel. Producer Nick Gold of World Circuit, arranger Juan de Marcos González and Ry Cooder instead gathered older Cuban players at Havana's EGREM studios in March 1996. The United States embargo made Cooder's participation legally awkward. Wim Wenders' 1999 documentary made the musicians international stars.","Listen to the tres, the small Cuban guitar with doubled strings that gives son its metallic, chiming lead. The album's title comes from a members' social club in the Havana suburb of Marianao that had closed decades earlier; the name was a memory, not a venue. Many of the players had been unrecorded or forgotten for years.","It won a Grammy in 1998 and became one of the best-selling world-music albums ever made. Some Cuban critics complained that it froze Cuba in a pre-revolution nostalgia for foreign consumption, while contemporary Cuban musicians were making different music. Others see it as a genuine rescue of a generation's art.","Start with Chan chan, then Dos gardenias for Ferrer's crooning and a Rubén González piano solo. Then watch the Wenders film. For contrast, hear Celia Cruz's New York salsa, the same roots transplanted and electrified. Then seek out Ibrahim Ferrer's own solo album, made soon after, which continues the story."],"violeta-parra-las-ultimas-composiciones":["The last album Violeta Parra released, and the one containing Gracias a la vida, her hymn of thanks for the senses, for sound and sight, for the alphabet and the paths of the feet, all of which give her both laughter and tears. Elsewhere Volver a los diecisiete celebrates the renewal of love; Run run se fue pa'l norte tells of a lover's departure; Rin del angelito is a lament for a dead child in Chilean folk ritual. The sound is spare: voice, strings, a few friends.","Parra had spent two decades collecting rural Chilean folk song, painting, and making embroidered tapestries shown at the Louvre in 1964. By 1966 she had set up a performance tent in the La Reina district of Santiago, which struggled to draw audiences. She recorded this with her children Isabel and Ángel Parra and the Uruguayan musician Alberto Zapicán. She took her own life in February 1967.","Gracias a la vida is built on repetition: each verse begins with the same thanks, then names a different gift, so the song becomes a litany. Its gentle strummed pattern is played in a way that almost hides how unusual the harmony is. Knowing the singer's state of mind at the time turns the gratitude into something much more complicated.","Barely noticed commercially at first, Gracias a la vida became, especially through Mercedes Sosa's version, one of the most widely sung Latin American songs. Parra is now regarded as the mother of the nueva canción movement. Scholars debate how to read the late songs in light of her suicide; many resist reducing them to a farewell.","Listen to Gracias a la vida and notice the rough, unprettified voice; then hear Mercedes Sosa's version and compare the two singers' emotional temperatures. Continue with Víctor Jara, whom Parra inspired. Then return to the album's quieter songs, such as Rin del angelito, which show how deeply her writing is rooted in rural Chilean ritual and verse forms."],"mercedes-sosa-gracias-a-la-vida":["Mercedes Sosa sings Violeta Parra's song of thanks for life, for the senses, for sound, sight and love, with a deep, measured contralto over guitar and the soft thud of the Argentine bombo drum. Where Parra's original is rough and intimate, Sosa's is broad and dignified, a slow processional that grows with each verse. It sounds less like a confession than a prayer spoken on behalf of a crowd.","Sosa recorded it on her 1971 album Homenaje a Violeta Parra, four years after Parra's death. She had helped launch the Argentine Nuevo Cancionero movement in 1963, committed to folk music with a social conscience. In 1978, under Argentina's military junta, she was arrested on stage in La Plata and soon went into exile in Europe; she returned in 1982, and this song became a centrepiece of her homecoming concerts.","Listen to the bombo legüero, the large Argentine drum she often played herself: its low pulse gives the song the tread of a march or a procession. Sosa slows Parra's tempo and holds the long notes, so the list of gifts becomes monumental. Heard after her exile, the thanks became a political statement about surviving.","It is the version most of the world knows and one of the anthems of Latin American democracy movements. Some prefer Parra's frailer original as more personal; Sosa's admirers answer that she turned a private song into a public one. After her death in 2009, Argentina held three days of national mourning.","Listen twice, first to Sosa, then to Parra, and notice what each voice makes of the same words. Then hear Sosa's Alfonsina y el mar for her tender side, and Joan Baez's version of Gracias a la vida for the North American folk-revival connection."],"mercedes-sosa-alfonsina-y-el-mar":["A zamba, the slow Argentine folk dance in a lilting triple time, sung by Mercedes Sosa with piano. The song imagines the poet Alfonsina Storni walking into the sea, drawn by its voices and creatures, seen tenderly as an entry into a dream rather than a death. The melody rises and falls like a swell; Sosa sings it with a restraint that makes the image almost unbearably gentle.","The music is by the pianist-composer Ariel Ramírez, the words by the historian Félix Luna, for their 1969 concept album Mujeres argentinas, portraits of notable Argentine women sung by Sosa. Storni, a celebrated modernist poet, drowned herself at Mar del Plata in 1938 while ill with cancer. Ramírez was also the composer of the Misa Criolla, a folk-mass that had made him famous.","Listen to the piano's rocking figure: it is both the zamba's dance step and the motion of waves. The lyric never names suicide; it describes a walk, a sleep, a conversation with the sea, and the tact is the point. The rhythm, a triple metre that sometimes feels like two, gives the song its floating, unsettled quality.","It became one of Sosa's signature songs and one of the most covered Argentine songs, recorded by singers and classical guitarists alike. Some critics find later orchestral versions over-sweetened; Sosa's plain piano versions remain definitive. It has become a standard of Latin American repertoire, sung in concert halls as well as folk clubs, and Storni's own poetry has gained readers from it.","Hear it in Sosa's original with piano. Listen for the waves in the accompaniment. Then compare Gracias a la vida to hear her public voice, and Villa-Lobos's Bachianas Brasileiras No. 5 aria for another South American song of longing. Read one of Storni's poems before listening."],"victor-jara-te-recuerdo-amanda":["A short, quiet song for voice and fingerpicked guitar. Víctor Jara remembers a young woman, Amanda, running through the rain to meet Manuel at the factory gate for five minutes during his break; one day Manuel goes to the mountains and does not come back. The melody is simple and tender; Jara's voice is plain, warm and unhurried, and the tragedy is told without a single raised note.","Jara, a theatre director as well as a singer, was a central figure of Chilean nueva canción, following Violeta Parra. The song appeared on his 1969 album Pongo en tus manos abiertas, as Chile moved toward the 1970 election of Salvador Allende, whom Jara supported. Amanda and Manuel were the names of his parents. After the military coup of 11 September 1973, Jara was arrested, tortured and murdered in a Santiago stadium.","The song never explains why Manuel went to the mountains, where guerrillas fought, and the omission is its force. Listen to the guitar: a gentle, rocking pattern that barely changes, like the repetition of daily routine, which makes the missing return all the more final. The five minutes in the lyric are a working man's whole allotment of love.","It is one of the best-loved songs in Latin America and has been recorded in many languages. After Jara's murder, the stadium where he died was renamed after him in 2003, and in 2023 a Chilean court convicted former army officers of his killing. His songs are now sung as memorials to all victims of the dictatorship.","Listen once with a translation, then once without, following only the guitar. Then hear Violeta Parra's Gracias a la vida for the tradition he inherited, and Pete Seeger or Woody Guthrie for the North American folk singers he admired. Any of his original recordings is best."],"juan-luis-guerra-bachata-rosa":["Juan Luis Guerra and his band 4.40 mix two Dominican forms: the fast, accordion-and-drum merengue and the guitar-led bachata, a lovelorn song style from the countryside. Burbujas de amor is a slow bachata full of whimsical romantic images; Como abeja al panal and the title track glow with close vocal harmony. The merengue tracks, including La bilirrubina, a joking song about lovesickness as a medical condition, drive at full speed.","Guerra, from Santo Domingo, studied at Berklee College of Music in Boston, where he absorbed jazz harmony and vocal-group arranging. Bachata was then a looked-down-on music of the rural poor and urban migrants, played in bars and often banned from radio. Bachata rosa, polished and poetic, brought it into middle-class homes across the Spanish-speaking world. The timing was right: Latin pop was booming.","Listen to the vocal harmonies: Guerra borrowed the close-harmony style of the Manhattan Transfer and jazz vocal groups and grafted it onto rural Dominican forms. His lyrics, full of surreal metaphors, draw on Latin American poetry rather than bar-room plain speech. That combination of Berklee craft and bachata's heartache is the whole innovation.","The album sold in the millions and won a Grammy for best tropical Latin album. Traditional bachateros sometimes felt it gentrified their music; others credit it with finally legitimising the style, which in later decades became a global pop genre. Guerra is now regarded as the Dominican Republic's most important modern songwriter.","Start with Burbujas de amor and listen for the guitar's plucked, bell-like lead. Then La bilirrubina for merengue's speed. For the older, rougher bachata, look for recordings by rural guitarists of the 1970s and compare. Then play Siembra for a very different kind of literate Latin dance record."],"umm-kulthum-enta-omri":["A single love song that lasts about an hour. It opens with a long orchestral introduction, a small overture in itself, before Umm Kulthum enters with a line that she will then repeat, bend and stretch many times over. The shape is not verse and chorus but a chain of sections, each one returned to whenever the audience demands. The sound to recognise it by is the massed violin section playing in unison, a wall of strings moving as one voice.","Cairo, 1964. Umm Kulthum and the composer Mohammed Abdel Wahab were the two giants of Egyptian music and had never worked together; the pairing was widely credited to the encouragement of President Nasser, and the press called it the meeting of the summits. Ahmed Shafik Kamel wrote the words. It was premiered at one of her monthly Thursday concerts, broadcast live across the Arab world by radio, when streets from Casablanca to Baghdad reportedly went quiet.","Abdel Wahab was a modernist who loved Western dance bands and film music, and he brought that taste into her ensemble: listen for Western instruments and harmonised passages in the introduction, which purists heard as a provocation. Then notice how little of the song is fixed. Each recorded performance differs, because the singer lengthened whichever phrase made the hall cry out, and the orchestra had to follow her.","It became the most famous Arabic song of the century and remains the one people name first. Contemporary critics argued over whether Abdel Wahab had diluted her classical style with Western colour; the collaboration succeeded so well commercially that the argument was quickly buried. Today scholars read it as the high point of the golden age of Egyptian song, and as proof that tradition and modern orchestration could share one stage.","Do not start with a three-minute excerpt; the whole point is duration. Put on a full live recording, let the overture pass as you would a symphony's first movement, and listen for the audience. Their cries after a phrase are part of the music, the signal that she has reached tarab, the shared state of rapture. Then compare Al-Atlal to hear her with a more traditional composer."],"umm-kulthum-al-atlal":["The title means The Ruins, and the song looks back over a dead love as a traveller looks over an abandoned campsite. Riad Al Sunbati's setting is grave and spacious: long instrumental passages, a slow opening in which the voice seems to walk among the stones, then faster, more urgent sections as memory becomes accusation. The recognisable sound is the qanun, the plucked zither, answering every phrase of the voice with a shimmer of its own.","Premiered in 1966, the song set verses by the Egyptian poet Ibrahim Nagi, who had died in 1953, with some lines drawn from another of his poems. Sunbati was the composer most associated with Umm Kulthum's classical side, and the poem draws on the oldest image in Arabic verse, the lover weeping at the ruins of a beloved's camp. After the defeat of 1967 many listeners heard the song's loss as political as well as personal.","Listen for the way Sunbati moves between maqamat, the Arabic melodic modes, as the poem changes mood. Each shift brings different intervals, including notes that fall between the keys of a piano, so the ground under the melody literally changes colour. The celebrated closing section, where the lovers are asked to part, is set far more briskly than its sorrowful words suggest; the contrast is deliberate.","Many Arab critics and listeners rank it above Enta Omri as her supreme performance, precisely because it is less modern: classical poetry, a classical composer, maqam at full depth. Its line about freedom was chanted by protesters long after her death. Scholars debate how far the political reading was intended; the text predates 1967 by decades, but performances after the war gave it new weight.","Listen with the translation beside you, reading the poem's arc before you hear it. Then notice how the audience responds when a line is repeated: each repetition is a different ornament, a different emphasis, never a copy. If you know the lament tradition in Western song, from Dido to Schubert, you will recognise the stance of looking back, rendered here through a wholly different craft."],"abdel-halim-hafez-qariat-al-fingan":["The Fortune Teller of the Cup: a woman reads the coffee grounds in a young man's cup and tells him of a love he will never reach. Mohammed al-Mougy's setting unfolds as a long dramatic scene rather than a song, with an orchestral prelude, recitative-like passages and soaring refrains. Abdel Halim's voice is lighter and more intimate than Umm Kulthum's, closer to a crooner's, and that warmth against the vast orchestra is the sound to recognise.","The words are by the Syrian poet Nizar Qabbani, the most popular love poet of the Arab world. Abdel Halim, the Nightingale of the Nile, was by 1976 the idol of a generation, a film star and the voice of Nasser-era optimism, but he was also gravely ill from the schistosomiasis he had caught as a village child. This was among his last great concert pieces; he died in London in 1977.","Qabbani's poem is a lament disguised as a prophecy: the fortune teller's details about the beloved, locked away in a guarded palace, can be read as a portrait of love forbidden by class and custom. Listen too for moments where the tempo quickens toward a dance rhythm, then collapses back; the orchestra keeps pulling him toward hope and letting go.","The song became inseparable from the singer's death, and later listeners hear its fatalism as autobiography. A much-told concert episode, in which he rebuked part of the audience for whistling and heckling, remains one of the famous moments of Egyptian stage history, though accounts differ on details. Critics generally place it with his finest late work, the moment the romantic film idol became a tragic figure.","Begin with a live concert recording rather than a studio cut, since the audience's rising excitement is part of the form. Listen for the change in his voice when the fortune teller delivers the final verdict. Then play Enta Omri straight after: two styles of Cairene grandeur, one monumental and one confiding."],"fairuz-zahrat-al-madaen":["The Flower of Cities is a hymn to Jerusalem. Where Egyptian song stretches out for an hour, this is compact and dramatic: bells, a choir, then Fairuz's clear, cool voice, almost without vibrato, singing of the city's streets, churches and mosque. The middle rises to an anthem-like climax with the full chorus, then returns to prayer. The instant marker is that voice, pure and still, sitting above a large ensemble without ever straining.","Written by the Rahbani Brothers, Assi (Fairuz's husband) and Mansour, and released in 1967 in the aftermath of the Six-Day War, when Israel took East Jerusalem. The Rahbanis had built a new Lebanese song: short forms, European harmony and orchestration mixed with Levantine folk and Byzantine chant. Fairuz, a Christian singer beloved by Muslims and Christians alike, made the song a shared lament across the Arab world.","Listen for the church-bell sound and the choral writing, which borrow from the Eastern Christian liturgy Fairuz grew up singing, placed alongside references to the Al-Aqsa mosque; the song is deliberately built to belong to both faiths. And notice the brevity. The Rahbanis were cutting Arab song down to radio length at the very moment Umm Kulthum was stretching it further.","It became the standard musical expression of Arab grief over Jerusalem and is still played on anniversaries. Some critics find its anthem style too public for Fairuz's intimate gift; others regard it as the Rahbanis' most perfect synthesis. The Rahbani project itself divided opinion: traditionalists accused it of Westernising Arab music, while supporters credited it with making a modern Lebanese identity in sound.","Listen first for the voice alone, then for the texture behind it: chorus, bells, strings, each entering as the city is described. Compare it with Al-Atlal from the year before, where time and repetition do the work; here it is concentration. Then follow Fairuz forward to Li Beirut, her song for her own city in ruins."],"fairuz-li-beirut":["For Beirut: a love song to a city being destroyed. The melody will sound familiar to classical listeners, because it is the slow movement of Joaquín Rodrigo's Concierto de Aranjuez, with the cor anglais line given to Fairuz's voice. The arrangement keeps the guitar's strummed opening and the aching, rising phrase, but the tempo slows and the voice sits low and grave. It is the gesture people know it by across the Arab world.","Arranged by Ziad Rahbani, Fairuz's son, with words by the poet Joseph Harb, during the Lebanese civil war, which ran from 1975 to 1990 and tore Beirut apart. Fairuz stayed in the city through much of the war and refused to sing for any faction, which made her a rare shared figure. Ziad, a composer and satirist steeped in jazz, belonged to a harder, more ironic generation than his father's.","Rodrigo wrote his Adagio in 1939, just after the Spanish Civil War, and many have heard grief for a ravaged country in it. Whether or not Ziad Rahbani meant the echo, the borrowing carries one civil war's lament into another's. Listen also for the Andalusian flavour of the original melody, with its bending half-steps, which sits naturally in an Arab voice.","It became one of Fairuz's most loved songs and a civil-war anthem sung by all sides of a divided city. Some listeners in Europe first encountered it as a curiosity, a pop song on a classical tune; in Lebanon it is anything but. It has since been covered widely and is often played at memorials, most recently after the 2020 port explosion.","Play Rodrigo's Adagio first, then this. Notice what changes when an instrumental line acquires words and a single voice: the melody slows, the ornaments drop away, and the tune becomes speech. Listen to how the guitar remains as a memory of Spain beneath a Lebanese song."],"khaled-didi":["A dance song built on a rolling, heavily accented groove, bright synthesizer riffs and Khaled's voice: nasal, powerful, full of ornamental turns and long melismas that break through the pop production. The words, in Algerian Arabic, are a teasing plea to a girl. The track's instant marker is the chanted hook of the title, repeated like a playground call, over a beat that sits between North African rhythm and Western club music.","Khaled grew up in Oran, the port city where raï had developed out of rural Bedouin song in the bars and weddings of the early twentieth century. Raï was frowned upon by religious conservatives and by the Algerian state, and as the civil war of the 1990s approached, several raï figures were threatened or murdered. Khaled had moved to France in the late 1980s; this record, produced in Los Angeles by Don Was, was his international breakthrough.","Behind the modern production, the vocal line keeps the melodic habits of old raï and the older Bedouin song beneath it: short phrases circling a few notes, with sudden leaps and quavering ornaments. The dropped 'Cheb', meaning young man, from his name on this album was deliberate, a move away from the raï scene's youth branding toward international stardom.","It was a hit across France, the Arab world, and far more surprisingly India, and it remains the most famous raï song worldwide. Some raï fans regard it as a glossy compromise and prefer the rawer cassette recordings of the 1980s. It was banned in parts of the Arab world and criticised by Islamists, which in its time gave the playful song an unintended political edge.","Listen once for the beat and the hook, as a dance record. Then listen again only for the voice, and hear how much of an older, freer vocal art survives inside a four-minute pop song. For the roots, go to Cheikha Rimitti, the woman whose songs gave raï its outlaw reputation."],"cheikha-rimitti-sidi-mansour":["The grandmother of raï, recorded in her seventies with rock musicians. Rimitti's voice is rough, low and commanding, delivering short melodic cells over and over with small changes, as a village singer would at a wedding. Around her the band plays loose, driving grooves while the gasba, a reed flute, and the frame drum keep the older sound in view. The marker is her voice itself, hoarse and unhurried, owning every track.","Born in 1923 in western Algeria, Rimitti sang in the bars and festivals of the Oran region from the 1940s, and her frank songs about love, drinking and women's lives earned her notoriety and banning. She settled in France and was rediscovered in the 1990s as the source of the music that had made Khaled famous. This album paired her with the guitarist Robert Fripp and the bassist Flea, among others.","Her songs are not written in the Western sense; they are assembled from formulaic lines and refrains she had sung for decades, reshaped each time. Many younger raï singers recorded her songs as their own, and one of the pleasures here is hearing the original owner reclaim her material. Listen for how the rock rhythm section is made to follow her phrasing, not the other way round.","Critics welcomed it as overdue recognition; some purists found the Western rock backing awkward and preferred her acoustic recordings with gasba and drums alone. It helped establish her late international career, which continued until her death in Paris in 2006. Her place as the fountainhead of modern raï is no longer disputed.","Start with the title track and listen to how little the melody moves; the energy comes from repetition and from the grain of the voice. Then turn to Khaled's Didi, recorded two years earlier by a singer who grew up on her songs, to hear what the younger generation kept and what it polished away. Seek out her sparser early recordings too."],"rachid-taha-ya-rayah":["A cover of an Algerian standard about emigration, addressed to someone leaving for foreign lands who will return with nothing. The arrangement opens with a plucked mandole and a swaying string riff, then locks into an insistent rhythm under Taha's rasping, half-spoken voice. The recognisable sound is that circular string line, part Cairo film orchestra and part dance loop, which repeats like a tide.","The song was written by Dahmane El Harrachi, a master of chaabi, the urban popular music of Algiers, and he recorded it in the early 1970s. Taha, born in western Algeria and raised in France, had led the rock band Carte de Séjour in Lyon in the 1980s. This version appeared on his 1997 album Diwân, produced by the guitarist Steve Hillage, which reworked Algerian and Egyptian classics.","The subject is the life of North African immigrant workers in France, Taha's own family story, which makes the cover a kind of return address. Chaabi itself descends in part from the Andalusian music brought to Algiers by refugees from Spain, so the lament of leaving home rests on older layers of exile. Listen for that Andalusian turn in the melody's cadences.","It became a huge hit in France and the Arab world, and the song's revival carried El Harrachi's name to a new generation. Taha's style, which fused punk attitude with Arab tradition, was criticised by some traditionalists as disrespectful; he later won even more attention with a cover of The Clash's Rock the Casbah. He is now remembered as a key voice of the French-Algerian experience.","Hear El Harrachi's own recording first, a single voice with a small ensemble, then Taha's. The tune is identical; everything else, from the beat to the swagger, is new. Follow it with Khaled's Didi to hear two routes from Algeria into French pop."],"master-musicians-of-jajouka-pipes-of-pan":["Field recordings from a hill village in northern Morocco: massed rhaitas, double-reed pipes played with circular breathing, shrieking in close intervals over relentless drumming. There are no songs in the pop sense, only long blocks of sound that build and shift. Brian Jones added studio effects, phasing and echo, which make the sound swim. The marker is the rhaita chorus, loud and piercing enough to carry across valleys.","Jones, the Rolling Stones' founder, visited Jajouka in 1968, guided by the painter and writer Brion Gysin, who had long championed the musicians. The recordings were made during the rites associated with Boujeloud, a goat-skinned figure whom the Western visitors linked to the Greek god Pan. Jones died in 1969; the album came out in 1971 on the Stones' own label, with a sleeve full of psychedelic colour.","The Pan connection was largely the visitors' romantic theory; the villagers' own tradition ties the music to a local Sufi saint and to a pre-Islamic festival whose meaning is debated. Listen for the drums' interlocking patterns, which keep the pulse moving while the pipes seem to hover free of it. The studio effects mean this is as much Jones's psychedelic collage as a document.","It was one of the first albums of non-Western traditional music issued by a rock star, and it helped create the audience that would later be called world music. Ethnomusicologists have criticised the exotic framing and the treatment; the musicians' own group has since split into rival ensembles over the name. Still, the record introduced a whole generation to North African trance.","Play it loud and do not try to follow melodies; follow the pulse and the changes of density. Then hear Ornette Coleman's Dancing in Your Head, where he recorded with these musicians in 1973, and notice how much his harmolodic freedom has in common with the pipes' hovering."],"andalusi-nuba-moroccan-ala":["A nuba is a suite: many songs in a single melodic mode, arranged in movements that begin slowly and accelerate. A performance opens with unmeasured instrumental preludes, then the ensemble sings strophic poems in unison, with the violins played upright on the knee and the rabab, a bowed boat-shaped fiddle, giving a nasal edge. The sound to recognise is the choral unison, many men singing one line, and the steady quickening toward the end.","Tradition traces the form to Ziryab, a musician who came from Baghdad to Córdoba in the ninth century. As Muslims and Jews were driven out of Spain, the repertoire travelled to Fez, Tetouan, Tlemcen, Algiers and Tunis, each keeping its own branch. In Morocco the surviving texts were gathered in the late eighteenth century in a songbook associated with Muhammad al-Ha'ik; eleven nubas survive there, fragments of a legendary twenty-four.","Many of the poems are muwashshahat, a strophic form invented in al-Andalus, whose final lines were sometimes in the Romance vernacular of Spain rather than Arabic. So the same courts that made this music produced some of the earliest lyric fragments in a Spanish tongue. Listen for rhythmic cycles that stay constant within a movement while the melody drifts over them.","Scholars debate how much of today's nuba goes back to Andalusia and how much was rebuilt in North Africa over centuries; the Ziryab story is at least partly legend. What is undisputed is the continuity of transmission, teacher to pupil, across five hundred years. Conservatories in Fez and Rabat now teach it formally, and the repertoire is also sung in North African Jewish communities.","Choose a single nuba by a Fez or Tetouan orchestra and let it run through from the first prelude to the fast finale. Then listen to the Cantigas de Santa Maria and Mozarabic chant, heard in the same peninsula at the same time, and to flamenco's cadences: all of them share a memory of this music."],"anouar-brahem-thimar":["A trio with no drums: oud, reeds and bass, playing slow, spacious pieces that sit between Arab art music and chamber jazz. The oud states a modal theme; John Surman answers on soprano sax or the woody bass clarinet; Dave Holland's bass walks, drones or sings. There is a great deal of air in the sound. The marker is the bass clarinet and oud together, two low, dark instruments in quiet conversation.","Brahem, born in Tunis in 1957, trained in the Arab classical tradition before turning to his own compositions. He began recording for Manfred Eicher's Munich label ECM in 1991, whose clean, reverberant production became part of his signature. Surman is English and Holland played on Miles Davis's electric records; the album brought North African maqam into a European jazz world.","The oud's tuning and phrasing keep the Arabic modes intact, including notes between the Western semitones; Surman and Holland adapt to it rather than the other way around. Listen for how rarely the three play at full volume together. Brahem's writing often gives each instrument its own rhythmic cycle, so the pieces seem to float rather than march.","It was well received in the jazz press and helped make Brahem the best-known oud player in the West. Some Arab listeners regard his ECM work as a softened, Europeanised form of their music; admirers answer that he never pretends to be traditional. His later records, especially Le pas du chat noir, extended the approach.","Listen late at night, at low volume. Follow one instrument through a whole track and notice where it steps back for the others. Then compare Paco de Lucía's trio with McLaughlin and Di Meola: another encounter between Mediterranean tradition and jazz, but loud and fast where this is slow and hushed."],"nusrat-fateh-ali-khan-shahen-shah":["Qawwali, the devotional song of the Sufis of Pakistan and North India, performed by the tradition's greatest modern singer and his party. Each piece starts quietly with harmonium and a sung invocation, then the rhythm enters with tabla and handclaps and the song climbs toward ecstatic climaxes. Nusrat's voice is the marker: enormous range, and cascades of rapid improvised syllables, the sargam, in which he names the notes at dizzying speed.","Nusrat came from a family of qawwals in Faisalabad with a lineage going back centuries. Qawwali is sung at the shrines of Sufi saints to bring listeners toward a spiritual state of rapture. After his appearance at the WOMAD festival in 1985, Peter Gabriel's Real World label recorded him in England; this was among the first of those albums, aimed at listeners with no Urdu or Punjabi.","The rapid solfège passages come from Hindustani classical practice, not from qawwali proper, and Nusrat's use of them in devotional song was his innovation. Listen for how a single line of mystic poetry is repeated dozens of times; the repetition is the method, since each return is meant to strip away the listener's resistance. The claps are part of the rhythm section, not applause.","Western audiences were overwhelmed, and the records made him the first South Asian voice with a global following. Some in Pakistan objected that he made sacred music into entertainment; he answered that it was taken to new listeners. Critics now agree that his studio albums are best heard alongside live recordings, where the pieces stretch to half an hour.","Start at the beginning of a long track and do not skip; the payoff is at the end. Listen for the moment the whole party joins the refrain and the tempo lifts. Then set it beside a Hindustani raga performance and hear what he borrowed."],"nusrat-fateh-ali-khan-mustt-mustt":["Nusrat's voice set within Western production by the Canadian guitarist and producer Michael Brook. The songs are shorter than traditional qawwali, carried by ambient guitar textures, bass lines and programmed beats, while his improvisations soar over the top. The title track, built on a chanted refrain in praise of the Sufi saint Lal Shahbaz Qalandar, is the one people know.","Recorded for Real World after Nusrat's appearance on Peter Gabriel's soundtrack for The Last Temptation of Christ. Brook had developed an infinitely sustaining guitar and a taste for drifting textures, and the sessions were built by layering. Soon after release the Bristol group Massive Attack remixed the title track into a trip-hop single, which reached club audiences who had never heard of qawwali.","Brook recorded Nusrat's vocal improvisations and edited them around new backing, so some passages are assembled rather than performed live. Listen for how the refrain of the title track still follows the qawwali call-and-response form, the party answering its leader, even inside a studio loop. The devotional text, praising a saint, is unchanged by the dance-floor setting.","It is often cited as a founding album of fusion between South Asian tradition and electronic music, and it influenced the Asian Underground scene in Britain. Qawwali purists find it thin beside his live work. Nusrat went on to collaborate with Eddie Vedder and to score film; his death in 1997 at 48 cut short the arc.","Hear the title track twice: first the album version, where Brook's guitar drifts under the voice, then the Massive Attack remix, where a heavy slow beat takes over and the chanted refrain becomes a hook. Then play a traditional qawwali recording of the same saint's praise with harmonium and handclaps. Three settings, one voice; for the full art, go back to the concert albums."],"amalia-rodrigues-barco-negro":["Black Boat: a woman on a beach waits for a lover lost at sea, insisting he is still alive. The rhythm is unusual for fado, a slow, insistent pulse drawn from the Brazilian original, and over it Amália's voice rises with sharp, sobbing ornaments. The sound to recognise is the Portuguese guitar, a pear-shaped twelve-string instrument, weaving bright countermelodies around the voice.","The song adapts Mãe Preta, a Brazilian song about an enslaved nursemaid; the Portuguese censor of Salazar's dictatorship reportedly found the original subject unacceptable, and the poet David Mourão-Ferreira supplied new words. Amália sang it in the French film Les Amants du Tage in 1955, which made her an international star. She was already the queen of fado in Lisbon, and the film took her to Paris, New York and beyond.","The drumlike rhythm that sets this song apart from most fados comes from its Afro-Brazilian source, a reminder that fado itself emerged in Lisbon in the early nineteenth century partly from Brazilian dances brought home across the Atlantic. Listen for the moment the voice holds a note while the guitar keeps moving; that is where she shapes the lament.","It became one of the most covered fados, and the story of its censorship has become part of its legend, though details vary between accounts. Some fado purists considered Amália's film career a betrayal of tradition; she is now recognised as having redefined the art for the twentieth century.","Listen to the pulse first, which is the song's engine, then to how she accents the words over it. Then hear Mãe Preta in its Brazilian form, and compare her later Busto album, which is more poetic and less cinematic."],"amalia-rodrigues-busto":["Known by its cover, a photograph of a sculpted bust of the singer. The songs are by the French-born composer Alain Oulman, set to serious poetry, and they change fado from within: longer melodic lines, darker harmonies, more space for the voice to speak. Amália sings with restraint, holding back the sob until it counts. The marker is that combination of guitar-and-voice intimacy with the gravity of art song.","Lisbon, 1962, under the Estado Novo dictatorship. Fado had been promoted by the regime as a national symbol, and Amália was accused by some of serving it. Oulman, a composer from a family of Jewish bankers, was himself later arrested by the secret police for opposition activity. His settings brought poets such as David Mourão-Ferreira and Pedro Homem de Mello, and older poets as well, into the fado house.","Among the songs is Abandono, often called the Fado de Peniche, about a prisoner; Peniche was the fortress where political prisoners were held, and listeners understood the reference without anyone saying so. Listen for how Oulman's melodies sometimes avoid the expected fado cadence, holding a phrase open where tradition would close it.","Traditionalists attacked Oulman's music as not true fado at all; within a decade it had become the standard for serious fado singing, and the album is now regarded as her masterwork. The question of Amália's politics has been reconsidered since her death: evidence of her quiet support for opponents of the regime has emerged, and she is honoured in the National Pantheon.","Read the poems in translation before you press play, so the words are already in your head. Then listen for her pauses: the silence before a line is where she does her acting, and the guitars wait for her. Start with Povo que lavas no rio. Compare Barco Negro to hear the film star, and Carlos Paredes for the Portuguese guitar alone."],"carlos-paredes-guitarra-portuguesa":["Instrumental pieces for the Portuguese guitar, the twelve-string instrument that accompanies fado, played here as a solo voice. Paredes plays with ferocious speed and rhythmic drive, fast repeated notes ringing like a mandolin, and then with sudden, grave lyricism. A second guitar keeps the harmony. The best-known piece, Verdes Anos, written for a film, is the melody that many Portuguese know by heart.","Paredes, from a Coimbra family of guitarists, worked for years as a hospital clerk. In 1958 he was arrested by the secret police for his Communist sympathies and spent more than a year in prison, and he lost his job. His music is not fado song but a modern concert art built on it, and in Portugal under the dictatorship it carried an unspoken sense of dignity and resistance.","Listen for the strumming patterns and ringing open strings, which come from the Coimbra school of fado, older and more academic than Lisbon's. Paredes often phrases across bar lines, so the melody seems to lean forward. He is said to have practised silently in prison, fingering pieces on his arm; the story may be embellished, but it fits.","It was recognised at once as a landmark; after the 1974 revolution Paredes became a national figure, and many consider him the greatest Portuguese instrumentalist of the century. He was reluctant to record and published few albums, which adds to their weight. Some critics compare his status to Paco de Lucía's in flamenco.","Begin with Verdes Anos, then go to a fast piece and just listen to the right hand. Compare with Albéniz or Tárrega for the classical guitar's route to Iberian song, and with Amália to hear the same instrument as accompanist."],"antologia-del-cante-flamenco":["An anthology on several LPs of flamenco song forms, each performed by a singer with a single guitar: soleares, siguiriyas, tonás sung without accompaniment, fandangos, malagueñas, bulerías and many more. The effect is encyclopaedic, a map of the whole family. The recognisable sound is the guitarist Perico el del Lunar's crisp accompaniment framing voices that crack, rasp and wail across long melismas.","Recorded in Madrid and released in 1954 by the French label Ducretet-Thomson and Spain's Hispavox. Flamenco had become dominated by commercial staged shows, and the project set out to document the older, deeper song forms at risk of vanishing. Perico el del Lunar chose the singers and the repertoire; the scholar Tomás Andrade de Silva wrote the notes. It revived the ideal behind Falla and Lorca's 1922 Concurso de Cante Jondo.","Several of the singers were not famous stars but respected artists who knew specific older styles, and some forms were reconstructed from memory. That makes the anthology partly an act of scholarship and partly an act of invention. Listen for the tonás, sung with no guitar at all, which sound closest to chant and to the Andalusi and Jewish melodic traces flamenco carries.","It won major prizes in France and became the reference collection for the flamenco revival of the 1950s and 1960s; many later singers learned forms from it. Scholars have since noted that it presents a particular, somewhat purist view, underrating the Gitano family traditions of Jerez and Utrera. It remains the starting point.","Do not play it straight through. Take one form at a time: a soleá, then a siguiriya, then a bulería, and listen for how the rhythm cycles differ. Then hear Falla's El amor brujo, whose songs imitate exactly these forms."],"camaron-la-leyenda-del-tiempo":["The great flamenco singer of his age, with his heart-torn, sandpaper voice, set among rock and jazz instruments: electric bass, keyboards, drum kit and, on one track, a sitar. Several songs set poems by Federico García Lorca. The guitar of Tomatito remains central, and the rhythms are still flamenco's own, but the sound is something new. The title track, with its slow bass-led groove, is the marker.","Spain in 1979, four years after Franco's death, was opening to every kind of experiment. The producer Ricardo Pachón gathered Camarón, then known through his classic duo records with Paco de Lucía, with young Andalusian rock musicians. Lorca, murdered by Nationalists in 1936, had been only partly accepted under Franco; singing him was itself a statement.","The song Volando voy is by the rock musician Kiko Veneno and became a flamenco standard. The Lorca texts come from his plays and his poems about Gitano Andalusia, closing a circle with the poet who had championed cante jondo in 1922. Listen for the way the drum kit plays around the flamenco rhythm cycle rather than on a rock backbeat.","It sold poorly at first and was rejected by many purists, who reportedly returned copies to shops. Within a decade it was recognised as the founding record of nuevo flamenco, and it is now widely named as the most important flamenco album. Camarón's early death in 1992, at 41, made him a legend.","Start with the title track and let the bass guide you; then play a traditional Camarón recording with Paco de Lucía to hear the voice undressed. Compare Crumb's Ancient Voices of Children, another setting of Lorca from a very different world."],"paco-de-lucia-entre-dos-aguas":["A rumba, flamenco's cheerful Cuban-derived form, played as an extended guitar improvisation over a steady two-chord vamp. Bongos and an electric bass lay down a loose, danceable groove while Paco's guitar races through ornate runs, rapid-fire picking and the percussive rasgueado strums of the right hand. The marker is the bass ostinato and the sense of a jam session: a flamenco track you can dance to.","Recorded for the album Fuente y caudal in 1973, reportedly almost by accident, when the record was short and the band improvised to fill it. Paco, born in Algeciras in 1947, was already the most admired flamenco guitarist of his generation. Released as a single, it became a surprise hit, one of the best-selling instrumental records in Spain, and brought flamenco guitar to listeners far outside the tradition.","The bongos and electric bass were almost unheard of in flamenco then; this track opened the door for the percussion and bass that became normal in later flamenco. Listen for the picado runs, alternating fingers striking single notes at speed, which Paco pushed to a precision few guitarists had approached. The title means Between Two Waters.","Purists dismissed it as commercial, a flamenco guitarist playing to the pop charts. Its success, however, gave Paco the money and the freedom for everything experimental he did afterwards. Guitarists now treat the track as a technical study, transcribed and copied note for note, and critics mark it as the moment flamenco guitar became an international solo voice in popular music rather than an accompaniment to song and dance.","Listen once for the groove, as you would any dance record. Then listen again only for the guitar's right hand, and try to follow a single run from start to finish; it is harder than it sounds. Afterwards play his Falla album, which turns the same technique toward a composer, and Camarón's records with him, where the guitar returns to serving a voice."],"paco-de-lucia-interpreta-a-manuel-de-falla":["Falla's music, chiefly from El amor brujo, arranged for Paco's flamenco group. The Ritual Fire Dance, the Pantomime and the songs become guitar pieces, with flamenco singers and palmas taking the place of orchestra and mezzo-soprano. The effect is a homecoming: themes Falla drew from flamenco returned to the players. The marker is the Ritual Fire Dance's trills and hammering chords, now struck on guitar strings.","Falla had written El amor brujo in 1915 for the Gitana dancer Pastora Imperio, and in 1922 he co-organised the Concurso de Cante Jondo in Granada to defend flamenco's old song forms. Paco, by 1978, was the tradition's most famous instrumentalist and a composer in his own right, and the album was a tribute that also claimed Falla as one of flamenco's own.","Listen for how Paco turns Falla's orchestral textures into strummed rasgueados and rhythmic palmas: every sustained string chord in the original becomes a percussive pattern. Where Falla imitates a guitar in the orchestra, the arrangement simply hands the line back to one, which reveals how guitar-like Falla's thinking was.","Classical listeners found it surprising and flamenco listeners found it scholarly; both camps gradually warmed to it. Some critics feel the arrangements lose Falla's harmonic subtlety; others hear them as proof of the music's roots. It is now seen as an important bridge between concert and flamenco worlds.","Play Falla's own El amor brujo first, ideally a recording with a flamenco-trained mezzo-soprano, then this. Listen for what disappears, the orchestral colour and the harmonic shading, and what appears, the stamping rhythm of dance. Then hear Miles Davis's Sketches of Spain for a third reading of Spanish sources, this time through jazz harmony and Gil Evans's orchestra."],"paco-de-lucia-friday-night-in-san-francisco":["A live concert by three acoustic guitarists: Paco de Lucía, John McLaughlin and Al Di Meola. They play duets and trios in which flamenco, jazz and rock-fusion technique collide, trading breakneck runs and quotations. The atmosphere is a friendly contest, with the audience cheering each exchange. The marker is Mediterranean Sundance, a Di Meola and de Lucía duet built on a rumba rhythm.","Recorded at the Warfield Theatre in San Francisco in December 1980 and released in 1981. McLaughlin had led the Mahavishnu Orchestra and the Indian-jazz group Shakti; Di Meola had come from Chick Corea's Return to Forever. Paco had been drawn to jazz since the 1970s, and this tour gave flamenco a vast new audience in the jazz and rock world.","Each guitarist has a different rhythmic mother tongue: Paco's is the flamenco compás, McLaughlin's the swing and Indian cycles, Di Meola's the straight rock beat. Listen for moments where they briefly lock and then pull apart. There are also comic quotations of familiar tunes; the jokes are part of the show.","It became one of the best-selling acoustic guitar albums ever made, and a legend among guitarists. Some critics find it too much about speed and competition; others hear it as joyful. Paco's own later groups took the jazz lesson further, with flute, sax and bass.","Choose one player and follow him through a whole track, then play it again and switch to another. Listen especially for Paco's rasgueado bursts, the fanned strums of the right hand, which neither of the others can make. Afterwards hear Shakti with John McLaughlin, a gentler cross-cultural encounter, and Anouar Brahem's Thimar for the quiet opposite of this contest."],"enrique-morente-omega":["A flamenco singer and a Granada noise-rock band, Lagartija Nick, setting Lorca's Poet in New York and songs by Leonard Cohen. Morente's voice, schooled in the oldest cante forms, rides over distorted guitars, heavy drums and flamenco guitars. The sound is harsh, grand and often thrilling. The marker is the collision in the first minutes: a flamenco cry against a wall of rock guitar.","Morente, from Granada, was a scholarly singer who knew the old forms thoroughly and also sought out avant-garde encounters. Lorca had written Poet in New York during his visit to the city in 1929 and 1930, poems of alienation. Leonard Cohen revered Lorca and had named his daughter after him; the album brought the circle together.","Cohen's songs, including Take This Waltz, itself Cohen's version of a Lorca poem, are sung in Spanish, so Lorca returns to his own language via a Canadian. Listen for the way Morente keeps the melismatic style of cante jondo even when the rhythm is a rock beat. The album is long, and deliberately unrelenting.","It was initially met with confusion and hostility from both flamenco and rock audiences. It is now regarded as one of the key Spanish records of the 1990s and has been reissued and restaged. Critics place it with La leyenda del tiempo as a turning point in flamenco's modern history.","Start with the Leonard Cohen songs, which you may already know in English, and listen for how Morente bends their melodies into flamenco ornament. Then work back to the Lorca settings, where the rock band is at its loudest. Compare Camarón's La leyenda del tiempo to hear how far the experiment travelled in seventeen years, and Crumb's Lorca settings for a concert-hall answer."],"markos-vamvakaris-frangosyriani":["A love song to a Catholic girl from the island of Syros, sung by Markos Vamvakaris in his cracked, gravel voice over his own bouzouki. The rhythm is the hasapiko, a steady, lilting dance, and the bouzouki plays spare melodic figures between verses. The marker is the rough, unpolished sound of voice and bouzouki together, closer to a tavern than a studio.","Markos, born on Syros in 1905, worked in Piraeus as a butcher and dockworker and learned the bouzouki in the hashish dens of the port. Rebetiko, the urban music of the Greek underclass, had absorbed the Anatolian music of refugees expelled from Turkey after 1922. His recordings from the 1930s were the first to make the bouzouki the centre of the style.","The girl is Catholic because Syros had a long-established Catholic community, a detail that marks the song as local and specific. Listen for the bouzouki's melodic figures, which use modes the rebetes called dromoi, close cousins of Turkish makams and Arab maqamat. The rough recording quality is part of the tradition's character.","It is one of the most famous rebetiko songs and still widely sung. Under the Metaxas dictatorship of the late 1930s rebetiko was censored for its drug references and Eastern sound; Markos's reputation recovered in the revival of the 1960s, and he is now called the patriarch of rebetiko.","Listen first for the bouzouki between the verses, then for Markos's phrasing: he often half-speaks the words rather than singing them, like a blues singer. The rough sound is not a flaw. Then compare Tsitsanis's Cloudy Sunday to hear the style refined a decade later, and hear an old recording of Misirlou for the Anatolian modes both men inherited."],"tsitsanis-synnefiasmeni-kyriaki":["Cloudy Sunday: a slow zeibekiko, the heavy, limping dance in nine beats, in which the singer compares the grey day to his own heart. The melody is simple and grave, with the bouzouki carrying the introduction and the voices entering in a mournful unison. The marker is the dragging, uneven rhythm and the bouzouki's clear, bell-like tone.","Vassilis Tsitsanis, born in Trikala in 1915, wrote the song during the German occupation of Greece, in Thessaloniki, and recorded it in 1948 once the recording industry had revived. He refined rebetiko's rough tavern sound into something more melodic and widely acceptable, bringing it to a mass audience. Greece was then in the middle of its civil war.","Written under occupation, the song's grey Sunday was heard as a veiled picture of the occupied country. Listen for the zeibekiko's nine beats, grouped unevenly, which give the song its reeling gait. The dance was traditionally danced solo by a man, improvised and introspective, not for an audience.","It became perhaps the most famous rebetiko song and a kind of unofficial anthem of the occupation years. Tsitsanis is credited with making rebetiko respectable; some scholars argue he smoothed away its rawness, while others see him as its greatest composer. Many later singers have recorded the song.","Try counting the nine beats as you listen, feeling how they fall into uneven groups that make the dance lurch and recover. Then find a film of a zeibekiko being danced, arms spread, eyes down. Compare Markos's Frangosyriani to hear the rougher earlier style, and a Turkish folk song in an uneven rhythm to hear its Anatolian cousins."],"misirlou-1927":["The title means Egyptian girl, and the song is a love song to one. Its melody, built on a mode with an augmented second, the wide, exotic-sounding gap that Western ears hear as Middle Eastern, climbs and falls in a slow, swaying dance. The earliest known recording is a Greek version made in the United States in 1927, sung with a small ensemble.","The song circulated among Greek, Arab, Jewish and Armenian musicians in the Eastern Mediterranean and in the immigrant communities of New York. Its origin is unknown; several claims of authorship exist. Recorded first as a Greek rebetiko by Tetos Demetriades, it was later arranged for big band and taken up by Jewish klezmer and belly-dance musicians.","In 1962 the surf guitarist Dick Dale, of Lebanese descent, recorded a thunderous electric version, and in 1994 Quentin Tarantino used that version at the start of Pulp Fiction. So one Eastern Mediterranean mode crosses from a New York rebetiko session to Californian surf and a Hollywood title sequence. Listen for the augmented second in each version.","It is among the most widely travelled melodies of the twentieth century, and the arguments over its origin remain unsettled. Scholars use it as a case study in how songs move between communities. Its ubiquity in film has made it a cliché of exotic sound, which some critics see as a loss of its real history.","Collect three versions: an early Greek recording from the late 1920s, an Arab or klezmer version with clarinet or violin, and Dick Dale's surf guitar from 1962. Listen for the wide augmented-second step in each, the same interval played on bouzouki, clarinet and a reverb-drenched electric guitar. Then compare Rimsky-Korsakov's Scheherazade, a European fantasy built on similar sounds."],"itri-segah-tekbir":["The tekbir is the proclamation of God's greatness, sung in Islamic worship. Itri's setting is in the makam segâh, a mode built around a note that sits between the Western semitones, which gives it a floating, suspended quality. The melody is sung in unison by male voices, slowly, with long melismatic phrases. The marker is that central note, neither major nor minor, around which the melody circles.","Buhurizade Mustafa Itri (c. 1640–1712) was the leading composer and theorist of the Ottoman court in Istanbul, in the age of Sultan Mehmed IV. He wrote secular and sacred works, many of which survive only in oral tradition. This tekbir is still sung in mosques across Turkey at the Eid festivals, and it is one of the oldest continuously performed pieces in the Ottoman repertoire.","Listen for the segâh note itself: it sits roughly a quarter-tone below a Western E natural and is the reason the mode cannot be played on a standard piano. The melody moves in a very narrow range, so small changes in pitch carry great expressive weight. It is transmitted orally; written versions came much later.","Itri is regarded as the central figure of Ottoman classical music, and this tekbir as his best-known work, so familiar in Turkey that many people who never attend a concert know it by heart. Scholars debate how accurately his pieces have survived three centuries of oral practice, and some attributions to him are doubtful. It remains part of living worship rather than concert repertoire, which is itself unusual for music this old.","Hear it sung by a mosque choir during Eid if you can; otherwise find a recording by a Turkish religious ensemble. Listen for the central note and how it seems to hover. Then compare Gregorian chant and Scelsi's Quattro pezzi, other music built on one pitch's nuances."],"dede-efendi-ferahfeza-ayin":["An âyin is the music for the Mevlevi ceremony known in the West as the whirling of the dervishes. It unfolds in four sections, the selams, each slower and then faster, while the dervishes turn. The ney, an end-blown reed flute, opens with an improvised prelude, then voices sing Rumi's poetry over the small kettle drums. The marker is the ney's breathy, hollow tone, which the Mevlevis hear as the soul longing for God.","Hamamizade İsmail Dede Efendi (1778–1846) was the leading Ottoman composer of his time and a Mevlevi dervish himself, active at the court of Mahmud II during a period of Westernising reform. The Mevlevi order, founded after Rumi's death in 1273, used music as a path to God. This âyin, probably one of his late works, uses the makam ferahfeza.","Listen for the rhythmic cycles: the drums play long patterns, some many beats long, that repeat through each section. The ney's pitch bends, controlled by the angle of breath, give it the microtonal intervals of makam. The four selams correspond to stages of the soul's journey in Mevlevi thought.","Dede Efendi's âyins are considered the high point of the Mevlevi repertoire. After 1925 the Turkish Republic closed the Sufi lodges, and the ceremony survived as a cultural performance; scholars debate how the music changed as it moved from worship to stage. It is now inscribed as intangible heritage.","Watch a film of the ceremony first, so you can picture the white skirts turning, then listen to the music alone with your eyes closed. Follow the ney through its opening improvisation and notice how the drums enter beneath it. Then compare qawwali, another Sufi music, which drives toward ecstasy where this one aims at stillness."],"tanburi-cemil-bey-recordings":["Improvised preludes, the taksims, and composed pieces played on the tanbur, a long-necked lute, and on the kemençe, a small bowed fiddle. The playing is fast, fluent and emotionally direct, moving through makam after makam with extraordinary control of the microtones. The recordings are crackly 78s; the voice of the instrument still comes through. The marker is the kemençe's sharp, singing tone.","Tanburi Cemil Bey (1873–1916) lived through the last decades of the Ottoman Empire in Istanbul and was the most celebrated instrumentalist of his age. European gramophone companies had set up in the city to record local music for local buyers, and he made many sides for them in the years just before the First World War. He was also a theorist and wrote a treatise on Ottoman instruments; he died young, in 1916.","Listen for the microtonal inflections in the taksims: Cemil Bey slides between pitches that fall between Western semitones, the heart of the makam system. His improvisations on these records were widely copied and memorised by later players. He also played the yaylı tanbur, a bowed version of the lute, which he popularised.","These recordings are now considered among the most important documents of Ottoman classical music, and players still learn his taksims by ear from them. They are also a lesson in what early recording preserved and lost: the three-minute limit of a 78 forced him to compress improvisations that in a salon might have run far longer. Their survival owes much to collectors and to careful modern reissues.","Choose a kemençe taksim first, since the bowed fiddle's singing line is the most direct way in. Then try a tanbur piece and listen for the long decaying notes. Compare Hindustani raga recordings of roughly the same era, and then Partch or La Monte Young, Western composers who went looking for the notes between the piano keys that Cemil Bey took for granted."],"asik-veysel-uzun-ince-bir-yoldayim":["I Am on a Long and Narrow Road: a philosophical song in which life is a journey between two doors, walked day and night. Veysel sings in a plain, steady voice, accompanying himself on the bağlama, whose strummed and plucked strings drone under the melody. The melody is simple and repeated for each verse. The marker is the bağlama's buzzing ring and the singer's calm, unhurried delivery.","Âşık Veysel Şatıroğlu (1894–1973), blind from childhood smallpox, came from a village near Sivas in central Anatolia. The âşıks are wandering minstrel-poets, heirs to a tradition going back centuries. In the 1930s and 1940s the Turkish Republic promoted folk culture, and Veysel taught at the Village Institutes; this song, probably composed earlier, became famous through recordings around mid-century.","The imagery of the road between two doors, birth and death, comes from the Alevi and Sufi traditions that shaped Anatolian folk poetry. Listen for the bağlama's frets, which include microtonal positions allowing the makam intervals. Veysel's steady rhythm is closer to recitation than to song.","It is one of the most famous Turkish folk songs, taught in schools and covered by rock, pop and jazz musicians. Veysel is regarded as the greatest âşık of the twentieth century, and his house near Sivas is now a museum. Some scholars question the way the young Republic's cultural policy turned him into a national symbol, smoothing away the Alevi religious dimension of his poetry.","Read a translation first, then listen without it and let the bağlama's buzzing drone and the steady strum carry you from verse to verse. Look for Veysel's own recordings rather than later pop covers; his plain, unhurried voice is the point. Then compare a Delta bluesman alone with a guitar, or Dylan's early songs: the lone singer-poet facing death is a familiar figure."],"ofra-haza-yemenite-songs":["Songs from the Yemenite Jewish tradition, many setting devotional poems by the seventeenth-century rabbi and poet Shalom Shabazi, sung by the Israeli pop star Ofra Haza. The arrangements combine traditional percussion, including hand drums and struck metal, with strings, keyboards and a modern rhythm section. Haza's voice is bright, agile and richly ornamented. The marker is Im Nin'alu, with its soaring unaccompanied opening phrase before the drums enter.","Haza, born in Tel Aviv in 1957 to parents who had come from Yemen, was already a leading pop singer in Israel when she recorded this album of the songs she had heard at home. Yemenite Jews had arrived in Israel in large numbers around 1949 and 1950, and their music was long treated as marginal beside European-derived culture. Released in 1984, the album later found an international audience through dance remixes.","In 1987 the British duo Coldcut sampled Im Nin'alu for their remix of Eric B. and Rakim's Paid in Full, and the same year M/A/R/R/S used her voice on Pump Up the Volume. A seventeenth-century Hebrew poem became a hip-hop hook. Listen for the ornamentation, which comes from Arabic and Jewish liturgical singing.","It made Haza the best-known Israeli singer internationally and introduced Yemenite Jewish music to millions who had never heard of it. Some critics complained that pop production smoothed the tradition; others credited her with restoring pride in a community's heritage. Her international career continued through the 1990s until her death in 2000, at 42, and her voice remains one of the most recognised samples in dance music.","Start with Im Nin'alu, then play Eric B. and Rakim's Paid in Full in the Coldcut remix and wait for her voice to arrive. The same phrase lives in two worlds. Then look for a traditional Yemenite recording, voices and drum alone, to hear the source, and compare Reich's Tehillim, another modern setting of Hebrew devotional words."],"javanese-gamelan-ketawang-puspawarna":["Puspawarna, 'Kinds of Flowers', is a short piece in ketawang form: a repeating cycle of sixteen beats closed each time by the deep stroke of the great hanging gong. Around a plain melody carried by the bronze metallophones, softer instruments weave their own lines: the bowed rebab, the bamboo flute, the gender with its rippling mallets, and a solo female singer who floats above the beat. Drums steer the tempo; the whole thing breathes rather than marches, slowing into its final gong.","The piece belongs to the court of the Mangkunegaran in Surakarta, central Java, where princes were also poets and patrons of music; its verses are usually credited to Mangkunegara IV, who ruled from 1853. It served to greet the prince. In 1977 a recording made at the Paku Alaman palace in Yogyakarta was chosen for the Golden Record carried by the Voyager space probes, so this court welcome is now leaving the solar system.","Listen to the gongs as a clock rather than as punctuation. The small gongs mark the halves and quarters of each cycle; the great gong arrives only at the end and is felt as arrival, not as a beat. Everything elaborate, including the singer's free-floating line, is aiming at that stroke. The tuning, slendro, divides the octave into five near-equal steps that no piano can play.","Western ears first met Javanese gamelan at the Paris Exposition of 1889, where Debussy heard it and never forgot it; for decades it was admired as exotic colour. Ethnomusicologists since Jaap Kunst and Mantle Hood have insisted on it as a classical system with its own theory, modes and repertory. The attribution to Mangkunegara IV is traditional and probably covers the text more securely than the music.","Find the Voyager recording and simply count: feel the sixteen-beat cycle, wait for the gong, and notice how the drummer draws the ensemble into the final slowdown. Then play Debussy's 'Pagodes' from Estampes and hear what a Parisian piano made of these layered bells. A full palace ensemble on a good modern recording shows the soft instruments best."],"balinese-kecak":["Kecak has no instruments at all. A large circle of bare-chested men sits around a flickering lamp and chants the syllable 'cak' in interlocking patterns, each group placed a fraction off the others, so that the sound becomes a single seething rhythm faster than any one person could produce. A leader keeps time with a hissed pulse, a singer intones a melody, and within the circle dancers enact episodes from the Ramayana: the abduction of Sita, the monkey army, the battle.","The chorus comes from sanghyang, a trance ritual in which the men's chant supported entranced dancers. In the early 1930s the dancer Wayan Limbak and the German painter Walter Spies, living in Bali, reshaped it into a staged drama with a Ramayana story, partly for the film 'Insel der Dämonen' and for the island's first tourists. It was, in effect, a modern invention built from ritual parts.","The chant is the gamelan translated into voices. The 'cak' groups reproduce the interlocking kotekan of Balinese metallophones, in which two parts each play only some of the notes and together make a continuous stream; the hissing leader stands where the gong-keeper would. Once you hear it as an orchestra of mouths, the circle's geometry makes sense.","Kecak has been accused of being a tourist confection, and it is certainly younger than its reputation. Scholars now treat it as a case study in how tradition is made: Balinese performers adopted it, varied it and made it their own, and villages still compete to perform it. Its sound reached Western composers and film-makers long before most of them knew its history.","Watch a filmed performance rather than just listen: the circle, the waving hands and the lamp are half of it. Then focus on one layer, perhaps the hissing leader, and let the rest swirl round it. Afterwards try Reich's Clapping Music, which makes a whole piece from two parts sliding against each other."],"balinese-legong-lasem":["Two young dancers in gold brocade move in exact mirror image, eyes flicking, fans quivering, to a gamelan that races and stops on a hair. The Lasem story tells of a king who abducts a princess, and of the bird of ill omen that warns him before his death in battle; a third dancer, the attendant, frames the tale. The music runs in sections of shifting speed, from glittering fast figuration to slow, suspended passages where the dancers barely move.","Legong grew up in the princely courts of south Bali, traditionally danced by girls before adolescence; one legend places its origin in a prince's dream at Sukawati early in the nineteenth century. Its orchestra, the gamelan pelegongan, is lighter than the processional ensembles. In the 1930s the Canadian composer Colin McPhee lived in Bali, transcribing this repertory, and later played his transcriptions with Benjamin Britten.","Two paired drummers, not a conductor, lead everything; the dancers' sudden freezes and head-snaps are cued by drum strokes. Listen too to the fast metallophone figuration: it is split between two players, one taking the on-beat notes and the other the off-beat notes, so that neither alone plays the tune. The instruments are also tuned in pairs slightly apart, making the whole ensemble shimmer.","European visitors in the 1920s and 1930s made legong the emblem of 'unspoiled Bali', a view that says more about them than about Bali. Balinese musicians have kept developing it, and the post-war kebyar style changed its speed and brilliance. Britten's visit in 1956 left its mark on his ballet The Prince of the Pagodas, and Reich's study of Balinese gamelan fed his own pulse music.","Seek out film, since the dance and music are inseparable. Start by watching the drummers' cues land in the dancers' bodies. Then close your eyes during a fast section and try to follow a single metallophone. Compare Britten's gamelan scenes in The Prince of the Pagodas and hear how much, and how little, an orchestra can borrow."],"gagaku-etenraku":["Etenraku is the best-known piece of gagaku, the court orchestra music of Japan. Its sound is unmistakable: the shō, a mouth organ of seventeen bamboo pipes, holds shimmering clusters of notes that swell and fade with the player's breath; the hichiriki, a short, piercing double reed, sings the melody with slides between pitches; a transverse flute doubles it. Lute and zither mark the beats with sparse plucked patterns, and a small drum and gong frame the slow cycle.","Gagaku arrived from Tang China and Korea in the seventh and eighth centuries and was organised at the Heian court in Kyoto into the form it has kept. Etenraku belongs to the tōgaku, 'music of Tang', repertory. It has been played by hereditary court musicians for more than a thousand years, and the Imperial Household Agency's music department in Tokyo still performs it.","The tempo is not fixed: the piece starts almost without pulse and gradually settles, and the gap before each drum stroke stretches in a way Western musicians find hard to copy. The shō's chords are not harmony in the Western sense but a sustained halo; they change a moment before the melody does. A popular folk song, Kuroda-bushi, is probably descended from a sung version of this melody.","Western composers heard gagaku as sound rather than tune. Hidemaro Konoye arranged Etenraku for Western orchestra in 1931, and Messiaen imitated its textures in Sept haïkaï after visiting Japan. Scholars such as Laurence Picken have argued that the modern slow tempo is itself a historical change, and that in Tang times this music ran several times faster.","Listen first only to the shō: follow its breathing clusters and let the melody pass over them. Then pick out the hichiriki's slides. Play Messiaen's 'Gagaku' movement next and hear strings and oboes impersonating these instruments. A recording by the Imperial Household ensemble is the reference."],"shakuhachi-shika-no-tone":["Two bamboo flutes call to each other across an imagined autumn valley, as a stag and a doe answer one another from distant hills. Each phrase is a single breath: a note swells from breathy noise into tone, bends down, flares up, and dies away. The second flute answers, sometimes overlapping, and gradually the calls come closer until the two voices meet and sound together. There is no pulse, only breath and the silence between.","Honkyoku, 'original pieces', were the spiritual repertory of the komusō, the begging monks of the Fuke sect of Zen who wandered Edo-period Japan in basket hats, playing the shakuhachi as meditation rather than performance. Kurosawa Kinko collected and codified many of these pieces in the eighteenth century, and his school still teaches them. When the sect was abolished in 1871, the music passed to lay players.","The 'imperfections' are the point. The rushing air of muraiki, where the breath is deliberately audible, and the pitch shading made by tilting the head, are techniques, not faults. So is the silence: the Japanese idea of ma treats the gap between phrases as part of the music, as charged as the notes. Listen for how long the silence lasts, not just the sound.","Shakuhachi players dispute whether honkyoku is music at all or suizen, 'blowing Zen'. Western interest grew after the war, fed by Zen's vogue among artists such as Cage. Takemitsu's November Steps in 1967 put shakuhachi and biwa in front of the New York Philharmonic and made the instrument's sound famous; purists argue over whether that helped or diluted the tradition.","Listen in a quiet room, at low volume, and do not wait for a tune. Follow one flute's breath from start to end, then notice how the other answers. Afterwards hear the shakuhachi in Takemitsu's November Steps and consider what an orchestra adds, and takes away."],"yatsuhashi-kengyo-rokudan-no-shirabe":["Rokudan, 'six steps', is the most famous piece for solo koto. It is built from six sections of equal length, each a variation on the same underlying frame, and it gets steadily faster from the first to the last, before a sudden slowing at the very end. The sound is plucked and bright; the left hand presses the strings behind the movable bridges to bend a note or add a quiet sigh after it is struck.","The piece is credited to Yatsuhashi Kengyō, a blind musician who reached the highest rank of the guild of blind musicians and worked in Kyoto in the seventeenth century. He is also credited with the hirajōshi tuning heard here, which gave koto music its characteristic mix of wide and very narrow steps. Instrumental pieces of this kind, danmono, were rare; most koto music accompanied song.","The acceleration follows jo-ha-kyū, the Japanese principle of a slow opening, a development and a rush to the close found in Noh and the tea ceremony alike. Each section also has the same fixed number of beats, the first slightly longer, so the structure is almost mathematical. One scholar has even proposed that the piece descends from a Gregorian Credo brought by Jesuit missionaries, a hypothesis most specialists doubt.","Every koto student learns Rokudan, and it has been recorded countless times, sometimes with shakuhachi or a second koto added. The attribution to Yatsuhashi is traditional rather than documentary, and the piece may have been shaped by several generations of blind masters. The musicologist Tatsuo Minagawa's suggestion of Christian roots has kept it unusually visible in Western scholarship, even among those who reject it.","Count the six sections by listening for the return of the opening shape, and feel each one moving a little faster. Listen for the bent notes after the pluck, the koto's signature. Then hear Miyagi's Haru no Umi, which shows how far the koto travelled in two and a half centuries."],"miyagi-michio-haru-no-umi":["A duet in three parts. The koto sets up a gently rocking ripple like small waves, and the shakuhachi floats a long melody over it, with gull calls and the creak of oars suggested in passing. A faster central section brings livelier interplay, the two instruments tossing figures back and forth, before the calm opening music returns. It is short, tuneful and immediately pictorial.","Miyagi wrote it in 1929 after a visit to the coast of the Inland Sea, and it was introduced to the wider world when the French violinist Renée Chemet, touring Japan, recorded a version with him on violin. Miyagi was a blind koto master who had grown up on Western music as well as Japanese, and led the Shin Nihon Ongaku, 'New Japanese Music', movement of the 1920s.","Listen to the koto's accompaniment: it behaves like a Western piano part, with repeated patterns and a sense of chords, something the older koto repertory avoids. The form, slow-fast-slow with a return, is Western too. Yet the melodic bends and the shakuhachi's breathy attacks remain wholly Japanese. The piece is a quiet argument that the two need not be enemies.","Traditionalists found Miyagi too Western; modernists later found him too sweet. The public never wavered: Haru no Umi is played on Japanese television and in shops every New Year and is probably the best-known Japanese instrumental piece at home. Recent writers treat Miyagi as a genuine reformer, and his invention of a seventeen-string bass koto is still in use.","Hear the koto and shakuhachi version first, then the violin version, and decide which blends better with the plucked strings. Picture the scene as you go: waves, boats, birds. Afterwards try Rokudan for the older koto style, and Takemitsu's November Steps for a very different, far harsher fusion of East and West."],"guqin-liu-shui":["The guqin is a long, low zither played on a table, its silk or metal strings producing a soft, woody sound with sliding tones and bell-like harmonics. Liu Shui paints water from mountain spring to river: quiet harmonics like droplets, then a famous central passage where the player's fingers roll and sweep rapidly across the strings to make a torrent, before the music widens into a calm current and fades.","The piece is tied to the ancient legend of the qin player Bo Ya and his friend Zhong Ziqi, who understood whether Bo Ya was thinking of mountains or of water; when Zhong died, Bo Ya broke his instrument. The tablature first appears in the Ming prince Zhu Quan's collection of 1425. The torrent section was added by the Sichuan player Zhang Kongshan in the nineteenth century.","Qin notation records strings, fingers and positions but hardly any rhythm, so every player must reconstruct the time, a practice called dapu. No two versions agree. The recording by Guan Pinghu was chosen for the Voyager Golden Record in 1977, alongside a Javanese gamelan piece, so this music about friends who understood each other is addressed to whoever finds it.","The qin was the instrument of scholars and officials, played for oneself or a friend rather than an audience, and Confucian writers considered it a moral discipline. In the twentieth century it nearly died out; recordings by Guan Pinghu and others preserved it. UNESCO recognised qin music in 2003, and there are now more players than at any time in the last century.","Find Guan Pinghu's recording and listen at low volume, as if in a small study. Wait for the torrent in the middle, then notice how quiet the ending is. Then hear Chou Wen-chung's Yü Ko, which transfers qin gestures to a Western ensemble."],"pipa-shimian-maifu":["A battle told on one lute. The pipa player musters the troops with drum-like strumming, lays out the army, springs the ambush, and then unleashes the fight: bursts of rapid tremolo, strings pinched together to make a buzzing clash, sharp cracks like arrows and clattering like hooves. The last sections describe the defeated general's flight and death. It is virtuoso programme music, loud and physical.","The story is the battle of Gaixia in 202 BC, where Liu Bang, founder of the Han dynasty, trapped his rival Xiang Yu. The music was printed in 1818 in a pipa collection edited by Hua Qiuping, though it is surely older. The pipa was the instrument of entertainers and courtesans rather than scholars, which is why its repertory is showier than that of the qin.","The clashing sound in the battle section comes from crossing two strings over each other with the left hand and striking them together, a technique almost unique to this piece. The whole work is a catalogue of pipa effects, and its section titles work like a newspaper account. The same battle ends in the Peking opera Farewell My Concubine, told from the loser's side.","Every pipa virtuoso records it; it is the instrument's calling card, and players compete in speed and violence. Some critics find it crude beside the reflective civil pieces such as Moonlight on the Spring River. Its fame reached cinema: Zhang Yimou's film House of Flying Daggers takes its Chinese title from the piece.","Read the section titles first, then follow the story as it unfolds. Listen for the crossed-string clash and for the sudden quiet of the final death scene. Then hear the pipa in Tan Dun's Ghost Opera, which places the same instrument beside a string quartet in a very different world."],"abing-erquan-yingyue":["A single erhu, the two-string fiddle held upright on the knee, plays a long, sorrowing melody that keeps returning, each time a little varied and more intense. The tone is thin and vocal, full of slides and small ornaments; there is no accompaniment, only the melody and the bow's grain. It is less a showpiece than a monologue, with the dignity of someone who has had a hard life.","Hua Yanjun, known as Abing, was a Taoist musician who went blind and lived as a street performer in Wuxi. In 1950 the musicologists Yang Yinliu and Cao Anhe recorded him on a wire recorder; he died that year. The title names a spring at Huishan in Wuxi, and was given at the recording session. The piece became a national symbol in the new People's Republic.","Only six pieces by Abing were recorded, and the recording itself is rough, with the wire's hiss audible. Listen to how freely he treats the pulse, lingering on some notes and hurrying others; later orchestral arrangements tend to smooth this out. The conductor Seiji Ozawa is reported to have said the piece should be heard kneeling.","Erquan Yingyue became a set piece for every erhu student and was arranged for Chinese orchestra, strings and even Western orchestras. Some scholars note that its 'suffering' was shaped by the political reading given to Abing's life, the blind poor man as victim of the old society. The original recording remains the benchmark.","Hear the 1950 recording first, however worn, and follow the returning melody. Then compare a modern erhu virtuoso and notice what polish adds or removes. Afterwards try Chen Gang's Butterfly Lovers Concerto for the Chinese melodic style in a Western frame."],"peking-opera-farewell-my-concubine":["The king of Chu, Xiang Yu, is surrounded; at night he hears his own soldiers' home songs sung by the enemy and knows the war is lost. His consort Yu Ji dances for him with two swords and then takes her own life. The sound is Peking opera's: a high, nasal fiddle, the jinghu, shadowing the voices; a percussion band of clappers, drums and gongs that marks every gesture; and stylised singing, the woman's role in a high falsetto.","Mei Lanfang, the greatest performer of female roles, created the opera in 1922 with the actor Yang Xiaolou, reshaping an older play. Mei's tours to Japan, the United States in 1930 and the Soviet Union in 1935 introduced Peking opera abroad, where Brecht and Eisenstein saw him. The sword dance became his signature, and the play Peking opera's best-known work.","The percussion leader, on a small drum, is the real conductor, cueing the other players and the actors' movements with strokes too fast to count. Many gongs mark an entrance, a thought, or a decision. Listen also for the change between the two main melodic systems, xipi and erhuang, which carry different moods. The whole play is the other side of the pipa piece Ambush from Ten Sides.","Peking opera was attacked as feudal in the 1960s and replaced by the 'model operas' of the Cultural Revolution, then revived. Chen Kaige's 1993 film Farewell My Concubine, about two actors who play Xiang Yu and Yu Ji through fifty years of Chinese history, brought the opera a global audience. UNESCO recognised Peking opera in 2010.","Watch a filmed performance, ideally with subtitles, and focus on Yu Ji's sword dance. Notice how each gesture is cued by the drum. Then see Chen Kaige's film, and compare Puccini's Turandot, a Western dream of China that could hardly be further from this."],"kunqu-peony-pavilion":["Du Liniang, a sheltered girl, wanders into her family's garden in spring, falls asleep and dreams of a young scholar; she wakes into longing, wastes away and dies. Years later the scholar finds her portrait, and her love brings her back from the dead. The music is Kunqu, older and softer than Peking opera: a bamboo flute, the dizi, leads, and the singing is slow and highly ornamented, with long melismas on single syllables.","Tang Xianzu wrote the play in 1598, in fifty-five scenes, in the late Ming dynasty; it is exactly contemporary with the first operas in Florence. Kunqu, from the Suzhou area, was the elite theatre of the Ming and Qing. A full performance takes about twenty hours, so it is usually staged in selections, above all the garden and dream scenes.","The rhythm of the singing follows the tones of the Chinese words, so the melody is partly dictated by the language. Listen also for the clapper: it marks strong beats, but the singer can stretch time against it. The dream scene's flower spirits were a Ming device for showing an inner state on stage, a solution to a problem Western opera solved with arias.","The play was a sensation in its own time; some readers were said to die of love for it. It was banned or censored at various points for its frank desire. Kunqu was listed by UNESCO in 2001, the first such listing, and the writer Pai Hsien-yung's 2004 'youth version' drew young audiences. Peter Sellars's 1998 staging with Tan Dun's music reached the West.","Start with the 'Interrupted Dream' scene on film. Listen to the flute and the way the voice lingers on each syllable. Then read the plot and watch the resurrection. Compare Monteverdi's Orfeo, written within a decade and also about love beyond death, to see two civilisations inventing opera at once."],"pansori-chunhyangga":["One singer, holding a fan, and one drummer tell a whole novel for up to eight hours. The voice is husky, forced, deliberately rough, swinging from speech to song to cries; the drummer on a barrel drum marks rhythmic cycles and calls out encouragement. The story: Chunhyang, a courtesan's daughter, secretly marries a nobleman's son who leaves for Seoul; she refuses a corrupt governor and is jailed, until her husband returns as a royal inspector.","Pansori grew up in the eighteenth century among travelling performers of southwestern Korea and rose to aristocratic and court patronage in the nineteenth, when the scholar Shin Jae-hyo wrote down and polished the texts. Chunhyangga is one of five surviving stories of an older twelve. Singers train for decades, traditionally by shouting against waterfalls, to build the voice's rasp. UNESCO recognised pansori in 2003.","The drummer is not an accompanist but a partner: his shouts of encouragement, chuimsae, are part of the performance, and audiences join in. The drum cycles move from the very slow jinyang to rapid patterns as the story speeds up. Singers belong to lineages and claim the particular versions of their teachers, so the 'work' exists in schools, not in one fixed text.","Once a low-status art, pansori became a national emblem in modern Korea. The first performance of a full story in one sitting was given only in 1968, by Park Dong-jin. Im Kwon-taek's 2000 film Chunhyang, built around a pansori performance, brought the form to Cannes. Debate continues over preserving old lineages versus creating new stories.","Start with a short excerpt, such as the prison lament or the love song, rather than the whole. Listen for the drummer's shouts and the rasp in the voice. Then compare Schubert's Winterreise: another single voice with one accompanist carrying a long story."],"gayageum-sanjo":["Sanjo, 'scattered melodies', is a suite for a solo instrument with drum, and on the gayageum it is the most celebrated form of Korean instrumental music. It begins in a very slow rhythmic cycle, with long, bent notes, and passes through several movements that get faster and faster, until the last section rattles along at speed. The player presses the strings behind the bridges to make notes waver, swell and fall.","Sanjo arose in southwestern Korea in the late nineteenth century, drawing on the improvised music of shaman rituals and on pansori; Kim Chang-jo is credited with its first form. Later masters developed their own versions, called ryu, and students learn a master's version note for note. The Japanese occupation and the war both nearly broke the lineages; they were rebuilt after 1960.","Behind every left-hand bend lies the singing voice: sanjo imitates pansori's range of expression, including its sobs and cries. Listen to the drummer too: the janggu's two heads, one high and one low, mark the rhythmic cycles, and the sense of acceleration comes as much from them as from the strings. Each ryu shows its master's personality.","Sanjo is seen in Korea as the peak of the classical instrumental tradition, though its origin is in the popular rather than the court world. Composers such as Isang Yun and Hwang Byungki drew on its expressive single tones. Scholars debate whether its fixed versions have lost the improvisation that gave birth to them.","Hear the whole thing through at least once to feel the acceleration. Listen for the left-hand bends in the slow opening. Then try Rokudan on the Japanese koto, a related zither with a similar speeding structure, and Isang Yun's music for how the sound moved into the modern concert hall."],"samulnori-uttari-pungmul":["Four players sit on the floor with four instruments: the kkwaenggwari, a small brass gong that cuts through everything; the jing, a large gong that booms and hums; the janggu, an hourglass drum; and the buk, a barrel drum. They start slowly and build through a series of rhythmic cycles to a crashing climax. The small gong leads, changing patterns with signals, and the others follow.","Samulnori was founded in Seoul in February 1978 by Kim Duk-soo and three colleagues, most from families of itinerant performers, who took the outdoor farmers' band music, pungmul, and made it a seated concert piece. Pungmul had accompanied village festivals, rituals and communal labour, and rapid urbanisation under military rule was destroying it. 'Uttari' refers to the central region whose rhythms this piece gathers.","The four instruments stand for weather: the small gong is thunder, the big gong wind, the hourglass drum rain, and the barrel drum clouds. Listen to how the players breathe together: Korean rhythm is often in groups of three, and the swing comes from the lengthening and shortening of the beat, not from a straight pulse.","Samulnori was an immediate success, and the group's name became the name of a genre practised by thousands of amateur and school groups. Some traditionalists objected that a moving, outdoor village practice had been frozen into a concert item, but it arguably saved the rhythms from oblivion. The group went on to collaborate with jazz musicians and Western percussion ensembles.","Watch a video first to see how the small gong leads with signals the others answer. Feel the three-beat swing under the surface, and notice the long build to the climax. Then compare Reich's Drumming, which also grows long percussion processes, and the Balinese kecak, another ensemble driven by interlocking parts."],"jongmyo-jeryeak":["Two ensembles, one on the upper terrace and one in the courtyard below, alternate through the day-long ritual at the Jongmyo Shrine in Seoul, honouring the kings of Joseon. The music is slow and grave: bronze bells and stone chimes hung in frames, the double-reed piri and the transverse flute, the bowed zither ajaeng, and singers chanting texts of praise. Lines of dancers move in formal patterns.","King Sejong, who also created the Korean alphabet, is credited with the two suites, Botaepyeong and Jeongdaeeop, composed for court banquets around 1447; his son King Sejo adapted them for the ancestral rite in 1464. The rite has been performed ever since, with interruptions during invasions and the colonial period, and is now given each May. UNESCO recognised the rite and its music in 2001.","Every piece begins and ends with the bak, a wooden clapper made of six slats, whose single crack is the signal to start or stop. Isang Yun used this sound in Réak. Listen too to the bells and stones: they are tuned in sets, and the melody moves between them, rather than as harmony.","The music is counted among the oldest orchestral traditions still performed anywhere, comparable only to Japanese gagaku. Scholars debate how far present practice reflects the fifteenth century, since invasions, the fall of the dynasty and the Japanese occupation all broke the chain of performers. The rite has also become a public spectacle in Seoul, which some see as a gain and some as a loss.","Watch a film of the ritual to see the two ensembles and the dancers. Listen for the clapper at the start and end, and the ring of the bells and stones. Then hear Isang Yun's Réak for the same sound world in a modern orchestra."],"misora-hibari-kawa-no-nagare-no-yo-ni":["A slow ballad in which Misora Hibari, at the end of a forty-year career, looks back on life as a river that flows on whatever happens. The arrangement is plain, strings and piano growing gently behind her, and the voice does the work: low and controlled, with a deep vibrato and the small catches in the tone that marked her style. It is more a quiet reckoning than a performance.","Released in January 1989, with words by Yasushi Akimoto and music by Akira Mitake, it was the last single she released in her lifetime; she died that June at 52. Misora had been Japan's biggest singer since she was a child star in the late 1940s, a symbol of the post-war years. The song was a farewell before anyone knew it.","Misora was the queen of enka, the sentimental ballad genre with its sobbing ornaments, but this song is not enka: its melody is closer to Western pop balladry. Listen for how she lets some of her enka habits in, a slight catch here, a bend there, and holds others back. It sums up her career by crossing its genres.","The song became one of the most loved in Japan, a regular at the top of polls of the greatest Japanese songs and a staple of karaoke and funerals alike, and it has been covered by many singers. Misora received the People's Honour Award posthumously in 1989, the first woman to do so. Critics now hear it as the close of the Shōwa era, which ended the same month it was released.","Listen once through without distraction, following only the voice and the way it holds back. Then compare one of Misora's earlier enka recordings to hear the style she was stepping away from. Afterwards try Teresa Teng's 'The Moon Represents My Heart', a ballad by East Asia's other great popular voice."],"kyu-sakamoto-ue-o-muite-aruko":["A bright, bouncing tune with a whistled refrain, sung by Kyu Sakamoto in a light voice with a gentle catch. The words describe someone walking with his head up so that his tears will not fall, remembering happier days; the cheerfulness of the music and the sadness of the words pull against each other. The arrangement is a small orchestra with strings and a steady swing.","Written by the lyricist Rokusuke Ei and the composer Hachidai Nakamura, it was released in 1961 and became a hit in Japan. A British label renamed it 'Sukiyaki', after a beef dish, since English speakers could not manage the title, and an instrumental cover charted in Britain. In June 1963 Sakamoto's original reached number one in the American charts, the first Asian-language song to do so.","Ei is said to have written the words after the defeat of the 1960 protests against the US–Japan security treaty, which would make it a political song hidden as a love song. The melody uses the Japanese pentatonic scale, so it sounds both folkish and modern. Sakamoto died in a plane crash in 1985.","The English title has been criticised as trivialising, a tourist menu pinned on a sad song, but the record opened a door for Asian pop in the West. It has been covered many times, including by A Taste of Honey in 1981 with new English words. It remains the only Japanese-language song to top the American charts.","Listen first to the whistle and the bounce, then notice the melancholy underneath them. Compare Elvis Presley's early ballads, which Sakamoto admired and whose vocal hiccup he borrowed. Then hear PSY's 'Gangnam Style', the Korean song that half a century later came closest to repeating the feat."],"mariya-takeuchi-plastic-love":["A mid-tempo disco-funk track with a rubbery bass line, choppy rhythm guitar, bright horns and a sweeping string arrangement, sung by Mariya Takeuchi in a warm, unhurried alto. The song describes a woman who dances and flirts through the city's nightlife to hide a heart broken by an old love, and the music matches her: glossy and danceable on top, melancholy underneath, with a long instrumental coda.","It appeared on Takeuchi's 1984 album Variety, produced and arranged by her husband Tatsuro Yamashita, at the height of Japan's economic boom, and was issued as a twelve-inch single in 1985. City pop, as the style came to be called, was the sound of a newly rich Tokyo: American soul and jazz-funk played by the best studio musicians. It was only a modest hit at the time.","The song's fame came thirty years later: in 2017 an unofficial YouTube upload of the extended version, with a photo of Takeuchi, gathered tens of millions of views, and the song became the emblem of a global city pop revival. Vaporwave and future-funk producers had been sampling such records, and Plastic Love became their anthem.","Critics once dismissed city pop as derivative, a polished copy of American records. The revival changed that, and Plastic Love is now treated as a classic; an official video followed in 2019. Debate continues about the revival's nostalgia for a Japan its new fans never knew, and about whether a recommendation algorithm can be said to have made a hit.","Listen to the extended version, with its long instrumental passages, and follow the bass line from start to finish. Notice how the strings arrive to lift the chorus. Then compare Chic's 'Good Times' and Earth, Wind & Fire's arrangements for the American models, and Yamashita's own album For You for the same studio sound."],"tatsuro-yamashita-for-you":["The album opens with 'Sparkle', a burst of clean, chopping guitar and horns that is the most recognisable sound in city pop. What follows is a set of polished songs moving between funk, soft rock and ballads, with Yamashita's multitracked harmonies and exact arrangements. The album closes with 'Your Eyes', an English-language ballad. The sound is warm, bright and expensive.","Yamashita had come up through the band Sugar Babe in the mid-1970s, whose record came out on Eiichi Ohtaki's label, and by 1982 he was one of Japan's leading songwriters and producers. For You was recorded with his regular band of studio musicians and released early that year. The cover illustration by Eizin Suzuki, a sunny, American-style scene, helped define the look of the genre.","Yamashita's rhythm guitar is the album's engine: he plays tight, muted strokes like a funk guitarist, but with a rock guitarist's attack. Listen too to his a cappella harmonies, often all sung by himself. The American soul influences are real, but the chords and melodic turns are unmistakably Japanese pop.","For You was a major hit in Japan and remains one of Yamashita's best-regarded albums, a reference point for Japanese producers. The city pop revival made it famous abroad, alongside Plastic Love, and it is now frequently reissued on vinyl. Yamashita's long caution about streaming has made his records harder to find outside Japan, which has only sharpened their mystique.","Start with 'Sparkle' and listen only to the guitar for the first minute. Then go through the album and follow the stacked harmonies. Compare the polished jazz-rock of American studio records of the same years, and his wife Mariya Takeuchi's Plastic Love, which he arranged."],"happy-end-kazemachi-roman":["A soft folk-rock album with gently strummed and picked guitars, loose, unhurried drums and the voices of Eiichi Ohtaki and Haruomi Hosono, singing in Japanese. The songs are lyrical and understated, with touches of country and psychedelia and no showing off. 'Kaze wo Atsumete', 'Gathering the Wind', is the best-known track; the others paint a Tokyo of wind, streetcars, summer and memory.","Happy End were Hosono, Ohtaki, the guitarist Shigeru Suzuki and the drummer and lyricist Takashi Matsumoto, and Kazemachi Roman, released in 1971, was their second album. They stood at the centre of a public argument about whether Japanese rock should be sung in Japanese; many insisted only English would do. Matsumoto's 'wind city' was the Tokyo of his childhood, erased by the building boom before the 1964 Olympics.","The band's model was Buffalo Springfield, and the interlocking guitars show it. But the melodies follow the rhythm and accents of spoken Japanese, which was the whole point of the project. 'Kaze wo Atsumete' was used in Sofia Coppola's film Lost in Translation in 2003, which quietly introduced the band to listeners who had never heard of them.","The album was not a big seller, but it is now regarded as the founding record of Japanese-language rock and routinely heads lists of the best Japanese albums. Its members went on to shape Japanese pop: Hosono formed Yellow Magic Orchestra, Ohtaki made the city pop classic A Long Vacation, and Matsumoto became one of the country's most successful lyricists.","Start with 'Kaze wo Atsumete', then listen through as a whole, since the album is built like a walk through the city. Compare The Band and the Byrds for the American sound it adapted. Then hear Yellow Magic Orchestra's first album to see how far Hosono travelled in seven years."],"ryuichi-sakamoto-merry-christmas-mr-lawrence":["The main theme is recognisable in two notes: a delicate, five-note melody in bell-like synthesizer tones over a slow, rippling pattern, rising with a gentle, melancholy lift. The score around it mixes electronic textures with gamelan-like percussion, sampled sounds and passages of near-stillness. The main theme has since become a piano piece in its own right, one of the most played of its era.","Sakamoto, a founder of Yellow Magic Orchestra, wrote the score for Nagisa Oshima's 1983 film about a Japanese prisoner-of-war camp in Java, in which he also acted, playing the camp commandant opposite David Bowie. He had studied composition and ethnomusicology in Tokyo, and had never scored a film. The music won a BAFTA. A vocal version, 'Forbidden Colours', was made with David Sylvian.","The melody uses the same five-note scale as much East Asian music, but the harmonies underneath are Western and romantic, and several synthesizer sounds imitate the gamelan of the film's Javanese setting. Listen for how the theme is fragmented and recoloured through the score, never quite allowed to resolve until the final scene, where it carries the film's last close-up.","The theme became Sakamoto's most famous piece and he returned to it all his life, in solo piano, trio and orchestral versions. Some critics consider the rest of the score uneven beside it. It is now seen as a pioneering electronic film score, alongside Vangelis's Blade Runner, and as proof that a pop musician could write film music of real weight.","Listen to the original main title, then to one of the solo piano versions Sakamoto played in concert late in life, and notice how the melody survives without its electronic halo. Then compare Vangelis's Blade Runner for another synthesizer score of the same years, and Yellow Magic Orchestra's BGM for where Sakamoto came from."],"joe-hisaishi-my-neighbor-totoro":["A film score for Hayao Miyazaki's story of two sisters who move to the countryside and meet forest spirits. It runs from a bouncy marching song for the opening credits to 'The Path of the Wind', a hushed, mysterious piano and strings piece. The title song, sung by Azumi Inoue, is known by every Japanese child. The sound mixes orchestra, synthesizers and simple piano.","Hisaishi had begun as a minimalist composer, influenced by Reich and Riley, before Hayao Miyazaki hired him for Nausicaä of the Valley of the Wind in 1984. Totoro was released in 1988 as a double bill with Isao Takahata's Grave of the Fireflies, a pairing of rural wonder and wartime tragedy. Composer and director would work together on every Miyazaki feature for the next four decades.","The minimalism never left: listen for short repeated patterns under the melodies, especially in 'The Path of the Wind', where a piano figure turns over and over beneath the tune. The simple songs hide sophisticated harmonies, and the Japanese countryside is painted almost entirely with Western instruments rather than koto or shakuhachi, as a child's world, not a postcard.","Totoro did modest business on release but grew into a classic, and its title character became Studio Ghibli's emblem. Its songs are sung in Japanese schools. Hisaishi's Ghibli scores are now performed in concert halls around the world, sometimes with Hisaishi conducting, and critics have come to treat him as a composer rather than just a supplier of tunes.","Start with 'The Path of the Wind', listening for the repeated piano pattern, then the cheerful title song. Watch the bus-stop scene in the rain with the music in mind. Compare John Williams's E.T., another film about children and an otherworldly friend, and then hear Reich to find Hisaishi's roots."],"utada-hikaru-first-love":["A debut album of R&B-inflected pop, sung by a sixteen-year-old in Japanese laced with English phrases. The sound is sleek and late-night: programmed beats, soft electric keyboards, gentle bass, and a voice that slides and ornaments in the manner of American R&B singers. 'Automatic', the laid-back first single, set the tone; the title track closes the album as a slow, string-lined ballad about the end of a first love.","Utada was raised between Tokyo and New York, the child of the enka singer Keiko Fuji and a producer, and wrote much of the album as a teenager. First Love was released in March 1999 and sold more than seven million copies in Japan, becoming the best-selling album in Japanese history. It marked the full arrival of American R&B in the mainstream of J-pop, just as the domestic record industry peaked.","Listen to the phrasing: Utada fits Japanese syllables to R&B rhythms, stretching and clipping words in a way many producers thought the language could not bear. Something of the mother's enka may survive in the expressive slides. The title track, the last on the record, is sung almost without ornament, as if the singer had dropped the style to say it plainly.","The album was a sensation and remains a landmark; Utada is widely seen as the defining Japanese singer-songwriter of their generation. Some critics at the time heard it as imitation American R&B, but its songwriting has outlasted that view. The title track found a new audience through a 2022 Netflix series named after it.","Start with 'Automatic' and listen to how the voice sits behind the beat, then jump to the title track. Play the album late at night, as it was clearly made to be heard. Compare TLC's CrazySexyCool and Janet Jackson's The Velvet Rope for the American models, and then Lauryn Hill for a contemporary."],"seo-taiji-and-boys-nan-arayo":["A mix of rapped verses, new jack swing beats and a soaring pop chorus, the first Korean hit to combine them. The track runs on programmed drums, sampled stabs and synthesizers, with a hard, swinging groove. In the words, a young man desperately insists on his love for someone slipping away. On stage the trio danced in the style of American hip-hop videos, which Korean television had not seen from Korean performers.","Seo Taiji had played bass in the heavy metal band Sinawe; with the dancers Yang Hyun-suk and Lee Juno he formed Seo Taiji and Boys. They debuted on a television talent show in April 1992, where the judges gave them low marks, but the song became one of the biggest hits in Korean history. South Korea had become a democracy only five years earlier, and a youth market was waiting.","Rap in Korean was widely thought impossible, since the language lacks English stress patterns; Seo solved it by chopping phrases into short, percussive bursts. The chorus, by contrast, has a very Korean melodic shape. The group later fought government censorship openly, and their protests helped end the pre-screening of song lyrics in 1996.","The song is taken as the birth of modern K-pop, the moment the idol industry became possible. Critics have debated its originality, since its American sources are obvious, and plagiarism accusations followed some of the group's later work. But its effect on Korean youth culture is not in doubt, and its members built the industry that followed.","Listen for the rapped Korean verses against the sung chorus, and notice how new the combination sounded in 1992. Then hear Run-DMC and Janet Jackson's Rhythm Nation for the American sources, and BTS's 'Spring Day' for how far the idea travelled in twenty-five years."],"psy-gangnam-style":["An electro-house track with a pounding four-on-the-floor beat, a squealing synthesizer riff and PSY's rapped and chanted verses, capped by a shouted hook anyone can join. The video shows him performing a horse-riding dance in absurd places, from a stable to a sauna to a lift. The song mocks the flashy lifestyle of Seoul's wealthy Gangnam district. It is comedy and club music at once.","PSY, Park Jae-sang, had been a well-known Korean entertainer for a decade, famous for comic songs and marathon concerts, and was signed to YG Entertainment. The video, released in July 2012, spread across the world through YouTube and social media and in December became the first video to reach a billion views. It reached number two in the United States and topped charts in many countries.","The satire is aimed at Korea's new money: Gangnam is a district of luxury shops and plastic surgery clinics, and PSY plays a man pretending to be far richer and cooler than he is, in a rundown setting. Most non-Korean listeners missed the joke entirely and heard only a party song, which is itself part of its story.","Some critics saw a novelty hit and a one-off; others saw a turning point in global pop, proof that a song in a language most listeners did not speak could spread through the internet without radio. Its success also fed debate about Western audiences laughing at, rather than with, an Asian performer. It opened doors for the K-pop wave that followed.","Watch the video first; it is half the work. Then listen for how the build-up and drop follow club music conventions. Compare Kyu Sakamoto's 'Sukiyaki', the earlier Asian-language song that reached the top of the American charts, and BTS for what came after."],"bts-spring-day":["A mid-tempo ballad that opens on a soft synthesizer pad and guitar and slowly adds a steady beat, sung and rapped in turn by the seven members. The song speaks of missing someone through a long winter and waiting for spring to bring them back. The mood is wistful rather than dramatic, rising to a warm, anthemic chorus and then dissolving again into quiet.","Released in February 2017 on You Never Walk Alone, an expanded edition of the album Wings, the song was co-written by the members RM and Suga with the group's producers. BTS were on the verge of becoming the first K-pop group with a mass Western following, and this song arrived just as their fan base, ARMY, was growing into a global force.","The song and video have been read as a memorial to the victims of the 2014 Sewol ferry disaster, in which many students died. The video includes images such as yellow ribbons, and a hotel sign referring to a story by Ursula K. Le Guin about a city whose happiness depends on one child's suffering. Once you know this, the waiting for spring sounds less like romance and more like mourning.","Many fans and critics consider it BTS's finest song, and it stayed in the Korean streaming charts for years after release, an unusual feat in a fast-moving market. It showed that K-pop could be reflective and grief-stricken as well as dance music. The members have never officially confirmed the Sewol reading, which leaves listeners free to hear their own loss in it.","Listen to how the song passes between rap and singing, and how the chorus opens up. Watch the video for its imagery, then read about the Sewol disaster. Compare Seo Taiji and Boys and PSY to trace K-pop's journey from a Seoul talent show to the world's biggest stages."],"cui-jian-nothing-to-my-name":["A rock song built on a slow, heavy groove, with a rasping, strained voice, electric guitars and a Chinese double-reed horn, the suona, which plays a wailing solo. In the words a young man tells a woman he has nothing to offer, and she laughs at him; he keeps asking when she will go with him. The mood is raw and frustrated, building from a mutter to a shout.","Cui Jian, a classically trained trumpeter who had discovered Western rock on smuggled tapes, performed the song in May 1986 at a large concert in Beijing and caused a sensation, the first real rock song most Chinese listeners had heard. It became the unofficial anthem of the student protests in Tiananmen Square in 1989, where Cui performed for the students. He has faced performance restrictions since.","The suona, used at Chinese weddings and funerals, gives the song a folk sound and makes it unmistakably Chinese rather than a copy of Western rock. Listeners in 1986 heard the lyric as more than a love song: a whole generation with nothing, facing a system that laughed at it. Cui never had to say so.","The song is regarded as the birth of Chinese rock, and Cui as its father. Its association with 1989 has made it politically sensitive in China, though Cui still performs. Western critics compared him to Dylan and Springsteen, which he has sometimes resisted, and Chinese critics now debate whether rock ever regained the urgency it had in these few years.","Listen for the suona solo and how it cuts through the guitars. Then compare Bob Dylan's 'Blowin' in the Wind' and Public Enemy's 'Fight the Power', another anthem of 1989, and Teresa Teng's 'The Moon Represents My Heart' for the soft music Chinese youth had loved before this."],"teresa-teng-the-moon-represents-my-heart":["A slow, gentle love song in Mandarin, sung by Teresa Teng in a soft, intimate voice close to the microphone. The arrangement is spare: strings, guitar and a light rhythm, leaving the voice in front. The words have the singer's lover ask how deep her love is, and she answers that the moon represents her heart. The melody is simple and swaying, easy to sing along to, which is much of its power.","Written by the composer Weng Ching-hsi with words by Sun Yi, it was first recorded by another Taiwanese singer in the early 1970s; Teng's 1977 version made it famous. Teng was a Taiwanese star with a huge following across Asia. In mainland China her music was banned as decadent in the early years of reform, but cassettes were widely copied and smuggled.","The song was a symbol of the thaw after the Cultural Revolution; a saying went that 'Old Deng rules by day, little Deng rules by night', comparing Deng Xiaoping with Teresa Teng. Listen for how softly she sings, almost whispering: after years of revolutionary songs sung at full voice, the intimacy itself felt like a political act.","It is probably the best-known Chinese popular song in the world, sung at weddings and karaoke from Taipei to San Francisco. Teng's intimate style was attacked in China as 'spiritual pollution' but shaped generations of singers there. She died in 1995, and her reputation has only grown; the song is now accepted even in the mainland.","Listen to Teng's soft voice and the way she places the words gently behind the beat. Then compare Misora Hibari's farewell ballad for a Japanese counterpart, and Cui Jian's 'Nothing to My Name' to hear what the next generation of Chinese youth shouted instead."],"messiaen-sept-haikai":["Seven 'Japanese sketches' for piano and a small orchestra with only eight violins for strings. Movements depict the park at Nara, the lake at Yamanaka, the gagaku court music, the shrine gate at Miyajima standing in the sea, and the birds of Karuizawa, framed by an introduction and coda. The sound is bright and hard, with bird calls, bells and clusters.","Messiaen visited Japan in 1962 with the pianist Yvonne Loriod, whom he had married the previous year, on a concert tour. He heard gagaku and Noh, visited temples, shrines and gardens, and notated the songs of Japanese birds, as he did everywhere. He wrote the work on his return to Paris, and it was first performed there in 1963, with Loriod at the piano.","In the 'Gagaku' movement the eight violins play high, sustained clusters without vibrato to imitate the shō, the bamboo mouth organ, while a trumpet and double reeds take the piercing line of the hichiriki. It is one of the closest imitations of an Asian ensemble by a Western composer, and it sounds nothing like the rest of Messiaen's music.","The work is performed less than Messiaen's larger pieces but is admired as a vivid travel diary, full of colour and precise observation. Some Japanese listeners have found the gagaku imitation charming but inaccurate. Scholars see it as a turning point in Western composers' engagement with Japanese music, which Takemitsu and others would explore from the other direction.","Start with the 'Gagaku' movement, then listen to a gagaku performance of Etenraku and hear what Messiaen was copying. Then play the whole work and follow the birds of Karuizawa. Compare Mayuzumi's Bugaku, written by a Japanese composer the same year, and Takemitsu's November Steps."],"lord-kitchener-london-is-the-place-for-me":["A calypso in the old Trinidadian manner: a small band with clarinet and muted trumpet trading short phrases, a guitar and piano keeping a loping two-step, and over it Lord Kitchener's warm, slightly nasal tenor, carefully enunciating every syllable. The song is a list of pleasures, a newcomer praising the city, its manners, its famous places, the feeling of being at the centre of the empire. It lasts barely three minutes and never raises its voice.","Kitchener, born Aldwyn Roberts in Arima, Trinidad, sailed to Britain on the Empire Windrush in June 1948. A newsreel crew filmed him on arrival at Tilbury singing an early version of this song unaccompanied. He settled in London and Manchester, played West Indian clubs, and recorded the finished song for the small Melodisc label in 1951, when calypso records were sold mainly to Caribbean migrants and to a few curious British jazz fans.","The song's optimism is the point of tension. Kitchener wrote it before he had lived in the city, and within a few years he was writing sharper calypsos about rent, cold and colour bars. Listen to the band too: the clarinet lines owe as much to New Orleans jazz as to Port of Spain, a reminder that calypso had long been in conversation with American records before it ever reached London.","For decades it was a period curiosity. It returned to view in 2002, when Honest Jon's Records named a series of compilations of London calypso after it, and again around the seventieth anniversary of the Windrush landing, when the newsreel clip became one of the defining images of Caribbean migration to Britain. Some listeners hear innocence; others hear a performer shrewdly flattering his new audience.","Watch the 1948 Pathé newsreel first, then play the 1951 record, and notice how a bare shipboard tune has become a dressed-up dance number. Listen for the way Kitchener lands on the ends of lines, a calypsonian's diction built to carry jokes across a tent. Follow it with Mighty Sparrow's Jean and Dinah to hear how quickly the genre sharpened."],"harry-belafonte-calypso":["Eleven songs of Caribbean origin or flavour, sung by Harry Belafonte with a guitar-led band, hand percussion and a chorus that answers him like dock workers across a yard. It opens with Day-O, the Banana Boat Song, built on a Jamaican work song, and includes Jamaica Farewell and I Do Adore Her. The sound is polished and theatrical: Belafonte acts each song, slowing and whispering, then swelling to a full-throated call.","Belafonte was born in Harlem to a Jamaican mother and a Martinican father and spent part of his childhood in Jamaica. By 1956 he was a successful nightclub and folk singer at RCA Victor. Many of the songs came from the songwriter Irving Burgie, who adapted Jamaican mento and Trinidadian calypso material. Released at the height of the long-playing record's first boom, it is widely reported to be the first LP by a single artist to sell a million copies.","Much of what America heard as calypso here is actually Jamaican mento, a rural style with banjo and rumba box, and several songs are folk material reworked and newly copyrighted, a tangle of credits argued over ever since. Listen to Day-O's structure: the call of the tallyman counting bunches of bananas is a genuine work-song form, and the chorus is doing the answering the labourers once did.","It topped the American album chart for weeks and set off a brief calypso craze in 1957, complete with imitations and a Hollywood film cash-in. Trinidadian calypsonians were often scornful, hearing a smoothed, generic product with none of the topical wit of the Port of Spain tents. Its reputation has since grown with Belafonte's own, as a civil-rights activist who used his fame to fund the movement.","Start with Day-O and listen to the chorus behind him; then Jamaica Farewell for the gentler, homesick Belafonte. Compare the album with a real Trinidadian calypso such as Mighty Sparrow's to hear the difference between a concert-hall staging and a tent performance. The original mono pressing has the most presence."],"mighty-sparrow-jean-and-dinah":["A brisk calypso with horns riffing between the lines and a bouncing rhythm meant for a carnival road march. The young Sparrow sings with a swagger that is half boast and half commentary, the tune simple enough for a whole street to shout back. The song mocks the fortunes of certain women who had made their living from American servicemen, now that the Americans are leaving, and cheers the local men who expect to win back the island.","Trinidad hosted a large United States naval base at Chaguaramas during and after the Second World War, and the base's scaling-down in the 1950s was a live political subject as the island moved toward independence. Slinger Francisco, born in Grenada and raised in Port of Spain, won both the Calypso King title and the Road March with this song at Carnival in 1956, still only about twenty, and became the dominant calypsonian of the next generation.","The song is really about sovereignty. Beneath the bawdy surface lies a sharp nationalist point: the foreign power is going, and islanders will run their own affairs. It is also a reply, a decade later, to the Andrews Sisters' 1945 hit Rum and Coca-Cola, itself lifted from a Trinidadian calypso; Sparrow is taking the subject back from America.","It made Sparrow a star overnight and set off a long rivalry with Lord Kitchener that shaped calypso for twenty years. Later listeners have been uneasy with its view of the women it describes, and scholars now read it both as anti-colonial satire and as an example of how calypso's wit often worked at women's expense. Its place in Trinidad's story of independence is not in doubt.","Listen for how the horn riff and the chorus are built for walking: this is music for a moving crowd, not a seated audience. Then hear Sparrow's later records from the 1960s to follow the growth of his band sound. Pair it with Lord Kitchener's London Is the Place for Me for the migrant's side of the same era."],"bob-marley-simmer-down":["A fast ska record: horns blaring the offbeat, a walking bass, a drummer driving hard, and three young voices in close, slightly ragged harmony. Bob Marley leads, with Peter Tosh and Bunny Livingston answering. The song is an appeal to the rude boys, the restless young men of West Kingston, to calm down before trouble finds them. It is raw, urgent and over in under three minutes.","The Wailers auditioned for Clement 'Coxsone' Dodd at his new Studio One in Kingston in 1963, and this was among their first recordings, made with the session men who were about to become the Skatalites. Jamaica had become independent in August 1962, and Dodd's records were tested first on his sound system, the huge mobile discos that played to crowds in the city's yards. The record went to the top of the Jamaican charts.","The Wailers here sound like an American doo-wop group trying to keep up with a Jamaican band. Their harmony model was the Impressions and other Chicago vocal groups, whose records reached Kingston on radio from Miami and New Orleans; listen to the way the voices stack. The horn players behind them are among the finest jazz musicians on the island, playing a pop session.","For years it was known only to collectors and Jamaican audiences; after Marley's international fame, the Studio One sides were reissued and reassessed. Critics now treat them as essential to his story, showing that the social preacher of the 1970s was already there at eighteen, and that the Wailers began as a vocal group rather than a band.","Listen first to the horns and bass, the ska engine, then to the three voices, and notice how Marley's lead already cuts through. Then play Catch a Fire, ten years later, to hear how much slower and heavier the same singers became. Compilations of the Wailers' Studio One sides give the fuller picture."],"millie-small-my-boy-lollipop":["Two minutes of bright, bouncing ska-pop: a choppy guitar on the offbeat, a shuffling beat and a sixteen-year-old singer whose high, girlish voice seems to be smiling through every line. A harmonica solo breaks in halfway. The song is a simple, sweet declaration of teenage love, and the whole record is pitched to sound like fun on a transistor radio.","Millie Small came to London from Jamaica at the invitation of Chris Blackwell, whose small Island label had been licensing Jamaican records for migrants. The song was an American rhythm-and-blues number first recorded in 1956 by Barbie Gaye. Blackwell had it arranged by the Jamaican guitarist Ernest Ranglin, and licensed it to Fontana because Island could not handle a hit. It reached number two in both Britain and the United States.","The harmonica soloist is disputed; the long-told story that a young Rod Stewart played it has been denied, and another session player is usually credited. Listen past the novelty and you hear the ska offbeat, lightly scrubbed, reaching millions who had never heard of Kingston. The money it earned let Blackwell build Island Records, the label that would later carry Bob Marley to the world.","Dismissed by many at the time as a novelty, it is now seen as the moment Jamaican music first went global, several million copies across the world. Jamaican purists heard a watered-down ska; later historians point out that without it there might have been no Island Records and no international reggae. Small herself left music within a few years and spoke of receiving little of the money.","Listen for the guitar chop on the offbeat, the one ska fingerprint left in the pop arrangement. Then play Barbie Gaye's 1956 original to hear what Ranglin changed, and the Skatalites' Guns of Navarone for what ska sounded like at home. It is short: play it twice."],"prince-buster-al-capone":["An instrumental ska stomp with a gangster-film frame. It opens with a car's screeching tyres and Prince Buster's spoken warning, then the band hits a fast, lurching groove: horns blasting a riff, piano and guitar stabbing the offbeat, Buster breaking in with shouts and asides. Most of the record is band, not singing: it is a dance track built around a character.","Cecil Campbell, a former boxer who took the name Prince Buster, started out as a bodyguard for Coxsone Dodd's sound system and then built his own, the Voice of the People, becoming one of the first producers to press records by local musicians. Released in Jamaica in the mid-1960s, Al Capone was licensed in Britain on the Blue Beat label, which sold ska so steadily to migrants and mods that 'bluebeat' became a British name for the music. It reached the UK top twenty in 1967.","The gangster theme is a mirror: American crime films played to packed houses in Kingston, and the city's rude boys borrowed their style. Buster's shouted interjections over the band are an early version of the deejay's art, talking over a rhythm, which would become toasting and, in time, rapping. The rhythm itself is a Jamaican reading of American shuffle rhythm and blues.","It became a talisman for British youth cultures, from mods to the late-1970s 2 Tone revival: the Specials built their first single, Gangsters, on it, and Madness named themselves after another Prince Buster song. Critics in Jamaica tend to rank Buster higher as producer and personality than as singer; this record catches both at once.","Enjoy the opening sound effect, then follow the horns, which carry the melody. Listen for Buster's spoken breaks as the first sign of the deejay tradition. Afterwards play U-Roy's Wake the Town to hear where talking over a record went, and the Clash's first album to hear ska's afterlife in punk."],"the-skatalites-guns-of-navarone":["An instrumental: the theme from a Hollywood war film, taken at ska speed. Horns in unison state the tune like a brass band on parade; the piano and guitar hammer the offbeat; the bass walks; the drummer punches accents on the third beat. Then the soloists take turns, jazz-style, before the full section returns. It sounds like a big band from Kingston playing a march for dancing.","The Skatalites formed in 1964 from Kingston's best session players, several of them trained at the Alpha Boys School, a Catholic institution famous for its band programme. They played on hundreds of records for Coxsone Dodd and Duke Reid. The tune is Dimitri Tiomkin's theme for the 1961 film The Guns of Navarone. The band broke up in 1965 after its trombonist, Don Drummond, was arrested for killing his partner; the record became a UK hit only in 1967.","Listen to Lloyd Knibb's drums: the accents falling where a jazz drummer would not put them, together with hits on the rim, give ska its forward lurch, and the pattern is often said to draw partly on Rastafarian burru drumming. Tommy McCook, Roland Alphonso and the others were jazz players first; their solos quote bebop phrasing. Guns of Navarone is film music turned into a dance by musicians who would rather have been playing Coltrane.","In Jamaica the Skatalites were the house band of a whole era, but their fame abroad came posthumously, through reissues and 2 Tone. Critics now rank them among the great instrumental ensembles of the Americas. There is debate over how much ska owes to New Orleans rhythm and blues and how much to mento and burru; the band's own playing suggests all three.","Follow the unison horn theme, then listen only to the drums for a chorus. Compare it with the band's other famous sides, Eastern Standard Time and Freedom Sounds, and with a New Orleans record such as Fats Domino's to hear the offbeat change places. Any Studio One compilation of the band will do."],"alton-ellis-girl-ive-got-a-date":["A slow, swaying love song. The band has cut ska's tempo nearly in half; the bass has stopped walking and plays short, melodic phrases with gaps between them; the guitar still ticks on the offbeat, but gently. Over it Alton Ellis sings a smooth, soul-inflected tenor with a small backing chorus, apologising to a girl as he leaves for someone else. It is quiet, intimate and made for close dancing.","Recorded for Duke Reid's Treasure Isle studio in Kingston in 1966, it is one of the records most often named as the start of rocksteady, the short-lived style between ska and reggae. A hot summer, it was said, made fast ska too tiring to dance to; more likely, the singers were following the slower American soul records of the day, from Curtis Mayfield and the Impressions to the Stax singers. Ellis followed it with Rock Steady, which gave the style its name.","Listen to the bass: this is where the Jamaican bass line becomes the lead instrument, and the space around it would later become dub. Four years later this very rhythm was reused by Duke Reid for U-Roy's Wake the Town, one of the first great toasting records. The same backing track thus stands at the start of both rocksteady and the deejay tradition.","Ellis was long revered in Jamaica as the godfather of rocksteady while almost unknown abroad; he spent his later years in London performing to devoted audiences. Historians still argue about which record 'invented' rocksteady, with this, Hopeton Lewis's Take It Easy and others in contention. Few doubt that Ellis was its finest singer.","Put it next to Guns of Navarone and feel the tempo drop. Listen to the bass and the space between its notes, then to Ellis's phrasing, borrowed from American soul. Follow it with U-Roy's Wake the Town to hear the same rhythm reborn. Treasure Isle compilations give the whole rocksteady world."],"the-paragons-the-tide-is-high":["A rocksteady love song in the smoothest manner: three voices in soft close harmony, a lilting rhythm, a lightly strummed guitar and a round bass line. The lead singer, John Holt, compares his stubbornness in love to someone holding on against a rising tide. The melody is short and circular, and the record floats rather than drives.","The Paragons were a vocal trio at Duke Reid's Treasure Isle, the studio that defined rocksteady's sweet, polished sound in 1966 and 1967, and a rival to Coxsone Dodd's Studio One. Holt wrote the song. Jamaican vocal groups modelled themselves on American harmony groups such as the Impressions and the Drifters, whose records came in on Miami radio. The Paragons dissolved by the end of the decade; Holt became one of Jamaica's best-loved solo singers.","The song's afterlife is larger than its first life. In 1980 Blondie's version, with steel drums and horns added, topped the charts in the United States and Britain; many listeners still do not know it began in Kingston. The Paragons' own rhythms were reused by deejays too: U-Roy toasted over their Wear You to the Ball, one of his first hits.","In Jamaica it was a moderate hit rather than a monument; its canonisation came through the Blondie cover and later through Atomic Kitten's 2002 version, which made the song familiar to yet another generation. Critics hold the Paragons' original up as an example of rocksteady's understated grace, and of how a Jamaican song could become global pop without its authors being heard.","Hear the Paragons' version first, then Blondie's, and notice what an American new-wave band kept (the melody) and what it added (a Caribbean costume). Listen in the original for the gentle bass and the three voices breathing together. Follow with Alton Ellis for rocksteady's other great voice."],"toots-and-the-maytals-do-the-reggay":["A dance-craze song that names a music. Over a skipping, choppy rhythm, faster than rocksteady and with the organ and guitar pecking at the offbeat, Toots Hibbert sings with the raw fervour of a gospel preacher, calling on everyone to join the new dance, while the two Maytals answer him. It is short, joyful and a little rough, the sound of a band excited by its own groove.","Toots and the Maytals recorded it for the Chinese-Jamaican producer Leslie Kong at his Beverley's label in 1968, as rocksteady was being pushed into a quicker, more clipped beat. Hibbert had recently come out of prison after a drug conviction, and was one of Jamaica's most popular singers. The record is generally credited as the first to use the word reggae, then spelled here with a y, in its title.","Nobody is quite sure where the word came from. Hibbert said it came from 'streggae', Kingston slang for a raggedy, everyday style; others connect it to 'raggay' or to the word for a ragged garment. Listen to the organ's doubled chop, 'bubbling' on the offbeat: that new keyboard pattern is the reggae fingerprint, and it is here in embryo.","Its importance is as a name and a threshold rather than a finished statement; its fame outside Jamaica is recent. Critics admire Hibbert's voice above all, often compared with Otis Redding's for its grain and urgency, and Toots and the Maytals remain for many the most soulful of the early reggae groups. Hibbert died in 2020, still touring.","Listen for the organ's double chop and the bass locking with the kick drum, then to Toots's voice, which is gospel through and through. Play it after Girl I've Got a Date to hear rocksteady quickening into reggae, and follow with Pressure Drop and 54-46 That's My Number."],"desmond-dekker-israelites":["A lean early-reggae record: a jaunty, bubbling rhythm, a busy bass, a chorus of backing singers, and Desmond Dekker's high, keening voice complaining of getting up early, working hard and still having nothing to eat. His family has left and his clothes are torn. The tune is catchy enough to hide the misery; the record's bounce and its sorrow run side by side.","Dekker, born Desmond Dacres in Kingston, had already had a rude-boy hit with 007 (Shanty Town). Israelites was produced by Leslie Kong and released in Britain on Pyramid, a label serving Caribbean migrants and the skinheads who adopted Jamaican music. In April 1969 it became the first Jamaican record to top the British singles chart, and it reached the American top ten.","The title draws on Rastafarian language, in which Black Jamaicans are the exiled Israelites, suffering in Babylon; the song is a complaint about poverty cast in biblical terms. Its Jamaican patois was famously misheard by British and American listeners, spawning a long tradition of mistaken versions. Listen to the rhythm guitar and the bass: they are already fully reggae, a year after the word appeared.","It was Jamaica's first big international number one, and for years the most famous Jamaican record abroad, until Bob Marley. Critics have occasionally dismissed it as pop reggae, but it is now valued as a clear-eyed song of hardship with a hook to carry it, and for how it reached British working-class youth before rock critics had noticed reggae at all.","Listen to it twice: once for the bounce, once for the words, described plainly as poverty and exile. Notice the backing chorus answering Dekker. Then play 007 (Shanty Town), from the Harder They Come soundtrack, and Toots's Pressure Drop for Kong's other great productions."],"toots-and-the-maytals-pressure-drop":["A reggae song of reckoning: a tight, loping rhythm with a stuttering guitar and organ on the offbeat, the Maytals chanting a simple, insistent refrain, and Toots Hibbert's rough, gospel-drenched voice warning someone who has done wrong that the pressure will fall on them in turn. It repeats and repeats, gaining force by insistence, like a spiritual turned into a curse.","It was another Leslie Kong production, made in 1969 at the height of early reggae's fast, lean phase. Jamaica was a young nation with deep inequalities, and the song's idea of moral retribution was part of the language of the poor. In 1972 it was chosen for The Harder They Come, the film that carried reggae to cinemas in Britain and America, and its soundtrack album made the song internationally known.","The chorus is a call-and-response pattern straight from the Revivalist church music Hibbert grew up singing, and the song's warning has the shape of an Old Testament prophecy. Its later history is a map of reggae's reach: the Clash recorded it in 1979, and it has been covered by punk, soul and rock bands ever since, often with its menace intact.","Through the film it became one of the most loved reggae songs abroad, and it is often named as Toots's finest record. Critics note that the international audience first heard it as ambience in a crime film; its gospel roots have been more appreciated since Hibbert's death in 2020 prompted a new look at his whole career.","Listen to the repeated refrain gather weight, and to how Toots cracks his voice on the high notes. Then hear it on the Harder They Come soundtrack, in context, and follow it with the Clash's version to hear what punk heard. Do the Reggay and 54-46 That's My Number complete the early Maytals."],"jimmy-cliff-many-rivers-to-cross":["A slow ballad closer to a hymn than to reggae. A church organ holds long chords, a piano rolls gently, and Jimmy Cliff sings in a clear, pleading voice about loneliness, weariness and a long road still ahead, rising near the end to a cry backed by a gospel choir. There is no offbeat skank here at all: it is soul music with a Jamaican singer at its heart.","Cliff, from rural St James in Jamaica, had his first hits as a teenager in Kingston and moved to London in the mid-1960s at Chris Blackwell's invitation. He wrote the song, he later said, out of frustration with his struggle to break through in England. It appeared on his 1969 album for Trojan and Island, and was later placed on the soundtrack of The Harder They Come, becoming one of his best-known recordings.","The record's models are American: the organ and choir come from the Black church via soul singers such as Sam Cooke and Otis Redding. Listen to the final verse, where the choir enters and Cliff lets his voice break. It has been covered many times, most famously by UB40 and Harry Nilsson, but the original's plainness is rarely matched.","It was not a big hit on release, but it grew into one of the most covered songs by any Jamaican writer. Critics have long debated whether Cliff's gift lay in reggae or in soul; this song argues that the distinction mattered less than his voice. It is now a fixture of memorial services as well as reggae compilations.","Hear it on a quiet night, and listen for the organ holding everything together. Then compare it with Sam Cooke's A Change Is Gonna Come, which it closely resembles in spirit. Follow it with The Harder They Come to hear the same singer at full, defiant speed."],"the-abyssinians-satta-massagana":["A slow, solemn reggae hymn. Three voices in close, sustained harmony float above a heavy, rolling bass and a spare, deliberate beat; a horn line answers in the gaps. The words, partly in Amharic, the language of Ethiopia, give thanks and praise to God and look toward a promised land in Africa. It moves like a procession, calm and devotional.","The Abyssinians, Bernard Collins and the brothers Donald and Linford Manning, recorded it at Coxsone Dodd's Studio One in 1969. Dodd reportedly did not see its commercial potential and shelved it, so the group bought the tape back and released it on their own Clinch label in 1971, when it became a Jamaican hit. It was among the first records to carry Rastafarian devotion so fully into the charts.","The Amharic phrase in the title is usually translated as giving thanks, a reference to Ethiopia, which Rastafarians regard as Zion, and to its last emperor, Haile Selassie. The rhythm has been reused on dozens of other records, including deejay and instrumental versions, making it one of the most recycled riddims in Jamaican music. Listen to how the harmony never hurries.","It is now regarded as one of the foundation stones of roots reggae and as its most beautiful hymn. Critics often place it beside Burning Spear's Marcus Garvey as the purest expression of the Rastafarian spirit in music. The Studio One back story is part of the legend, a warning about producers' judgment and ownership in Jamaica.","Listen to the three voices as a choir, and to the bass beneath them, and give it full volume. Then follow it with Burning Spear and Culture, the other great Rasta vocalists, and compare it with a spiritual such as Amazing Grace, from a different diaspora of the same continent."],"u-roy-wake-the-town":["A deejay record: U-Roy's voice, excited, rhythmic and full of whoops and catchphrases, talking and half-singing over a rocksteady backing track, with snatches of the original singer allowed through between his interjections. He calls the whole town to wake up and listen. The effect is of a party host commenting live on a record he loves, turning it into something new.","Toasting, talking rhythmically over records on a sound system, had been a feature of Kingston dances for years. Duke Reid, the Treasure Isle producer, had U-Roy toast over his rocksteady backing tracks, including Alton Ellis's Girl I've Got a Date, and released the results in 1970. U-Roy's records reportedly held several top chart places in Jamaica at once. The same period saw the arrival of 'versions', instrumental B-sides made to be talked over.","Listen for Alton Ellis's voice peeping through: U-Roy is performing a duet with a four-year-old record. A few years later a Kingston-born teenager called Clive Campbell, who had moved to the Bronx, began playing records at parties as Kool Herc, and brought the Jamaican practice of talking over breaks to New York; hip-hop grew from that seed. U-Roy's records are part of that line.","It put deejays on record for the first time as stars and made U-Roy the originator of a lineage running through Big Youth and I-Roy to dancehall. Critics have debated how far hip-hop descends from Jamaican toasting; the Kool Herc connection makes the link concrete, though American traditions of rhyme and radio patter mattered too. U-Roy died in 2021.","Play Alton Ellis's Girl I've Got a Date first, then Wake the Town, and hear how the deejay treats the original as a stage. Notice his timing, often landing just off the beat. Then play Rapper's Delight and Grandmaster Flash to hear the Bronx version of the idea a decade later."],"jimmy-cliff-the-harder-they-come":["A defiant, driving reggae song: a bright organ, a choppy guitar, a steady beat and Jimmy Cliff singing about a poor man who refuses to be crushed and would rather fight than bow down, the chorus warning that the bigger they are the harder they fall. It sounds more like a rock anthem than most reggae of the time, with a strong, upbeat melody meant for a big screen.","The song is the title theme of Perry Henzell's 1972 film The Harder They Come, the first Jamaican feature film, in which Cliff played Ivan, a country boy who comes to Kingston, records a hit, becomes an outlaw and is shot by the police; the story drew on a real Jamaican gunman of the 1940s. The soundtrack album, on Island, collected songs by Cliff, Toots and the Maytals, Desmond Dekker, the Melodians and others.","The film is also about the music business: Ivan's record is bought from him for a pittance by a producer, a sharp portrait of how Kingston studios worked. The soundtrack's other songs, Rivers of Babylon, Johnny Too Bad, 007, amount to a greatest hits of early reggae. Listen to Cliff's delivery, which borrows the swagger of American soul singers.","The film played for years on the American midnight-movie circuit and became a cult classic, and many critics credit the soundtrack album with doing more than any single record to bring reggae to North America. Some hear the song as reggae adjusted for rock audiences; others argue its defiance was the real thing. It remains Cliff's defining statement.","Watch the film, then play the soundtrack album from beginning to end: it is the best one-disc introduction to early reggae. Listen in the title song for the organ riff and for Cliff's refusal to soften. Follow it with Bob Marley's Catch a Fire, released within months."],"bob-marley-catch-a-fire":["Nine songs, credited to the Wailers, that sound like reggae made for album listening: slow, heavy grooves, the bass of Aston 'Family Man' Barrett, the drums of his brother Carlton, Bob Marley's voice leading Peter Tosh and Bunny Wailer. It opens with Concrete Jungle, a song about being trapped in the city, and moves through Slave Driver and 400 Years, about the legacy of slavery, to the gentler love songs Stir It Up and Kinky Reggae.","Chris Blackwell signed the Wailers to Island in 1972 and gave them money to record an album in Kingston. He then brought the tapes to London, where the rock guitarist Wayne Perkins and the keyboard player John Bundrick overdubbed parts and the mix was reworked for rock audiences. Released in 1973, it was the first reggae album packaged and marketed like a rock record, originally in a sleeve shaped like a lighter.","Listen to the guitar solo in Concrete Jungle: that is Perkins, an American rock session player, added in London, and a sign of how the record was pitched. The original Jamaican mixes, released in 2001, sound rawer and darker. Also notice how often Tosh's baritone takes the lead or harmony line; the Wailers were still a band of three equals here.","It sold modestly at first, but critics in Britain and America took to it and it established the Wailers as an album act. Purists later objected to the rock overdubs, and the debate over the two mixes continues; most now agree the London version was the key that opened the door. It is widely listed among the greatest albums of its decade.","Start with Concrete Jungle and Slave Driver, then Stir It Up for Marley the love-song writer. If you can, compare the Jamaican mix with the familiar one. Then play Burnin', from later the same year, and Exodus to hear where Marley took the sound alone."],"lord-shorty-endless-vibrations":["A long, relentless dance record: calypso's melody and wit over a faster, heavier beat, with a pumping bass drum, horn riffs, keyboards, and a layer of hand drumming that owes as much to Indian percussion as to African. Lord Shorty's voice rides on top, less a storyteller than a leader urging the crowd on. The effect is of calypso sped up, thickened and aimed straight at the dance floor.","By the early 1970s calypso seemed to be losing young Trinidadians to American soul and to Jamaican reggae. Garfield Blackman, known as Lord Shorty, set out to renew it, and around 1973 and 1974 experimented with mixing in the rhythms of Indo-Trinidadian music, including the dholak drum, from the island's large community of South Asian descent. He called the result 'sokah', the soul of calypso; the spelling later became soca.","The Indian element is the thing most listeners miss. Trinidad's population is roughly half of South Asian descent, and Shorty's borrowing was a musical claim that the nation's carnival should include everyone. Listen to the busy percussion beneath the bass drum. Shorty later converted to Rastafari and, as Ras Shorty I, abandoned soca for a gentler style he called jamoo.","Historians generally name Shorty as the father of soca, though the style was quickly taken up by other arrangers and bands and changed fast, especially in the New York recording scene. Some calypsonians saw soca as a betrayal of the lyric tradition; its triumph at carnival was complete by the 1980s. His exact first soca record is still argued over.","Listen for the thudding bass drum and the Indian hand drums together; that combination is soca's DNA. Compare it with Mighty Sparrow to hear calypso's narrative focus giving way to rhythm. Then play Arrow's Hot Hot Hot to hear where soca went next, and a carnival soca set of today."],"bob-marley-no-woman-no-cry":["A slow, swaying seven-minute live performance. A warm organ introduction, a gently rocking beat, and Bob Marley singing to a woman in Trenchtown, the Kingston yard where he grew up, remembering friends, cooking over a fire, the hard times, and reassuring her that things will be all right. The crowd sings along; the I Threes harmonise; the organ solo in the middle sounds almost churchlike.","Recorded at the Lyceum Theatre in London in July 1975, on the first tour after Peter Tosh and Bunny Wailer had left, when the band had become Bob Marley and the Wailers with the singers Rita Marley, Marcia Griffiths and Judy Mowatt. The song had appeared in a studio version on Natty Dread in 1974, but it was this live recording, released on the album Live! and as a single, that became a British hit and made Marley a star.","The song is credited to Vincent Ford, a friend who ran a soup kitchen in Trenchtown, and his royalties are widely believed to have been Marley's way of keeping the kitchen going. The title's meaning is often misunderstood: it is consolation, not a warning. Listen to the London audience singing the refrain, a sign of how deeply reggae had reached the city's migrant and punk communities.","The live version is now almost universally preferred to the studio one; critics call it the definitive Marley performance, and it is one of his most recognised songs. Some writers argue that its gentleness made Marley safe for Western listeners, softening the militancy of his other work; others hear in it the heart of his politics: the community of the poor.","Listen to the organ introduction, then the crowd's first entrance, and let the song unfold at its own pace. Compare it with the studio version on Natty Dread. Then hear Exodus for the band at its peak in the studio."],"burning-spear-marcus-garvey":["Ten songs of slow, deep, hypnotic roots reggae, led by Winston Rodney, who performs as Burning Spear. His voice is grave, rough and incantatory; he repeats phrases like a preacher, often moaning between words. Behind him a band plays heavy, circling grooves with a grave horn section. The songs honour Marcus Garvey, the Jamaican-born leader of the Back to Africa movement, and remember slavery, from Slavery Days to Old Marcus Garvey.","Rodney came from St Ann, the same parish as Garvey and Bob Marley, and Marley introduced him to Coxsone Dodd, for whom he recorded in the late 1960s. Marcus Garvey was produced by Lawrence Lindo, known as Jack Ruby, who owned a sound system in Ocho Rios, and was released on Island in 1975. The mid-1970s were a peak of Rastafarian influence in Jamaica, and Garvey, who died in 1940, was being rediscovered as a prophet.","Garvey himself, prophesied by Rastafarians to have foreseen the crowning of an African king, is here more than a subject: the album is a kind of oratorio in his memory. Listen to Rodney's repetition, which comes from the chanting of Nyabinghi Rastafarian gatherings. A companion dub album, Garvey's Ghost, followed in 1976.","It is almost universally rated among the best reggae albums ever made; critics prize its seriousness and austerity. Some Jamaican fans disliked Island's remixing for the international market, and the original Jamaican release is sought after. Rodney has continued to tour and record for fifty years, and won Grammy awards in 2000 and 2009.","Put on the title track and Slavery Days, and listen to the horns and to Rodney's voice as a slow ritual. Do not expect a pop hook; let the repetition work. Then hear Satta Massagana and Two Sevens Clash, and Garvey's Ghost for the dub."],"peter-tosh-equal-rights":["Eight tough, militant roots reggae songs led by Peter Tosh's deep baritone. It opens with his own version of Get Up, Stand Up, the song he co-wrote with Marley, and includes Downpressor Man, a reworking of the spiritual Sinner Man; Stepping Razor, a warning about his own sharpness; and songs on apartheid, African unity and the title song, a demand for justice over peace. The band, with Sly Dunbar and Robbie Shakespeare, is taut and heavy.","Tosh had left the Wailers in 1974, partly over the way Island had promoted Marley at the others' expense. His first solo album, Legalize It, made his name as reggae's angriest voice. Equal Rights followed in 1977, during a year of political violence in Jamaica and as the struggle against apartheid in South Africa drew world attention. Tosh was often beaten by police for his outspokenness, and was murdered in a robbery at his home in 1987.","Downpressor Man takes an old African American spiritual, known in folk revival and in Nina Simone's version, and reshapes it into a Jamaican curse. Stepping Razor was written by Joe Higgs, the singer who had taught the young Wailers harmony. Listen to Sly Dunbar's precise drumming, which would soon make the Sly and Robbie rhythm section the most in-demand in the world.","Critics have always ranked Equal Rights as Tosh's best album and among the most politically direct records in reggae. It sold less than Marley's albums of the same year, and Tosh's reputation has been overshadowed by his old bandmate's. Recent writing tends to argue that Tosh's anger has aged as well as Marley's hope.","Begin with Get Up, Stand Up and compare it with the Wailers' version from Burnin', then play Downpressor Man, noticing the spiritual beneath it. Listen for the drums of Sly Dunbar. Pair it with Bob Marley's Exodus from the same year for the two roads out of the Wailers."],"bob-marley-exodus":["Ten songs split into two moods. The first side is spiritual and political: Natural Mystic, fading in from silence; So Much Things to Say; Guiltiness; The Heathen; and the seven-minute title track, a long, hypnotic call to leave Babylon. The second side is love songs and good feeling: Jamming, Waiting in Vain, Turn Your Lights Down Low, Three Little Birds, and One Love, a closing hymn of unity.","In December 1976, days before a peace concert in Kingston, gunmen attacked Marley's home at 56 Hope Road; he, his wife and his manager were wounded. He played the concert and then left for London, where he lived for more than a year. Exodus was recorded there in early 1977, with a band now including the guitarist Junior Marvin. Jamaica's political violence between the two main parties runs through the record.","One Love, the closing song, is also a medley: it weaves in part of Curtis Mayfield's People Get Ready, the Chicago gospel-soul song the young Wailers had loved, and credits it. Natural Mystic's gradual fade-in is a studio trick meant to draw you in from the distance. Listen for the difference between the two sides, which reflects an exile's divided mind.","It was Marley's breakthrough in Britain and Europe, with several hit singles, and Time magazine named it the album of the twentieth century in 1999. Some fans prefer the harder albums before it, and critics have debated whether the second side's softness is a retreat; most now hear it as a deliberate balance of struggle and comfort.","Listen to the first side in order, especially the title track, then the second side as a set of lighter songs. When One Love arrives, listen for People Get Ready. Then follow with Redemption Song and Peter Tosh's Equal Rights, from the same year."],"culture-two-sevens-clash":["Ten songs of roots reggae led by Joseph Hill, whose light, urgent voice is shadowed by two harmony singers. The rhythms are loping and brisk, the horns bright, the mood part celebration and part warning. The title song announces a coming judgment day; others honour Marcus Garvey, recall slavery and call on listeners to look to Africa. It sounds fresh and lively rather than heavy, despite its apocalyptic subject.","Culture were Joseph Hill with Albert Walker and Kenneth Dayes. They recorded the album with the producer Joe Gibbs and his engineer Errol Thompson in Kingston, released in Jamaica in 1977. The title song refers to a prophecy, attributed to Marcus Garvey, that disaster would come when the two sevens met: 7 July 1977. As the date approached, many in Kingston reportedly stayed home, and the record became a sensation.","The sevens were a real public event: shops shut, and the song fed the nervousness it described. Listen to how cheerful the tune is: prophecy delivered in a skipping rhythm. In Britain the album found an enthusiastic audience among punks, for whom 1977 was also a year of apocalyptic feeling, and the Clash and their circle were known reggae fans.","It was a hit in Jamaica and was widely admired abroad after its British release. Critics rank it with Marcus Garvey and Satta Massagana among the essential roots records. Hill continued to lead Culture until his death on tour in 2006, after which his son Kenyatta took over.","Start with the title song and I'm Not Ashamed, then listen for Hill's lead and the answering harmonies. Compare it with Burning Spear's slower gravity, and then with the Clash's first album, released a few months earlier in London's own year of the sevens."],"augustus-pablo-east-of-the-river-nile":["An instrumental album. The lead voice is the melodica, a small reed instrument blown through a tube and played on a keyboard, which Augustus Pablo makes sound reedy, plaintive and slightly exotic, in minor-key melodies that seem to come from the Middle East or Ethiopia. Beneath, a heavy, spacious reggae band rolls on, with echo on the drums and guitar. The effect is meditative, like a desert road at dusk.","Horace Swaby took the name Augustus Pablo in the early 1970s, when he made his name with the instrumental Java, and became known for his 'Far East' sound. A devout Rastafarian, he founded his own Rockers label and sound system, and worked closely with the mixing engineers of the dub scene. East of the River Nile, released on Rockers in 1977, collects his mid-1970s instrumentals and is regarded as his most complete album.","The melodica was a toy or a schoolroom instrument; Pablo made it a voice of Rastafarian longing for Africa, the 'east' of the title being Ethiopia. Listen for his use of minor scales, uncommon in Jamaican pop. He also played piano and clavinet on many of his records. His dub collaborations lie outside this album, which is closer to devotional instrumental music.","Critics treat Pablo as the most distinctive instrumentalist of roots reggae, and this album as his quiet high point, though he was always a cult figure rather than a star. He died in 1999. Later electronic and ambient musicians have often named him as a model for mood-driven, repetitive, instrumental music.","Put it on late, and listen to the melodica as a singing voice. Notice how the bass and drums leave space around it. Then play Java, his early single, and Satta Massagana for the same spiritual climate with voices. It sits well beside modal jazz."],"bob-marley-redemption-song":["Just a voice and one acoustic guitar. Bob Marley, alone, sings a slow, folk-like song about slavery, about the pirates who sold his ancestors, about freeing the mind and about how long the prophets will be killed while the world looks on. The guitar is strummed gently and plucked; the melody is plain. It closes his last studio album, Uprising, and sounds like a testament.","By 1980 Marley was the world's most famous reggae musician and had played Zimbabwe's independence celebrations. He had also been diagnosed in 1977 with a melanoma, which by 1980 had spread; he collapsed while jogging in New York that September, and died in May 1981 at thirty-six. Redemption Song, recorded that year, was his last album closer, and he performed it alone in his final concerts.","A key line of the song paraphrases a 1937 speech by Marcus Garvey delivered in Nova Scotia, about freeing oneself from mental slavery; Marley is quoting a prophet in his farewell. There is also a full-band version, released on a single, which shows how deliberately stark the album version is. The unaccompanied form places Marley in the tradition of Bob Dylan and the folk protest singers.","It was not a hit at the time, but it has become one of Marley's most revered songs, and the one most often chosen to represent him in memorials and schoolrooms. Critics call it his last will. Some reggae fans find the solo acoustic setting a concession to Western folk; most hear it as the song's natural form.","Listen alone, quietly, and let the voice carry it. Compare it with Dylan's Blowin' in the Wind and Sam Cooke's A Change Is Gonna Come, its two American kin. Then hear the band version, and finally Exodus, to see the whole arc from the Wailers to this."],"arrow-hot-hot-hot":["A soca party song: a fast, pumping beat, bright horn riffs, a chanting chorus and Arrow's upbeat voice calling on everyone to feel the heat and join the dance. The tune is simple, built from a few short phrases repeated with rising excitement, and the arrangement is clean and punchy. It is designed to make a crowd move together and shout back the title.","Arrow, born Alphonsus Cassell on the small island of Montserrat, was a leading soca musician of the late 1970s and 1980s. Hot Hot Hot was released in 1982 and spread across the Caribbean, Latin America and Europe. It became a staple of parties, sports events and carnivals, was reportedly adopted as an official song of the 1986 football World Cup in Mexico, and was covered in 1987 by Buster Poindexter, whose version became an American hit.","Montserrat, with a population of about twelve thousand, produced the best-selling soca record ever. The song's lyrics are partly in the local creole, and the rhythm is soca at its most concentrated: calypso's melody reduced to a chant, with a disco-era dance-floor pulse. Arrow ran a clothing shop in Plymouth, the island capital later buried by a volcano.","It was the first soca song to become a global hit and is the genre's best-known record outside the Caribbean. Critics sometimes dismiss it as a wedding-party novelty, and many soca fans prefer Arrow's other work or harder carnival records. As a cultural export, though, few Caribbean songs have travelled so widely. Arrow died in 2019.","Enjoy it as it was meant: loud, with a crowd. Listen to the horns and the bass drum, and how quickly the chorus becomes something to shout. Then play Lord Shorty's Endless Vibrations to hear where soca began, and a modern carnival soca set to hear where it went."],"tabou-combo-new-york-city":["A long, joyful kompa groove. Interlocking guitars play bright, circular patterns, the drums hold a steady, rolling beat with a cowbell and a prominent tom-tom, horns punch in with riffs, and the singers trade lines in Haitian Kreyòl, English and French. The song is about the lure and the strangeness of the city where so many Haitians had gone. It is danced as a couple dance, smooth rather than frantic.","Tabou Combo formed in 1968 in Pétion-Ville, near Port-au-Prince, as a young band playing kompa, the Haitian dance music created in 1955 by the saxophonist Nemours Jean-Baptiste. Under the Duvalier dictatorship many Haitians emigrated, and the band moved to New York in the early 1970s, where they played to the growing diaspora. New York City, released in 1974, probably became their biggest international hit, notably in France.","The band's new-home song captures the double life of the diaspora: the lyric mixes languages as the Haitian community in Brooklyn did. Listen to the guitars, which weave together like a small orchestra, in a way that owes something to Congolese rumba as well as to Cuban son. That guitar weave would later travel to Guadeloupe and Martinique and feed zouk.","Tabou Combo became known as the ambassadors of kompa, and this record is their signature. Critics outside Haitian music paid little attention for years; interest has grown with reissues of Haitian music from the 1970s. Kompa remains the dance music of the Haitian diaspora in New York, Miami, Montreal and Paris.","Let it play long; kompa rewards patience. Listen to the two guitars and the cowbell, then to the language switching. Then play Kassav's Zouk-La Sé Sèl Médikaman Nou Ni to hear its French Caribbean offspring, and a New York salsa record of the same year for its neighbour."],"kassav-zouk-la-se-sel-medikaman-nou-ni":["A glossy, fast dance song in Antillean Creole. A bright synthesizer and horn riff, crisp guitar patterns and a propulsive, slightly mechanical beat, layered with traditional percussion, carry a chorus that proclaims zouk as the only medicine. The singers alternate male and female leads, and the production gleams with the sound of 1980s studio technology. It is instantly danceable and full of optimism.","Kassav' formed in 1979 around Pierre-Édouard Décimus, his brother Georges, and the guitarist Jacob Desvarieux, bringing in singers such as Jocelyne Béroard and Jean-Philippe Marthély. They wanted to modernise the music of Guadeloupe and Martinique, then dominated by Haitian kompa, by combining the local gwo ka drumming and biguine with rock, funk and electronic production. The song, from 1984, became the anthem of the style they named zouk, from the Creole word for a party.","The title's joke is serious: 'medicine' means that zouk is the Antilles' answer to economic hardship and dependency on France, a claim of cultural autonomy through dance. Listen beneath the synthesizers for gwo ka drum patterns, rooted in slave-era music of Guadeloupe. Kassav' recorded largely in Paris, and the record also reached Africa, where it shaped Congolese and Cape Verdean pop.","It is widely called the song that launched zouk, and it made Kassav' the most successful band in the French Caribbean. Critics debated whether the slick production diluted Caribbean tradition; history has sided with the band, whose music spread across Francophone Africa and inspired Angolan kizomba. Desvarieux died in 2021.","Listen for the synthesizer riff and the Creole chorus, then try to hear the hand drums underneath. Compare it with Tabou Combo's New York City, its Haitian ancestor, and with Arrow's Hot Hot Hot, its soca cousin. Kassav's live recordings show the band's full power."],"wayne-smith-under-mi-sleng-teng":["A stark, bouncy dancehall record built from a cheap electronic keyboard. The rhythm is a short, jerky bass-and-drum loop, all synthesizer and drum-machine, with none of the live band of 1970s reggae. Over it the young Wayne Smith sings a nasal, cheerful melody boasting that he no longer needs a certain drug because he has sleng teng instead. It is simple, catchy and clearly the sound of a new era.","Smith and a friend, Noel Davey, found the pattern in the preset rhythms of a small Casio home keyboard, slowed it down and took it to the producer Lloyd James, known as King Jammy, in Kingston in 1985. The record exploded on the sound systems and was immediately covered, with reportedly hundreds of versions on the same rhythm. It launched Jamaica's 'digital' era, in which computer and drum-machine rhythms replaced session bands.","The song's content concerns marijuana, but its real subject is technology. Listen to how thin the sound is, and how that thinness is the point: a whole economy of cheap, fast production followed. Stories differ about which preset was used and who designed it; the rhythm may have been modelled on a rock and roll pattern, a Japanese factory's reading of American music, sent back through Jamaica.","It is widely regarded as the most influential single record in dancehall, the moment Jamaican music went digital. Older musicians complained that machines were putting players out of work; younger producers saw the freedom of cheap tools, a debate that runs parallel to hip-hop's use of drum machines. Smith died in 2014.","Listen to the bass loop first, the whole record is built on it; then to Smith's easy melody. Compare it with any 1970s roots album to hear the change from band to machine. Then play Sean Paul's Get Busy to hear where digital dancehall went, and Afrika Bambaataa's Planet Rock for a parallel machine revolution in the Bronx."],"sean-paul-get-busy":["A dancehall track at its most internationally polished: a fast, skipping drum-machine rhythm with a stuttering, syncopated snare and a sliding synthesizer hook, and Sean Paul's rapid, half-sung, half-chanted patois flowing over it. He calls on dancers to move and praises their dancing. The flow is so quick and rhythmic that it functions almost as another percussion part.","Sean Paul Henriques, from Kingston, was part of the late-1990s dancehall scene. Get Busy, from his 2002 album Dutty Rock, uses the Diwali riddim, created by the producer Steven 'Lenky' Marsden, which was used by several artists that year. Released as a single in 2003, it reached number one on the American Billboard Hot 100, and Dutty Rock won the Grammy for best reggae album.","The riddim is shared: the same Diwali rhythm supports Wayne Wonder's No Letting Go and many other records, the Jamaican tradition of versions carried into the 2000s. Its hand-clapped rhythmic pattern owes something to Indian and Trinidadian carnival music, hinted at in its name. Listen to how Paul's words fall between the claps.","It was a landmark for dancehall's commercial success in America, and Sean Paul became one of the best-selling Jamaican artists after Marley. Some Jamaican critics and fans regard his success as pop-dancehall at the expense of grittier deejays; others credit him with opening the market for a generation, and his influence on later global pop, from Rihanna to Afrobeats, is plain.","Listen first to the rhythm alone, then to the voice as another drum. Compare Wayne Wonder's No Letting Go on the same riddim. Follow with Under Mi Sleng Teng to hear the digital rhythm's origin, and with Missy Elliott, whose productions shared this rhythmic world."],"goldsmith-planet-of-the-apes":["A film score that sounds like a modernist concert piece dropped into a Hollywood blockbuster. Goldsmith writes jagged, near-atonal lines for brass and strings, then surrounds them with odd percussion: stainless-steel mixing bowls, a ram's horn, the squealing Brazilian cuíca, woodwinds fed through an echo machine. Long stretches are nearly silent. The cue everyone remembers is the hunt in the cornfield, where piano, drums and shrieking horns drive the apes' charge in hammering, lopsided rhythms.","Franklin J. Schaffner's film, from Pierre Boulle's novel, opened in early 1968, the year of 2001 and of a Hollywood studio system coming apart. Goldsmith, trained partly in Miklós Rózsa's film-scoring classes and hardened in live television, wanted the music to sound as alien as the planet. Twentieth Century Fox let him avoid any lush theme; the result earned an Academy Award nomination and showed that a mass-market science-fiction film could carry the language of Bartók and the post-war avant-garde.","Goldsmith borrowed the twelve-tone method, where a fixed row of all twelve notes generates the melody, but used it loosely, as colour rather than dogma. Listen to how rarely the full orchestra plays together: most cues are chamber music for a few odd instruments. The echo machine on the woodwinds repeats each phrase a fraction later, so a single flute seems to answer itself from across a canyon.","Critics in 1968 barely noticed the music; film-music writers soon treated it as a turning point, and it is now routinely cited as one of the most radical scores ever used in a mainstream American film. Some argue its percussion inventions matter more than its tone rows. Later Apes films, including Goldsmith's own sequel work and Danny Elfman's 2001 remake score, are measured against it.","Start with the main title: no tune, just scratching, clicking and single notes placed in silence, and ask what that does to your sense of danger. Then hear the hunt cue and notice there is no melody at all, only rhythm. Compare Bartók's Music for Strings, Percussion and Celesta. Seek a complete-score reissue rather than the short original LP."],"goldsmith-chinatown":["A noir score built on a strange ensemble: four pianos, four harps, a small string section, percussion, and one solo trumpet. The pianos and harps are often prepared or played inside, their strings scraped and plucked to make dry, rattling textures. Over this unsettled ground the trumpet sings a slow, smoky love theme that sounds like 1930s Los Angeles remembered through heat haze. It is short, sparse, and nearly all atmosphere.","Roman Polanski's film, produced by Robert Evans and written by Robert Towne, originally had a score by Phillip Lambro that was rejected late. Goldsmith was brought in and wrote and recorded his replacement in roughly ten days. He had been scoring films and television for two decades by then, and this became one of his most admired works, nominated for an Academy Award in a year it lost to Nino Rota and Carmine Coppola's music for The Godfather Part II.","The love theme is not really romantic: it drifts without resolving, as if the trumpet cannot find the end of the sentence, which fits a story in which nobody gets what they want. The trumpet solos were played by the studio veteran Uan Rasey. Listen for the pianos being struck and brushed on their strings, a trick from the avant-garde used here to suggest a city with something rotten under the floorboards.","Because it was a rushed replacement, Chinatown is often held up as proof that the right sound can matter more than time spent. Critics now place it among the finest American film scores, and the trumpet theme has been imitated in countless detective films and parodies. Some listeners find the album too thin away from the pictures; others treat it as a model of economy.","Play the main title and follow only the trumpet, then listen again for the rustling pianos and harps underneath, which are doing most of the work. Compare Miles Davis's improvised trumpet for Ascenseur pour l'échafaud, another film noir carried by one horn. The Varèse Sarabande reissue pairs it well with Herrmann's Vertigo in a single evening."],"goldsmith-the-omen":["A horror score in which a chanting choir becomes the monster. The main title, Ave Satani, sets a mock-liturgical Latin text for chorus in heavy, pounding blocks, answered by brass and drums, like a black mass sung by a cathedral choir. Elsewhere Goldsmith writes a tender family theme for strings and woodwinds, so the sweet music of the household keeps being overrun by the chant. The choir is the sound people remember.","Richard Donner's film followed The Exorcist into a mid-1970s boom in religious horror. Goldsmith, by then one of Hollywood's busiest composers, persuaded the producers that a choral approach would frighten more than any orchestral effect. The score won him the Academy Award for Original Score, the only Oscar of a career that brought eighteen nominations, and Ave Satani was also nominated as Best Original Song, an unusual honour for a piece sung in Latin.","The Latin of Ave Satani was assembled to invert Christian prayer, turning phrases from the Mass against themselves, so a choir sounds devout while singing the opposite of devotion. Goldsmith's rhythmic setting, with syllables hammered on off-beats, owes a clear debt to Orff's Carmina Burana and to Stravinsky's choral writing. Listen too for how the gentle family theme is quietly bent into minor as the film goes on.","The Omen was a hit and its chant entered popular culture, quoted and parodied in horror films, trailers and cartoons for decades. Some critics called it crude beside Goldsmith's subtler work; others argue the Oscar rewarded the right score, since it invented a sound that genre composers still reach for. The irony that his only Oscar came for a Satanic chant is part of the lore.","Hear Ave Satani first, then play Orff's O Fortuna immediately after and note how much Goldsmith took, and how much darker he made it. Then find the quieter family cues and follow how they are corrupted. A complete-score release gives the full arc; the original album is shorter but has the essentials."],"goldsmith-alien":["A score of cold air and distant signals. It opens with soft, echoing trumpet and flute calls over hushed strings, as if something is breathing in the dark. Goldsmith colours the orchestra with exotic sounds: the serpent, an old bass wind instrument, a didgeridoo, conch shells, woodwinds fed through an echo unit. Much of it is quiet and suspended; when the creature strikes, dissonant brass and percussion explode and then vanish back into stillness.","Ridley Scott's film was a low-budget-looking project that became a landmark of science-fiction horror. Goldsmith wrote a lyrical main title that Scott found too romantic; he rewrote it, and even then Scott and editor Terry Rawlings replaced several cues with music from Goldsmith's earlier Freud score and ended the film with Howard Hanson's Second Symphony. The composer was unhappy, and the conflict became one of the best-known stories of a director overruling a scoring.","Hanson's Romantic Symphony over the end credits is a real classical concert work, not written for the film, and it gives the survivor's escape a warmth Goldsmith's own music withheld. Listen for the didgeridoo's low drone in the planet-surface scenes: an ancient instrument used to suggest something older than humans. The echoing trumpet in the opening recalls the offstage calls of Mahler and Ives.","For years audiences knew only the film's edited version of the music. When the complete score as Goldsmith conceived it was released, many critics argued it was more coherent than what reached the screen, and debate continues over whether Scott's collage served the film better. Either way, its hush and texture shaped a generation of science-fiction horror scores.","Start with the main title and count how long you wait for a tune; it never quite arrives. Then listen to the final minutes of Hanson's Symphony No. 2 and imagine it after the terror. Compare Ligeti's Atmosphères, which Kubrick used in 2001. Seek the complete-score edition to hear Goldsmith's intentions side by side with the film version."],"sakamoto-the-last-emperor":["A sweeping score in which a Western orchestra, Chinese instruments and electronics trade places. Sakamoto's main theme rises slowly on strings with the ache of a late-Romantic adagio; elsewhere Chinese plucked and bowed instruments carry court music, and a restless string piece, Rain, drives forward over insistent rhythm in one of the film's great scenes. Music by David Byrne and Cong Su fills other stretches, so the album is a three-way portrait of a century.","Bernardo Bertolucci's epic about Puyi, the last Qing emperor, was the first Western feature allowed to film inside Beijing's Forbidden City. Sakamoto, who also played the Japanese officer Amakasu, was asked to compose much of the score on short notice. He, Byrne and Cong Su shared the Academy Award for Original Score, a Golden Globe and a Grammy, the first Oscar won by a Japanese composer.","Listen for how Sakamoto uses Western harmony exactly when the story turns to Puyi's Westernisation and imprisonment, and Chinese colours for the court he loses. Rain is scored almost as a Baroque concerto for strings, a steady ground under swirling figures, which lends a private domestic crisis the gravity of history. The same melodic shapes as his Mr. Lawrence theme surface in the slower cues.","The Oscar made Sakamoto a film composer in world demand. Some critics found the shared authorship uneven and preferred Sakamoto's cues to Byrne's; others enjoy the patchwork. Sinologists have questioned the film's chinoiserie, and the music sometimes shares that charge, but the main theme and Rain remain concert favourites and stand beside Morricone's epics of the same decade.","Begin with the main title and Rain back to back: one long-breathed, one relentless. Then compare Morricone's The Mission, another orchestral epic where a Western composer writes for a colonial encounter. Sakamoto's later trio and solo-piano arrangements let you hear the tunes stripped of spectacle."],"sakamoto-the-revenant":["Almost the opposite of a Hollywood score: long, slow, very quiet. Low strings hold soft chords that change so gradually they feel like weather; electronic hums and crackles sit under them like wind through ice. There are few themes, and the main cue repeats a small, rising string phrase with enormous patience. The effect is less narration than breathing, the sound of a body surviving in a frozen landscape.","Alejandro González Iñárritu's frontier survival film was Sakamoto's first project after treatment for throat cancer diagnosed in 2014. He worked with the German electronic artist Alva Noto, his long-time duo partner, and Bryce Dessner of The National contributed further music. Iñárritu wanted music that felt like the natural world rather than commentary on it, and the score was recorded around the film's long single-take shooting style.","Many of the most important sounds are not notes at all: bowed noise, filtered hiss and resonant drones from Alva Noto's electronics, blended so closely with strings that you cannot tell where the orchestra ends. Listen to the main theme's repetitions: each one is slightly different in voicing, a minimalist technique borrowed from Pärt and Feldman more than from film tradition.","Reviewers praised the score's restraint and its fusion with the film's sound design, though some found it too static to stand alone. It drew major award nominations and is now heard as the start of Sakamoto's late style, continued in his album async, where illness, time and natural sound became his subjects.","Listen in the dark, loudly enough to hear the low end, and give the main theme its full length. Compare Arvo Pärt's Cantus in Memoriam Benjamin Britten, Jóhann Jóhannsson's Arrival, and Brian Eno's On Land. Then return to Merry Christmas, Mr. Lawrence and hear how far the composer travelled."],"zimmer-the-lion-king":["An animated-film score that marries a Hollywood orchestra to South African choral singing. Lebo M.'s voices call and answer in Zulu over drums and strings, lending the savannah scenes grandeur and warmth. Zimmer's own orchestral cues are big and direct: a stampede of pounding percussion and brass, a mournful string lament for the death of a father, and a triumphant homecoming. The songs by Elton John and Tim Rice sit alongside, but the score carries the story.","Disney's 1994 feature was a huge commercial hit. Zimmer, a German-born synthesizer specialist who had moved from London to Los Angeles, had scored Rain Man and Driving Miss Daisy, and brought in Lebo M., a South African singer then living in exile, to create the choral sound. Zimmer has said the story of a son losing his father touched him because he lost his own father as a child. The score won the Academy Award.","The opening call that starts the film is not in the orchestral score at all: it is Lebo M. improvising in Zulu, and Zimmer built the music around his voice. Listen to the stampede cue for synthesizers doubling the orchestra, a Zimmer hallmark that thickens the sound beyond what live players alone make. The father's theme returns, transformed, in the final ascent to Pride Rock.","The score won the Oscar, and the choral sound became central to Julie Taymor's 1997 stage musical. Some critics call the African elements decorative, a Western frame around borrowed voices; others credit Lebo M.'s central role as genuine collaboration. For many listeners born around 1990 it was their first orchestral music.","Start with the opening, then jump to the stampede and listen for where the synthesizers hide inside the orchestra. Follow the father's theme from its first quiet appearance to the final return. Compare Holst's Jupiter for a similar broad, singable orchestral nobility, and then the Broadway cast album to hear the score's afterlife."],"zimmer-inception":["A score of weight and pulse rather than melody. Its famous sound is an enormous, low, blaring brass chord, sustained and distorted, that became known as the braam. Beneath it Zimmer lays ticking string ostinatos, synthesizer pulses and the guitar of Johnny Marr. The closing cue, Time, is the emotional heart: four slow chords repeating and swelling, from a single piano to full orchestra, a minimalist build rather than a tune.","Christopher Nolan's heist film set in layered dreams was a major blockbuster. Zimmer, running a large studio of composers in Santa Monica, wrote with Nolan closely from the script stage, as he had for The Dark Knight. The brass sound was recorded with a large low-brass section and heavily processed. Trailers used it, and within two years the braam was the sound of nearly every action trailer in Hollywood.","The whole score is built from Edith Piaf's Non, je ne regrette rien, the song the dreamers use as a wake-up signal. Slow the song down, as time slows in each dream level, and its brass accompaniment becomes the braam; its rising triplets become the pulsing strings. Zimmer confirmed the derivation, and once heard it cannot be unheard.","Inception was nominated for the Academy Award and lost to Trent Reznor and Atticus Ross's The Social Network. Critics split over its bluntness: some say it narrowed film music toward loud, textural pulse and away from melody; others that it redefined what a score could be. The origin of the braam itself was disputed by trailer composers, and the argument continues.","Hear Time first, and notice how little changes and how much it moves you anyway; that is a minimalist trick. Then play the Piaf song and a brass cue side by side, slowing the song in your head. Compare Philip Glass's Koyaanisqatsi and the brass chorales of a Bruckner adagio."],"zimmer-interstellar":["A score dominated by a church organ. Zimmer writes slow, repeating figures for organ and piano that build over minutes into vast, trembling chords, with strings, woodwinds, choir and electronics swelling around them. There is a tick-like pulse in the docking and wave scenes, and a gentle, rocking piano theme for the father and daughter. It sounds at once sacred and mechanical, like a cathedral travelling through space.","Before Nolan told him anything about the film, he gave Zimmer a single page about a father leaving his child and asked for a day's music in response; the small piano and organ piece that resulted became the seed of the score. The organ was the four-manual instrument of the Temple Church in London, recorded with a large ensemble. Zimmer said he wanted the organ as a symbol of science and faith, both of which try to reach beyond the human.","The organ's opening chords deliberately recall the first bars of Richard Strauss's Also sprach Zarathustra, which Kubrick used in 2001: A Space Odyssey, the film Interstellar answers. Listen in the planet-of-waves scene for a steady ticking: each tick marks a passage of time on Earth, where one hour on that planet equals seven years, so the pulse is the film's physics made audible.","Nominated for the Oscar, it lost to Alexandre Desplat's Grand Budapest Hotel. Some critics found the organ overwhelming the dialogue in cinemas, and the film's sound mix was argued over; the album, heard alone, won many admirers, and the organ cues are now performed in concert. It is often called Zimmer's most personal score.","Play the track titled for the first day of the story and hear the seed of the score. Then put on Strauss's Zarathustra opening and Ligeti's Atmosphères, and hear the two halves of 2001 that Zimmer fuses. Listen through good speakers or headphones that can hold the organ's lowest pipes."],"zimmer-dune":["A score that tries to sound like no culture on Earth. A solo female voice keens and ululates, bagpipes are processed into a vast battle drone, drums and metallic percussion hammer through desert ceremonies, and custom-built instruments and synthesizers moan like wind over dunes. There is little conventional orchestra and almost no Western harmony. The sound of the sandworm's approach, a deep rushing roar with a voice above it, is what stays with you.","Denis Villeneuve's adaptation of Frank Herbert's novel had been Zimmer's dream project since he read the book as a teenager, and he turned down Christopher Nolan's Tenet to do it. He and his team spent months building sounds before any scene was cut, avoiding any borrowed ethnic idiom. Vocalist Loire Cotler created much of the vocal language. The score won the Academy Award, Zimmer's second after The Lion King.","The bagpipes, a Scottish instrument, are used for House Atreides, a noble family facing ruin, and treated electronically until they stop sounding Scottish. Listen for how the voice's long, wavering lines borrow nothing from any real musical tradition's scales yet feel ancient. Vangelis's Blade Runner, on which Villeneuve's own sequel had built, echoes in the synthesizer swells.","Admirers called it a leap into invented sound; detractors found it relentless and more sound design than music. It won the Oscar, Golden Globe and Grammy, and the sequel score continued its world. It is often set against Howard Shore's Lord of the Rings as two opposite answers to how to score an invented world: new sounds against leitmotif.","Listen at night with good bass, starting with the sandworm and ritual cues. Then hear the calm desert music and notice how the voice becomes a guide. Compare Vangelis's Blade Runner and Stockhausen's Gesang der Jünglinge for voices dissolved into electronics, then Shore's Lord of the Rings for the opposite approach."],"shore-the-lord-of-the-rings":["Three film scores forming one continuous symphonic cycle, woven from dozens of recurring themes. There is a lilting tin-whistle tune for the Shire and its hobbits, a heroic brass fellowship theme, a cold, rocking figure for the Ring's evil, the solo Hardanger fiddle of Rohan, and wordless or Elvish-texted choirs for the elves. Themes pass between instruments and moods as characters change, so the music itself tells the story across more than ten hours of film.","Peter Jackson filmed all three parts of Tolkien's novel at once in New Zealand; Shore, a Canadian best known for dark scores for David Cronenberg, wrote over several years as the films were edited. The music was recorded largely in London with the London Philharmonic Orchestra and several choirs, with sung texts in Tolkien's invented languages translated for him. It won Academy Awards for the first and third films.","Shore works like Wagner in the Ring cycle: a theme for a person, a place or an idea returns in altered form, sometimes buried in the bass. Listen for the fellowship theme appearing only in fragments once the fellowship breaks apart, whole again only at the end. The Ring's own theme often sounds beneath a character's theme when that character is tempted.","Hailed as one of the largest achievements in film music, the scores were studied theme by theme in Doug Adams's book and turned into Shore's six-movement Lord of the Rings Symphony. Some critics find the Wagner debt too obvious; others say the scale earns it. Live-to-projection concert tours have made it a staple for orchestras and a doorway to classical music for new audiences.","Start with the Shire music, then the Rohan fiddle, then the Mines of Moria choir, three different worlds. After that, follow the fellowship theme through all three films. Compare Wagner's Das Rheingold for the model, and seek the expanded complete recordings if you want every thread; otherwise the three original albums tell the story."],"shore-crash":["A cold, glittering chamber score for an unusual group: several electric guitars, harps and woodwinds. The guitars play clean, chiming, slowly shifting figures, the harps shimmer beside them, and woodwinds add breath and chill. There are no big themes, only overlapping patterns that circle like traffic on a flyover at night. It sounds metallic, erotic and detached, exactly the emotional temperature of the film.","David Cronenberg's adaptation of J. G. Ballard's novel about people aroused by car crashes caused a scandal at Cannes in 1996, where it won a special jury prize. Shore had scored nearly all Cronenberg's films since The Brood in 1979, and here chose to drop the orchestra entirely. The choice of electric guitars, instruments of rock and car stereos, placed the music inside the film's world of machines and motorways.","The overlapping guitar patterns owe much to Steve Reich's phasing and to Glenn Branca's guitar orchestras, but Shore keeps them soft and never lets them build to a climax, so the music withholds release the way the characters do. Listen for the harps doubling the guitars' attacks, blurring where one instrument ends and the other begins.","The film divided critics sharply; the music was widely admired even by those who disliked the movie, and it is often named Shore's most original score. It is less known than his fantasy work, and some listeners are surprised the same composer wrote Middle-earth, but among Cronenberg admirers it is a cult favourite.","Play it after dark on a drive, then hear Reich's Electric Counterpoint and notice the family resemblance. Contrast it with Shore's Lord of the Rings to hear how completely a composer can change skin. Sonic Youth's Daydream Nation shows the rock side of the same guitar sound."],"elfman-batman":["A dark, gothic superhero score. Elfman's main theme rises from the low brass in a brooding, minor-key march, turns into a surging, heroic climb, and falls back into shadow. Choirs add a cathedral gloom, and a waltz twists the Joker's scenes into carnival menace. The orchestra is thick and romantic, full of pounding timpani and horn calls, and the sound feels like the film's sets: tall, black and ornamental.","Tim Burton's film was the blockbuster of summer 1989. Elfman, lead singer of the Los Angeles band Oingo Boingo, had no formal training in orchestration and had scored only a few films, including Burton's Pee-wee's Big Adventure and Beetlejuice. The studio was nervous; Elfman worked with orchestrators Steve Bartek and Shirley Walker to realise his demos. Prince recorded a separate song album for the film, which sold heavily.","Elfman's hero was Bernard Herrmann, and the Batman theme's stepwise, spiralling rise in the brass owes much to Herrmann's Vertigo and to the swashbuckling of Korngold. Listen for how the theme almost never completes itself triumphantly in the major, reflecting a hero who is never at peace. Shirley Walker later adapted its spirit for the animated series.","Doubters thought a rock musician could not write for orchestra; the score's success silenced most of them, though claims about how much his orchestrators contributed recurred for years. It set the dark-orchestral model for superhero films until the pulse-driven scores of the 2000s, and its main theme is still recognised within two notes.","Start with the main title, following the low brass as it climbs out of the dark. Then hear Herrmann's Vertigo prelude for the source of its spiralling motion, and Williams's Superman theme for the brighter opposite. The expanded reissue gives the full score beyond the short original album."],"elfman-edward-scissorhands":["A fairy-tale score of wordless children's voices, celesta and gently swinging waltzes. The main theme is a slow, music-box melody over pizzicato strings, taken up by a choir singing on a single vowel. The most famous cue, the Ice Dance, accompanies snow falling from Edward's ice sculpture in swirling runs of strings and voices. The music is bittersweet throughout, a Christmas story told by a lonely outsider.","Tim Burton's fable of an unfinished artificial man in pastel suburbia was the third Burton–Elfman collaboration. Elfman, still fronting Oingo Boingo, wrote a score that leaned less on brass and more on the colours of Tchaikovsky's ballets and Hollywood Christmas music. He has often named it the favourite of his own scores, and its sound became the template for Burton's later fantasies.","The celesta, the tinkling keyboard instrument, was introduced to the orchestra's public by Tchaikovsky in The Nutcracker's Sugar Plum Fairy, and Elfman uses it here in the same spirit: a bell-like sound for magic and snow. Listen for how the waltz accompaniment keeps skipping a beat of expected emphasis, making the dance slightly unsteady like its hero.","Warmly received, the score has become one of the most imitated sounds in fantasy film and in Christmas advertising; the Ice Dance and the choral main theme appear on countless compilations. Some find the whimsy saccharine, but it is widely thought Elfman's most individual work and the source of the Burton sound.","Hear the Ice Dance first and follow the choir's swirl. Then play Tchaikovsky's Waltz of the Flowers and Prokofiev's Cinderella midnight scene for the ballet ancestry. The choir sounds best on good headphones, where you can hear the breathy children's voices distinct from the strings."],"elfman-the-simpsons-theme":["Under a minute of zany, bright orchestral music. A choir sings the family's name on a rising, wide-leaping phrase; then a bustling tune on brass and woodwinds skips through odd intervals and sudden shifts of harmony, like a cartoon running late. Midway a saxophone takes a short solo, and the whole thing ends with a comic crash. It is one of the most-heard pieces of orchestral music ever written.","When Matt Groening's animated family moved from short sketches to its own series on Fox in December 1989, Groening asked Elfman for a theme with a retro, early-television feel. Elfman, then scoring Batman, wrote it quickly and has since said it was the most listened-to piece he ever wrote. The show's longevity has broadcast it in thousands of episodes across dozens of countries.","The tune uses an old-fashioned scale with a raised fourth note, the so-called Lydian mode, which gives it a slightly off-kilter, sunny wrongness; that is why it sounds happy and odd at once. The saxophone solo in the opening was varied from episode to episode as Lisa plays it, so there are many versions. Elfman has cited Nino Rota's Fellini scores among his models.","Few theme songs have lasted like it; it has been rearranged for bands, orchestras and punk groups. Critics praise its economy: a character sketch of a whole family in thirty seconds. Some argue it is Elfman's most important work precisely because it is so short and so heard. Few pieces of orchestral music reach so many ears every week.","Hum the sung opening and notice the odd leap at the top. Then compare Rota's music for Fellini's 8½, where circus brass and melancholy mingle in the same way. Seek Elfman's own concert arrangement to hear it with a full orchestra and without the dialogue."],"greenwood-phantom-thread":["Lush, old-fashioned romance, almost continuous under the film. Greenwood writes flowing piano over full strings, slow sighing melodies, and graceful waltz-like motion that recalls 1950s film music. The main theme returns in many guises: tender, then suffocating, then eerie. Unlike his earlier scores for Paul Thomas Anderson, there is little harsh dissonance; the unease lives inside the sweetness, as the romance inside the film curdles.","Anderson's film about a controlling London couturier in the 1950s was the fourth Greenwood–Anderson collaboration. Greenwood, Radiohead's guitarist and a trained violist, wanted a sound close to period romantic cinema and to the lush string arrangements of that decade. The film uses more music than most, the score running under long scenes almost like an opera orchestra. It earned an Academy Award nomination.","The music is so beautiful that it is easy to miss how often it will not resolve, holding a chord a beat too long or moving in a direction the ear did not expect. Listen for a repeated rising piano figure that becomes more insistent as the heroine gains power over the dressmaker. The score's model is less Hollywood than the chamber-like romance of Brief Encounter and Rachmaninoff.","Critics praised it as Greenwood's warmest work and many called it the best score of its year. Some thought its continuous lushness smothered the film, which is perhaps the point. It has since been performed in concert with the film and is often named with There Will Be Blood as the peak of his collaboration with Anderson.","Hear the main title and then a later cue with the same theme, noticing the change of mood with the same notes. Compare Rachmaninoff's Second Piano Concerto and Herrmann's Vertigo, both stories of fixation. Then return to There Will Be Blood to hear how far Greenwood travelled between them."],"greenwood-the-power-of-the-dog":["An anti-western score. Instead of wide open-prairie chords, Greenwood uses a cello plucked with fingers like a banjo, its quick, nervous figures sounding like a trapped animal. A player piano adds mechanical, off-balance runs, strings slide in unsettling bends, and a French horn gives a lonely, bruised call. The music feels tight and airless even as the film shows huge Montana landscapes.","Jane Campion's adaptation of Thomas Savage's novel, set on a 1920s Montana ranch, explores a cruel cowboy with hidden desire. Greenwood, trained as a violist and experienced in writing for strings, scored it in England. Campion wanted to avoid the familiar Americana of Copland and the classic westerns. The score was nominated for the Academy Award, losing to Hans Zimmer's Dune.","The banjo-like cello is a joke with a sting: the instrument of European concert halls imitating a folk instrument of the American West, just as the film's cowboy has a hidden cultured past. Listen for the player piano's slightly uneven timing, a machine imitating a person and failing, and for the strings slipping between notes when menace rises.","Critics praised its refusal of western cliché and its menace; some felt it more interesting as texture than as music alone. It is often set against Copland's open-prairie Americana, which it deliberately rejects, and sits with Greenwood's other work for Anderson and Lynne Ramsay as film music that behaves like contemporary concert composition.","Listen to the opening cues for the plucked cello, then play Copland's Billy the Kid to hear what Greenwood refused. Compare Penderecki's Threnody for the sliding strings. It works best heard in full: the cues are short and cumulative, so let the claustrophobia build."],"gudnadottir-chernobyl":["A score made almost entirely from the sounds of a real nuclear power station. Low hums, metallic clanks, throbbing pulses, hissing air and distant doors are layered and processed into slow, dense drones; a faint female voice rises through them like a ghost. There are no melodies in the usual sense, only sound that grows in pressure and occasionally breaks into a heavy mechanical rhythm. It is closer to industrial ambient than to orchestral scoring.","The HBO miniseries about the 1986 disaster was a global success. Guðnadóttir, an Icelandic cellist based in Berlin, had worked on Jóhann Jóhannsson's scores and scored Sicario: Day of the Soldado after his death. For Chernobyl she spent time in the decommissioned Ignalina nuclear plant in Lithuania, where the series was filmed, recording the building itself. The score won an Emmy.","The voice you hear is Guðnadóttir's own, processed until it sounds like breath inside a machine. Listen for where the drones swell just before disaster in the story: the building is literally singing its danger. Her method, turning a place into its own music, descends from Pauline Oliveros's deep listening and from musique concrète, the Paris tradition of composing with recorded sound.","The score won an Emmy and a Grammy and was acclaimed as a new model of television scoring, where music and sound design fuse. Some viewers barely noticed it as music, which is its intent; critics discussed whether it should be called composition at all. It made her name the same year as Joker.","Listen in a quiet room at moderate volume and let your ears adjust; the details emerge after a few minutes. Compare Brian Eno's On Land and Jóhannsson's Arrival, then Penderecki's Threnody for a Cold War ancestor of sound as dread. Heard back to back with Joker, it shows her range."],"gudnadottir-joker":["A cello-centred score for a descent into violence. A single cello plays long, groaning lines in its low register, slow and wavering, joined gradually by strings and deep brass until the orchestra becomes a wall of pressure. The most famous cue, played as Arthur dances in a public bathroom, is a slow, mournful melody that seems to move his body. The palette is narrow, dark and inward, a character's mind turned into sound.","Todd Phillips's film re-imagined the Batman villain as a mentally ill failed comedian in a decaying 1980s Gotham. Guðnadóttir wrote music from the script before shooting, and some was played on set; Joaquin Phoenix improvised the bathroom dance to her cello piece. The score won the Academy Award, Golden Globe, BAFTA and Grammy, making her only the third woman to win a scoring Oscar.","Guðnadóttir plays the cello herself, and much of the score is built from single cello takes layered and slowed, so a whole orchestra can feel like one tortured voice. Listen for how the bathroom dance cue never quite settles into a regular pulse: Phoenix's movements follow the music's hesitation, not the other way round.","The film's sympathy with its violent protagonist was fiercely debated; the score was near-universally praised. Some critics note that its extended, drone-like approach owes much to her work with Jóhannsson and to the minimalists, and that its restraint helped change what a comic-book film could sound like, far from Elfman's gothic Batman.","Listen to the bathroom dance cue, then Arvo Pärt's Fratres and Górecki's Third Symphony first movement for the slow-building lament tradition behind it. Compare Elfman's Batman for Gotham scored the opposite way. Headphones help you hear how many layers of one cello there are."],"giacchino-the-incredibles":["A brassy retro score in the style of 1960s spy films. Blaring trumpets and trombones, bongos, tight snare drum and swinging bass drive action cues, with jazz-band punches and sudden stops. The superhero family's theme struts like a Las Vegas show band; slower cues bring in lush strings and mysterious vibraphone. It is a celebration of the big-band orchestral sound of 1960s cinema and television.","Brad Bird's Pixar film about a family of retired superheroes was set in a stylised early-1960s world. John Barry, composer of the James Bond scores, was first attached but withdrew, and Giacchino, known then for video-game music and the television series Alias, stepped in. It was his first major feature; he recorded with a large brass-heavy orchestra to capture the period sound.","The score is full of swing-era jazz gestures written for a symphony orchestra: shout choruses, brass stabs, walking basses. Listen for the family theme hidden in fast action cues, rhythmically disguised as a brass riff. The model is Barry's James Bond scores, Henry Mancini's television themes and Ellington's big band.","Critics praised it as a joyful pastiche that never became parody, and it launched Giacchino's career in film. Some prefer his later, more personal work; others consider it his most purely enjoyable, and its brassy jazz sound became a Pixar and Marvel reference point. The sequel in 2018 brought the same brass back, with the family theme intact.","Put on the action cues and just listen to the brass: how it hits, stops and swings. Then hear an Ellington big-band number and Bernstein's West Side Story dances for jazz-meets-orchestra ancestors. It is best heard loud, with the drums cranked up."],"giacchino-up":["A score built almost entirely on a single waltz. The theme, first heard as a playful, bouncy tune for piano and orchestra, returns as the backbone of a four-minute wordless montage showing a couple's whole marriage, then in slower, sadder and grander forms through the adventure. The orchestra is lush but light: strings, woodwinds, piano and gentle brass. The montage cue, Married Life, is what most listeners remember.","Pete Docter's Pixar film follows an old widower who flies his house to South America with balloons. Giacchino had become Pixar's go-to composer after The Incredibles and Ratatouille. Docter asked him to make the waltz carry the story's heart, so the theme had to work as joy, grief and memory. The score won the Academy Award and a Grammy. Every cue grows from that one tune.","Listen closely to Married Life: the waltz is played cheerful and bouncy while the couple are young, slows and sinks into minor when they learn they cannot have children, then brightens again, and finally thins to a lonely piano. It is theme and variations used as biography. The bouncy early version is based on a simple piano waltz, like one from a turn-of-the-century parlour.","Up's opening montage is often called one of the most moving sequences in animated film, and the score's role widely credited. Some critics find Giacchino sentimental; others say the sentiment is earned by the single-theme discipline. The waltz is now a concert-hall encore and a wedding favourite. It also made Giacchino a household name beyond animation.","Watch or hear Married Life with attention to how the waltz changes key and speed. Then follow the theme through the adventure music. Compare Satie's Gymnopédie No. 1 for a slow waltz of loneliness, and Rota's La strada for circus melancholy in the same spirit."],"badalamenti-blue-velvet":["A score that swings between lush, dark Romantic strings and dreamy pop. The main title is a slow, gloomy orchestral piece in a Russian style, all heavy strings and low brass, suggesting a nightmare under small-town sweetness. Elsewhere a floating, reverb-soaked ballad, Mysteries of Love, sung by Julee Cruise, introduces a sound of weightless yearning. The record mixes these with 1950s and 1960s pop songs that the film turns sinister.","David Lynch's film was his first with Badalamenti, a New York songwriter and arranger hired initially to coach Isabella Rossellini's singing of the title song. When Lynch could not afford the rights to This Mortal Coil's recording of Song to the Siren, he and Badalamenti wrote Mysteries of Love together, with Julee Cruise singing. Lynch asked for an orchestral score that sounded like Shostakovich.","The main title is close to a Shostakovich pastiche, and Lynch reportedly had the composer's late symphonies in mind; listen for the heavy string unisons and brooding brass. Mysteries of Love is the seed of the whole Lynch–Badalamenti world: its floating reverb and slow synthesizer pads grew into Twin Peaks four years later.","Blue Velvet shocked critics in 1986 and has since become a canonical American film. The score was less noticed than the film's pop songs but is now valued as the beginning of one of cinema's great composer–director partnerships. Some find the Shostakovich imitation too close; others hear it as knowingly placed, like the vintage songs.","Hear the main title, then a slow movement of Shostakovich, and notice the resemblance. Then play Mysteries of Love and imagine it enlarged into Twin Peaks. The original album, with its pop songs, gives the film's full musical world. Listen after dark."],"badalamenti-twin-peaks":["Slow, dreamlike music for a small-town murder mystery. The main theme is a deep, reverb-drenched guitar melody over soft synthesizer pads and a gently throbbing bass. Laura Palmer's Theme begins as a dark, sinister chord pattern on synthesizer and swells into an aching, romantic melody before sinking again. Julee Cruise's floating vocal songs and cool finger-snapping jazz fill out a record that sounds like a dream half-remembered.","David Lynch and Mark Frost's television series premiered on ABC in April 1990 and became a phenomenon. Lynch and Badalamenti worked side by side: Lynch described a scene, a mood, a character in the woods, and Badalamenti improvised at the keyboard until the image appeared in sound. The soundtrack album was a hit, and the main theme won a Grammy for pop instrumental performance.","Laura Palmer's Theme was composed in a single sitting: Badalamenti began with low, dark chords as Lynch described a girl in the woods, then broke into the love theme as Lynch described her approaching, and back into the dark as Lynch said she was retreating. That improvised shape is the structure of the piece. The music's slow, suspended harmonies anticipated dream pop and trip-hop.","Critics instantly recognised the score as something new for television, where themes were then brisk and bright. It has since influenced dream pop, shoegaze, ambient and film music, and the 2017 revival brought it back. Some argue it is more a mood than a score; admirers say that is the achievement.","Play Laura Palmer's Theme and listen for the moment it opens from darkness into melody. Then hear the main title and Julee Cruise's Falling, the same theme with voice. Compare Brian Eno's ambient records and Kate Bush's Hounds of Love for kindred dreaminess. Listen late at night."],"mica-levi-under-the-skin":["Unnerving, alien string music. Violas and violins slide slowly between pitches, detuned slightly so the notes rub; a ticking percussion pulse and a tense three-note figure underlie the scenes where the alien lures men into a black void. There are almost no melodies in the normal sense. A few moments of eerie, fragile beauty surface, then disappear. The sound is the film's alien presence.","Jonathan Glazer's film stars Scarlett Johansson as an alien who hunts men in Glasgow, much of it shot with hidden cameras. Levi, a London musician then in their mid-twenties known for the experimental pop band Micachu and the Shapes and trained at the Guildhall School, had never scored a film. Glazer sought something with no reference points, and Levi spent months experimenting with sounds.","Many of the most alien effects are ordinary instruments played slightly out of tune or slid slowly between notes, a technique from Penderecki's Threnody and from spectral and microtonal composers. Listen for the three-note figure that marks the seduction scenes; it becomes more distorted and sluggish as the alien begins to feel human.","Critics hailed it as one of the most original film scores of the century. It won prizes at European and critics' awards, and Levi was soon in demand, later earning an Oscar nomination for Jackie. Some viewers found it unbearable, which is arguably its success. Many composers now name it as a key influence.","Listen alone in the dark and let it unsettle you. Then play Penderecki's Threnody and Scelsi's Anahit for the tradition of sliding and microtonal strings. Compare Guðnadóttir's Chernobyl for another score where sound and dread merge. Give it the full length of the album, not a single cue."],"mica-levi-jackie":["A score of grief played on strings that sag and bend. Levi writes a slow, stately theme that sounds like a grand funeral march, then lets the strings slide downward between notes so the grandeur drains away. Flute and clarinet add thin, breathy lines. It feels like an elegant public mask slipping to reveal a private collapse, the subject of the film.","Pablo Larraín's film follows Jacqueline Kennedy in the days after John F. Kennedy's assassination in November 1963. Levi, after Under the Skin, was asked for music that could carry both public ceremony and private breakdown. Levi scored it in London; it earned an Academy Award nomination. The film stays close to Natalie Portman as the widow, in long close-ups, and the music often occupies the silence where she does not speak, doing the work of an inner voice.","The sliding strings suggest pitch being slowed down, as if a recording were running out of power. It connects to Samuel Barber's Adagio for Strings, broadcast after Kennedy's death in 1963 and heard at the time as America's music of mourning; Levi's Adagio seems to fall apart as it proceeds. Listen for the moment in the main theme where the pitch sinks.","Critics praised its daring, and some said the film's unusual power came from the score. Others found it too insistent. It confirmed Levi as a major voice in film music, and it is now often paired with Under the Skin as proof of her range. The score is sometimes cited as proof that experimental technique can carry a mainstream period drama.","Hear the main title first and listen for the downward slides. Then play Barber's Adagio for Strings and compare the two American mournings. Finally revisit Under the Skin to hear a similar technique turned to horror. Notice how short most cues are: grief arrives in fragments."],"reznor-ross-the-social-network":["An electronic score of humming synths, glitchy pulses and soft piano. The opening cue, Hand Covers Bruise, sets a simple, lonely piano melody over a low, distorted synthesizer drone that grows in menace. Elsewhere pulsing electronic rhythms drive coding and deal-making. A crunched electronic version of Grieg's In the Hall of the Mountain King accompanies a rowing race. It is cool, modern, and the opposite of lush orchestra.","David Fincher's film about the founding of Facebook asked Trent Reznor of Nine Inch Nails and his collaborator Atticus Ross for their first feature score. They worked in their Los Angeles studio with synthesizers and processed piano, creating a sound close to their ambient and industrial work. The score won the Academy Award and Golden Globe, beating Hans Zimmer's Inception, and changed how Hollywood viewed electronic musicians.","The Grieg arrangement is a joke with depth: the Henley regatta, a symbol of old wealth, set to a 19th-century tune played by machines, making the Winklevoss twins sound quaint as the digital world overtakes them. The approach recalls Wendy Carlos's synthesized classics. Listen to Hand Covers Bruise for how the piano stays simple while the drone underneath grows harsher.","The Oscar win was a watershed, the first for an electronic score by rock musicians, and opened doors for many others. Some traditionalists grumbled; most critics saw it as long overdue. Its piano-over-drone sound has been widely imitated. Its sound, minimal and synthetic, now seems as characteristic of the 2010s as brass fanfares were of the blockbusters of the late 1970s, and a generation of film composers from indie and electronic backgrounds followed through the door it opened.","Start with Hand Covers Bruise and notice the drone rising under the piano. Then hear the Grieg and the original. Compare Tangerine Dream's Phaedra and Vangelis's Blade Runner for the synthesizer lineage, and Brian Eno's ambient albums for the quiet side."],"reznor-ross-gone-girl":["Soothing electronic music that gradually turns sick. Soft synthesizer tones, gentle pulses and warm pads sound at first like relaxation music, but underneath there are wobbling pitches, hisses and small distortions that make the calm feel false. As the film's plot unfolds, the music drifts further from comfort. It is ambient music with a smile that hides something. The whole record stays at a hush.","Fincher's adaptation of Gillian Flynn's thriller about a marriage and a disappearance was his third collaboration with Reznor and Ross. Fincher described hearing music in a spa that was trying too hard to calm him, and that insincere comfort became the brief. The pair created the score in their Los Angeles studio, blending synthesizers, processed sounds and piano. It earned a Golden Globe nomination.","Listen for how the synth pads are slightly out of tune with one another, making the sweet chords feel queasy. The score's model is ambient music of the 1970s, especially Eno's Music for Airports, but with that music's calm deliberately poisoned. Minimalist repetition, from Reich and Glass, gives the cues their patience.","Critics praised the score's inventiveness; some found it too subtle to notice. Many consider it among Reznor and Ross's best work and a key example of how electronic scoring can be ironic as well as atmospheric. Its ironic use of soothing sound has been imitated in psychological thrillers and prestige television, where a calm surface signals danger, and it is often set beside Crash and The Social Network as a study in cold, withheld emotion.","Hear a gentle early cue and ask what feels off. Then play Eno's Music for Airports and compare the two calms. Reich's Music for 18 Musicians offers the minimalist repetition in its purest form. Listen at low volume, as you would in a waiting room, and see how long it takes to feel uneasy."],"reznor-ross-soul":["A score of two worlds. Reznor and Ross provide the music of the afterlife realms: floating, luminous synthesizers, soft pulses and gentle, childlike melodies. Jon Batiste provides the New York world with live jazz piano, bass and drums. The film's story crosses between them, so the score does too, from Batiste's lively improvisation to the electronic calm of the Great Before.","Pete Docter's Pixar film follows a jazz pianist whose soul is separated from his body. Reznor and Ross worked for the first time on an animated film, writing in their Los Angeles studio; Batiste, a New Orleans pianist and bandleader, composed and performed the jazz. The score won the Academy Award, Golden Globe and BAFTA, a rare triple for a collaboration between electronic and jazz musicians.","The electronic music avoids the harsh edges of Reznor and Ross's thrillers, using gentle arpeggios and soft sounds close to children's music, which suits the unborn souls. Listen for how Batiste's jazz piano sometimes glides into the electronic world, reshaping itself in synth sound. The division mirrors the film's question about what makes life worth living.","Critics welcomed the double score as a model of collaboration and praised both halves, though some thought the jazz was underused. It widened Reznor and Ross's reputation beyond darkness and was a milestone in bringing jazz musicians into Pixar's world. It also showed that composers from industrial rock could write for children with warmth, and its jazz cues introduced many young viewers to live improvisation, much as older animated films introduced classical music.","Hear the electronic cues first, then Batiste's jazz, then the moments they meet. Compare Bill Evans's Peace Piece for a jazz pianist's meditative side, and Giacchino's Up for another Pixar story about a life's meaning. Then return to The Social Network to hear the same machines turned toward unease."],"vangelis-1492-conquest-of-paradise":["Epic synthesizer music with a choir. The main theme, Conquest of Paradise, is a slow, stately hymn for massed voices over pounding drums and swelling synth strings, singing words that sound like Latin. Other cues mix synthesized pipe organs, bells and exotic instruments to suggest the Old World and the New. The sound is grand and direct, a stage for a historical epic built mostly from electronic instruments.","Ridley Scott's film on Christopher Columbus, released for the 500th anniversary of his first voyage, reunited him with Vangelis ten years after Blade Runner. Vangelis, who by then had long used synthesizers as an orchestra of his own, performed almost everything himself, with an added choir. The film was a commercial disappointment, but the main theme became enormously popular in Europe.","The choir's text is not real Latin but sounds like it, a way of suggesting ecclesiastical grandeur without meaning, like the cathedrals of Columbus's Spain. Listen for the synthesizer string sections, which are not meant to imitate an orchestra perfectly but to create a larger-than-real sound, the lineage of Wendy Carlos and Tangerine Dream.","Critics found the film weak and the score bombastic, but the theme took on a life of its own, becoming a sports anthem, notably in German boxing, and a staple of ceremonies. Some listeners find it kitsch; others admire its simple force. It is heard far more often than the film is seen.","Play the main theme loudly and listen for the drums under the choir. Then compare Orff's O Fortuna, the model for choral grandeur over percussion, and Vangelis's own Blade Runner for his quieter side. Wendy Carlos's Switched-On Bach shows the electronic lineage."],"irving-berlin-alexanders-ragtime-band":["A brisk march-song in verse-and-chorus form, about two and a half minutes in most early recordings. The chorus is an invitation shouted across a street: come on and hear the best band in the land. It quotes a bugle call and, cheekily, a scrap of Stephen Foster's “Swanee River” played in ragtime. For all the title's promise there is very little real syncopation in it; the energy comes from the repeated short phrases and the sense of a crowd being summoned.","Berlin was twenty-three, a Russian-Jewish immigrant from the Lower East Side working as a staff songwriter on Tin Pan Alley, the cluster of New York publishers who sold songs as sheet music for parlour pianos. He could not read music and picked out melodies in a single key. Vaudeville singers, above all Emma Carus, took the song on tour in 1911, and its sheet music sold on a scale that made Berlin famous across America and Europe almost overnight.","The song is about ragtime rather than an example of it. Scott Joplin's rags are full of offbeat accents; Berlin's tune sits squarely on the beat and lets the lyric do the advertising. That gap is the point: it sold the idea of Black dance music to a white middle-class public in a safe, march-like package. Listen for the bugle call and the Foster quotation, both nods to older American parlour music.","It was the biggest popular hit of its decade and helped trigger the ragtime craze in Europe as well as America. Later writers have been less kind, noting that Black composers who actually built ragtime saw nothing like its rewards. Jazz musicians nevertheless kept it: Bessie Smith and Louis Armstrong both recorded it, turning Berlin's four-square tune into something genuinely swinging, and the 1938 film that borrowed its title kept the song in circulation.","Start with an acoustic recording from 1911 to hear the brassy vaudeville original, then jump to Bessie Smith's 1927 version, where a blues singer bends every phrase. The contrast tells you what jazz did to Tin Pan Alley. Listen for the bugle call near the start of the chorus and try counting how rarely the tune actually syncopates."],"gershwin-someone-to-watch-over-me":["A slow ballad in the standard thirty-two-bar AABA pattern, with a wistful introductory verse. The voice rises by small steps and then sinks back, as if hoping and then correcting itself; underneath, the harmony keeps sliding through chromatic side-steps that give the tune its ache. Ira Gershwin's lyric is plain-spoken, almost shy: a woman admitting she needs protecting, in the everyday idiom of 1920s New York.","Written for Oh, Kay!, a musical comedy with a book by Guy Bolton and P. G. Wodehouse about bootleggers on Long Island during Prohibition. Gertrude Lawrence, the English star, sang it on Broadway in November 1926. George Gershwin was at the height of his double career, two years after Rhapsody in Blue, writing a hit show most seasons while pushing into concert music. Ira was becoming one of the wittiest lyricists in the business.","The song was reportedly first conceived as a fast, rhythmic number; George slowed it right down while playing it through, and the ballad appeared. Onstage Lawrence sang it to a rag doll, which turns the plea into something lonely and slightly comic rather than purely romantic. Listen to the verse: many singers skip it, but its uncertain, wandering harmony sets up the chorus's relief.","It outlived Oh, Kay! almost at once and became one of the most recorded Gershwin songs. Jazz musicians valued its harmonic motion; singers valued its intimacy. Ella Fitzgerald, Frank Sinatra and Linda Ronstadt each made it their own, and it has drifted from torch song to lullaby depending on tempo. Debate is mostly about speed: slower readings emphasise the melancholy, while the original show pacing was lighter.","Hear Ella Fitzgerald's version from her Gershwin Song Book with Nelson Riddle, which includes the verse, then a straight instrumental jazz reading to hear how the chords carry it without words. Notice how often the melody repeats a note while the harmony shifts beneath it; that is where the feeling comes from."],"kern-show-boat":["A two-act musical spanning forty years in the lives of the performers on a Mississippi showboat, from the 1880s to the 1920s. Its score mixes operetta waltzes, ragtime, cakewalks, spirituals and Tin Pan Alley ballads. The songs everyone knows are “Ol' Man River”, sung by the Black dockworker Joe as the river rolls past indifferent to human trouble, “Make Believe”, “Can't Help Lovin' Dat Man” and “Bill”. The sound is lush, full-orchestra Broadway with a Black chorus as its conscience.","Jerome Kern and Oscar Hammerstein II adapted Edna Ferber's 1926 novel, and Florenz Ziegfeld produced it at his own theatre, opening in December 1927. Ziegfeld was famous for leggy revues; this was something else, a story show taking on racial injustice, a mixed-race marriage broken up by Mississippi law, gambling and abandonment. Broadway audiences in the late 1920s mostly expected musical comedy or imported Viennese operetta, and Show Boat borrowed from both while pointing elsewhere.","Listen to the opening chorus, “Cotton Blossom”. Its tune, slowed and turned upside down, is commonly heard as the seed of “Ol' Man River”, so the river song grows out of the labour song. “Bill”, one of the most admired numbers, was not new: Kern had written it with a lyric by P. G. Wodehouse for an earlier show and it had been dropped. Julie's “Can't Help Lovin' Dat Man” quietly gives her away as Black, because the servants recognise it.","It was a hit and has been revived constantly, but it has never been a settled text. The opening lyric's racial slur was softened in stages and later removed; numbers were added and cut for films (1929, 1936, 1951) and revivals. Paul Robeson made “Ol' Man River” his own and rewrote its words as protest. Scholars still argue whether the show challenges or reproduces stereotype. Hal Prince's 1994 production reframed it strongly around race.","Seek John McGlinn's 1988 complete recording with Frederica von Stade and Teresa Stratas, which restores the original score. Then watch Paul Robeson sing “Ol' Man River” in the 1936 film. Listen for how the river theme returns at the end of Act 2, the older characters grown grey while the tune has not changed."],"gershwin-i-got-rhythm":["A fast, cheerful AABA song built from almost nothing: the chorus melody is four notes going up and down in short, clipped phrases, with the lyric “I got rhythm, I got music, I got my man, who could ask for anything more?” The bounce comes from where the words land against the beat. Beneath it the harmony runs round a quick, circling chord pattern that repeats in each A section, and a bridge that climbs through a chain of chords before snapping home.","Written for Girl Crazy, a George and Ira Gershwin musical that opened in October 1930 as the Depression deepened. The song made a star of Ethel Merman in her Broadway debut. The pit band reportedly included young jazz players who would soon lead swing bands, among them Benny Goodman and Gene Krupa. George went on to write his own concert Variations on the tune for piano and orchestra in 1934.","The song's chords became a kind of public property. Jazz musicians call them “rhythm changes”, and hundreds of new tunes were written over them, from Charlie Parker's “Anthropology” to Duke Ellington's “Cotton Tail” and Thelonious Monk's “Rhythm-a-ning”; even the Flintstones theme uses them. The printed song has a two-bar tag at the end, making thirty-four bars; jazz players quietly trimmed it to thirty-two. Merman famously held one long note through a whole chorus.","It was an instant hit and never left the repertory. Its long afterlife is almost entirely a jazz story: after the blues, rhythm changes are the most important chord sequence in the music, and learning to improvise over them is a rite of passage. Some purists prefer to hear the song as Gershwin's show tune and bristle at its reduction to a practice framework; most musicians see the two as one continuous history.","Hear a vocal version first, Merman or Judy Garland, then a bebop tune on the same chords, such as Parker's “Anthropology”, and try to follow the hidden song underneath. Finally try Gershwin's own “I Got Rhythm” Variations. Listen for the bridge: it is where the song, and every tune based on it, changes key."],"cole-porter-night-and-day":["A love song of obsession. The verse repeats a single note over and over, the “beat, beat, beat of the tom-tom”, while the harmony shifts beneath it. The chorus then pours out in long chromatic phrases, restless and circling, longer than the usual thirty-two-bar song. The rhymes are dense and internal, and the feeling is less romance than a fixed idea that will not leave the singer alone, day or night.","Cole Porter wrote it for Fred Astaire in Gay Divorce, which opened on Broadway in 1932; it was Astaire's last stage show before Hollywood, and the 1934 film version, The Gay Divorcee, kept the song while dropping almost everything else. Porter, a wealthy Yale-educated Midwesterner who had spent the 1920s in Paris and Venice, was by then the most sophisticated wit on Broadway, specialising in songs that sounded both elegant and faintly illicit.","The verse is a trick: one note repeated for many bars, like a drum or a dripping tap, while the chords move around it. It is the musical picture of obsession, and it sets up the chorus's sudden flood of movement. Porter told several stories about the inspiration, including a Moroccan call to prayer; treat them as Porter being Porter. Astaire's limited vocal range shaped how low and conversational the chorus sits.","It became Porter's best-selling song and the title of his heavily fictionalised 1946 film biography, with Cary Grant. Singers love and fear it: the length and chromatic line make it hard to phrase, and the verse is often dropped, which sacrifices the whole effect. Sinatra recorded it several times across his career, each version slower and darker, which tells you how the song's meaning shifted from wit toward torch song.","Listen first to Astaire's own recording for the lightness it was written for, then Ella Fitzgerald's version in her Cole Porter Song Book, which keeps the verse. Count how many times the singer repeats the same note in that verse before the chorus breaks free."],"rodgers-my-funny-valentine":["A slow ballad in a minor key, unusual for a love song. The melody moves in short, halting steps, while inside the chords a single line slides down by semitones, bar after bar, like something slowly giving way. Lorenz Hart's lyric teases the beloved for an unphotogenic face and a mouth “a little weak”, then asks him not to change a hair. The song ends by climbing into a sudden major-key brightness.","Richard Rodgers and Lorenz Hart wrote it for Babes in Arms, a 1937 musical about teenagers putting on a show to avoid being sent to a work farm. Mitzi Green sang it to a character named Valentine. Rodgers and Hart were Broadway's most productive team of the 1930s; Hart's lyrics, bittersweet and self-mocking, were already shadowed by the alcoholism and loneliness that would end the partnership.","That sinking inner line, one semitone at a time against a held minor chord, is a stock device, but Rodgers stretches it across the whole first phrase so it becomes the song's emotional engine. The lyric is also a small subversion: a love song that lists the beloved's faults. In the show, “Valentine” was a man's name, and the song's gender often flips depending on who sings it.","Babes in Arms was a hit but the song's real life began in 1950s jazz. Gerry Mulligan's quartet with Chet Baker recorded it as an instrumental in 1952, Baker then sang it in a small, unguarded voice, and Miles Davis made it a set piece for over a decade. It is now among the most recorded standards of all. Some find it over-exposed; the best versions still sound private.","Start with Chet Baker's sung version from Chet Baker Sings (1954), then Miles Davis's long live reading from 1964. Listen for the descending inner line under the opening words; once you hear it, you hear it in every version."],"kern-all-the-things-you-are":["A broad, slow love song of thirty-six bars, longer than the norm, whose melody moves mostly in long, even notes while the harmony travels through several keys. Each phrase begins in a new place and the tune follows, so the song feels like a journey rather than a repeated shape. Hammerstein's lyric is devotional, almost hymn-like: the beloved is the promised kiss of springtime, the dearest things one knows.","Jerome Kern and Oscar Hammerstein II wrote it for Very Warm for May, a 1939 Broadway show that closed after a few weeks. Kern was in his mid-fifties, working mostly in Hollywood, and reportedly thought the song too complex to be popular. It escaped the flop at once through radio and dance-band recordings, and within a few years jazz musicians had made it their own, which by some accounts Kern disliked, as he preferred his songs played as written.","The best moment is a pun only a musician's ear catches. At the end of the bridge the melody holds one note; the harmony underneath quietly reinterprets it so that the same pitch now belongs to the home key, and the song slides back without a join. The famous introduction used in many jazz versions is not by Kern at all; it came from bebop players in the 1940s, and Charlie Parker built his own tune, “Bird of Paradise”, over the chords.","Its route from failed show to indispensable standard is typical of the songbook. Jazz players love its modulations: it is one of the great pieces for learning how chords move between keys. Some critics hear Hammerstein's lyric as stiff next to the music; singers who slow down and trust the long notes make it work.","Hear Ella Fitzgerald's Jerome Kern Song Book version for the song, then Charlie Parker's “Bird of Paradise” for what jazz did with it. Listen for the held note at the end of the bridge: it is the song's hinge."],"arlen-over-the-rainbow":["A slow, yearning ballad that opens with an octave leap on the word “Some-where”, a jump that makes the voice reach before it has said anything. The tune then descends in small steps and climbs again, a shape like a rainbow's arc. A light middle section, “Someday I'll wish upon a star”, answers with bouncing, childlike phrases. In the film it lasts under three minutes and is sung by a girl in a Kansas farmyard.","Harold Arlen and the lyricist Yip Harburg wrote it for MGM's The Wizard of Oz (1939), sung by sixteen-year-old Judy Garland as Dorothy. Arlen, a Buffalo cantor's son, had made his name with blues-inflected Cotton Club songs such as “Stormy Weather”; Harburg was a socialist whose Depression song “Brother, Can You Spare a Dime?” had become an anthem. The hope in the lyric is personal and also a Depression-era dream of somewhere better.","The song was almost cut. Studio executives reportedly felt it slowed down the Kansas opening and that a star should not sing in a barnyard; it was restored and won the Academy Award for Best Original Song. The octave leap on “Some-where” returns at the end, so the song finishes on the same reaching gesture that opened it. Harburg's lyric carefully avoids ever saying what is over the rainbow.","It became Garland's signature and, arguably, the best-known American song of the century. Its meanings multiplied: a wartime song of home, a gay anthem after Garland's death in 1969, and in Israel Kamakawiwoʻole's 1993 ukulele medley a new, gentle life for a later generation. Some singers bury it in sentiment; Garland's own film take is simpler and younger than her later concert versions.","Watch the film scene first, then Garland's 1961 Carnegie Hall performance to hear what twenty years did to it. Listen for the octave leap and notice how both the verse-free film version and later versions depend on how far the singer lets that leap reach."],"rodgers-pal-joey":["A two-act musical set in the cheap Chicago nightclub world, following Joey Evans, a charming, lying singer-dancer who takes up with a rich, older married woman, Vera, who buys him a club of his own. Many of the numbers are staged as floor-show routines, pointedly tacky. The standout is Vera's “Bewitched, Bothered and Bewildered”, a waltz-song in which a sophisticated woman admits, with wit and dismay, that she has fallen for a heel.","Rodgers and Hart built it with John O'Hara from his New Yorker stories, written as letters from Joey. It opened in December 1940 with Gene Kelly as Joey and Vivienne Segal as Vera; George Abbott directed. At a time when Broadway heroes were decent young men, a musical centred on a cad and a kept arrangement was daring. It was Rodgers and Hart's last major success before Rodgers teamed with Hammerstein.","Listen to the lyric of “Bewitched”: many reprise verses are frank about sex in a way radio would not allow, so the version most people know is cleaned up. The nightclub numbers, like “Chicago” and “The Flower Garden of My Heart”, are deliberately bad, parodies of third-rate floor shows, written by people who knew exactly how good songs are made. Hart's lyrics everywhere undercut feeling with a wisecrack.","The New York Times critic Brooks Atkinson famously asked whether one could draw sweet water from a foul well. The 1952 revival, after the war and after Rodgers and Hammerstein had changed the rules, was far better received and ran longer than the original. The 1957 film with Frank Sinatra softened Joey. Critics now see the show as the first adult musical anti-hero, ancestor of Cabaret and Company.","Start with “Bewitched, Bothered and Bewildered”, ideally in a recording with the full verse sequence, then listen for the parody floor-show numbers. Compare Joey with Macheath in The Threepenny Opera: two charming louts, one satirised by Weill and Brecht, one watched almost fondly by Rodgers and Hart."],"rodgers-oklahoma":["A two-act musical set in Indian Territory in 1906, on the eve of statehood. The plot is small: will Laurey go to the box social with the cowboy Curly or the brooding farmhand Jud? The songs, including “Oh, What a Beautiful Mornin'”, “The Surrey with the Fringe on Top”, “People Will Say We're in Love” and the title number, are folk-plain in melody and each moves the story or reveals character. The first act ends in a long dream ballet.","It was the first collaboration between Richard Rodgers and Oscar Hammerstein II, adapted from Lynn Riggs's play Green Grow the Lilacs for the struggling Theatre Guild. It opened on 31 March 1943, in wartime, when a story about American land and community carried real weight. Agnes de Mille, fresh from choreographing Copland's cowboy ballet Rodeo, created the dances. It ran over five years and 2,212 performances.","It opens against every Broadway habit: no chorus line, no overture-then-girls, just a man singing unaccompanied offstage on an empty stage while a woman churns butter. De Mille's dream ballet does real dramatic work: Laurey's fears about Jud are danced, not sung, and the dancers replace the actors. Jud is given a dark solo, “Lonely Room”, that makes the villain briefly human. The title song was added out of town, where the show was called Away We Go!","It was a sensation and redefined the Broadway musical as a “book musical” in which song, dance and story are integrated; it received a special Pulitzer. Its Decca cast recording helped make original-cast albums a standard product. Later critics note the show's cheerful erasure of the Native Americans in whose territory it is set; Daniel Fish's 2019 revival reframed it in darkness and gave Jud more sympathy.","Hear the opening number first, then the dream ballet with the full orchestra, which the 1955 film preserves. Listen for how “People Will Say We're in Love” is a love song disguised as a denial: the characters confess by listing what not to do."],"bernstein-on-the-town":["Three sailors on twenty-four hours' shore leave in New York in wartime: Gabey searches for “Miss Turnstiles”, a subway poster girl; Chip and Ozzie are picked up by a lusty cab driver and an anthropologist. The score opens with “New York, New York”, a blast of brass and bustle, and includes the rueful “Lonely Town”, the comic “I Can Cook Too” and the bittersweet farewell “Some Other Time”. Long dance episodes carry much of the story in symphonic jazz.","The show grew out of Fancy Free, the 1944 ballet Bernstein and Jerome Robbins made about three sailors. Bernstein, twenty-six and newly famous as a conductor, joined his friends Betty Comden and Adolph Green, who wrote book and lyrics and played two leads; George Abbott directed. It opened in December 1944 with an integrated cast and the Japanese-American dancer Sono Osato as the all-American Ivy, while Japanese Americans were still being held in camps.","Behind the fun is wartime fear: the sailors have one day, and “Some Other Time” is a song about postponing life because tomorrow may not come. The dance music is serious Bernstein, full of shifting metres and Stravinskian brass; you can hear it as a sketch for West Side Story. The 1949 film with Gene Kelly and Frank Sinatra cut most of the score, which the studio judged too highbrow.","It was a hit and made Bernstein a Broadway name overnight, though his conducting mentor Serge Koussevitzky reportedly disapproved of the detour. It has been revived less than it deserves, partly because the dance music needs large forces. Modern productions stress its diversity and melancholy. Most musicians now rate the three dance episodes, played as a concert suite, among Bernstein's best orchestral writing.","Try Michael Tilson Thomas's complete recording, then the Three Dance Episodes as a concert piece. Listen to “Lonely Town” for how a Broadway ballad can turn a crowded city into an empty one, and to “Some Other Time” for the show's aching heart."],"rodgers-carousel":["Set in a Maine fishing town in the 1870s, the show follows Billy Bigelow, a violent carnival barker, and Julie Jordan, the mill worker who marries him. Billy dies during a robbery and returns from the afterlife for one day to see his daughter. Instead of an overture, the curtain rises on the “Carousel Waltz”, a whirling pantomime of the fairground. The score holds “If I Loved You”, the seven-minute “Soliloquy” and “You'll Never Walk Alone”.","Rodgers and Hammerstein's second show adapted Ferenc Molnár's Hungarian play Liliom, moving it to New England. It opened in April 1945, as the war in Europe was ending, and a song promising a grieving young woman that she would never walk alone landed in a nation full of widows. Rodgers later called it his favourite of all his musicals.","“If I Loved You” is a love song that never admits it; Billy and Julie spend a whole scene describing what they would do if they were in love. The “Soliloquy” is essentially an operatic scena: Billy imagines a son, then realises it might be a daughter and his whole mood turns. The “Carousel Waltz” is really a small tone poem, several waltzes chained together while the fairground assembles in mime.","It was a success, and many critics now consider it the most musically ambitious Rodgers and Hammerstein score. The drama is harder: Julie's line that a blow can feel like a kiss troubles modern audiences, and recent productions struggle with how to stage it. “You'll Never Walk Alone” escaped entirely, via Gerry and the Pacemakers' 1963 single, to become the anthem of Liverpool Football Club.","Hear the “Carousel Waltz” first, orchestra alone, then the bench scene with “If I Loved You”. The 1994 Lincoln Center/National Theatre production has a strong recording. Compare Billy's Soliloquy with a Puccini monologue and notice how close Broadway came to opera."],"irving-berlin-annie-get-your-gun":["A fictionalised story of the sharpshooter Annie Oakley and her romance and rivalry with the marksman Frank Butler in Buffalo Bill's Wild West Show. The score is almost a greatest-hits album of one composer: “There's No Business Like Show Business”, “Doin' What Comes Natur'lly”, “They Say It's Wonderful”, “I Got the Sun in the Morning”, “The Girl That I Marry” and the competitive duet “Anything You Can Do”. The sound is brassy, direct, built for Ethel Merman's trumpet voice.","Rodgers and Hammerstein produced it and Herbert and Dorothy Fields wrote the book. Jerome Kern was originally to compose the score but died suddenly in November 1945; Berlin, then in his late fifties and not used to writing to someone else's book, took it on. It opened in May 1946, just after the war, and ran over a thousand performances; Merman made Annie a career-defining role.","Berlin's songs are tuned to Merman's straight-ahead delivery: short vowels, big sustained notes at the end of lines. The story goes that he almost cut “There's No Business Like Show Business” because he misread a colleague's reaction as dislike. “Anything You Can Do” is a battle of the sexes that ends with neither side winning, and the plot's resolution, Annie losing on purpose, has troubled audiences since.","It was a huge hit, and “Show Business” became Broadway's unofficial anthem. Later revivals had to deal with its caricatured Native American material; the 1999 Broadway version, revised by Peter Stone, cut “I'm an Indian Too” and reframed the ending. Critics have long argued whether Berlin's score is a real theatre score or a string of hits; either way, nearly every number is a standard.","Hear the original cast album with Merman, then the 1950 film with Betty Hutton. Listen to “Anything You Can Do” for how Berlin makes a song out of pure contradiction. Count how many songs you already know before you have heard the show."],"cole-porter-kiss-me-kate":["A backstage musical about a touring company performing a musical version of The Taming of the Shrew, in which the divorced leads fight on and off stage. Scenes alternate between the modern theatre and Shakespeare's Padua, so the score switches between sophisticated Porter numbers such as “Another Op'nin', Another Show”, “So in Love” and “Too Darn Hot”, and mock-Elizabethan ones like “I've Come to Wive It Wealthily in Padua”. Two gangsters stop the show with “Brush Up Your Shakespeare”.","Cole Porter was widely thought finished by 1948: a 1937 riding accident had left him in constant pain and his recent shows had flopped. Sam and Bella Spewack's book, inspired by the Lunts' famously bickering Shrew, gave him a frame for every style he knew. It opened in December 1948 with Alfred Drake and Patricia Morison, ran over a thousand performances and won the first Tony Award for Best Musical.","Porter, known for songs about sophistication, here writes pastiches. “Wunderbar” is a parody of a Viennese operetta waltz, sung by the leads nostalgically remembering the corny show they met in. “So in Love” is written in a minor key and yearns rather than glitters. “Brush Up Your Shakespeare” packs Shakespeare's titles into outrageous rhymes; listen for how many plays Porter manages to make filthy.","Its triumph restored Porter's reputation, and many consider it his best score. Its sexual politics, borrowed from Shakespeare, are the main debate: Kate's final speech of submission is framed ironically in some productions and played straight in others. The 1999 Broadway revival was a hit, and the show still plays widely. The 1953 film was shot in 3-D.","Start with the original cast album with Alfred Drake, then compare “Wunderbar” with a Johann Strauss waltz. Listen for the move from the brassy backstage songs to the mock-Italian Shrew numbers; Porter is playing with style like a costume box."],"nat-king-cole-nature-boy":["Under three minutes of hushed, strange balladry. Nat King Cole sings over lush strings a story about “a very strange enchanted boy” who wandered far and taught that the greatest thing is simply to love and be loved in return. The melody is in a minor key, winding and slightly exotic, and Cole sings it softly, with the clear diction and warm, low voice that made him the model crooner. There is no rhythm section swing at all.","The song came from eden ahbez, a Brooklyn-born wanderer with long hair and sandals who lived rough in Los Angeles and is often called a proto-hippie; he got the manuscript to Cole through backstage contacts. Capitol released Cole's recording in 1948, and it went to number one. Cole had been known as the pianist-leader of a jazz trio; this record, a singer in front of strings, pushed him decisively into pop singing.","The melody drew a plagiarism claim from the Yiddish composer Herman Yablokoff, who said it copied his song “Shvayg mayn harts”; the matter was settled with a payment. Some listeners also hear a resemblance to the slow movement of Dvořák's Piano Quintet in A. None of that dims the record's real trick: how little Cole pushes, so the odd lyric sounds like a confidence rather than a sermon.","It was a surprise hit and remains one of Cole's defining records. Jazz musicians took up the minor melody: Miles Davis, John Coltrane and many others recorded it. For Cole, a Black star in segregated America, the move from jazz trio to string-backed pop made him hugely successful and also drew criticism from jazz fans who missed the pianist. Now it is heard as a crossover landmark.","Hear Cole's original, then Coltrane's 1965 version for how jazz musicians darkened it. Listen to how Cole places consonants: the words are perfectly clear even at a whisper. Then hear a King Cole Trio side like “Straighten Up and Fly Right” to meet the pianist he left behind."],"loesser-guys-and-dolls":["A musical fable of Broadway gamblers and the women who put up with them. Nathan Detroit needs a venue for his floating crap game; his fiancée Adelaide has waited fourteen years; the high-roller Sky Masterson bets he can take a Salvation-Army-style missionary, Sarah Brown, to Havana. The score includes “Fugue for Tinhorns”, “A Bushel and a Peck”, “Adelaide's Lament”, “If I Were a Bell”, “Luck Be a Lady” and “Sit Down, You're Rockin' the Boat”.","Frank Loesser, who had written wartime and Hollywood hits, composed music and lyrics; Abe Burrows wrote the book after an earlier draft was discarded, drawing on Damon Runyon's stories of New York low life. It opened in November 1950 with Robert Alda, Vivian Blaine, Sam Levene and Isabel Bigley. It ran for more than a thousand performances; the 1955 film starred Marlon Brando and Frank Sinatra.","The opening “Fugue for Tinhorns” is not a real fugue but a three-voice round, three gamblers each insisting on a different horse, their lines stacking up like a Baroque canon in Runyonese. Loesser's lyrics everywhere copy Runyon's odd formal speech, with no contractions and elaborate politeness among crooks. The story goes that the 1951 Pulitzer for drama was intended for the show but blocked because Burrows had been named before anti-Communist investigators.","It is often called the perfect musical comedy: every song fits character and moment, and no number is filler. Revivals have been consistent successes, notably 1992 on Broadway with Nathan Lane and Faith Prince, and several major London productions. Some critics find its comedy of lovable crooks and nagging fiancées dated; its craft is rarely questioned.","Start with “Fugue for Tinhorns” and try to follow each of the three voices, then hear “Adelaide's Lament”, where a showgirl reads a psychology textbook and finds she has a cold from not being married. The 1992 revival cast recording is bright and complete."],"sinatra-in-the-wee-small-hours":["Sixteen slow songs about loneliness and lost love, sung at night-time tempos. Sinatra, now a baritone with a dark, conversational voice, is backed by Nelson Riddle's small-orchestra arrangements: strings, woodwinds and a soft rhythm section, with plenty of space. The songs come from the songbook's best writers, including Rodgers and Hart, Arlen, Ellington, Porter and Hoagy Carmichael, and the pacing never lifts: a single mood held for the length of an LP.","It was recorded for Capitol in Hollywood in early 1955, at a point when the twelve-inch long-playing record was new and could hold an extended sequence. Sinatra's career had collapsed around 1950 and been rebuilt through film and his Capitol records; his marriage to Ava Gardner was falling apart, and listeners heard the album as autobiography. It is often called one of the first concept albums.","The final track, “This Love of Mine”, has lyrics co-written by Sinatra himself in 1941, so the record ends with his own words. Ellington's “Mood Indigo”, usually an instrumental, becomes a torch song here. Riddle leaves the orchestra almost static under many verses, so the smallest change of colour, a solo woodwind entering or a string swell, lands like a sigh.","It was a major success and is routinely ranked among the greatest albums of popular music. Critics have credited it with defining the album as a mood rather than a collection of singles, well before rock albums claimed that ground. Few dispute its quality; the real argument is whether it or Only the Lonely (1958) is Sinatra's darkest and best record.","Listen straight through, late at night, as it was sold to be heard. Start with the title song, then Rodgers and Hart's “Glad to Be Unhappy”. Notice where Sinatra breathes: he often runs phrases longer than expected, learnt, he said, from watching Tommy Dorsey's trombone."],"sinatra-songs-for-swingin-lovers":["Fifteen songbook standards at a relaxed, medium swing: “You Make Me Feel So Young”, “Pennies from Heaven”, “Anything Goes”, “Makin' Whoopee”, “Too Marvelous for Words” and, most famously, Cole Porter's “I've Got You Under My Skin”. Riddle's arrangements build slowly, with muted brass and strings over an easy beat, then open up for climaxes. Sinatra swings behind the beat, confident and loose, the mirror image of In the Wee Small Hours.","Recorded for Capitol in Hollywood in late 1955 and early 1956, it confirmed the Sinatra comeback and the Sinatra–Riddle partnership. The pairing of a mood album of ballads with a mood album of swing became Capitol's model. The rock and roll explosion was starting the same year, and this record's adult sophistication represents the other road American pop could take.","In “I've Got You Under My Skin”, Riddle builds the arrangement toward a trombone solo by Milt Bernhart, captured only after many takes. Listen for how the brass climbs step by step behind Sinatra until the solo erupts, then everything falls back to leave the voice exposed. Riddle described the medium-slow swing tempo as close to a heartbeat.","It was a huge seller and remains many listeners' definition of Sinatra. Critics praise the chemistry: Riddle's arrangements lean on space and slow build rather than wall-of-brass volume. Some jazz critics grumbled that it was not jazz; admirers answer that it swings harder than much jazz of the time. “Under My Skin” is often cited as the perfect Sinatra–Riddle record.","Put on “I've Got You Under My Skin” and follow the arrangement's slow build to the trombone solo. Then compare with the same year's rock and roll singles to hear two Americas at once. Play the album after In the Wee Small Hours for the contrast."],"loewe-my-fair-lady":["A musical of Shaw's Pygmalion: the phonetics professor Henry Higgins bets he can pass off Eliza Doolittle, a Cockney flower seller, as a duchess. The score mixes music-hall numbers for her dustman father (“With a Little Bit of Luck”, “Get Me to the Church on Time”), soaring songs for Eliza (“Wouldn't It Be Loverly”, “I Could Have Danced All Night”), and half-spoken patter for Higgins. “The Rain in Spain” is the triumphant breakthrough; “I've Grown Accustomed to Her Face” the reluctant confession.","Alan Jay Lerner wrote book and lyrics, Frederick Loewe the music. It opened in March 1956 with Rex Harrison, Julie Andrews and Stanley Holloway, directed by Moss Hart, and ran for 2,717 performances, then a Broadway record. Rodgers and Hammerstein had reportedly tried and failed to adapt Pygmalion. Its cast album became one of the best-selling records of the decade.","Rex Harrison could not really sing, so Lerner and Loewe wrote Higgins's songs to be half spoken on pitch, like patter; his rhythms carry the melody. “The Ascot Gavotte” is a joke: a society chorus frozen in a stiff, stately dance, dressed by Cecil Beaton in black and white. Loewe, trained in European operetta, gives Eliza's songs a Viennese lift, while her father gets English music hall.","Critics loved it and many called it the perfect musical. The main debate, even then, was the ending: Shaw insisted Eliza would not marry Higgins, while the musical implies she returns to him. Some modern productions, including Bartlett Sher's 2018 revival, have left her ambiguously walking out. In the 1964 film, Audrey Hepburn's songs were dubbed by Marni Nixon.","Hear the original Broadway cast album with Harrison and Andrews. Listen for how differently the three main characters sing: operetta for Eliza, music hall for Doolittle, speech-song for Higgins. The “Rain in Spain” scene shows the whole show in miniature."],"rodgers-the-sound-of-music":["A novice nun, Maria, becomes governess to the seven children of the widowed Captain von Trapp in 1938 Salzburg, falls in love with him and escapes with the family as Austria is absorbed into Nazi Germany. The score alternates Latin-flavoured choruses for the nuns with songs for the children, such as “Do-Re-Mi” and “So Long, Farewell”, and warm ballads including “My Favorite Things”, “Climb Ev'ry Mountain”, “Edelweiss” and the title song.","It was the last show by Rodgers and Hammerstein, with a book by Howard Lindsay and Russel Crouse based on Maria von Trapp's memoir. It opened in November 1959 with Mary Martin; Hammerstein, already ill, died in August 1960. The 1965 film with Julie Andrews became one of the highest-grossing films of its time, and it is the film most people know.","“Do-Re-Mi” is an actual solfège lesson: the song teaches the syllables for the scale and then scrambles them into a tune. “Edelweiss”, the last song Hammerstein wrote, sounds so much like an Austrian folk song that many people, including some Austrians, have taken it for a national anthem. The nuns' opening chorus draws on Catholic chant. John Coltrane later made “My Favorite Things” a modal jazz epic.","Critics initially found it sentimental, and some still do; audiences never agreed. The Broadway run was long and the film eclipsed everything. Austrian audiences were long indifferent to it, and the real von Trapps disliked some changes. Its politics have been reread: beneath the charm it is a story of refusing collaboration with fascism, a thread modern productions tend to strengthen.","Hear “My Favorite Things” in the show, then Coltrane's 1961 recording to hear the same waltz become a soprano saxophone meditation. Listen for the nuns' Latin at the opening and for how “Edelweiss” works as a quiet act of patriotism."],"kander-and-ebb-cabaret":["Berlin, 1929–30. An American writer, Cliff, falls in with Sally Bowles, a singer at the seedy Kit Kat Klub, while his landlady, Fräulein Schneider, is courted by a Jewish fruit seller, Herr Schultz. The story scenes alternate with numbers at the club presided over by the leering Emcee: “Willkommen”, “Two Ladies”, “If You Could See Her”, “Money” and the title song, each a sly commentary on the rise of the Nazis outside.","John Kander composed and Fred Ebb wrote lyrics; Joe Masteroff wrote the book from Christopher Isherwood's Berlin stories and John Van Druten's play I Am a Camera. Hal Prince directed; it opened in November 1966, with Joel Grey as the Emcee. Fräulein Schneider was played by Lotte Lenya, Kurt Weill's widow and the original Jenny of The Threepenny Opera, linking the show to the Berlin it portrays.","“Tomorrow Belongs to Me” is a newly written pastiche of a German folk song, sung by a boy with a clear voice, and it is so convincing that it has been mistaken for a real Nazi song. The Kit Kat Klub numbers are written in a Weill-Brecht style, full of tinny brass and cynical punch lines. The final line of “If You Could See Her” lands a shocking antisemitic joke; it was softened during the original run after complaints and restored in later productions.","It was a big hit and won the Tony for Best Musical. Bob Fosse's 1972 film with Liza Minnelli and Joel Grey restructured it, keeping only the club numbers, and won eight Oscars. Sam Mendes's 1993 London revival, brought to Broadway in 1998 with Alan Cumming, made it darker and more explicit. Critics now rank it among the key concept musicals.","Start with the Fosse film, then the 1998 revival recording. Listen for how the Emcee's songs grow harsher as the Nazis rise, and compare the club band's sound with Weill's Threepenny Opera. The final “Willkommen” reprise is the show's chilling turn."],"lloyd-webber-jesus-christ-superstar":["A rock opera on the last week of Jesus's life, told largely from the point of view of Judas, who fears that his friend's fame will bring disaster. It is sung through, with no spoken dialogue. The score pairs rock band with orchestra and choir: Judas's opening “Heaven on Their Minds”, Mary Magdalene's gentle “I Don't Know How to Love Him”, the anguished “Gethsemane” and the brassy, gospel-tinged title song.","Andrew Lloyd Webber, twenty-two, and lyricist Tim Rice first released it in 1970 as a double concept album, with Deep Purple's Ian Gillan as Jesus and Murray Head as Judas, because producers would not risk a staging. The album was a bestseller, especially in America. A Broadway production followed in 1971 and London in 1972, where it ran for eight years. A film appeared in 1973.","Lloyd Webber writes in odd metres to unsettle the rock pulse: the trial and scourging scenes lean on irregular groupings of beats. The musical language is a patchwork: Motown and gospel for the title song, folk ballad for Mary Magdalene, a ragtime vaudeville turn for King Herod. Judas is given the best tunes and the most sympathetic motives, the provocation at its heart.","It was both hugely popular and controversial. Some Christians thought it blasphemous; others welcomed a Jesus who spoke to young people. Some Jewish groups criticised the portrayal of the priests. Critics disagreed on whether Lloyd Webber's mix of rock and orchestral writing was a new theatre music or merely spectacle; most now see the original album as his most vital score.","Start with the 1970 concept album, not a later cast recording. Hear “Heaven on Their Minds”, then “Gethsemane”. Compare it with Bach's St Matthew Passion: both tell the same story through characters' voices, one through arias and chorales, the other through rock numbers."],"sondheim-follies":["Former showgirls of the Weismann Follies gather for a reunion in the crumbling theatre about to be demolished, haunted by the ghosts of their younger selves. Two unhappy couples replay the choices they made thirty years ago. The score alternates bitter present-day songs with deliberate pastiches of the 1920s and 1930s: “Broadway Baby”, “I'm Still Here”, “Losing My Mind”, “Could I Leave You?” Finally the characters break down in a surreal “Loveland” sequence of vaudeville turns.","Sondheim wrote music and lyrics, James Goldman the book; Hal Prince and Michael Bennett directed. It opened in April 1971 as a lavish, expensive production that lost money despite critical attention. It is part of the run of concept musicals Sondheim and Prince made in the early 1970s, and it reflects the moment when the old Broadway showgirl revues of Ziegfeld were becoming history.","The pastiches are affectionate but exact. “Losing My Mind” is written in the style of Gershwin's “The Man I Love”, “Beautiful Girls” nods to Irving Berlin's Follies anthem “A Pretty Girl Is Like a Melody”, and “I'm Still Here” charts a whole career through decades of songwriting styles. Sondheim uses the golden age as ghostly accompaniment to the characters' regret. The ghosts are staged as silent younger doubles, walking through the present.","It divided critics at first; some found it cold, others were overwhelmed. Its reputation grew through a starry 1985 concert recording and several major revivals, notably the National Theatre's 2017 production. Many now rank it among Sondheim's greatest scores. Arguments remain over which version, with different song choices and endings, is definitive.","Start with “Losing My Mind”, then Gershwin's “The Man I Love” to hear the model, and “I'm Still Here”. Then listen to the 2011 Broadway revival or 2017 National Theatre recording. Notice how the “Loveland” numbers mimic the old revue styles, but each is a nervous breakdown."],"sondheim-a-little-night-music":["A comedy of mismatched couples in turn-of-the-century Sweden, gathered at a country house for a weekend in which the summer night “smiles three times”. The score is almost entirely in triple time: waltzes, mazurkas and other dance rhythms in three, a sustained musical conceit. A quintet of “Liebeslieder” singers comments like a Greek chorus. Its most famous song, “Send in the Clowns”, is sung by the actress Desirée when she realises she has left love too late.","Sondheim wrote music and lyrics, Hugh Wheeler the book, from Ingmar Bergman's 1955 film Smiles of a Summer Night; Hal Prince directed. It opened in February 1973 with Glynis Johns and Len Cariou and won the Tony for Best Musical. After the darkness of Company and Follies, it was a lighter, more romantic show, and a financial success.","“Send in the Clowns” was written for Glynis Johns, whose voice was lovely but short-breathed, so Sondheim built it from short phrases separated by pauses; the hesitancy is the character. The whole score treats triple time with extraordinary variety, including patter in waltz time, a canon for the young lovers and a trio of simultaneous monologues. The title nods to Mozart's Eine kleine Nachtmusik.","It was admired from the start. “Send in the Clowns” became a hit for Judy Collins and later Frank Sinatra, Sondheim's one major pop success. Some critics find the show too perfect and cold; many consider it his most elegant score. The 1977 film was poorly received. Revivals often stress its sadness more than its farce.","Hear the original cast recording, then listen for triple time throughout, and try tapping along to how many different dances Sondheim makes. Then hear “Send in the Clowns” sung by an actor rather than a pop singer, where the pauses and breath matter as much as the notes."],"kander-and-ebb-chicago":["Subtitled “a musical vaudeville”, it follows Roxie Hart and Velma Kelly, two murderesses in 1920s Chicago, as the lawyer Billy Flynn turns their trials into celebrity entertainment. Each number is framed as a vaudeville act: “All That Jazz”, “Cell Block Tango”, “Mister Cellophane”, “Razzle Dazzle”, “Nowadays”. The band plays onstage, and the sound is hot 1920s dance music filtered through 1970s Broadway brass.","Kander wrote the music, Ebb the lyrics, and Ebb with Bob Fosse the book, from Maurine Dallas Watkins's 1926 satirical play drawn from real cases. Fosse directed and choreographed, with Gwen Verdon, Chita Rivera and Jerry Orbach. It opened in June 1975, in the post-Watergate years, and it was overshadowed that season by A Chorus Line.","Several numbers are modelled on real vaudeville styles or performers: “Mister Cellophane”, the husband's lament that no one notices him, echoes Bert Williams's famous song “Nobody”. The whole show is a cynical joke about celebrity and justice; the bandleader introduces each act to the audience as if we were at a club, which makes us part of the crowd that loves the murderers.","The original production did well without triumphing. The 1996 Encores! concert staging, directed by Walter Bobbie with choreography by Ann Reinking in Fosse's style, transferred to Broadway and became the longest-running revival in Broadway history. The 2002 film won the Oscar for Best Picture. Critics now see it as a sharp satire whose themes of celebrity crime have only grown more relevant.","Hear the 1996 revival recording, then watch Fosse-style choreography on film. Listen to “Mister Cellophane” and then Bert Williams's “Nobody” to hear the model. Notice how the band stays onstage: the whole show is a concert that happens to have a trial in it."],"lloyd-webber-phantom-of-the-opera":["A sung-through musical based on Gaston Leroux's novel: at the Paris Opera in the 1880s a masked, disfigured composer living beneath the house makes the young soprano Christine his pupil and obsession. The score is anchored by the pounding organ riff of the title song and the long melodic lines of “The Music of the Night”, “All I Ask of You” and “Think of Me”, with spectacle including a crashing chandelier and a candlelit underground lake.","Lloyd Webber composed it as a vehicle for his then wife Sarah Brightman, with lyrics by Charles Hart (and Richard Stilgoe) and direction by Hal Prince. It opened in London in October 1986 with Brightman and Michael Crawford, and on Broadway in January 1988. The Broadway production ran until 2023, the longest in Broadway history, and the show became the flagship of the megamusical era.","Lloyd Webber includes three mock operas within the show. “Hannibal” parodies French grand opera, “Il Muto” is a Mozart-style opera buffa in Italian, and the Phantom's own “Don Juan Triumphant” uses harsh, modernist writing with whole-tone scales to suggest a genius ahead of his time. The Phantom's music is linked to a descending chromatic line that echoes through the score.","It was hugely popular and less loved by critics, who found it melodramatic and derivative, and Pink Floyd's Roger Waters complained of a resemblance to his band's “Echoes”. Supporters value its melody and theatrical grip. It won the 1988 Tony for Best Musical over Sondheim's Into the Woods, a contest often cited as spectacle beating craft.","Hear the original London cast recording with Crawford and Brightman. Listen to the overture's organ and to “The Music of the Night”, then the mock-Mozart “Il Muto”. Compare with Puccini: Lloyd Webber is writing opera for people who do not go to the opera."],"sondheim-into-the-woods":["Fairy tales intertwined: Cinderella, Jack and the Beanstalk, Little Red Riding Hood and Rapunzel, joined by an invented Baker and his Wife, who are sent by a Witch to find items to lift a curse. Act 1 ends in “happily ever after”; Act 2 shows the consequences, when a Giant's widow comes for revenge. The score is built from quick recurring phrases, including the opening “I wish”, and ends in the ballads “No One Is Alone” and “Children Will Listen”.","Sondheim wrote music and lyrics and James Lapine the book, their second collaboration after Sunday in the Park with George. It opened in November 1987 with Bernadette Peters as the Witch and Joanna Gleason as the Baker's Wife. Lapine drew on Bruno Bettelheim's psychoanalytic readings of fairy tales, and many heard in Act 2's deaths an allegory of AIDS, though the authors resisted a single reading.","The opening is a musical engine: a repeated set of chords and the words “I wish” recur throughout, so every character begins with desire. The five magic beans get their own short motif, which runs through the score. “Agony” is a comic duet for two princes that becomes, in Act 2, a reprise about new conquests. The Narrator is killed in Act 2: the story loses its teller.","It won Tonys for score and book but lost Best Musical to The Phantom of the Opera. It has become one of Sondheim's most produced shows, especially in schools. A 2014 Disney film softened some darker material. Some critics find Act 2 preachy; others think it the reason the show matters.","Hear the original cast recording and follow the opening prologue, noticing how many stories are set in motion in one number. Then hear “No One Is Alone”, and compare Act 1 with Act 2 as you would a fairy tale and its adult retelling."],"larson-rent":["A rock musical about a year in the lives of young artists in New York's East Village, many living with HIV, struggling to pay rent and cope with poverty, addiction and loss. Almost entirely sung, it uses rock, gospel, tango and R&B: the ensemble anthem “Seasons of Love” counts a year as 525,600 minutes; “Take Me or Leave Me”, “La Vie Bohème” and “I'll Cover You” are its other famous numbers.","Jonathan Larson wrote book, music and lyrics over several years, updating Puccini's La bohème to AIDS-era New York. He died suddenly of an aortic aneurysm on the night of the final dress rehearsal before the first preview at New York Theatre Workshop in January 1996. The show transferred to Broadway in April, won the Pulitzer Prize and the Tony, and ran for twelve years.","The Bohème parallels are everywhere: Mimi stays Mimi, Rodolfo becomes Roger, Marcello becomes Mark, Musetta becomes Maureen. Roger, a songwriter, struggles to write one great song, and when he picks up his guitar, listen for a quotation of the tune of Musetta's Waltz from Puccini. “La Vie Bohème” is a list-song celebrating everything that the establishment despises.","It was a sensation among young audiences and made Broadway attractive to a new generation. Critics admired its energy and noted its unfinished roughness, which Larson's death froze in place. Some criticised its portrayal of poverty and gentrification from the artists' side; the playwright Sarah Schulman argued it borrowed from her novel. Its influence on later musicals, including Hamilton, is widely acknowledged.","Hear the original Broadway cast recording, then Puccini's La bohème to spot the parallels, particularly Act 1's candle scene, which Rent mirrors in “Light My Candle”. Start with “Seasons of Love” and “La Vie Bohème”."],"lin-manuel-miranda-hamilton":["A sung-through musical biography of Alexander Hamilton, the orphaned Caribbean immigrant who became George Washington's aide and America's first Treasury Secretary, told largely in hip-hop and R&B. Founding fathers and their women are played by actors of colour. The score moves from “Alexander Hamilton” and “My Shot” through the cabinet rap battles to the duel with Aaron Burr and Eliza's closing “Who Lives, Who Dies, Who Tells Your Story”. King George sings Beatles-style breakup pop.","Lin-Manuel Miranda wrote book, music and lyrics, inspired by Ron Chernow's 2004 biography, and first performed a draft of the opening song at the White House in 2009. It opened at the Public Theater in January 2015 and on Broadway in August 2015, directed by Thomas Kail, with Miranda as Hamilton. It won the 2016 Pulitzer Prize for Drama and eleven Tony Awards.","It is full of quotations. “Ten Duel Commandments” is modelled on the Notorious B.I.G.'s “Ten Crack Commandments”. Washington's introduction nods to Gilbert and Sullivan's “Modern Major-General”. The musical themes return and change: Eliza's “helpless” and Angelica's “satisfied” share music but tell the same night from different sides, the second song literally rewinding the first.","It was a cultural phenomenon and one of the most celebrated musicals of the century, praised for bringing hip-hop to Broadway and for its casting. Historians have debated its portrayal of Hamilton as an abolitionist hero and the absence of enslaved characters. Some argued its optimism fit the Obama years too neatly; others say its ambivalence about legacy is the heart of it.","Hear the Broadway cast recording, or watch the 2020 filmed performance. Start with the opening number and listen for the recurring motifs; then “Satisfied”, which rewinds the story. Afterwards try the rap albums Miranda quotes, and Sondheim."],"bing-crosby-brother-can-you-spare-a-dime":["A song of the Great Depression in a minor key, written by Jay Gorney with lyrics by Yip Harburg, and sung by Crosby with a small studio orchestra. The singer is an ordinary man who built the railroads and fought in the war, now standing in a breadline asking a stranger for a coin. The melody sounds like an old Yiddish lullaby or a Russian folk tune, and it rises to an anguished climax where the narrator reminds the listener that they once marched side by side.","The song came from the 1932 Broadway revue Americana, and it reached the radio and record shops in the worst year of the slump, when a quarter of American workers were unemployed. Crosby and Rudy Vallée both had hits with it that autumn. Crosby was twenty-nine, newly solo after his years with Paul Whiteman's Rhythm Boys, and becoming the most popular singer in the country through radio and short films.","Listen to how little Crosby pushes. Earlier stage singers belted to reach the back of a theatre; Crosby sings into an electrical microphone as if speaking to one person, which makes the bitterness of the last verse feel private rather than theatrical. Gorney's tune was reportedly adapted from a lullaby he had heard in Russia as a child, which explains its old-world sorrow inside a Broadway song.","It was treated as the anthem of the Depression almost at once, and some radio executives disliked it as too gloomy or even subversive in an election year. Harburg later wrote the lyrics for The Wizard of Oz. The song has been recorded countless times since, but historians of popular music usually single out the Crosby version as the one that made crooning capable of social comment.","Hear it once for the story, following the man from farm to railroad to trenches to breadline. Then listen again for Crosby's tone: warm and low, with a slight catch at phrase ends. Compare it with Billie Holiday's Strange Fruit, a later song that also refuses to be pleasant, and with the jaunty dance records of the same year."],"bing-crosby-swinging-on-a-star":["A bouncy, talky novelty song by Jimmy Van Heusen with lyrics by Johnny Burke, sung by Crosby with a vocal quartet answering him. Each verse describes an animal, a mule, a pig and a fish, and asks the listener, in effect, whether they would rather be that creature or a well-behaved child who goes to school. The tempo is an easy swing, the band light, and Crosby half-sings, half-chats his way through it with obvious enjoyment.","The song was written for Going My Way, the 1944 film in which Crosby played a young Catholic priest winning over a gang of street kids; it won the Academy Award for Best Original Song, and Crosby won the Oscar for Best Actor. It was recorded in wartime for Decca, when Crosby was the biggest entertainer in America on radio, record and film all at once.","The backing voices belong to the Williams Brothers, a young family quartet from Iowa; the youngest of them, Andy Williams, would become one of the great crooners of the next generation, so the record quietly passes the torch. Notice, too, how Crosby lets the rhymes land late and relaxed, a trick of phrasing borrowed from jazz musicians such as Louis Armstrong, whom he admired.","It topped the charts for weeks and became a children's standard, sung in schools for decades. Critics have tended to treat it as Crosby at his most genial rather than his most serious, and it is often set against his darker Depression recordings. Its gentle moralising now sounds of its time, but the lightness of the performance has kept it alive.","Enjoy it as a piece of comic timing: listen for the small pauses Crosby leaves before each punchline and how the quartet leans in behind him. Then play Andy Williams's Moon River, eighteen years later, to hear the boy in the background grown into a star with a very different, smoother sound."],"sinatra-songs-for-young-lovers":["Eight standards on a ten-inch LP, sung at medium and slow tempos with a compact orchestra of strings, reeds and rhythm section. The songs include Rodgers and Hart's “My Funny Valentine” and “Little Girl Blue”, Gershwin's “A Foggy Day”, Porter's “I Get a Kick Out of You” and “Violets for Your Furs”. The sound is intimate and lightly swinging; Sinatra sounds younger and more wistful than on the later swing albums, and every song is a little story of courtship.","This was Sinatra's first album for Capitol, recorded in Hollywood in late 1953 just as his career revived after the From Here to Eternity film role. Several of the charts were adapted from arrangements by George Siravo, with Nelson Riddle conducting at the start of what became one of the great singer-arranger partnerships. The long-playing record was still new, and a themed set of songs was a novel idea.","It is sometimes called the first concept album, though the concept is modest: songs for couples, arranged as a set. Listen to how the strings stay out of the way in the verses and enter only for colour, a restraint Riddle would refine. The version of “My Funny Valentine” is worth setting beside Chet Baker's from the same year: two very different kinds of tenderness.","It sold well and reestablished Sinatra as a recording artist, but it has always lived in the shadow of the twelve-inch albums that followed. Critics now hear it as a sketch for In the Wee Small Hours and Songs for Swingin' Lovers, and it was later expanded on reissue. Its short running time is part of its charm.","Play the whole twenty-odd minutes in one sitting. Start with “A Foggy Day” for the easy swing and “Violets for Your Furs” for the quiet storytelling. Then play the matching songs on Chet Baker Sings and hear how a trumpeter-singer and a band-singer approach the same material from opposite ends."],"sinatra-where-are-you":["Twelve slow ballads of loss, wrapped in lush, high strings. Gordon Jenkins's orchestra is bigger and more cinematic than Riddle's, with long sustained violin lines and very little rhythm section, so the songs seem to float. The programme includes “The Night We Called It a Day”, “I Cover the Waterfront”, “Laura”, “Autumn Leaves” and a new version of “I'm a Fool to Want You”. Sinatra's voice, placed close to the microphone, sounds tired and grave throughout.","It was Sinatra's first Capitol album without Nelson Riddle, and one of his earliest recorded in stereo, when Capitol was beginning to sell the new two-channel format. Jenkins was already famous for sweeping string arrangements, notably for Nat King Cole and for his own hits. Sinatra alternated deliberately between upbeat swing records and sad ballad records during these years, and this was the ballad side of the ledger.","Listen to how Jenkins uses the strings almost like a choir: long held chords rather than busy countermelodies. That slow harmonic movement leaves space in which every breath Sinatra takes is audible. The remake of “I'm a Fool to Want You”, a song he co-wrote in 1951 during his turmoil over Ava Gardner, lands differently in this plush setting than in the stark original.","Some critics find Jenkins's strings too sweet next to Riddle's subtlety; others think the extra lushness makes the loneliness more acute, like a large empty room. It is usually ranked just below In the Wee Small Hours and Only the Lonely among Sinatra's ballad albums, and it has gained admirers since as a model for later orchestral pop.","Try it late at night on good speakers or headphones so the stereo strings open around you. Begin with “Laura” and “Autumn Leaves”, then compare the same mood with Only the Lonely, where Riddle's darker, sparer writing gives Sinatra less to lean on."],"sinatra-come-fly-with-me":["A musical trip around the world in twelve songs, with brassy, witty arrangements by Billy May. The title song, written for the album by Sammy Cahn and Jimmy Van Heusen, sets off like a jet climbing, and the itinerary runs through “Isle of Capri”, “Moonlight in Vermont”, “Autumn in New York”, “April in Paris”, “London by Night”, “Brazil”, “Blue Hawaii” and “On the Road to Mandalay”. May's trademark is sliding saxophones and punchy trumpets that sound like a band having fun.","Recorded in Hollywood in late 1957, it caught the dawn of the jet age, when commercial transatlantic flights were about to open, and the album cover showed an airliner and a smiling Sinatra thumbing a lift. It went to number one on the Billboard album chart. Sinatra had chosen May for his exuberance, as a contrast to Riddle's more even swing and Jenkins's strings.","The words of “On the Road to Mandalay” are Rudyard Kipling's, set to music by Oley Speaks in 1907; Sinatra's jokey alterations reportedly upset the Kipling estate, and the track was withheld from some British pressings for years. Listen, too, for May's use of exotic musical clichés for each destination, done with a wink: tourist postcards in sound.","It was a big seller and remains one of Sinatra's best-loved records; the title song became one of his signature numbers. Some critics find it more of a party than a statement compared with the ballad albums, while others regard May's charts as among the most joyful big-band writing of the decade. It has also become a symbol of a glamorous, mid-century idea of travel.","Begin with the title track and “April in Paris”, then set that beside Count Basie's April in Paris to hear where May's band sound comes from. Listen for the moments when the whole brass section swells behind a single held note from Sinatra, then drops away."],"sinatra-no-one-cares":["Eleven torch songs in the bleakest possible order, again with Gordon Jenkins's strings. The title song by Sammy Cahn and Jimmy Van Heusen opens a sequence that includes Arlen's “Stormy Weather”, “A Cottage for Sale”, “I Can't Get Started”, “Here's That Rainy Day”, “I'll Never Smile Again” and Cy Coleman's “Why Try to Change Me Now”. The tempos never rise; the album is one long evening spent alone, and Sinatra's voice sounds heavier and more weathered than it did five years earlier.","Recorded in Hollywood in early 1959, it followed the success of Only the Lonely, and Capitol was happy to let Sinatra keep mining the vein of despair that sold so well on LP. Sinatra was in his mid-forties, at a peak of fame in film and in Las Vegas, and his ballad albums were understood by fans as his private self, in contrast to the swaggering public one.","The closing song, “None but the Lonely Heart”, is an English version of Tchaikovsky's romance to a poem by Goethe, originally written for voice and piano in 1869. So one of the bleakest pop records of the decade ends with a Russian art song, and Jenkins gives it a lush, almost operatic treatment that shows where the torch-song tradition's roots partly lie.","It was well received but has always been overshadowed by Only the Lonely, and some listeners find the unrelieved sadness hard to sustain. Admirers argue that it is the most consistent of the Jenkins albums and contains some of Sinatra's finest singing of “I Can't Get Started”. It has grown in stature as critics have reassessed his catalogue album by album.","Listen to the first two songs and the last together to feel the frame of the record. Then play Tchaikovsky's original None but the Lonely Heart and hear the same melody in its first home. Pair with Billie Holiday's Lady in Satin for a darker, rougher take on the same form."],"tony-bennett-i-left-my-heart-in-san-francisco":["A slow, broad ballad by George Cory and Douglass Cross in which a traveller, seeing the great cities of the world, admits that none of them matters beside the city on the bay. The arrangement, led by Bennett's pianist Ralph Sharon, opens quietly with piano and strings, and Bennett builds from a soft, close verse to a big, open-throated final chorus that climbs to a held high note. It is a song of homesickness sung by a New Yorker.","Cory and Cross had written the song years earlier and passed it to Sharon, who suggested it to Bennett for an engagement in San Francisco in 1961. Columbia released it in 1962 as the B-side of another song, but radio disc jockeys turned it over. It won two Grammy Awards, including Record of the Year, just as the Beatles and rock were about to push this style off the charts.","Much of the song's appeal lies in its verse, the free, rubato introduction before the main tune, which many cover versions cut. Listen for how Bennett's jazz training shows even in this big pop setting: he delays phrases, leans on the ends of lines and keeps the tone conversational until the final climb. The song was adopted as one of San Francisco's official songs.","It made Bennett a household name and became his signature for the next six decades, sometimes to his frustration as a jazz singer. Critics have sometimes dismissed it as sentimental, but musicians admire its craft and Bennett's control. It is often cited as the last great hit of the pre-rock songbook style.","Start by listening for the moment the rhythm section enters after the introduction, and then for the final chorus where Bennett opens his voice fully. Compare it with Sinatra's Come Fly with Me, which travels for fun, and with Bennett's own duo album with Bill Evans for the intimate side of the same voice."],"tony-bennett-bill-evans-album":["Nine songs for voice and piano alone. Bill Evans plays with his characteristic soft touch and rich, clustered harmonies, and Bennett sings with more restraint than on his hits. The programme includes “Young and Foolish”, “The Touch of Your Lips”, “Some Other Time” from Bernstein's On the Town, “My Foolish Heart”, “Days of Wine and Roses” and Evans's own “Waltz for Debby” with lyrics. The two often trade the melody, and each song feels like a private conversation.","It was recorded in Berkeley, California, in 1975 for Fantasy Records, at a time when Bennett had left Columbia after disputes over pressure to record contemporary pop and rock material. Evans, already a legend of jazz piano, rarely accompanied singers. The duet project gave Bennett a chance to prove himself as a jazz artist, and the two made a second album, Together Again, soon after.","Listen to Evans's introductions: each is a small improvisation that reharmonises the tune before Bennett enters, so the singer steps into a changed landscape. “Some Other Time” is the song whose opening chords Evans had used as the basis for his own famous “Peace Piece” in 1958, so the album quietly links back to one of his most important solo recordings.","It was well received by jazz critics and has grown in reputation, often named among the finest voice-and-piano records in jazz. Some listeners find Bennett's big voice occasionally too operatic for Evans's delicate playing, but most hear the contrast as the point. It marked Bennett's reinvention as a jazz singer, a role he would claim fully in later decades.","Start with “Some Other Time”, then play Bill Evans's Peace Piece and hear the same few chords turned into a meditation. Listen for how Evans drops out or thins his playing when Bennett sings softly. Try it after I Left My Heart in San Francisco to hear the showman become the chamber musician."],"dean-martin-thats-amore":["A comic love song in a lilting waltz-like rhythm, by Harry Warren with lyrics by Jack Brooks, full of mock-Italian imagery about the moon, pizza and romance. Martin sings it with a relaxed grin in his voice, sliding into notes and adding little Italian flourishes, while the orchestra plays a tarantella-flavoured accompaniment. It is short, sunny and built to be sung along to after the first chorus.","The song was written for The Caddy, a 1953 comedy starring Martin and his partner Jerry Lewis, and it was nominated for the Academy Award for Best Original Song. It became Martin's first big hit, at a time when Italian-American singers such as Perry Como, Tony Bennett and Martin were at the heart of American popular music and the postwar suburbs.","Behind the joking there is a knowing play on ethnic identity: Martin, born Dino Crocetti in Ohio, performs an exaggerated Italian-ness for a mainstream audience, and does it with enough irony that he seems to be in on the joke. Listen, too, for how carefully the band's mandolin-like figures stay out of his way; the arrangement is simpler than it sounds.","It was a huge seller and has never gone away, returning through films such as Moonstruck in 1987. Critics have often been sniffy about it as a novelty, but it is also admired as a model of effortless delivery. Some now hear it as a period piece of friendly stereotype; others as a joyful celebration of immigrant culture.","Enjoy the swing of the rhythm and Martin's laziness, which is carefully calculated. Then play Everybody Loves Somebody, eleven years later, to hear how he could turn the same casualness into something softer and sadder. Compare Sinatra's precise diction with Martin's slurred, easygoing one."],"dean-martin-everybody-loves-somebody":["A gentle, rocking ballad with a triplet piano rhythm, a wordless backing chorus and swelling strings, all under Martin's warm, unhurried baritone. The song reassures the listener that love will come to everyone eventually, and the arrangement builds from a soft start to a full, almost church-like final chorus. It is only a few minutes long, and its sound is closer to early-1960s pop balladry than to big-band swing.","The song dated from 1947, written by Irving Taylor and Martin's pianist Ken Lane, and Martin had already recorded it once. His 1964 remake for Frank Sinatra's label Reprise, arranged by Ernie Freeman, was designed for the pop charts. It reached number one in August 1964, displacing the Beatles' A Hard Day's Night, a small victory for the older style in the middle of the British Invasion.","Listen to the piano's repeated triplet chords: that pattern came from rock and roll and doo-wop ballads, not from the swing era. So the record that beat the Beatles did it partly by borrowing from the music that had displaced crooners. Martin reportedly sent Elvis Presley a teasing telegram when it went to number one, a sign of how the generations were watching each other.","It became Martin's theme song and the signature tune of his long-running television show. Critics tend to treat it as a pleasant, well-made pop record rather than a major artistic statement, but it is a key document of how the crooners survived into the rock era by adapting their sound.","Listen for the moment the full chorus and strings come in, and for Martin's refusal to push: he stays relaxed even at the climax. Then compare it with Elvis Presley's Heartbreak Hotel to hear the two sides of American singing that competed on the charts."],"sarah-vaughan-with-clifford-brown":["Nine standards sung by Vaughan with a small bebop band featuring the trumpeter Clifford Brown, flautist Herbie Mann and tenor saxophonist Paul Quinichette, with Jimmy Jones on piano and Roy Haynes on drums. The songs include “Lullaby of Birdland”, “April in Paris”, “Embraceable You”, “September Song” and “Jim”. Vaughan's voice ranges from dark, almost baritone low notes to bright, clear high ones, and she treats it like a horn, bending and reshaping melodies.","It was recorded in New York in December 1954 for EmArcy, Mercury's jazz label, which let Vaughan make pure jazz records alongside her commercial pop sides for Mercury. Brown was twenty-four and the brightest new trumpeter in jazz; he would die in a car crash eighteen months later. Vaughan had come up through the bebop bands of Earl Hines and Billy Eckstine alongside Charlie Parker and Dizzy Gillespie.","Listen to the ends of songs, where Vaughan often trades short improvised phrases with Brown, singer and trumpeter imitating each other. In “Lullaby of Birdland” she scats a passage that sounds like a bebop horn solo. Vaughan was a trained pianist and knew harmony, which is why her changes to the melody always fit the chords, however daring.","It is widely considered Vaughan's finest studio album, and Vaughan herself was said to consider it her favourite. It sold modestly at first but became a jazz reference record. Critics praise the balance between her virtuosity and the band's lightness. The main argument is whether her operatic range sometimes overwhelms the lyrics, a charge most listeners dismiss here.","Start with “Lullaby of Birdland” for the scat exchange and “Embraceable You” for her ballad voice. Then listen to Clifford Brown with Strings, made weeks later, to hear him in a lusher setting. Compare with Ella Fitzgerald's Gershwin songbook for a brighter, simpler approach to the same material."],"sarah-vaughan-sings-george-gershwin":["A double album of Gershwin songs, sung with large studio orchestras in varied settings: some swinging with brass, others slow with strings, a few with small groups. The programme ranges across Gershwin's catalogue, from “Summertime” and “The Man I Love” to “Someone to Watch Over Me”, “Embraceable You” and less familiar songs. Vaughan's voice, rich and wide, sweeps over the arrangements, and her vibrato and slides give the familiar tunes an almost operatic grandeur.","Released by Mercury in 1957, it was part of the fashion for singer-songbook albums sparked by Ella Fitzgerald's Cole Porter set on Verve the year before. Vaughan was at the height of her commercial career, alternating jazz albums with pop hits. The double-LP format signalled that Gershwin, dead for twenty years, was now treated as a classic composer worthy of a complete-works approach.","Listen for how Vaughan treats the slow songs almost like arias, holding notes long and colouring them with dynamic swells, while in the swing numbers she keeps her tone light and horn-like. Gershwin's melodies often move by small steps, and Vaughan's habit of leaping an octave or bending a phrase shows how much freedom a trained jazz singer found inside them.","It was well received and remains a favourite, though critics have tended to prefer her small-group records, finding the large orchestras occasionally lush to the point of distraction. Its comparison with Ella's Gershwin set, which came two years later, is an old debate: Ella's clarity against Vaughan's grandeur.","Pick three songs and play Vaughan's and Ella's versions side by side: “The Man I Love” works well. Notice how Ella keeps the melody plain and Vaughan transforms it. Then listen to Gershwin's own Rhapsody in Blue to hear the orchestral world these songs came from."],"dinah-washington-dinah-jams":["A live-in-the-studio jam session in which Washington sings long, loose versions of standards with an all-star band including trumpeters Clifford Brown, Maynard Ferguson and Clark Terry, and Max Roach on drums. Each song runs long, with extended horn solos between the vocal choruses. The programme includes “Lover, Come Back to Me”, “I've Got You Under My Skin”, “You Go to My Head” and a medley. Washington's voice is sharp, bluesy and precise, cutting through the band like a trumpet.","It was recorded in August 1954 in Los Angeles before a small invited audience, a recording format EmArcy used to capture the spontaneity of a club. Washington, the most successful rhythm-and-blues singer of the era, rarely got the chance to make a pure jazz record. Brown and Roach were then touring together as co-leaders of their famous quintet.","Listen to the trumpet chases: three very different players, Brown's warm, flowing lines, Ferguson's screaming high notes and Terry's humorous agility, trade phrases, and Washington holds her own against them. Her diction is striking: every word comes through clearly, even at speed, a skill from gospel singing in Chicago churches.","It is widely regarded as Washington's finest jazz album and a classic of the singer-with-jam-session format. Critics praise it as a rare chance to hear her improvising at length. Some find the long instrumental passages leave too little room for the voice, but most treat the balance as the point.","Begin with “Lover, Come Back to Me” and listen for how Washington attacks the first note without warming up. Then compare Sarah Vaughan's album with Clifford Brown from the same year: two great singers, one trumpeter, very different temperaments. Notice how Washington never scats; her improvisation lives in timing and emphasis."],"dinah-washington-what-a-diffrence-a-day-makes":["A slow love song, in which the singer marvels at how one day with a new lover has transformed her life, set with a steady rhythm section, strings and a soft backing chorus. Washington sings the melody almost straight, with her bright, clipped tone and bluesy bends at phrase ends, and the arrangement keeps a gentle, rocking beat that makes the old ballad sound like modern pop. It is under three minutes long.","The song began as a Mexican bolero, “Cuando vuelva a tu lado”, by the composer María Grever in 1934, with English words by Stanley Adams. Washington's recording for Mercury, produced by Clyde Otis with arrangements by Belford Hendricks, was her biggest pop hit and won a Grammy for best rhythm-and-blues performance. It brought her to a white mainstream audience at the end of the 1950s.","Listen for how the backing rhythm is not swing but a rock-steady triplet beat, the pattern of late-1950s rhythm-and-blues ballads. That choice shows the record's crossover design: jazz singer, Latin song, pop strings, rhythm-and-blues groove. Washington reportedly disliked the strings and chorus, but her phrasing keeps the record from turning sweet.","Jazz critics at the time sometimes dismissed it as a commercial sell-out, but it is now admired as a model of crossover, and it prefigured the pop soul of the 1960s. Washington died in 1963 at thirty-nine, and this record became her best-remembered, sometimes at the cost of her jazz work.","Listen once for Washington's precise enunciation and bluesy slides, then again for the beat underneath. Compare with Dinah Jams to hear the same singer in pure jazz, and with Aretha Franklin, who later recorded a tribute album to Washington, to hear where this sound led."],"carmen-mcrae-sings-lover-man":["Twelve songs associated with Billie Holiday, sung by McRae with a small swinging band that features cornetist Nat Adderley and tenor saxophonist Eddie “Lockjaw” Davis. The programme includes “Lover Man”, “Them There Eyes”, “Yesterdays”, “Miss Brown to You”, “God Bless the Child” and “Strange Fruit”. McRae's voice is lighter and more ironic than Holiday's, with crisp diction and a slight edge, and she sings behind the beat in the Holiday manner.","Recorded for Columbia in 1961, two years after Holiday's death, it was one of the first tribute albums to her. McRae had known Holiday since the late 1930s, and Holiday recorded a song McRae had written, “Dream of Life”, in 1939. McRae also worked as a pianist, which shaped her harmonic sense. The album appeared during a period when jazz singers were struggling against rock and roll for record-company attention.","Listen to how McRae alters Holiday's phrasing rather than copying it: she keeps the delayed timing but makes the vowels shorter and the consonants harder, which turns sorrow into something more worldly. Her “Strange Fruit” is quiet and restrained, and the band drops out almost entirely, a brave choice for a song Holiday had made her own.","It was admired on release and has come to be regarded as one of McRae's finest records and among the best Holiday tributes. Critics note that it avoids imitation and shows McRae's own personality. Some prefer her later, more mature recordings, but this remains the one most recommended to newcomers.","Play Holiday's God Bless the Child and then McRae's, and listen for the difference in weight: Holiday heavy with feeling, McRae cooler and sharper. Start the album with “Lover Man”, then listen for Adderley's cornet answering her phrases and for the way she lets the band breathe between verses."],"carmen-mcrae-carmen-sings-monk":["Thirteen compositions by Thelonious Monk sung with lyrics, backed by a small band with the tenor saxophonist Clifford Jordan. The songs, including “'Round Midnight”, “Ruby, My Dear”, “Straight, No Chaser”, “Well, You Needn't” and “Rhythm-a-Ning”, have awkward leaps and jagged rhythms that few singers dare attempt. McRae, by then in her late sixties with a darker, grainier voice, negotiates them with ease, and her wry phrasing suits Monk's humour.","Recorded in 1988 and released in 1990 on the Novus label, it came near the end of McRae's career and eight years after Monk's death. Most of the lyrics were written by jazz writers such as Jon Hendricks, Abbey Lincoln and others, some specifically for this project. The late 1980s saw a revival of interest in bebop classics among record companies and young musicians.","Monk's melodies were written for piano and horns, not voices, so they often jump across wide intervals or land on unexpected notes. Listen for how McRae hits those odd notes with confidence; she had been a pianist herself and understood the harmony from the inside. Her voice's lower, husky register in old age suits Monk's gruff, oblique style.","It was praised on release as one of the best vocal jazz albums of its decade and received a Grammy nomination. Critics have called it a late-career triumph and one of the few convincing attempts to sing Monk's tunes. Some lyricists' words are clumsy, but McRae's delivery makes them work.","Play Monk's own 'Round Midnight and then McRae's to hear the tune given words. Listen for the tricky leaps in “Ruby, My Dear”. Then go back to her Holiday tribute from thirty years earlier to hear how her voice deepened."],"peggy-lee-black-coffee":["A small-group jazz album of late-night songs, sung by Lee with a quartet built around a muted trumpet and the pianist Jimmy Rowles. The songs include the title track, “I've Got You Under My Skin”, “Easy Living”, “My Heart Belongs to Daddy”, “It Ain't Necessarily So” and “A Woman Alone with the Blues”. Lee's voice is soft and cool, almost whispered, and she keeps it low and conversational so every syllable seems confided.","It was first released by Decca as an eight-song ten-inch LP in 1953, then expanded to twelve songs in 1956. Lee had made her name with Benny Goodman's band during the war and had many pop hits for Capitol, but here she made a pure jazz record at a time when singers were being pushed toward commercial material. She was also a songwriter, co-writing hits with her husband, guitarist Dave Barbour.","Listen for how little Lee sings: she barely raises her voice above a murmur and lets the microphone do the work, a lesson learned from Crosby and extended here. The title song's lyric is about a woman sitting up all night with coffee and cigarettes waiting for a lover who does not come; its slow blues form links this cool nightclub album to much older traditions.","It was admired by jazz critics and has become Lee's most celebrated album, often ranked among the great vocal jazz records. Some regard it as the start of the hushed, intimate style that Julie London and others followed. The 1956 expansion is the usual version heard today, and its extra songs are generally judged the equal of the originals.","Listen in the dark and start with the title track, then compare it with Sinatra's In the Wee Small Hours from the same years. Notice how Lee's quietness forces the listener to lean in, and listen for the muted trumpet answering her."],"peggy-lee-fever":["A song about desire as a physical heat, performed with almost nothing: a walking string bass, a drummer's light touch, finger-snaps and Lee's soft, sly voice. The tension comes from restraint; the arrangement barely changes, but the key rises step by step through the verses, so the heat seems to increase. Lee sings close to the microphone, stretching words and pausing between phrases so the silence becomes part of the music.","The song had been a rhythm-and-blues hit for Little Willie John in 1956, written by Eddie Cooley and Otis Blackwell. Lee's Capitol version in 1958 rewrote parts of the lyric, adding verses about famous lovers from history and literature, and she stripped the arrangement down to bass, drums and snaps. It became her biggest hit in years and is now her signature.","The finger-snaps were part of the record's design, and they turned the song into an act of sly restraint: instead of shouting about passion, Lee withholds. Listen, too, for the key changes between verses, each one a small upward shift that the ear feels as increasing tension without noticing why. Lee's added verses were reportedly uncredited in the songwriting royalties.","It was an instant success and has been covered many times, from Elvis Presley to Madonna. Critics consider it a model of minimalism in pop arrangement, and it is often cited as one of the sexiest records of the 1950s despite its restraint. Some argue that Little Willie John's original deserves more credit, and his version is increasingly recommended alongside Lee's.","Listen first for what is not there: no piano, no horns. Then count the key changes. Compare with Little Willie John's original to hear how Lee turned a rhythm-and-blues shout into cool jazz, and with Black Coffee to hear her hushed style in its first form."],"peggy-lee-is-that-all-there-is":["A spoken-word song in which Lee recites, in a flat, weary voice, memories of disappointment: a house fire seen as a child, a trip to the circus, a first love. After each memory she sings a short, waltzing chorus that shrugs off the disappointment and suggests we might as well keep drinking and dancing. The orchestra, arranged by Randy Newman, plays a cabaret-style accompaniment with a slightly sour, Brechtian flavour.","It was written by Jerry Leiber and Mike Stoller, the rock-and-roll songwriters behind Hound Dog, and was based on Thomas Mann's 1896 short story Disillusionment. Lee recorded it for Capitol in 1969, when she was nearly fifty and the era of crooners seemed over; it became an unexpected hit and won her a Grammy. The late 1960s were a time of disillusionment, and the song's mood fitted.","The arrangement nods to Kurt Weill's Berlin cabaret music, with its bittersweet, slightly out-of-tune oompah, and so the record links rock-and-roll songwriters to European art song. Listen for how Lee's speaking voice becomes more and more resigned through the verses, and how the final verse contemplates death in a matter-of-fact way.","Some listeners found it depressing and odd on release, but it was a hit and critics have come to see it as one of the most original pop records of its era. It has been discussed as an existential statement and as a bridge between cabaret and pop. Its reputation has grown steadily, and it is often cited as the most daring record of her late career.","Listen as though to a short story: follow each memory, then notice how the chorus changes meaning as the verses darken. Then play Kurt Weill's Threepenny Opera to hear where the orchestral colour comes from, and Randy Newman's own albums for his dry wit."],"julie-london-julie-is-her-name":["Thirteen standards sung by London with only the jazz guitarist Barney Kessel and a bassist. Her voice is low, smoky and close, almost a murmur, and the sparse accompaniment leaves every breath and syllable exposed. The album opens with “Cry Me a River”, a slow song of bitter farewell, and continues through “I Should Care”, “Say It Isn't So”, “Easy Street” and “Laura”. The feeling is late-night intimacy, as if someone were singing across a small room.","It was London's first album, made for the new Liberty label in Hollywood in 1955. She was an actress with little singing experience, and the album's success made her a star; its cover photograph was part of the appeal. “Cry Me a River” was written by Arthur Hamilton, and it became a million-selling hit. The album benefited from the new high-fidelity recording that made small, quiet ensembles sound present.","Listen to Kessel's guitar: he plays chords and short melodic answers that frame each phrase, a lesson in how little accompaniment a singer needs. London's voice is very limited in range, and the whole album is built around that limitation, keeping every song low and close. Its restraint owes much to Peggy Lee's Black Coffee.","It was a big success and has remained London's best-known record, often praised for its minimalism and mood. Some jazz critics have dismissed her as more a stylist than a singer, but others argue that her restraint is a legitimate art. The album is now a cult favourite and regarded as a classic of the intimate cool-jazz style.","Play it quietly at night and listen for the space between the notes. Start with “Cry Me a River”, then compare with Peggy Lee's Black Coffee and Chet Baker Sings for other versions of the whispered 1950s style, and notice how much the silences between phrases carry."],"chet-baker-it-could-happen-to-you":["Ten standards sung by Baker with a quartet led by the pianist Kenny Drew, with Baker's trumpet appearing in solos between vocal choruses. The songs include “I'm Old Fashioned”, “You're Driving Me Crazy”, “It Could Happen to You”, “My Heart Stood Still”, “Everything Happens to Me” and “How Long Has This Been Going On?”. Baker's voice is soft, high and androgynous, almost without vibrato, and here he also scats, improvising wordlessly as if with his trumpet.","Recorded in New York in 1958 for Riverside, it was Baker's first vocal album after moving east from California, and the backing is more hard-driving than on his West Coast records. Baker was already struggling with heroin addiction, which would bring arrests and exile in Europe over the next years. The album was his attempt to follow up the success of Chet Baker Sings.","Listen for Baker's scat solos: he sings improvised lines in exactly the style of his trumpet playing, so you hear the same musical mind in two instruments. The New York rhythm section plays harder than his California colleagues, and his soft voice floats against it, a contrast that gives the album a tension absent from his earlier vocal records.","It was well received but has always stood in the shadow of Chet Baker Sings. Some critics prefer this album for its livelier backing and the scat singing. Others find Baker's singing an acquired taste, fragile to some ears and weak to others, a debate that has followed his vocal work throughout.","Listen first to the title track, then to “You're Driving Me Crazy” for the scat. Compare with Chet Baker Sings to hear the West Coast versus New York backing. Then play Julie London's Julie Is Her Name for another whisper-close voice from the same years."],"mel-torme-with-the-marty-paich-dek-tette":["Twelve standards sung by Tormé with a ten-piece band of Los Angeles jazz musicians, arranged by Marty Paich. The ensemble includes French horn and tuba alongside trumpets and saxophones, giving a mellow, rounded sound, and the arrangements are tight and swinging. The songs include “Lulu's Back in Town”, “When the Sun Comes Out”, “Fascinating Rhythm”, “The Lady Is a Tramp” and “Lullaby of Birdland”. Tormé's voice is light and velvety, and his phrasing is as precise as a horn player's.","It was recorded in 1956 for Bethlehem, a small independent jazz label. Tormé, known as “The Velvet Fog”, had been a teen idol in the 1940s and co-wrote “The Christmas Song” with Bob Wells in 1945. By the mid-1950s he wanted to be taken seriously as a jazz singer. Paich's ensemble was modelled on the Miles Davis nonet of Birth of the Cool, with its unusual brass colours.","The instrumentation is the secret: the French horn and tuba give the ensemble the soft, dark chords of Birth of the Cool, and Tormé's light voice floats over them almost like another horn. Listen for how the arrangements voice the band in close harmony underneath him, and how Tormé answers the band's riffs with perfect timing. He was also a skilled drummer and arranger.","It was well received by jazz critics and is widely considered Tormé's finest album and one of the great vocal jazz records of the 1950s. Some find it too polished, lacking the emotional weight of Sinatra or Holiday. Admirers argue that its precision is its expressive point, and it has been reissued many times, sometimes as Lulu's Back in Town.","Start with “Lulu's Back in Town” and listen to how the band and voice lock together. Then play Miles Davis's Birth of the Cool to hear where the band sound comes from. Compare with Sinatra's Songs for Swingin' Lovers for another singer-arranger partnership."],"johnny-mathis-chances-are":["A slow, tender ballad by Robert Allen and Al Stillman, sung by Mathis with a lush orchestra arranged by Ray Conniff. The song is a shy confession of love, and Mathis sings it in a high, smooth, sweet tenor with a fast, gentle vibrato. The arrangement is soft and understated, with a steady rhythm, strings and a piano. The record is barely three minutes long and depends entirely on the voice.","Mathis was twenty-one, a young singer from San Francisco who had turned down a place in the Olympic trials for the high jump to sign with Columbia. The label's producer Mitch Miller steered him from jazz toward romantic ballads, and “Chances Are” reached number one on a Billboard chart in 1957. It came at the height of rock and roll, and showed that a crooner could still reach the teenage market.","Listen to Mathis's upper register: he sings high notes with a light, open tone that sounds almost like a falsetto, a sound different from the baritone crooners who dominated the 1950s. His training, which included classical and opera lessons as a teenager, gave him breath control that lets him hold long, soft phrases without strain.","It was a huge hit and became one of Mathis's signature songs. Critics have sometimes found his style too sweet, but others praise his vocal control and his influence on later romantic ballad singers. It is often cited as a key record of the late-1950s romantic ballad, bridging the crooner tradition and teen pop.","Listen for Mathis's softness and his high notes, then compare with Sinatra's In the Wee Small Hours to hear the difference between a young tenor's sweetness and an older baritone's weariness. Try it alongside Nat King Cole's Nature Boy for another smooth crossover voice."],"johnny-mathis-johnnys-greatest-hits":["A collection of Mathis's early hit singles, gathered on one LP: “Chances Are”, “It's Not for Me to Say”, “Wonderful! Wonderful!”, “The Twelfth of Never”, “Wild Is the Wind” and others. The songs are romantic ballads sung in Mathis's high, sweet tenor, with lush studio orchestras. As an album, it plays like a series of variations on one mood: young love, sung softly and sincerely.","Columbia released it in 1958, when Mathis was among the label's biggest sellers. It is often said to be one of the first albums to gather a singer's hit singles, and it stayed on the Billboard album chart for nearly ten years, a record that stood until Pink Floyd's Dark Side of the Moon. It fitted a new market: the long-playing record as a purchase for the home, played over and over.","“Wild Is the Wind”, the theme song Mathis sang for a 1957 film, was written by Dimitri Tiomkin and Ned Washington; Nina Simone later made a very different, darker version, which in turn inspired David Bowie. Listen to Mathis's reading as the starting point of that lineage: the same melody, but sung with innocence rather than anguish.","It was an enormous commercial success, and critics have tended to discuss it more as a phenomenon than as art, noting its role in establishing the greatest-hits format. Some find its sweetness cloying; others admire the consistency of the performances and the arrangers' craft.","Listen to “Wild Is the Wind”, then play Nina Simone's version for a startling contrast. Enjoy the album as a portrait of late-1950s romantic pop, and notice how the arrangements vary while Mathis's voice stays constant. Hear it as a snapshot of the home record player at the end of the fifties."],"andy-williams-moon-river":["A slow waltz by Henry Mancini with lyrics by Johnny Mercer, sung by Williams with soft strings and a gentle choir. The song is a dreamy reverie about a river and a wanderer's hopes, and Williams sings it in a smooth, warm, clear tenor, never raising his voice. The arrangement is spare at the start and swells gently, and the melody's gentle upward reaches give it a sense of openness and longing.","The song was written for the 1961 film Breakfast at Tiffany's, where Audrey Hepburn sang it with a guitar; it won the Academy Award for Best Original Song. Williams sang it at the Oscar ceremony in 1962 and recorded it for the album Moon River and Other Great Movie Themes. It became the theme song of his NBC television show, which ran through the 1960s.","Williams never released it as a hit single, yet it became his signature through television and album sales, a sign of how crooners were moving from the charts into variety shows. Listen to the melody's range: it spans little more than an octave, reportedly because Mancini kept it within Hepburn's modest voice, and Williams, who could sing far higher, keeps it deliberately plain.","It became Williams's defining performance, though many other singers recorded the song. Critics have sometimes dismissed his smoothness as bland, but others praise his control and restraint. The song itself is widely regarded as one of the great film songs, and Williams's version is the most familiar.","Listen for the moment the choir enters, and for Williams's unhurried breath control. Compare with Tony Bennett's I Left My Heart in San Francisco from the same year to hear two crooners at the last peak of their style before the Beatles."],"andy-williams-its-the-most-wonderful-time-of-the-year":["A brisk, bright Christmas song in a fast waltz rhythm, written by Eddie Pola and George Wyle, sung by Williams with a choir and a busy orchestra of bells, brass and strings. The song lists the pleasures of the season, parties, snow, carollers and friends, and the tempo never slows. It is barely two and a half minutes long and runs at a breathless pace, with Williams's smooth voice riding on top.","It was written for Williams's NBC television Christmas special and released in 1963 on The Andy Williams Christmas Album; Wyle was the show's vocal director. The Williams family Christmas shows became an annual American ritual through the 1960s. The same year, Phil Spector released his rock-and-roll Christmas album, a very different take on the holiday.","The song was not a big hit in its first years and gained its present status only through decades of radio play at Christmas; it is now among the most-played holiday songs. Listen to the orchestration: the sleigh bells, the celesta and the vocal chorus are carefully layered, and the waltz rhythm gives it a spinning, dancing feel.","It is now one of the most familiar Christmas songs in the United States, played every year. Critics rarely write about it seriously, but it is admired for its craftsmanship and energy. Some find its relentless cheer overwhelming, and its lyric's mention of ghost stories has puzzled listeners.","Listen once for the orchestration: bells, celesta, choir. Then compare with Nat King Cole's The Christmas Song and Irving Berlin's White Christmas for slower, more reflective seasonal songs, and Phil Spector's Christmas album for the rock version of the same year."],"nina-simone-little-girl-blue":["Simone's debut, a trio album of standards and one original, with her own piano at the centre. The songs include “Mood Indigo”, “Don't Smoke in Bed”, “Little Girl Blue”, “Love Me or Leave Me”, “My Baby Just Cares for Me”, “I Loves You, Porgy” and her “Central Park Blues”. Her voice is deep and grave, and her piano is classical in touch: she plays counterpoint, runs and Bach-like inventions around the songs, so each track is a small piano recital as well as a vocal.","It was recorded in New York in late 1957 for Bethlehem and released in 1958. Simone, born Eunice Waymon in North Carolina, had trained as a classical pianist and hoped to study at the Curtis Institute, which rejected her; she began playing in Atlantic City bars to make money and was asked to sing. She sold the rights to this album for a flat fee, and never earned royalties from it.","In “Little Girl Blue”, Simone plays the Christmas carol “Good King Wenceslas” as a countermelody on the piano under Rodgers and Hart's song, so two tunes run at once. In “Love Me or Leave Me”, her piano solo turns into a Bach-style fugue. Her “I Loves You, Porgy”, from Gershwin's opera, became her only top-twenty pop hit in the United States.","It sold modestly but “I Loves You, Porgy” was a hit, and the album has come to be seen as one of the most remarkable debuts in jazz. “My Baby Just Cares for Me” became a hit again in 1987 after a perfume advertisement. Critics praise the fusion of classical piano and jazz singing that made Simone unlike anyone else.","Listen to the title track and try to hear both tunes at once. Then play “Love Me or Leave Me” and listen for the fugue in the piano solo. Compare with Bach's Well-Tempered Clavier for the source of her counterpoint."],"nina-simone-at-town-hall":["A concert album, partly recorded at New York's Town Hall in September 1959, that shows the range of Simone's early repertoire: the Appalachian folk song “Black Is the Color of My True Love's Hair”, Gershwin's “Summertime”, the film song “Wild Is the Wind”, “The Other Woman” and “Cotton Eyed Joe”. She accompanies herself on piano, with bass and drums, and moves between folk, jazz and art song in one set. Her voice is dark and slow.","It was Simone's first album for Colpix, the label she joined after Bethlehem, and her first major New York concert, a debut at a respected hall usually used for classical recitals. That venue mattered to her: she thought of herself as a classical musician forced into popular music. Some tracks were recorded in the studio and presented as part of the concert.","Listen for her piano introductions, which often quote classical figures and patterns; she constructs each song as a small concert piece. Her “Wild Is the Wind” is slower and darker than Johnny Mathis's original, turning a film ballad into an art song; David Bowie later cited her version as the model for his own.","It was well received and helped establish her as a concert artist rather than a nightclub singer. Critics have praised its range and its folk-song performances in particular. Some discographers note the confusion between live and studio tracks, but most listeners hear it as a coherent portrait of the young Simone.","Start with “Black Is the Color of My True Love's Hair” for her folk side, then play “Wild Is the Wind” and compare it with Johnny Mathis's version. Listen for how she slows the tempo far beyond what other singers would dare."],"nina-simone-in-concert":["A live album recorded at Carnegie Hall in 1964, in which Simone mixes love songs with protest songs. The set includes “I Loves You, Porgy”, “Plain Gold Ring”, Kurt Weill's “Pirate Jenny”, “Old Jim Crow”, “Don't Smoke in Bed”, “Go Limp” and her own “Mississippi Goddam”. Her voice has grown harder and more commanding, and her piano drives the band. The mood swings from tender to furious to bitterly funny.","It was her first album for Philips, recorded in spring 1964 at the height of the civil rights movement. “Mississippi Goddam” was written in fury after the murder of the activist Medgar Evers and the bombing of a Birmingham church that killed four Black girls in 1963. It was banned by radio stations in several Southern states. This record marked Simone's turn from nightclub stylist to political artist.","“Mississippi Goddam” is set to a jaunty show-tune rhythm, and Simone jokes that it is a show tune for a show not yet written; the cheerful music makes the anger more biting. In “Pirate Jenny”, from Weill and Brecht's Threepenny Opera, she turns a German cabaret song about a servant's revenge into a story set in a Southern town, and the audience's nervous silence is audible.","It was controversial on release and has since been hailed as one of the great political records of the 1960s. “Mississippi Goddam” is now regarded as a key civil-rights anthem. Critics have praised Simone's courage in risking her career, and the album is often ranked among her most important.","Start with “Mississippi Goddam” and listen for the gap between the jolly rhythm and the words. Then play Weill's Threepenny Opera, and hear Simone reclaim its song. Compare with Max Roach's We Insist! and Sam Cooke's A Change Is Gonna Come."],"nina-simone-pastel-blues":["A blues album that moves from quiet laments to a ten-minute storm. The songs include “Be My Husband”, sung with only hand-claps and foot-stomps, “Nobody Knows You When You're Down and Out”, “Trouble in Mind”, “Strange Fruit” and the long closing “Sinnerman”. Simone's piano, guitar and rhythm section build to a driving, repetitive groove in “Sinnerman”, a spiritual about a sinner running from judgement, which ends in a pounding, almost trance-like climax.","Recorded in 1965 for Philips, it followed Nina Simone in Concert and reflected her growing interest in Black musical traditions and the civil rights movement. “Sinnerman” was a traditional spiritual that Simone had learned from her mother's church services. “Strange Fruit”, the anti-lynching song made famous by Billie Holiday, was an act of homage and protest at a time of violence in the South.","“Be My Husband” is performed with only voice and percussion, recalling work songs and field hollers. In “Sinnerman”, listen for how the piano, drums and clapping build layer by layer until the whole band is chanting; it anticipates the hypnotic grooves of later soul and even electronic dance music. The track has been sampled widely in hip-hop.","It was well received and “Sinnerman” has become one of Simone's most famous recordings, used in many films and sampled by producers. Critics praise the album's rawness and range. Some have argued that its “Strange Fruit” is the most harrowing version after Holiday's; others prefer Holiday's original.","Play “Sinnerman” loud, and listen for how the rhythm keeps building. Then go back to “Be My Husband” and “Strange Fruit”. Compare with Billie Holiday's Strange Fruit and Mahalia Jackson's gospel recordings to hear the church and blues traditions Simone drew on and remade."],"edith-piaf-la-vie-en-rose":["A slow, swaying love song, barely three minutes long, in which Piaf's voice rises out of a soft bed of strings and a gently rocking rhythm. The melody moves in small, caressing steps, almost spoken, before opening into a broad refrain that she leans on with her whole weight. The singer describes how the world changes colour when her lover holds her and speaks to her. What you remember is the voice: hard-edged, vibrating quickly, rolling its consonants, never sweet.","Piaf wrote the words in 1945, in a Paris just freed from German occupation and hungry for tenderness. Because she was not yet registered with the French authors' society as a composer, the music was credited to the guitarist and songwriter Louiguy. Other singers performed it first, but Piaf's own recording, released on Columbia in 1947, made it hers. She soon sang it in English on tours of the United States, where it became her calling card.","The song is built on a trick of contrast: a gentle, rosy melody delivered by a voice formed in the streets of Belleville, singing for coins. Listen to how Piaf holds back in the verse, keeping her tone narrow and almost conversational, then unleashes the full vibrato only on the refrain. That tightening and release, rather than the tune itself, is where the emotion lives.","It was an instant success and has never really left the air, recorded by Louis Armstrong, Grace Jones and hundreds of others and used in countless films. Some French critics once saw it as sentimental next to Piaf's darker realist songs. Today it is treated as the song that carried chanson around the world after the war, the tune foreigners hum when they think of Paris.","Listen first only to the voice: notice how she darkens and brightens single words and how little she moves the melody until the refrain. Then hear Louis Armstrong's 1950 version, relaxed and swinging, for a completely different idea of the same song. Piaf's later live recordings at the Olympia show how much rawer she could make it."],"edith-piaf-non-je-ne-regrette-rien":["Two and a half minutes of defiance. A march-like pulse on snare drum and low brass sets up a melody that climbs in short, stubborn phrases, each one pushed higher than the last. Piaf sings of sweeping away the past, good and bad alike, and starting again for a new love. The orchestra swells behind her in blocks, the drums pound, and the final notes are held with a force that sounds like a vow rather than a performance.","Charles Dumont wrote the music and Michel Vaucaire the words, and they brought the song to Piaf in 1960, when she was ill, exhausted and in debt. She took it at once, and it became the centrepiece of her return to the Paris Olympia at the end of that year, a run of concerts that also rescued the struggling hall. She dedicated her recording to the French Foreign Legion, then fighting in the Algerian war.","The legionnaires took the dedication seriously: after the failed generals' putsch in Algeria in 1961, soldiers of a disbanded parachute regiment reportedly sang it as they were marched away, and it has stayed in the Legion's repertoire. Listen, too, for how short the melody's phrases are, each a single breath; it is written for a singer whose lungs were failing, and turns that limit into punch.","It was a huge hit and became Piaf's last great signature before her death in 1963. Some hear in it pure self-assertion, others a darker refusal to face what one has done, and its political uses have kept that argument alive. It has since been sampled, parodied and used in films from war dramas to Inception, where its slowed-down brass becomes part of the score.","Listen to the snare drum and brass under the first verse and notice how march-like the song is; then follow how each phrase is pitched a little higher. Compare it with La vie en rose, thirteen years earlier: the same voice, now rougher and more commanding. Seek out the live Olympia recording for the crowd's roar."],"charles-trenet-ya-d-la-joie":["A bouncing, swinging song in which Trenet, bright-voiced and grinning, reels off a list of absurd, cheerful images: the whole city of Paris waking up drunk on happiness, monuments and machines behaving like children on holiday. A small dance band plays with a jazz lilt, brass punctuating his lines. The tempo never lets up, and the melody hops in wide, playful leaps rather than the sighing steps of older French song.","Trenet first gave the song to Maurice Chevalier, who introduced it in a Paris revue in 1937; Trenet's own recording followed in 1938, just as he launched his solo career after the break-up of his duo with Johnny Hess. France was in the afterglow of the Popular Front, with its first paid holidays, and American swing and Django Reinhardt's Hot Club were changing the sound of Paris nightlife.","Listen to the rhythm under the words: the band is swinging, not marching, which was new in French popular song. Trenet sings slightly ahead of and behind the beat like a jazz singer, and his nonsense images owe as much to the Surrealist poets he admired as to music hall. The happiness is so relentless that some listeners, knowing what was about to happen in 1939, hear an edge of mania in it.","Critics and audiences hailed Trenet as 'le fou chantant', the singing madman, and the song made him a star almost overnight. Later generations of songwriters, Brassens among them, credited Trenet with showing that chanson could be poetic, funny and modern at once. It is sometimes dismissed as a period novelty, but its mixture of swing and whimsy set the template for post-war French song.","Don't try to follow every image; just ride the rhythm and listen to how Trenet bends the tune around the beat. Then play Django Reinhardt's Dinah or Duke Ellington's It Don't Mean a Thing to hear where the swing came from. Trenet's own 1938 recording is the one to find, with its crackle and period dance band."],"charles-trenet-la-mer":["A slow, rocking song about the Mediterranean seen from the shore: the water glinting, white birds, houses soaked in rain, the sea compared to a shepherdess watching her flock of waves. The orchestra rolls under the voice in long swells, strings and harp suggesting light on water, and the melody rises and falls in wide, calm arcs. Trenet sings with less clowning than usual, softly, as if describing something he has just seen from a train window.","Trenet wrote it with his pianist Léo Chauliac, reportedly in 1943 on a train running along the lagoons near the coast of Languedoc, close to his native Narbonne. It was first recorded by another singer, Roland Gerbeau, in 1945; Trenet's own version, released in 1946, became one of the best-selling French records ever. In 1959 Bobby Darin turned it into Beyond the Sea, with new English words.","The English version is not a translation: Beyond the Sea is about a lover waiting across the water, while the French is a pure landscape, a song almost without people. Listen, too, to the orchestra's harp and high strings, which borrow the shimmering textures of Debussy and Ravel, the concert-hall French sea translated into three minutes of popular song.","It was a hit at once and has been recorded, by some counts, several thousand times, which makes it one of the most covered songs of the century. French critics long treated it as lovely but slight next to Trenet's wittier songs; it has since come to be heard as his most perfect, a song that sums up the southern French coast as surely as a Matisse window.","Listen to it with your eyes closed and follow the orchestra rather than the voice: the waves are in the strings. Then play Debussy's La mer for the symphonic version of the same water, and Bobby Darin's Beyond the Sea for the swinging American one. Trenet's 1946 recording is warmer than his later remakes."],"georges-brassens-la-mauvaise-reputation":["Just a voice, a strummed guitar and a plucked double bass keeping a steady, jaunty two-beat. Brassens, in a plain baritone with a southern accent, describes a man who lives quietly by his own rules in a small town and is hated for it: he stays in bed on the national holiday, cheers the wrong people, looks away when others want a lynching. The tune is simple and hummable; the bite is all in the words.","Brassens was thirty and almost unknown when the singer Patachou heard him and put him on stage at her Montmartre cabaret in 1952. The producer Jacques Canetti signed him to Polydor, and this became the title song of his first record. France was conservative, Catholic and still settling wartime scores; Brassens, who had been sent to forced labour in Germany, wrote for the anarchist press and distrusted every uniform.","The accompaniment never changes: Brassens always worked with just guitar and bass, usually his friend Pierre Nicolas, so that nothing could distract from the words. Listen to how he fits long, supple sentences into a tiny tune, with rhymes as intricate as a classical poet's; he admired François Villon and the old ballad forms, and this is a medieval outlaw's song dressed in a cardigan.","Some of Brassens's early songs were kept off French radio as offensive to decency or to authority, which only spread his fame by word of mouth. Critics were puzzled by a singer who did no showmanship at all. Within a decade he was a national institution, and in 1967 the Académie française gave him its poetry prize. This song remains his manifesto.","Listen once for the bass and guitar, which simply keep time like a clock, and once for the words, following the story in a translation. Then hear De André's Italian version or Brassens's own later songs to see how the formula never changed. The 1952 recording, dry and close, is the one."],"georges-brassens-les-copains-d-abord":["A cheerful, rolling song in Brassens's usual lineup of voice, guitar and double bass, with a tune that sounds like it has always existed. It describes a small boat whose crew are friends, and uses that boat to talk about friendship itself: it is no famous ship, it never sails for glory, but it holds together through storms because no one on board pretends to be a hero. The refrain invites a room to sing along.","Brassens wrote it in 1964 for Yves Robert's film Les Copains, a comedy about a gang of friends playing pranks across provincial France, and it appeared that year on his album of the same name. By then he was fixed in his routine: a small house in the south of Paris, cats, pipe, a band of old friends, and long runs at the Bobino music hall where audiences knew his songs by heart.","The song is a web of puns on sailing words and on the phrase in its title, which also means 'on board'. Brassens slips in allusions to famous shipwrecks and to Montaigne's essay on friendship; the grand boats of French history and myth are named only to be dismissed. Under the jokes lies a serious moral: friendship as the one institution he trusted.","The film is half-forgotten; the song became Brassens's most widely sung, taught in schools and bawled at weddings, scout camps and funerals across the French-speaking world. Some critics find it the gentlest thing he wrote, lacking the anarchist bite of his early work. Admirers answer that its craft, the way every line lands on a rhyme and a double meaning, is his art at its fullest.","Listen for the bass, which bounces like a boat on a swell, and for how easily the refrain could be sung by a crowd. Then play Brel's Amsterdam, which puts sailors in a very different mood. Any of Brassens's Bobino concert recordings will let you hear an audience singing along."],"jacques-brel-ne-me-quitte-pas":["A slow plea to a departing lover that grows from near-whisper to near-collapse. A piano sets out a hesitant, circling figure; strings gather; Brel's voice, rough and trembling, piles up promise after promise, offering everything he has and then more, until he is ready to erase himself entirely. The same refrain returns again and again, each time more abject. It is less a song than a nervous breakdown in slow motion.","Brel, a Belgian from Brussels who had come to Paris in 1953, wrote it in 1959 after the end of his affair with the singer Suzanne Gabriello. It appeared that year on his fourth album, arranged by François Rauber, his long-time orchestrator. Brel was then breaking through at the Bobino and Olympia music halls with a style of performance that shocked audiences: sweating, gesturing, singing as if his life depended on it.","Listen to how little the melody itself changes: the drama is carried by Brel's voice and by Rauber's strings, which thicken verse by verse. And the opening melody has often been traced to Liszt's Sixth Hungarian Rhapsody, which Brel probably knew; a nineteenth-century piano tune becomes the skeleton of a modern love song. Brel's 1972 re-recording is slower and more restrained.","It became Brel's most famous song and one of the most covered in French, sung by Nina Simone, Dusty Springfield, Scott Walker and Sting; in English it was softened as If You Go Away. Some listeners, notably feminist critics, hear it as a song of humiliation rather than love, and Brel himself reportedly called it a song about cowardice, not romance. That tension is why it lasts.","Hear the 1959 original first, then Brel's 1972 remake, and notice how differently he paces the build. Then compare Nina Simone's version, which turns the plea into something proud. Try listening without a translation: the sound of the voice alone tells you most of the story."],"jacques-brel-amsterdam":["A song that starts almost murmured and ends in a roar. Over a slow, repeating melody in the orchestra, Brel paints the port of Amsterdam through its sailors: they sleep, they are born and die, they eat fish with their hands, they drink, dance clumsily and go with women for money. With each verse the orchestra grows louder and the tempo presses forward, and Brel's voice goes from description to disgust to a kind of hoarse pity.","Brel never recorded Amsterdam in a studio. It exists in his recording from the Paris Olympia in October 1964, released by Barclay as a live album, where the audience's ovation at the end is part of the track. He was then at the height of his stage career, touring relentlessly, and within three years would give up concerts altogether, exhausted by the physical toll of performances like this one.","Listen to the melody: it closely resembles the old English tune Greensleeves, stretched into a slow, obsessive circle that repeats without ever resolving. Brel builds the whole song as one long crescendo, the same four-line shape again and again, so that the effect comes from pressure rather than development. The applause at the end starts before the last note has faded.","The Olympia recording made it one of Brel's signatures, and it became a staple of English-language cabaret rock through Scott Walker's 1968 version and David Bowie's, recorded in 1971. Some French listeners find the portrayal of the sailors cruel; others hear it as compassionate. Critics generally agree that the live recording is inseparable from the song: this is a performance, not a composition.","Listen with the volume high enough to feel the crescendo, and track how many times the melody repeats before the explosion. Then hear Scott Walker's version, and Bowie's, and notice how much of Brel's fury each one keeps. The 1964 Olympia album is the only version by Brel himself."],"leo-ferre-avec-le-temps":["A slow, bare song for voice and strings about what time does to love: how it wears away faces, voices, the memory of a name, until even the passion that once mattered most becomes a dull indifference. There is no chorus in the usual sense, only a returning phrase, and the strings move in long sustained lines that barely support the voice. Ferré sings very quietly, almost speaking, then lets a few words break open.","Ferré wrote it in 1969, reportedly after the end of his long marriage to Madeleine Rabereau, and recorded it the following year for Barclay. He was then in his fifties, an anarchist poet-songwriter who had set Baudelaire, Verlaine and Rimbaud, and who was moving toward long orchestral pieces he often conducted himself. France was still in the aftershock of May 1968, which had made him a hero to the young.","Listen to what the strings do not do: they never swell into a climax, and the arrangement withholds the release that the melody seems to ask for. The song's desolation comes partly from that refusal. Ferré's diction is also exact; he lands on the consonants of each word as if carving them, a habit from his years reciting poetry.","It became his most famous song and a standard for other singers, from Dalida and Catherine Sauvage to Alain Bashung, as well as translated versions across Europe. Some find it self-pitying; most French critics now place it among the finest songs in the language. Its fame has partly overshadowed Ferré's more radical and political work, which he himself rated more highly.","Listen in a quiet room, late, and follow the strings, which hang like mist around the voice. Notice where Ferré raises his voice and how rarely he does it. Then hear Brel's Ne me quitte pas for the opposite response to lost love, begging rather than numbness."],"charles-aznavour-la-boheme":["An old painter looks back on his youth in Montmartre: a cold studio, hunger, a young woman who modelled for him, nights of work and talk, the feeling that they were poor but alive. The song alternates a lilting, almost waltzing verse with a broad refrain in which Aznavour's thin, reedy tenor swells with nostalgia. The last verse returns to the present, when he goes back to the old street and finds it changed beyond recognition.","Aznavour wrote the music and Jacques Plante the words for a 1965 operetta, Monsieur Carnaval, where another singer introduced it. Aznavour's own recording, released that year on Barclay, became one of his biggest hits. Montmartre by then was a tourist district; the bohemian painters' quarter the song remembers belonged to the early twentieth century, to Picasso and Modigliani, and had long since disappeared.","The title nods to Henri Murger's stories and to Puccini's opera La bohème, which told a similar tale of poor artists in Paris. But Aznavour turns that story into memory: the drama is not the love affair but the gap between then and now. Listen to how the final refrain is sung more slowly and heavily, the same words now weighted by loss.","It became Aznavour's signature, recorded in many languages and sung at concerts until his death in 2018. Critics sometimes found his early voice too frail and his performances too theatrical, but this song won even sceptics. It is now treated as the model of the nostalgia chanson, and a reminder that Aznavour was a craftsman of form as much as a romantic.","Follow the difference between the brisk verses and the slow, swelling refrain, and listen for how Aznavour breaks off at the final line. Then play Puccini's La bohème, or just its first act, to hear the operatic version of the same Paris. Aznavour's later live recordings are slower and more reflective."],"charles-aznavour-comme-ils-disent":["A song sung in the first person by a gay man who lives with his mother in a Paris flat, works as a drag performer in a nightclub, and loves a young man who will never love him back. Over a slow, sad orchestral arrangement, the verses describe his daily chores and nightly act with tenderness and no irony; the refrain turns on the word people use about him, and his insistence that nature alone made him as he is.","Aznavour recorded it in 1972, when homosexuality was still classified as a mental illness in France and a legal age of consent discriminated against gay men. He was a superstar, and his management reportedly feared the song would damage him. He sang it on stage anyway, often to silence and, at first, to hostility, and it became one of his best known songs in France.","Listen to how undramatic the music is: no ironic cabaret touches, no camp, just a sober melody and orchestra, which is exactly what made the song radical. Aznavour gives his narrator the same dignity he would give a lover in any other song. The title means 'as they say', and the whole song is built around that gap between what others say and what the man knows.","It was one of the first songs in mainstream French popular music to present a gay man sympathetically and in his own voice, and it is now regarded as a landmark. Some later listeners find the character too lonely and pitiable, a victim rather than a free person. Others argue that in 1972 compassion was itself a political act, and that its quiet realism has worn better than many louder anthems.","Read a translation first, then listen to how Aznavour shapes the quiet verses and lets the refrain rise. Compare it with his La Bohème, where the same voice sings nostalgia. Then play Bowie's Ziggy Stardust, from the same year, for a very different approach to gender on stage."],"barbara-nantes":["A song about arriving too late. Barbara tells how she received a message that her estranged father was dying in Nantes and travelled there through the rain, only to find he had died before she arrived. The piano moves in slow, steady steps like footsteps on wet stone; her voice, high, clear and fragile, stays almost level until the last verses, when it thins to a whisper. The rain in the song is also in the accompaniment.","Barbara's father, who had left the family years before, died in Nantes in December 1959; she reached the city after his death. She began the song then and finished it only in 1963. It appeared on her 1964 album Barbara chante Barbara, the record on which she stopped interpreting Brel and Brassens and emerged as a writer of her own songs, performing at the piano in black.","In her posthumously published memoirs, Barbara revealed that her father had abused her as a child. That knowledge changes the song: the grief is not simple mourning but something more tangled, a reconciliation that never happened. Listen for the restraint in her voice; nothing in the music tells you what it is withholding, and that is the point.","The song established Barbara as a songwriter of rare intimacy and is still among her most admired. Critics praised its understated craft from the start; after her memoirs appeared, discussion of it deepened. Some regard it as the single finest French song of the 1960s, a model for the confessional songwriting that followed across Europe.","Listen to the piano alone for a minute and hear the footsteps and the rain. Then notice how Barbara keeps her voice level through the story and lets it break only at the end. Compare Joni Mitchell's Blue for another woman at the piano turning private pain into song."],"barbara-l-aigle-noir":["A dream narrated in song. Barbara describes waking, or half-waking, to see a great black eagle descending from the sky, landing beside her, and speaking to her of a lost childhood before taking her up with it. The piano rolls in wide arpeggios and the orchestra gathers like weather, while her voice floats high and eerie. The melody rises as the bird rises, and the song ends suspended, as though still dreaming.","Barbara released it in 1970, and it became by far her biggest-selling record, taking her from Left Bank cabaret to a mass audience. She was then living outside Paris and performing at larger halls. She always said the song came from a dream, and she refused to explain what the eagle meant, which only deepened the public's fascination.","Listen to the piano figures, which circle like wingbeats and never settle. The song mixes the childhood imagery of fairy tales with something darker, and since her memoirs were published many listeners have read the eagle as an image of the father who abused her. Barbara herself kept silent on the question; the song lets both readings live side by side.","It was a huge popular success and one of the best known French songs of its decade. Some early critics found it too vague, a mystical fantasy. Later listeners heard its ambiguity as its strength, and it is now the song most identified with Barbara. Its interpretation remains debated, which is precisely what keeps it alive.","Listen first as if it were only a fairy tale, and enjoy the orchestration and the rising melody. On a second hearing, notice the details of childhood Barbara mentions and how the music darkens. Compare Kate Bush's Wuthering Heights, another high, eerie voice singing from a dream."],"jean-ferrat-nuit-et-brouillard":["A slow, grave song about the deportations of the Second World War: trains crossing Europe at night, the people crammed inside, their names, ages and faiths, the camps at the end of the line. Over a sombre, march-like orchestral arrangement, Ferrat's warm baritone stays steady and restrained, refusing any theatrical outburst. The melody is simple enough to be a folk song; the weight comes from the calm, level delivery.","Ferrat's father, a Jewish immigrant from Russia, was deported from France in 1942 and murdered at Auschwitz. Ferrat took his title from Alain Resnais's 1956 documentary on the camps, which in turn took it from the Nazi decree that made prisoners vanish into 'night and fog'. He recorded it in 1963, with arrangements by Alain Goraguer, at a moment when France and West Germany were signing a treaty of reconciliation.","Listen to what Ferrat does not say: his father is not named, and the song speaks for all the deported rather than for one family. The steadiness of the tempo, never slowing for effect, mirrors the trains themselves. Goraguer, who also arranged for Gainsbourg, keeps the orchestra dark and sparse, so that the voice carries the whole memorial.","French broadcasters were reportedly reluctant to air the song, feeling it reopened wounds at a delicate diplomatic moment, but Ferrat performed it on television and it became his breakthrough. It is now taught in French schools as a memorial text. Critics praise its restraint, and it is often cited as one of the few popular songs to treat the Holocaust with adequate seriousness.","Listen with a translation and let the steady pace carry you. Notice how the voice never rises to a shout. Then play Billie Holiday's Strange Fruit, another song of atrocity delivered without melodrama, and Steve Reich's Different Trains, which remembers the same trains from the other side of the Atlantic."],"jean-ferrat-la-montagne":["A broad, swinging song about the mountains of southern France and the young people who leave them. Ferrat describes villages emptying as the young go to the city for office jobs, cheap flats and processed food, while the old stay behind with their goats and vineyards. The orchestra gives it a sweeping, lyrical lift, and the refrain opens out like a view from a high ridge. His baritone is warm and conversational, not preachy.","Ferrat wrote it after settling in Antraigues-sur-Volane, a village in the Ardèche, where he would live for the rest of his life. France in the 1960s was urbanising fast, with the countryside losing people to Paris, Lyon and the new tower-block suburbs. The song, arranged by Alain Goraguer, was released in 1964 and became one of the biggest French hits of its decade.","Listen to the contrast between the orchestra's sweeping romantic gestures and the plain details of the words; Ferrat sets modern consumer goods, plastic and packaged meals, against the old life in a grand, almost symphonic setting. It is protest disguised as a pastoral postcard, and much of its bite is in the smallest nouns.","It became Ferrat's signature song and an anthem for rural France, still sung in villages today. Some urban critics found it nostalgic and conservative; admirers argue that it anticipated the ecological concerns of later decades. Its reputation rose further as rural depopulation continued, and it is now regarded as one of the great French songs of place.","Listen to the orchestral introduction and the way the refrain opens up; then follow a translation to catch the concrete details. Compare Paolo Conte's Genova per noi, which looks from the land toward the sea with similar unease, and Shirley Collins's Anthems in Eden for English rural loss."],"domenico-modugno-vecchio-frack":["A slow, dreamy song about an elegant old man in tails, top hat and gloves, walking alone through a sleeping city at dawn. Modugno describes the empty streets, the fading street lamps, the man's quiet farewell to the moon, and his final walk toward the river. The melody drifts over a soft, lilting accompaniment, almost a lullaby, and Modugno sings with a hushed, storytelling tenderness quite unlike the booming voice of his later hits.","Modugno wrote it in 1955, and it is often said to have been inspired by the death of the Sicilian aristocrat Raimondo Lanza di Trabia, a famous playboy who died in Rome in 1954, a death widely believed to be suicide. Modugno was then a young actor and singer from Puglia who had been writing songs in southern dialects and was just beginning to write in standard Italian.","The song never says what happens at the river; the ending is left to the listener, and the tune simply fades. Listen to how the melody imitates a slow walk, each phrase a few steps, and how the accompaniment drops away near the close. It is a small tragedy told entirely through gesture and costume, which is why it works like a scene from a film.","It was one of Modugno's first successes in standard Italian and established him as a serious songwriter, not just a folk entertainer. It has been covered by many Italian singers and is often called one of the first true canzoni d'autore, songs in which the singer-writer's personal poetry matters more than the performance. Its fame was soon eclipsed by Volare three years later.","Listen to it as a short film: the dawn, the streets, the man, the river. Notice how quietly Modugno sings compared to Nel blu dipinto di blu. Then watch or listen to Nino Rota's music for Fellini's La strada, from the same years, for another Italian melancholy of the lonely performer."],"domenico-modugno-nel-blu-dipinto-di-blu":["The song everyone knows as Volare. It opens with a slow, dreamy verse in which the singer imagines painting his hands and face blue and being lifted into the sky, then bursts into the famous refrain, which flies upward in a single joyful leap. Modugno's voice is big, open and theatrical, and the orchestra swings with a lightness closer to American big-band pop than to the traditional Italian ballad. The final refrain soars and fades.","Modugno wrote the music and Franco Migliacci the words, reportedly inspired by the floating figures in Marc Chagall's paintings. It won the Sanremo Festival in February 1958, when Modugno flung his arms wide on stage, and then represented Italy at the Eurovision Song Contest. Released in the United States, it went to number one there, and in 1959 it won the first Grammy Awards for record and song of the year.","Listen to the opening verse, which most people have never heard: slow, strange and quiet, a dream before the flight. The famous refrain only works because of that hush. And notice Modugno's gesture in the recording, a sudden shout and release; the arms-outstretched pose at Sanremo became part of the song, and a break with the stiff, formal delivery of Italian singers before him.","It was an international sensation, covered by Dean Martin, the Gipsy Kings, David Bowie and many others, and it is often called the song that dragged Italian pop out of its sentimental, operatic past into the modern world. Some Italian critics disliked its showbiz sheen; most now see it as the birth of modern Italian song and a turning point for Sanremo.","Start at the very beginning and don't skip the slow verse: the whole song is about the change from stillness to flight. Then listen to Modugno's own later live performances and to Dean Martin's American version for contrast. Trenet's La mer shows the older, gentler style Modugno was breaking from."],"mina-il-cielo-in-una-stanza":["A slow, intimate love song about a small room that opens into the whole sky when the beloved is there, the walls vanishing, the ceiling giving way to trees and stars. The orchestra is hushed, with sustained strings and a soft, tolling rhythm, and Mina sings with extraordinary control, holding her huge voice back to a near-whisper before letting it bloom. The melody climbs in long, gradual lines and never hurries.","Gino Paoli wrote it, one of the young Genoese songwriters who were remaking Italian pop at the turn of the 1960s, and Mina recorded it in 1960, when she was twenty and already a television star known for wild, rock-and-roll performances. The song showed a different singer: mature, controlled, capable of the utmost delicacy. It became a number one hit in Italy and the song most associated with her.","Paoli later said the room that inspired the song was in a brothel, a detail that surprises listeners who hear only a pure love song. Listen, too, to Mina's breath control on the long notes: she sustains and swells phrases like an opera singer, but never uses operatic vibrato, a blend of technique and restraint that became her trademark.","It was an immediate hit and has been voted among the finest Italian songs of the century. Critics credited it, along with Paoli's other songs, with inventing a new Italian intimacy distinct from both opera and Sanremo glitz. Some later listeners find the arrangement dated, but Mina's performance is still treated as a benchmark of Italian singing.","Listen for how quietly Mina starts and how gradually she opens her voice; note the moment she finally lets it ring. Then hear Paoli's own recording, thinner and more fragile, for contrast. Compare Françoise Hardy's Tous les garçons et les filles, two years later, for the French side of the same new intimacy."],"mina-se-telefonando":["A song about a love affair ending on the telephone: a single call, and a relationship that seemed immense is suddenly small and over. The music is dramatic, ascending and very modern for 1966: a three-note figure repeats obsessively in the orchestra, rising step by step, while Mina's voice climbs with it. Strings, brass and a wordless choir build a wall of sound, and every refrain lifts higher than the last.","Ennio Morricone composed and arranged it, with words by Maurizio Costanzo and Ghigo De Chiara, as the theme for a variety show on Italian state television in 1966. Morricone was then writing the scores for Sergio Leone's westerns, and brought the same bold, unexpected orchestral gestures to pop. Mina, by then the queen of Italian television, sang it on the programme and recorded it as a single.","Morricone said the obsessive three-note motif came to him from the siren of police cars in Marseille. Listen for how the motif keeps rising a step each time, a device borrowed from classical sequence technique, and for the harmonies that shift underneath in surprising directions. The song's structure is unusual too: it modulates repeatedly rather than returning to a stable refrain.","It was a hit and has since become a favourite of musicians and critics, regularly named among the best Italian pop recordings. Françoise Hardy recorded a French version, and many others have covered it. Morricone's arrangement is now studied as a model of how orchestral ideas can transform pop, and it anticipates the lush, dramatic pop of later decades.","Listen for the three-note siren motif and follow it as it rises; then listen to what the bass and harmony do beneath it. Compare Morricone's The Good, the Bad and the Ugly, from the same year, for the same composer's sense of drama. Dusty Springfield's big Italian-derived ballads show the English side of the style."],"lucio-battisti-emozioni":["A slow, meditative song that moves like a walk through the countryside. Over strummed acoustic guitar and soft strings, Battisti describes simple experiences: riding a bicycle, watching a sunset, standing by a river, and asks whether anyone else feels what he feels. The refrain lifts on a single word, the song's title. His voice is thin, husky and unpolished, and he sings with a directness closer to folk and soul than to Italian bel canto.","Mogol, the lyricist who wrote almost all the words for Battisti's songs in their great decade together, supplied the text, and it was released in 1970 as the title song of a compilation album. The two had just founded their own record label, Numero Uno, to escape the conservatism of the big Italian companies. Italy was in its 'hot autumn' of strikes and student protest; Battisti's songs looked inward instead.","Listen to how unforced the singing is: Battisti rarely raises his voice and often seems to speak the melody, which was radical in a country where singers were judged by power and range. The chords are borrowed from American and British pop, and the song's slow unfolding owes much to soul ballads. Mogol's lyric deliberately avoids grand statements and lets small details carry the feeling.","It became one of Battisti's most loved songs and a classic of Italian pop. Critics at the time were divided; some thought his voice too weak for a star. Later generations have credited him with modernising Italian song, and Emozioni is often chosen to show his gift for simple melody and understatement.","Listen to the acoustic guitar and let the song take its time. Notice how quietly Battisti sings and how the strings sneak in. Then play Nick Drake's Bryter Layter, from the same year, for another soft-voiced singer finding intensity in understatement, and Battisti's Anima latina for where he went next."],"lucio-battisti-anima-latina":["An ambitious, dreamlike album that blurs Italian song, progressive rock and Latin American rhythms into one long flow. Tracks merge into one another, percussion and flutes drift in and out, and Battisti's voice is often buried in the mix, one instrument among many. The songs deal with love, identity and the gap between people, but the sound matters as much as the words: layered, humid, uncertain, closer to a film score than a pop record.","Battisti made it with his lyricist Mogol after travelling in South America, and they released it on their own label, Numero Uno, in 1974. By then Battisti was Italy's biggest-selling star and had stopped giving interviews or concerts, preferring to control everything in the studio. Italian rock was at its most adventurous, with bands such as PFM reaching audiences abroad.","Listen for the low placing of the voice: Battisti deliberately mixed himself back so that the lyrics were hard to hear, forcing listeners into the texture. The Latin influences are less in the rhythms than in the layered percussion and the humid sound of the flutes. Mogol's lyrics are more oblique than ever, sometimes deliberately incomplete sentences.","It sold well on the strength of Battisti's name, but many fans and critics were baffled, finding it formless. Over time it has come to be seen as his masterwork and one of the great Italian albums of the 1970s, admired by musicians across genres. It is now often compared to the experimental records of Caetano Veloso and to British progressive rock.","Listen straight through, without skipping, and treat it as one long piece. Pay attention to the percussion and flutes rather than trying to follow the words. Then play Caetano Veloso's Araçá Azul for a Brazilian experiment of the same years, and Battiato's La voce del padrone for the next step in Italian art pop."],"fabrizio-de-andre-la-canzone-di-marinella":["A gentle, lilting ballad that tells a tragic story as if it were a fairy tale. A young woman named Marinella meets a mysterious, princely stranger, and after their meeting she slips into a river and drowns; the song turns her death into an ascent to the stars. The melody is soft and swaying, the accompaniment simple, and De André's deep, calm baritone narrates with a tenderness that keeps the horror at a distance.","De André wrote it early in his career, when he was an unknown Genoese songwriter recording for a small label. He said it was based on a newspaper story he had read as a teenager about a young woman who had been killed and thrown into a river, and that he wanted to give her a different, gentler ending. The song made little impact until Mina recorded it in 1967.","Listen to how the melody and arrangement echo old ballads and lullabies; the song is built like a folk tale, with repeated phrases and a slow, circular form. De André's great model was Brassens, and the song's calm, almost amused narration of a terrible event is a Brassens technique. The fairy-tale surface is a deliberate refusal to exploit the real crime.","Mina's cover turned it into a hit and gave De André enough money and fame to devote himself to songwriting. It is now one of the most loved Italian songs. Some critics call it sentimental; most admire its poise and its refusal to sensationalise. It is often cited as the first sign of De André's gift for turning outcasts and victims into figures of dignity.","Listen to De André's version first, then Mina's, and notice how differently they tell the story. Pay attention to the repeated phrases and the lullaby rhythm. Then play Brassens's La mauvaise réputation to hear the master De André learned from."],"fabrizio-de-andre-la-buona-novella":["A concept album retelling the life of Jesus through the apocryphal gospels, with the focus on the people around him: Mary's childhood in the Temple, her marriage to the old Joseph, her son's execution seen through the eyes of three grieving mothers and a thief crucified beside him. The songs are gentle, often folk-like, with acoustic guitars, soft strings and a chorus. De André's baritone is calm and sad.","De André recorded it in 1969 and released it in 1970, at the height of the Italian student protests, and he said he saw Jesus as the greatest revolutionary in history. The arrangements were by Gian Piero Reverberi, and the band included musicians who would soon form the progressive-rock group PFM. De André drew on the Protevangelium of James and other texts excluded from the official Bible.","Listen to the song for the three mothers at the cross: it is a popular-song version of the old Stabat Mater, the medieval poem of Mary at the cross, set by Pergolesi and countless others. And in the thief's song, De André goes through the Ten Commandments one by one, showing how each has been broken by those who claim to keep them.","Some Catholic critics were offended, some leftist critics thought a religious album a retreat from politics. Over time it has come to be seen as one of De André's finest works and among the most thoughtful Italian concept albums. It has been performed in churches and theatres, and scholars have written extensively on its sources.","Listen straight through with a translation to hand. Notice how gentle and folk-like the music is, even at the most dramatic moments. Then compare Pergolesi's Stabat Mater or Bach's St Matthew Passion, to hear the old sacred forms De André was answering."],"fabrizio-de-andre-creuza-de-ma":["An album sung entirely in Genoese dialect, with instruments from all around the Mediterranean: Greek bouzouki, Arab oud, Turkish saz, bagpipes, frame drums. The seven songs portray the life of the port of Genoa and the sea beyond it, sailors, fishmongers, prostitutes, a corsair, a war-torn Lebanese city. The sound is dense and ancient, full of drones and odd rhythms, and De André's deep voice rides over it with calm authority.","De André made it with the multi-instrumentalist Mauro Pagani, formerly of PFM, who had travelled around the Mediterranean gathering instruments and tunes. It was released by Ricordi in 1984. De André, now living largely in Sardinia, had turned away from Anglo-American influences toward his own region's language and the older cultures of the Mediterranean, a move almost no Italian pop artist had made before.","The Genoese dialect itself is part of the music: it is full of Arabic and Spanish words from centuries of trade, and De André chose it partly for that reason. Listen for how the rhythms often fall in irregular groupings from Balkan and Middle Eastern music. And the song about the Lebanese city of Sidon was written about the Israeli invasion of 1982, from the point of view of a father with a dying child.","It was a surprise success and was hailed by critics in Italy and abroad; David Byrne named it one of the most important records of the decade. It is now widely regarded as one of the greatest Italian albums and a pioneering work of what would come to be called world music. Some purists question its mixing of traditions, but its influence is undeniable.","Listen to the whole album in one sitting, loudly, and don't worry about the words at first. Focus on the instruments and rhythms. Then read translations. Compare Amália Rodrigues's Barco Negro, or Cesária Évora, for other port cities singing their own languages to the sea."],"paolo-conte-genova-per-noi":["A slow, wry song about people from the inland hills going down to the port city of Genoa: the sea is enormous and frightening, the city dark and unreadable, and they feel like peasants in their Sunday best. Over a lazy, swaying piano and brushes, Conte's hoarse voice half-speaks the words in a tone both amused and melancholy. The melody is simple, almost a shrug, and the mood is of bewildered longing.","Conte, a lawyer from Asti in Piedmont who had been writing hits for other singers, recorded it on his second album in 1975; the Genoese singer Bruno Lauzi recorded it the same year. Conte was only beginning his career as a performer, shy and gravel-voiced, and his songs drew on jazz, tango and the small-town life of the Piedmontese countryside.","Genoa was not just any city for Italian songwriters: it was the home of Paoli, De André, Tenco and Lauzi, the great 'Genoese school' of the 1960s. Conte's song is partly an outsider's tribute to that tradition. Listen to the piano, which is closer to jazz than to pop, and to the rueful pauses Conte leaves between phrases.","It was admired by fellow musicians more than by the public at first, and Conte's fame came slowly. Today it is one of his most loved songs and is taken as a portrait of the Italian provincial's awe before the sea. Critics praise its wit and its precise, understated observations, typical of his art.","Listen with a translation and enjoy the small details. Notice the piano's jazz inflections and the way Conte sings behind the beat. Then play Via con me for the more swinging side of Conte, and Ferrat's La Montagne for a French song of the countryside and the city."],"paolo-conte-via-con-me":["A jaunty, swinging song in which Conte, gravel-voiced and deadpan, invites someone to come away with him, promising nothing much except a good time. The band plays in a 1930s swing style, with bouncing piano, clarinet and muted brass, and Conte punctuates the verses with scat syllables and a kazoo-like mumble. The refrain, partly in English, is catchy and insistent, and the whole thing is over in three minutes.","Conte released it in 1981 on his album Paris milonga. He was then in his forties, a lawyer from Asti who had come late to singing, but his stage career was taking off, especially in France, where audiences loved his mix of jazz, tango and Italian irony. He had loved American swing and jazz since his youth, and the song is a tribute to that music.","Listen to the English phrase in the refrain, sung with a deliberately stiff Italian accent: Conte is playing with the image of the provincial Italian enchanted by American culture. The scat and kazoo effects are not throwaway gags but part of a carefully built pastiche of 1930s swing records. The arrangement is densely crafted beneath its casual surface.","It became Conte's most famous song and has been used in many films and adverts around the world. Some critics found it lightweight compared to his more melancholy songs; most see it as a perfect example of his art of pastiche and irony. It made him an international figure and a cult artist in France and beyond.","Listen for the swing rhythm and the clarinet, and enjoy Conte's deadpan delivery and scat. Then play Django Reinhardt's Minor Swing or Ellington's Mood Indigo to hear the jazz Conte was echoing. Tom Waits's Swordfishtrombones, a few years later, offers another gravel-voiced song-and-dance man."],"franco-battiato-la-voce-del-padrone":["A short, bright, dazzling album of seven songs mixing new-wave synthesizers, rock guitars, classical violin and strange, collage-like lyrics that jump from Sufi mysticism to TV commercials to cheap tourist beaches. The hits, Centro di gravità permanente, Bandiera bianca and Cuccurucucù, are catchy and danceable, but full of odd harmonic turns and cut-up quotations. Battiato's voice is light, nasal and detached, like a narrator observing the modern world from above.","Battiato, a Sicilian who had spent the 1970s making avant-garde electronic records, turned to pop at the end of the decade, and this album, released by EMI in 1981, became the first Italian LP to sell a million copies. His chief collaborator was the violinist Giusto Pio, who co-wrote and arranged. Italy was emerging from the 'years of lead' of terrorism into a brash new consumer culture.","The title means 'His Master's Voice', the famous record-company logo, a sly joke on the album's commercial success and on conformity. The lyrics are cut-up collages: lists of names, languages, songs and ideas in which Gurdjieff's mysticism jostles with pop trivia. Listen for the classical violin lines and for the abrupt changes of rhythm, legacies of Battiato's experimental years.","It was a huge popular success and baffled critics who knew Battiato as an experimentalist. Some accused him of selling out; others hailed it as intelligent pop. Today it is regarded as a classic of Italian art pop and a model of how to combine mass appeal with intellectual ambition. Its hits are still played on Italian radio.","Play it loud and enjoy the hooks first. Then listen again with a translation to follow the collage of references. Notice the violin, synthesizers and odd harmonies. Compare Kraftwerk's Computer World and Talking Heads' Remain in Light, from the same years, for other brainy dance-pop records."],"franco-battiato-la-cura":["A slow, luminous love song in which the singer promises to protect and care for someone: to keep away fear and illness, to travel with them through time and space, to cure them. The melody is simple and rising, and the arrangement grows from soft keyboards to full strings. Battiato's voice is gentle and earnest, stripped of the irony of his 1980s records, and the song ends in a broad, calm swell of strings.","Battiato wrote it with the Sicilian philosopher Manlio Sgalambro, his lyricist from the mid-1990s onward, and released it in 1996 on the album L'imboscata. Battiato was then in his fifties, living near Etna in Sicily, and moving away from pop toward opera, film and meditation. Sgalambro, a pessimist philosopher, supplied a text that could be read either as a love song or as a spiritual vow.","Many listeners hear the song as addressed not to a lover but to the soul, or to a disciple by a teacher; Battiato practised meditation and was interested in Sufism and Eastern thought. Listen to how the strings enter gradually and never overwhelm the voice, and how the melody climbs by small steps like a prayer rather than a pop hook.","It became Battiato's most loved song, frequently voted the greatest Italian love song, and is often performed at weddings and funerals. Critics admired its sincerity after years of ironic pop. Some find it too sentimental, but its fame has only grown since Battiato's death in 2021, when it was sung across Italy in tribute.","Listen in a quiet space and let it unfold. Notice how the strings gradually take over. Then compare Ferré's Avec le temps for another meditation on love and time, and Battiato's La voce del padrone to hear how far he had travelled from the ironic, jumpy pop of fifteen years before."],"adriano-celentano-azzurro":["A bouncy, catchy summer song about being stuck alone in the city while the woman he loves is away on holiday. The verses are playful, with odd images of trains, baobabs and Sunday afternoons; the refrain opens into a sweeping melody that evokes the blue summer sky. Celentano sings with his characteristic loose, rhythmic, almost mocking style, and the arrangement combines strings, brass and a bright pop rhythm section.","Paolo Conte wrote the music, with Michele Virano, and Vito Pallavicini the words; Celentano released it on his own label, Clan Celentano, in 1968. Celentano was then Italy's biggest pop star, a former rock-and-roller turned all-round entertainer. It was a summer hit amid the student protests of 1968, a very different world from the song's lazy afternoons.","Conte's melody is more sophisticated than it seems, with jazz-inflected harmonies and a refrain that shifts unexpectedly. Listen to how the verses and the refrain almost belong to different songs, one playful, one soaring. Conte later recorded his own, much slower and more melancholy version, which reveals the sadness beneath the cheerful surface.","It was a huge hit and has become one of the best-known Italian songs, sung by Italian football fans, especially during the 2006 World Cup. Critics have often dismissed it as light pop, but musicians admire Conte's craft. It is now a national anthem of sorts, and its dual life, in Celentano's and Conte's versions, is itself a lesson in interpretation.","Listen to Celentano's version first, for the bounce and swagger, then to Conte's slower one. Notice how the refrain changes character. Then play Modugno's Nel blu dipinto di blu for another Italian song whose refrain flies into the sky, and Conte's Genova per noi for the songwriter's own voice."],"adriano-celentano-prisencolinensinainciusol":["A funky, hypnotic track built on a relentless groove of bass and drums, over which Celentano delivers a torrent of words that sound like American English but mean nothing at all. His delivery is rhythmic, half-spoken and half-chanted, much closer to rap than to song, while a female chorus answers with a repeated hook. The track never changes key or tempo; its power is in the repetition and the rhythm of the syllables.","Celentano released it in 1972 on his own label. He said he wanted to show how English sounds to Italians who don't speak it, and to make a point about how people fail to communicate. It was initially a flop in Italy but became a hit elsewhere in Europe, especially in Belgium and France, and then back home, helped by comic TV performances in which Celentano acted out the song with a partner.","The gibberish is carefully constructed: Celentano used real English words and fragments mixed with invented ones, sung with American intonation and slang rhythms. Listen for how the vocal line functions as percussion, a technique that would later define hip-hop. The title itself is a nonsense word in the same imaginary language.","For decades it was a novelty song, but in the 2000s it was rediscovered online and hailed as a precursor of rap. Critics and musicians now take it seriously as an experiment in sound and language. Some still see it as a joke; others argue that the joke is precisely the point, a satire of the global dominance of English-language pop.","Listen to the groove first, then to Celentano's vocal rhythm, as if it were a drum. Notice how convincing the nonsense English sounds. Then play Grandmaster Flash's Superrappin', from a few years later, to hear rap in its early form, and Talking Heads' Remain in Light for another experiment in rhythm and voice."],"depeche-mode-just-cant-get-enough":["A bright, bouncing three-and-a-half-minute dance song built almost entirely from cheap synthesizers. A chirping, interlocking keyboard riff opens it and never really stops; a second synth line answers it, a drum machine ticks underneath, and Dave Gahan sings a simple, breathless song about a crush so strong it will not let go. There is no guitar anywhere. The hook is the riff, not the voice, and it repeats until it becomes almost hypnotic.","Depeche Mode were four young men from Basildon, an Essex new town, signed to Daniel Miller's tiny independent label Mute in London. This was their third single, from the debut album Speak & Spell, and it was written by Vince Clarke, the band's first songwriter. Portable synthesizers had just become affordable enough for a working-class band to own, and the Human League, OMD and Soft Cell were already proving that a pop group could manage without guitars.","Almost as soon as this song made Depeche Mode famous, its writer left: Vince Clarke quit at the end of 1981, uneasy with touring and celebrity, and went on to form Yazoo and later Erasure. Martin Gore, who had written only a couple of the band's songs, took over. So this cheerful record is really a Vince Clarke song, and the gulf between it and what Gore wrote next is the story of the whole band.","It was a UK hit and it fixed the early image of Depeche Mode as a lightweight teen-pop act, a reputation they spent the rest of the decade escaping. For years the band treated it with mild embarrassment. Later listeners heard it more kindly, as a perfectly engineered piece of electronic pop, and it became a football-terrace chant in Britain and a staple of every eighties revival.","Listen to how many separate synthesizer parts there are, each doing one simple thing, and how they lock together like clockwork. Then play Yazoo's Only You to hear where Clarke went next, and Depeche Mode's Everything Counts, written by Gore only two years later, to hear how quickly the band grew darker and heavier."],"depeche-mode-everything-counts":["A clanking, metallic synth-pop single about money. Its rhythm is built from sampled sounds that ring like hammered pipes and struck metal, and its best-known melody is played on a nasal, reedy sampled instrument that sounds like a folk shawm or oboe. Martin Gore and Dave Gahan trade the verses, and the chorus, a wordless-seeming sing-along, sits over a bitter song about greed in business, the handshake that hides the grabbing hand.","This was the first big single after Vince Clarke's departure, written by Martin Gore for the album Construction Time Again, much of which was mixed at Hansa studios in West Berlin with the engineer Gareth Jones. Alan Wilder had joined as a musician. The band had discovered samplers, expensive new machines that could record any real-world sound and play it back from a keyboard, and they went out into building sites and scrapyards looking for noises.","The industrial sounds are not decoration; they are the argument. A song about the machinery of commerce is built from recordings of actual machinery, struck, scraped and looped into drum patterns, an idea the band took partly from German industrial groups such as Einstürzende Neubauten. The pretty tune on top and the hard metal underneath are the same contrast the words describe: a friendly surface over a cold transaction.","Reviewers noticed the leap at once: the teen-pop band of Just Can't Get Enough had become politically pointed and sonically adventurous in two years. A live version recorded at the Pasadena Rose Bowl in 1988 became a second hit and the climax of the concert film 101. It is now heard as the hinge of Depeche Mode's career, where Gore's voice as a writer first came through fully.","Listen first to the percussion alone and try to name the objects being struck. Then compare the studio single with the 101 live version, where tens of thousands of people sing the chorus back. Kraftwerk's Computer World, from two years earlier, is the obvious ancestor; New Order's Blue Monday, from the same year, is a sibling."],"depeche-mode-violator":["Nine songs and a few short instrumental links, about forty-seven minutes of dark, spacious, immaculately produced electronic music. The sound is sparse: deep synthesizer bass, programmed drums with the weight of a rock band, and occasional blues-inflected guitar. It includes Personal Jesus, built on a stomping, swamp-blues guitar riff, and Enjoy the Silence, with its melancholy synth pulse and chiming guitar. Dave Gahan's baritone and Martin Gore's high harmonies give the record its unmistakable double voice.","By the end of the eighties Depeche Mode had become a stadium act in the United States and across Europe, a status confirmed by their 1988 Rose Bowl concert. For this album they worked with the producer Flood, recording in Milan and Denmark and finishing in London, and deliberately stripped back the busy sampling of their mid-eighties records. When the band held a record-shop signing in Los Angeles in March 1990, the crowd was so large that police had to intervene.","Enjoy the Silence began as a slow, bare ballad on Martin Gore's demo, sung over a harmonium-like organ. It was Alan Wilder and Flood who heard it as a dance track and rebuilt it around a four-on-the-floor beat; Gore reportedly disliked the change at first. The guitar line that most listeners hum was added late. The record's cleanest moments are the result of arguments about what to leave out.","It was the band's commercial peak and their critical turning point: reviewers who had dismissed synth-pop as disposable began treating Depeche Mode as serious songwriters. Personal Jesus was later covered by Johnny Cash and Marilyn Manson, which says something about its range. Violator is now commonly named among the essential albums of electronic pop and as a bridge between eighties synth-pop and nineties industrial and alternative rock.","Start with Enjoy the Silence, then play Personal Jesus to hear how a blues riff survives inside a machine. Listen on good headphones: much of the drama lies in the space between sounds and in the low end. The instrumental codas between songs are worth hearing rather than skipping. Afterwards try New Order's Technique and Radiohead's Kid A, both electronic records made by bands."],"the-human-league-being-boiled":["A slow, heavy, sinister electronic record. A fat, sliding synthesizer bass line lurches forward with the swagger of a funk groove, a drum pattern made on the synthesizer thuds underneath, and bursts of harsh noise cut across it. Phil Oakey's deep, flat voice delivers a strange lecture about silkworms and the cruelty of boiling them for silk, wrapped in half-understood references to Buddhism. It sounds nothing like the polished pop the same group made three years later.","The Human League began in Sheffield, a steel city hit hard by the decline of heavy industry, where Martyn Ware and Ian Craig Marsh had been making electronic music with cheap synthesizers. They recruited Oakey, a hospital porter with a striking look, as singer. This debut single came out on Fast Product, a small independent label in Edinburgh run by Bob Last, in the thick of punk's do-it-yourself moment. Their heroes were Kraftwerk, Giorgio Moroder and funk records.","The groove owes more to black American funk and Philadelphia soul than to German electronics: Ware and Marsh loved dance music, and the bass line is essentially a funk bass played on a synthesizer. That hybrid, European machines playing American dance music, is exactly what Sheffield would export for the next decade. The track was re-recorded more than once, and the versions differ noticeably in tempo and weight.","It sold modestly, but other musicians heard it: David Bowie reportedly told people he had seen the future of pop after seeing the group live. Critics filed it with the industrial and post-punk underground rather than with pop. It has since become a touchstone for electronic musicians and a reminder that the Human League started as an avant-garde band, closer to Cabaret Voltaire than to Duran Duran.","Listen to the bass line and try moving to it; it is a dance record, however cold it sounds. Then jump straight to Don't You Want Me on the album Dare, three years later, and try to believe it is the same singer. Kraftwerk's Trans-Europe Express and Donna Summer's I Feel Love are the two records it sits between."],"the-human-league-dare":["Ten songs, about forty minutes, of hard-edged, precise electronic pop. Every sound comes from synthesizers and a drum machine, each part cleanly separated and punchy. Phil Oakey's baritone leads, and two teenage women, Joanne Catherall and Susan Ann Sulley, sing unpolished backing and answer lines. The singles are The Sound of the Crowd, Love Action, Open Your Heart and Don't You Want Me, a duet in which an ex-lover and the woman he made a star tell opposite versions of the same story.","In 1980 the original Human League split: Martyn Ware and Ian Craig Marsh left to form Heaven 17, leaving Oakey and the visual director Adrian Wright with the name and a tour booked. Oakey recruited Catherall and Sulley after seeing them dancing in a Sheffield nightclub, and later added Ian Burden and the guitarist Jo Callis, who played synthesizer here. The producer Martin Rushent, working for Virgin, programmed the album on new digital drum machines and synthesizer sequencers.","Oakey reportedly thought Don't You Want Me was the weakest song on the album and resisted its release as a single; it became the UK Christmas number one in 1981 and a US number one the following year. The amateur quality of the two women's voices is deliberate and crucial, making the gleaming machines sound human. A remixed instrumental version, Love and Dancing, credited to the League Unlimited Orchestra, followed in 1982.","Dare was a huge seller in Britain and broke synth-pop in America. Some early fans mourned the experimental band of Being Boiled, but critics quickly accepted it as a model of what pure electronic pop could be. Its production, Rushent's crisp, programmed precision, became a template for the decade, and it regularly appears on lists of the best British albums of the eighties.","Start with Don't You Want Me and listen to the way the two voices contradict each other. Then play Love Action and notice that the drums never vary; the interest comes from the layering. Go back to Being Boiled for the before, and forward to the Pet Shop Boys and Robyn's Dancing On My Own for the long after."],"pet-shop-boys-west-end-girls":["A slow, brooding dance song, nearly five minutes long, in which Neil Tennant half-raps and half-speaks the verses in a cool, very English voice before sliding into a sung chorus. Underneath, a deep synthesizer bass rolls, programmed drums keep a mid-tempo groove, and sirens and street noise drift in and out. A muted, jazzy trumpet line and a choir-like synth give it a nocturnal atmosphere. It sketches London at night: class, ambition, crowded streets and a sense of being trapped.","Neil Tennant was a journalist at the pop magazine Smash Hits; Chris Lowe was an architecture student. They met in a London hi-fi shop in 1981. A first version of this song, produced in New York by Bobby Orlando in 1984, was a club hit in a few places but flopped in Britain. After signing to Parlophone they re-recorded it with the producer Stephen Hague, and the new version reached number one in Britain and then in the United States.","Tennant has said the rapped verses were inspired by Grandmaster Flash's The Message, the New York hip-hop record about city pressure, and that the fragmented, many-voiced words owe something to T. S. Eliot's The Waste Land. There is also a sly historical reference to Lenin's rail journey to the Finland Station in 1917, slipped into a song about nightlife. The song is a collage, like Eliot's poem.","It was one of the defining British hits of the mid-eighties and launched what became the most successful duo in UK pop history. Critics admired the novelty of an English rap and the combination of club music with literary irony. Its reputation has only grown, and it is now routinely named among the finest British pop singles of the decade, admired as much by songwriters as by dance-floor audiences.","Listen for the moment Tennant switches from speaking to singing, and for the sirens and street sounds in the background. Then play Grandmaster Flash's The Message to hear one of its models. The album Actually shows where the duo went next; the Human League's Dare shows the Sheffield electronic pop they grew from."],"pet-shop-boys-actually":["Ten songs of grand, glossy, ironic electronic pop. The sound is huge: orchestral stabs, choirs, thunderclaps and church bells built from samples, over dance rhythms, with Neil Tennant's light, deadpan voice floating above. The hits are It's a Sin, a melodramatic confession of Catholic guilt; What Have I Done to Deserve This?, a duet with Dusty Springfield; Rent, a cool song about a love that is also a transaction; and Heart. Other tracks, like Shopping and King's Cross, look coldly at Thatcher's Britain.","Actually was the Pet Shop Boys' second album, made at the height of their fame with several producers, among them Stephen Hague and Julian Mendelsohn. Britain in 1987 was in its third term of Margaret Thatcher's government: a City boom, privatisation, sharpening division and the arrival of AIDS, which hung heavily over gay life. Tennant was not yet publicly out, but the album's songs of guilt, secrecy and money are now read in that light.","The duet with Dusty Springfield was the start of her comeback; the duo had admired her sixties records and gave her a hit two decades after her peak. King's Cross, the closing track, describes the station as a place of the lost and the desperate; months after the album appeared, a fire at King's Cross Underground station killed thirty-one people, and the song took on a painful second meaning.","It was a major commercial success in Britain and Europe and critics praised its wit and ambition, though some found the irony chilly. Over time Actually has been reassessed as one of the sharpest political pop records of the decade, and It's a Sin was renewed in 2021 when Russell T Davies named his television drama about the AIDS years after it.","Begin with It's a Sin at loud volume, then the Dusty Springfield duet. After that listen to Shopping and King's Cross carefully, attending to what Tennant is describing rather than to the gloss. Dusty in Memphis is the natural companion; Depeche Mode's Violator is the darker cousin from the same scene."],"eurythmics-sweet-dreams":["A cold, circling, minor-key electronic single. A pulsing synthesizer bass riff, bouncing between low and high notes, loops under a simple drum-machine beat, and Annie Lennox's deep, commanding contralto delivers a short, fatalistic meditation on how everyone is searching for something and how people use and are used by one another. Strings made on a synthesizer swell in the middle. There is almost no chorus in the usual sense; the riff itself is the hook.","Lennox and Dave Stewart had been in the pop group the Tourists, and their romantic relationship had just ended. Broke and depressed, they set up a small home studio above a picture-framing workshop in north London and recorded on cheap equipment. Stewart found the bass riff by accident while experimenting with a drum machine and a synthesizer; Lennox, reportedly lying on the floor in misery, got up and started singing over it. The song was released by RCA in early 1983.","The song is often heard as a celebration, but what it describes is coldly bleak: a world divided into people who exploit and people who are exploited. Lennox has said it reflected her depression at the time. The famous video, with Lennox in a man's suit and cropped orange hair in a boardroom and, oddly, a field with a cow, deliberately played with gender and power.","It reached number one in the United States and near the top in Britain, and the video made Lennox one of the defining images of early MTV. Critics praised its stark minimalism. It has been sampled and covered constantly, from Marilyn Manson's gothic version onward, and remains one of the most recognisable synthesizer riffs ever recorded.","Listen to how little is actually going on: one bass riff, one beat, one voice, some synthesizer strings. Notice how Lennox sings with authority and restraint rather than belting. Then play Donna Summer's I Feel Love, another song built on a single hypnotic bass sequence, and Madonna's Vogue, which borrows its poise and its cool."],"soft-cell-tainted-love":["A minimal, stark electronic version of a sixties soul song. A cold drum-machine beat with hard handclaps, a throbbing synthesizer bass, and a two-note electronic sting, a bink-bink that became famous in its own right, frame Marc Almond's theatrical, slightly wavering voice. The song describes a love that has turned poisonous and a lover who needs to escape. The twelve-inch version runs on into a cover of the Supremes' Where Did Our Love Go.","Soft Cell were Marc Almond and Dave Ball, who met as art students at Leeds Polytechnic. Tainted Love was originally recorded in 1964 by the American singer Gloria Jones, written by Ed Cobb; it had become a favourite on the Northern soul dance scene in England, where rare American soul records were played in all-night clubs. Soft Cell recorded it with the producer Mike Thorne for Stevo's label Some Bizzare, and it reached number one in Britain in 1981.","The choice of song was itself a statement: the duo had heard it in Northern soul clubs, and the record is a Northern soul classic translated into synthesizer pop. Almond's vulnerable, camp delivery also gave a song about a damaging love a new queer subtext in a period when openly gay pop was still risky. The two-note sting was reportedly created on a Synclavier synthesizer brought in by Thorne.","It was a global success and for years held a record for the most consecutive weeks spent on the American singles chart. Some critics dismissed Soft Cell as a novelty act, but the song's reputation has only grown. It has been sampled by Rihanna in SOS and covered by Marilyn Manson, and is now heard as one of the founding records of electronic pop.","Hear Gloria Jones's 1964 original first; it is faster and punchier, with horns. Then play Soft Cell's version and notice what the machines strip away and what Almond's voice adds. Listen to the long twelve-inch mix to hear the segue into Where Did Our Love Go, and follow up with the full album Non-Stop Erotic Cabaret."],"soft-cell-non-stop-erotic-cabaret":["Ten songs, about forty minutes, of seedy, witty, heartbroken electronic pop. Dave Ball's synthesizers are brittle and busy; Marc Almond sings like a torch singer lost in a nightclub. The songs are a tour of the underside of the city: Bedsitter, about a young man's lonely weekend nightlife; Seedy Films, about the porn cinemas of Soho; Frustration, about suburban boredom; and Say Hello, Wave Goodbye, a devastating farewell in a nightclub, with a smoky saxophone.","The album was recorded mostly in New York with the producer Mike Thorne, where Almond and Ball absorbed the club culture and the city's nightlife. It appeared at the end of 1981, just after Tainted Love had made them unexpectedly famous. The early eighties in Britain were a time of recession and high unemployment, and also of flamboyant club scenes where outsiders reinvented themselves; this record belongs to that world.","Almond has spoken of the influence of Jacques Brel, Scott Walker and the cabaret tradition, and the album's title and its characters recall Weimar Berlin as much as eighties London. Behind the synthesizer pop, the songs are really small theatre pieces about lonely people. The track Sex Dwarf was so provocative that its video, shot by Tim Pope, was never officially shown; it is mentioned here only in passing.","It sold strongly and was praised for its wit, though some critics found it too camp or trashy. Its reputation has risen steadily, and it is now considered a key album of British synth-pop and of queer pop, admired especially for Say Hello, Wave Goodbye and Bedsitter. Almond's later career as a torch singer confirmed that the cabaret was never a pose.","Play Say Hello, Wave Goodbye first, then Bedsitter and Seedy Films. Listen for how Almond's dramatic voice rides over the chilly machines. After that hear Scott Walker's Scott 4 and Brecht and Weill's Threepenny Opera, which Almond clearly loved, and the Pet Shop Boys' Actually, where the same city appears a few years later."],"omd-enola-gay":["A fast, cheerful-sounding synth-pop song about the atomic bombing of Hiroshima. A brisk, programmed drum pattern and a bright, bouncing bass line run under a lead melody played on a thin, reedy synthesizer, simple and singable. Andy McCluskey sings about the aircraft and its mission, the timing of the bomb and the question of whether it could ever be undone, but he does it at a skipping tempo that makes the song sound almost joyful.","Orchestral Manoeuvres in the Dark were Andy McCluskey and Paul Humphreys, friends from the Wirral near Liverpool who had been making electronic music since their teens. Enola Gay, released in 1980 on Dindisc, a Virgin subsidiary, was a single from their second album Organisation. It appeared at a time of heightened Cold War fear, with new missiles being deployed in Europe and the anti-nuclear movement growing in Britain.","The Enola Gay was the B-29 bomber that dropped the atomic bomb on Hiroshima in August 1945; it was named after the pilot Paul Tibbets's mother. The Hiroshima bomb itself was called Little Boy, and McCluskey's song glances at both names. The mismatch between the upbeat music and the grim subject was deliberate, and many listeners who danced to it had no idea what it was about.","It was a major hit in Britain and Europe. At the time some misread the title and objected to the song as crude, while others missed its meaning entirely. It has since become the best-loved OMD song and a staple of eighties revival radio, admired for its combination of a catchy hook with an unusual and serious subject.","Enjoy it first as a dance song, then listen again paying attention to the words. Notice the lead synthesizer line, which is really the chorus. Then try Architecture and Morality to hear OMD at their most ambitious, and Kraftwerk's Radio-Activity, another electronic record about nuclear power in a deceptively gentle voice."],"omd-architecture-and-morality":["Nine songs, a little under forty minutes, of lush, melancholy, almost choral electronic pop. The distinctive sound is the Mellotron, a tape-replay keyboard that plays recorded choirs and strings, used here to give the songs a cathedral-like glow. The three singles are Souvenir, sung by Paul Humphreys; Joan of Arc; and Maid of Orleans, a slow, swaying waltz with a folk-like melody. Around them are more abstract pieces with found sounds and industrial noise.","It was OMD's third album, made in 1981 at the peak of their early success and released on Dindisc. The band had been reading about the medieval saint Joan of Arc and listening to sacred choral music, and they wanted to make something grander and more emotional than their earlier records. They had also been influenced by Kraftwerk and by experimental electronic music, and the album mixes those strands.","The Joan of Arc songs grew from a visit McCluskey made to a French cathedral and his fascination with her story; that a song about a medieval martyr could become a hit single in the era of Duran Duran says much about how open pop briefly was. The whispering choir on Souvenir and elsewhere is the sound of old Mellotron tapes, a technology from the sixties repurposed for the eighties.","It sold several million copies across Europe, though the British music press was divided, some finding it pretentious. OMD's next, even more experimental album, Dazzle Ships, flopped. Today Architecture and Morality is widely considered their finest record and a high point of British electronic pop, and it was performed in full on reunion tours.","Start with Maid of Orleans and listen for the waltz time, unusual in pop, and the choir-like Mellotron. Then play Souvenir and Joan of Arc. Afterwards try Kraftwerk's Trans-Europe Express and Arvo Pärt's Spiegel im Spiegel for two very different kinds of slow, luminous, electronic-sounding stillness."],"tears-for-fears-mad-world":["A mid-tempo, melancholy synth-pop song. A bouncing, busy drum pattern and a funky synthesizer bass line give it a surprisingly danceable body, while Curt Smith sings in a soft, sad voice about watching the empty routine of the world and dreaming of dying. The chorus, in which the singer finds the world strange, is almost resigned. The song sounds both chilly and warm, with shimmering synth pads and a rich vocal harmony.","Tears for Fears were Roland Orzabal and Curt Smith, teenage friends from Bath. The band's name came from Arthur Janov's book on primal therapy, which Orzabal read as a way of understanding his troubled childhood. Mad World, written by Orzabal and sung by Smith, was their third single and their first hit, in 1982, before appearing on the debut album The Hurting in 1983, a whole record about childhood pain and psychology.","The song's gloomy thoughts are balanced by a danceable arrangement, a tension that runs through Tears for Fears. Two decades later, a stripped-down piano version by Michael Andrews and Gary Jules for the film Donnie Darko revealed how desolate the melody is without its beat, and in 2003 that cover became the UK Christmas number one, climbing higher than the original ever had.","It was a hit in Britain, and critics grouped the band with the new wave of synth-pop acts. Over time the song has come to be seen as one of the defining statements of teenage alienation in the eighties, and the Andrews and Jules cover made it familiar to a whole new generation who never knew the original.","Listen to the original first and notice how bouncy the drums are compared with the sad melody. Then play the Donnie Darko version and hear the song naked. Go on to the whole album The Hurting for Orzabal's psychology, and then Songs from the Big Chair to hear how much bigger the band grew in three years."],"tears-for-fears-songs-from-the-big-chair":["Eight songs, about forty minutes, of big, polished, emotionally searching pop. The synthesizers of the first album are now joined by guitars, piano, saxophone and heavy, echoing drums. Shout is a slow-building protest chant; Everybody Wants to Rule the World glides on a shuffling rhythm and a chiming guitar; Head Over Heels is a grand romantic song. The second half turns darker and more experimental, with The Working Hour, Mothers Talk and the closing Listen.","Made with the producer Chris Hughes and keyboard player Ian Stanley, the album came out in early 1985, when the Cold War, nuclear fear and Thatcher and Reagan's politics coloured British and American pop. The title refers to Sybil, a 1976 television film about a woman with multiple personalities who finds safety in her therapist's big chair; Orzabal's lyrics continue the band's interest in psychology and emotional pain.","Everybody Wants to Rule the World was one of the last songs recorded and was almost left off; Orzabal thought it was too slight. Its unusual rolling shuffle rhythm, rather than a straight rock beat, gives it its gliding quality. Shout, often misheard as a party anthem, is about the need to let out anger and grievances, a lesson from primal therapy turned into a stadium chant.","The album was a huge international success, with Shout and Everybody Wants to Rule the World both reaching number one in America. Critics admired its ambition and craft, though some found it grandiose. It is now regarded as one of the defining British albums of the mid-eighties and a model of sophisticated, emotionally serious pop.","Play Everybody Wants to Rule the World, then Shout, then Head Over Heels, and listen for how carefully each is built up. Then play the album's quieter second half straight through. Compare it with Mad World to hear the band's growth, and with Talk Talk's The Colour of Spring era for another eighties band moving beyond synth-pop."],"duran-duran-rio":["Nine songs, about forty-three minutes, of glossy, danceable new pop. The sound is a band, not just machines: John Taylor's busy, funky bass lines, Roger Taylor's disco drums, Andy Taylor's rock guitar and Nick Rhodes's shimmering synthesizers, with Simon Le Bon's yearning vocals. The singles are the bright title song, with its saxophone solo; Hungry Like the Wolf, with its prowling rhythm; and the ballad Save a Prayer. The album ends with the eerie, atmospheric The Chauffeur.","Duran Duran formed in Birmingham and were house band of sorts at the Rum Runner club, a centre of the New Romantic scene, where fans dressed in extravagant costume. Rio was their second album, produced by Colin Thurston for EMI. Its success in the United States came from the new cable channel MTV, which played their lavish videos, directed by Russell Mulcahy and filmed on location in Sri Lanka and Antigua, again and again.","The album's American breakthrough came only after it was remixed for the US market, with some tracks given a harder, dance-club punch; the original British and the American versions sound noticeably different. The cover painting is by the American illustrator Patrick Nagel. The Chauffeur, the last track, is the band's most experimental song, built on a cold, ticking sequencer pattern rather than on the rhythm section, and it hints at the art-rock ambitions the band rarely showed.","British critics often dismissed Duran Duran as vain, pretty boys, especially compared with more earnest post-punk bands. But Rio sold enormously, and its reputation has steadily grown; musicians later praised the rhythm section's playing, and critics now hear it as one of the most confident pop albums of the decade and a model of the video age.","Start with Hungry Like the Wolf and listen to the bass line, then the title track. Watch the videos: they are part of the work. Save a Prayer shows the band's romantic side, and The Chauffeur their experimental edge. Afterwards, go back to Roxy Music's For Your Pleasure and David Bowie's Heroes, the band's clear ancestors."],"yazoo-only-you":["A gentle, simple, sad love song. A soft, rippling synthesizer arpeggio and a steady drum-machine pulse underpin Alison Moyet's rich, bluesy alto as she sings about a love that leaves her lonely and about the one person who can make things right. The melody is plain and memorable and the arrangement is almost bare, which leaves all the warmth to the voice. The result sounds like a soul ballad played on electronic instruments.","After leaving Depeche Mode at the end of 1981, Vince Clarke placed an advertisement in a music paper for a singer and was answered by Alison Moyet, whom he knew from Basildon, where she had sung in blues and punk bands. Only You was a song Clarke had written earlier; it became Yazoo's first single on Mute Records in 1982 and a British hit. The duo called themselves Yaz in the United States.","The song was reportedly one Clarke had offered to Depeche Mode before he left. In 1983 the a cappella group the Flying Pickets released a version sung entirely by voices, mimicking the synthesizer parts vocally, and it became the UK Christmas number one, a neat demonstration that underneath the machines this is a simple, old-fashioned song.","Critics praised the pairing of Clarke's precise machines with Moyet's earthy voice, an unusual combination that became Yazoo's signature. Only You remains one of the most covered and loved synth-pop ballads. It helped establish that electronic pop could carry genuine tenderness, a point previously in doubt among rock critics.","Listen to the synthesizer arpeggio and notice how steadily it runs under the voice. Then hear the Flying Pickets' vocal version for a different angle. Go on to Upstairs at Eric's to hear the rest of the duo's short career, and play Depeche Mode's Just Can't Get Enough to hear Clarke's earlier, sunnier style."],"yazoo-upstairs-at-erics":["Eleven songs, about forty minutes, pairing Vince Clarke's bright, bubbling synthesizers with Alison Moyet's big, bluesy voice. Don't Go is the fast, stomping single with its bounding synth riff; Only You and Midnight are slower and tender; Bad Connection and Goodbye 70's are playful dance tracks. Among them sits a strange sound collage, I Before E Except After C, made from overlapping spoken voices and taped fragments. The album moves between pop joy and melancholy.","It was the debut album of Yazoo, Vince Clarke's new duo after leaving Depeche Mode, released by Mute in 1982. The title refers to the studio where it was made, owned by the producer Eric Radcliffe, where Clarke and Moyet worked upstairs. Moyet had sung in rhythm-and-blues and punk bands in Essex, and her voice was very different from the cool, detached singers typical of synth-pop at the time.","The odd collage I Before E Except After C, built from recorded voices, shows Clarke's interest in experimental electronic music and tape techniques, a strand of his work often overlooked. Moyet and Clarke reportedly did not get on easily, and Yazoo made only two albums before splitting in 1983; Moyet went on to a solo career, Clarke to Erasure.","It was a big success in Britain and helped make Moyet a star. Critics praised its combination of warmth and precision and saw it as proof that synth-pop could accommodate a soulful singer. Its reputation has held up well; Don't Go and Situation, a dance track added to some editions, became favourites in American clubs and influenced early house music.","Play Don't Go loud and notice the interplay between synth riff and voice. Then listen to Midnight and Only You for the ballads. Try the strange collage track once, straight through. Afterwards hear Frankie Knuckles's Your Love and other early house records, to which Yazoo's club tracks were close cousins."],"a-ha-take-on-me":["A bright, speedy synth-pop song. It opens with a fizzing drum-machine beat and a bouncing, instantly memorable synthesizer riff that runs up and down like a fanfare. Morten Harket's voice leaps across a wide range, ending the chorus in a famous high falsetto note. The words are simple and eager, a young man asking someone to take a chance on him because he will soon be gone. It lasts under four minutes and never relaxes.","a-ha were three young Norwegians, Morten Harket, Magne Furuholmen and Pål Waaktaar, who moved to London to make it in the pop world. Take On Me was released twice in 1984 and flopped both times. Warner Bros. then had it re-recorded with the producer Alan Tarney and paired with a new video; released again in 1985, it reached number one in the United States and near the top across Europe.","The riff was written by Furuholmen when he was a teenager, long before a-ha existed, and it circulated in several of the band's earlier songs before finding its home. The famous video, directed by Steve Barron, mixes live action with pencil-sketch animation drawn over filmed footage, a laborious technique called rotoscoping, and much of its charm lies in that hand-drawn look.","It became one of the defining songs of the mid-eighties and its video won several MTV awards. Critics initially treated a-ha as a teen-idol band, but the song's craft has been increasingly admired; Harket's vocal range is often singled out. The video has passed a billion views online, making it one of the most-watched music videos from before the internet era.","Listen for the riff at the start, then wait for the high falsetto at the end of the chorus. Watch the video, which is as much a part of the song as the music. Afterwards, listen to Duran Duran's Rio and the Human League's Dare to hear other video-age synth-pop, and The Weeknd's Blinding Lights, which borrows the same bright, galloping energy."],"talk-talk-its-my-life":["A moody, mid-tempo synth-pop song. Paul Webb's fretless-sounding bass slides through the song, Lee Harris's drums are crisp and steady, and airy synthesizers shimmer and call like distant birds. Mark Hollis's cracked, pleading voice sings about his right to his own life and his own mind, a quiet declaration of independence. The chorus lifts with a soaring synth line, but the mood stays restless and uneasy.","Talk Talk formed in London around Mark Hollis, with Webb on bass and Harris on drums, and were initially marketed by EMI as a New Romantic synth band in the Duran Duran mould. It's My Life was the title song of their second album, co-written by Hollis and Tim Friese-Greene, the producer and keyboard player who became Hollis's essential collaborator. It was a hit across Europe but did only modestly in Britain on first release.","In the video, Hollis refuses to mime properly, standing sullenly while a cartoon and animal footage run around him, a mocking reply to the video-star role the label wanted him to play. That refusal is the key to the whole song and to Talk Talk's later history: the fierce privacy that would lead Hollis to abandon pop completely within four years.","It was well received in Europe and grew in reputation. In 2003 the American band No Doubt had a major hit with a cover, introducing it to a new audience. Critics now hear it as the point where Talk Talk's craft began to exceed the synth-pop format, a stepping stone toward the extraordinary albums that followed.","Listen for the bird-like synth calls and the sliding bass. Watch the video to see Hollis's silent protest. Then jump directly to Spirit of Eden, only four years later, and try to believe it is the same band. Tears for Fears' Songs from the Big Chair is a useful comparison: another synth band growing up."],"talk-talk-spirit-of-eden":["Six long songs, about forty minutes, of hushed, spacious, mostly acoustic music. Long silences, a quiet organ, a muted trumpet, harmonica, a distant choir, and sudden bursts of loud, distorted guitar alternate with passages that are barely there at all. Mark Hollis sings softly, sometimes almost inaudibly, about faith, addiction, nature and the search for grace. The opening The Rainbow takes minutes to emerge from near-silence; I Believe in You, with a boys' choir, is the emotional centre.","After two synth-pop hit albums, Talk Talk spent about a year in Wessex Studios in London recording hours of improvisation by a large group of session musicians, which Hollis and producer Tim Friese-Greene then edited and shaped into songs. EMI, expecting another hit album, were baffled by the result. Hollis refused to release singles or tour, and the relationship with the label collapsed into legal dispute.","The choir on I Believe in You is from Chelmsford Cathedral, and the song is Hollis's reaction to heroin addiction, which he had seen ruin people around him, including his own brother. Many of the most beautiful moments are edits from hours of improvisation, including musicians' mistakes deliberately kept. Hollis cited Miles Davis's Sketches of Spain and the French composers Debussy and Satie as influences.","It sold poorly, and most critics were puzzled or hostile at first. Over time it has become one of the most revered albums of its era, praised for its courage and its textures, and it is seen as a founding record of what came to be called post-rock. Bands from Radiohead to Sigur Rós have acknowledged its influence.","Listen in a quiet room, at a good volume, all the way through; do not skip the silences. Start with The Rainbow and notice how long it takes to arrive. Then play Miles Davis's In a Silent Way and Morton Feldman's Rothko Chapel to hear other music built on space and patience, and Radiohead's Kid A for what it influenced."],"talk-talk-laughing-stock":["Six songs in about forty-three minutes, even quieter and more skeletal than Spirit of Eden. Guitar notes hang in the air, drums play loose, jazz-like patterns, strings and horns come and go, and Mark Hollis's voice is often barely a murmur. After the Flood builds slowly to a squalling guitar climax; Ascension Day ends abruptly, as if the tape had been cut; New Grass floats on a gentle, rolling drum groove for almost ten minutes. Silence is as important as sound.","After leaving EMI, Talk Talk signed to the Polydor jazz imprint Verve. Only Hollis, the drummer Lee Harris and Tim Friese-Greene remained at the core; the bassist Paul Webb had left. They again recorded improvisations by many musicians at Wessex Studios, reportedly working in near-darkness with candles and an oil-slide projector, then spent months editing. It was the band's last album; Hollis made one quiet solo record in 1998 and then withdrew from music entirely.","The abrupt ending of Ascension Day is deliberate: the song simply stops mid-phrase, a cut in the tape rather than a fade. Much of the album is assembled from separate takes by musicians who did not play together, and the players were sometimes not told what the song was. Listen closely and you hear chairs creaking, breathing and the room itself, kept in the mix.","It was barely noticed on release and sold poorly. Within a few years it became a cult reference point, and the critic Simon Reynolds cited Talk Talk when coining the term post-rock in 1994. Today it is widely admired as one of the purest statements of its kind, the endpoint of a band that walked away from pop success on principle.","Listen from start to finish, preferably on headphones, at night. Start with New Grass if you want a gentler way in. Compare with Spirit of Eden to hear how much further this goes into stillness. Slint's Spiderland, released the same year, and Mark Hollis's solo album are the natural next steps; Arvo Pärt's Für Alina shares its sense of space."],"the-cure-the-lovecats":["A playful, jazzy pop song with a walking double bass, tinkling piano, brushed drums and finger-snaps. Robert Smith sings in a mischievous, yelping voice about two lovers as a pair of cats, prowling and purring and tumbling together through the night. The song feels like a cartoon soundtrack or a cabaret number, with scat-like vocal flourishes and a loose, swinging rhythm entirely unlike the Cure's gloomier records.","In 1982 the Cure had released Pornography, a harrowing, heavy album, and the band nearly fell apart under the strain. Robert Smith responded by deliberately making lighthearted pop singles for their label Fiction, beginning with Let's Go to Bed and The Walk, both electronic. The Lovecats, released in 1983, was a sharp turn to acoustic jazz and became the band's first top-ten hit in Britain.","Smith has said the song was inspired partly by the Disney film The Aristocats, and partly by a drunken night and a story about a house full of cats. It was a deliberate joke on the Cure's gloomy image, but it also revealed Smith's gift for melody. Its jazz band, led by a double bass, has more in common with Tom Waits than with post-punk.","It surprised fans and critics, some of whom thought the band had sold out. But it made the Cure a mainstream pop act and showed that Smith could write successful, lighthearted songs. It is now regarded as one of the charming oddities of eighties pop, and the key to the band's later dual life as both gloom merchants and hit-makers.","Listen for the walking double bass and the piano, then compare with the Cure's Pornography or Siouxsie and the Banshees' Juju for the darker sound Smith was escaping. Just Like Heaven, four years later, shows the pop and melancholy combined. For more jazz-tinged whimsy, try Tom Waits or the Kinks."],"the-cure-just-like-heaven":["A fast, bright, bittersweet song, three and a half minutes long. It begins with an insistent drum pattern and a chiming, rolling bass line, then a cascading keyboard melody and a jangling guitar arrive before Robert Smith sings. The song describes a single dizzy night by the sea, a kiss and a moment of perfect happiness, then ends with a sense of loss, as if the memory might have been a dream. The long instrumental opening is half the song.","It came from the double album Kiss Me, Kiss Me, Kiss Me (1987), made at a studio in the south of France during the band's most commercially successful period. Smith has said the song was inspired by a trip to Beachy Head, the chalk cliffs on the English south coast, with Mary Poole, his girlfriend and later his wife, and the video was filmed there. It became the Cure's first top-forty hit in the United States.","The instrumental opening was reportedly first written for a French television music programme, which used it as a theme, before Smith added a vocal. The song's apparent romance has a sting: Smith has said the ending, where happiness slips away, is the point. Mary Poole appears in the video, dancing with Smith on the cliffs, a rare personal glimpse from a notoriously private band.","It became one of the Cure's most loved songs and a staple of college radio. The American band Dinosaur Jr made a loud, abrupt cover in 1989 that brought it to indie-rock audiences. Critics now often call it the best song Smith wrote, the perfect meeting of the Cure's melancholy and their pop gift.","Listen to the instrumental opening, noticing how each part enters in turn. Then follow the story in the words: the night, the kiss, the loss. Compare it with The Lovecats and with the Smiths' How Soon Is Now? for other British guitar bands of the eighties balancing gloom and pop."],"cyndi-lauper-girls-just-want-to-have-fun":["A bouncing, exuberant new-wave pop song, about four minutes long. Chirpy synthesizers and a skipping, reggae-tinged rhythm open it; guitar and bass fill in; and Cyndi Lauper's piercing, elastic voice sings about a young woman who wants to escape parental scolding, a boyfriend's control and the daily grind, and simply enjoy herself with her friends. The chorus is a chant that sounds like playground singing. Hiccups and squeals in the voice give it a cartoon energy.","The song was written by Robert Hazard, who recorded a demo in 1979 from a man's perspective. Lauper, a New York singer who had spent years in cover bands and a failed group called Blue Angel, rewrote the lyrics to make it a statement of female solidarity. It was the first single from her debut album She's So Unusual, released on Portrait in 1983, produced by Rick Chertoff with members of the Philadelphia band the Hooters.","In Hazard's original the song was about a man boasting of the girls who wanted him; Lauper's rewrite turned it into a song about women's freedom, a change she insisted on. The video, with its crowded, joyous street scenes, features her real mother and the wrestler Captain Lou Albano as her father. Lauper deliberately cast friends and family rather than models.","It reached number two in the United States and became an international hit. The video won an MTV award and made Lauper a star. Critics praised its humour and its message, and the song has since become an enduring feminist anthem, re-recorded by Lauper herself and covered by many. It is often named among the best pop songs of the decade.","Watch the video first; it is essential. Then listen to how Lauper's voice jumps from high squeaks to growls. Compare it with Madonna's Like a Virgin from the next year, another way to be a woman in eighties pop, and follow with Time After Time, Lauper's gentle ballad, to hear her range."],"cyndi-lauper-time-after-time":["A slow, tender ballad of about four minutes. A soft, ticking rhythm that sounds like a clock, gentle synthesizer chords and a chiming guitar make a quiet bed for Cyndi Lauper's voice, here unusually soft and intimate. She sings about a relationship in which one person will always be there for the other, catching them whenever they fall. The chorus rises gently, and the whole song has the sound of a promise kept.","Lauper wrote the song with Rob Hyman of the Hooters during the recording of She's So Unusual; it was one of the last songs finished. The title came from a TV listing for the 1979 film Time After Time, a science-fiction story in which H. G. Wells chases Jack the Ripper through time. The ballad was released in 1984 as the album's second single and became her first number one in the United States.","The ticking rhythm is an echo of the clock image in the title and the film. The song's quiet confidence comes partly from Lauper's restraint: after the hiccuping exuberance of Girls Just Want to Have Fun, she sings here without vocal tricks. In 1985 Miles Davis recorded an instrumental version, which he played in concert for the rest of his life.","It became Lauper's most enduring song, covered by artists from jazz to pop. Critics praised its warmth and simplicity, and Miles Davis's adoption of it gave it a unique place bridging pop and jazz. It is regularly listed among the best ballads of the eighties and has been used in countless films and television programmes.","Listen for the ticking rhythm and the soft guitar, and notice how Lauper keeps her voice restrained. Then hear Girls Just Want to Have Fun to feel the contrast. Find Miles Davis's version from his album You're Under Arrest for a trumpet reading of the melody, and compare with Yazoo's Only You, another tender electronic ballad."],"culture-club-do-you-really-want-to-hurt-me":["A slow, swaying pop song over a lilting reggae rhythm. The bass is deep and rounded, the drums lope rather than drive, and gentle keyboards and guitar fill the gaps. Boy George sings in a soft, smoky, soulful voice about a love affair in which he feels wounded and asks whether his lover truly means to cause him pain. The chorus is simple, quiet and sad, and the whole song feels more like lovers' rock than new wave.","Culture Club formed in London in 1981 around Boy George, a flamboyant figure from the New Romantic club scene, with Mikey Craig, Roy Hay and Jon Moss. Their mix of soul, reggae and pop, and George's androgynous style, made them different from the synth-led bands of the time. This was their third single, from the album Kissing to Be Clever, produced by Steve Levine for Virgin, and it reached number one in Britain in 1982.","The song was written in part about George's secret relationship with the band's drummer, Jon Moss, which was hidden from the public at the time. The reggae rhythm connects Culture Club to London's lovers' rock scene, the romantic British reggae of the late seventies. George's first appearance on Top of the Pops, singing this song, caused a national sensation over whether he was a man or a woman.","It was a huge international hit and made Culture Club one of the biggest bands of the early eighties. Critics praised George's voice and the band's blend of styles. Later the song came to be seen as an important moment in the visibility of queer identity in mainstream pop, and it remains the band's most admired song, alongside Karma Chameleon.","Listen for the reggae rhythm and the soft, deep bass. Notice how George sings quietly rather than belting. Then hear Bob Marley's Catch a Fire for the reggae roots and Smokey Robinson's Tracks of My Tears for the soul lineage. Compare it with Duran Duran's Rio, from the same London club scene, for a flashier approach."]});